.SS Precompiled binary, using curl
To avoid problems with macOS gatekeeper enforcing the binary to be
signed and notarized it is enough to download with \f[CR]curl\f[R].
.PP
Download the latest version of rclone.
.IP
.EX
cd && curl \-O https://downloads.rclone.org/rclone\-current\-osx\-amd64.zip
.EE
.PP
Unzip the download and cd to the extracted folder.
.IP
.EX
unzip \-a rclone\-current\-osx\-amd64.zip && cd rclone\-*\-osx\-amd64
.EE
.PP
Move rclone to your $PATH.
You will be prompted for your password.
.IP
.EX
sudo mkdir \-p /usr/local/bin
sudo mv rclone /usr/local/bin/
.EE
.PP
(the \f[CR]mkdir\f[R] command is safe to run, even if the directory
already exists).
.PP
Remove the leftover files.
.IP
.EX
cd .. && rm \-rf rclone\-*\-osx\-amd64 rclone\-current\-osx\-amd64.zip
.EE
.PP
Run \f[CR]rclone config\f[R] to setup.
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/
rclone config docs
.UE \c
\ for more details.
.IP
.EX
rclone config
.EE
.SS Precompiled binary, using a web browser
When downloading a binary with a web browser, the browser will set the
macOS gatekeeper quarantine attribute.
Starting from Catalina, when attempting to run \f[CR]rclone\f[R], a
pop\-up will appear saying:
.IP
.EX
\(dqrclone\(dq cannot be opened because the developer cannot be verified.
macOS cannot verify that this app is free from malware.
.EE
.PP
The simplest fix is to run
.IP
.EX
xattr \-d com.apple.quarantine rclone
.EE
