.SS Docker installation
The rclone developers maintain a \c
.UR https://hub.docker.com/r/rclone/rclone
docker image for rclone
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
These images are built as part of the release process based on a minimal
Alpine Linux.
.PP
The \f[CR]:latest\f[R] tag will always point to the latest stable
release.
You can use the \f[CR]:beta\f[R] tag to get the latest build from
master.
You can also use version tags, e.g.
\f[CR]:1.49.1\f[R], \f[CR]:1.49\f[R] or \f[CR]:1\f[R].
.IP
.EX
$ docker pull rclone/rclone:latest
latest: Pulling from rclone/rclone
Digest: sha256:0e0ced72671989bb837fea8e88578b3fc48371aa45d209663683e24cfdaa0e11
\&...
$ docker run \-\-rm rclone/rclone:latest version
rclone v1.49.1
\- os/arch: linux/amd64
\- go version: go1.12.9
.EE
.PP
There are a few command line options to consider when starting an rclone
Docker container from the rclone image.
.IP \(bu 2
You need to mount the host rclone config dir at
\f[CR]/config/rclone\f[R] into the Docker container.
Due to the fact that rclone updates tokens inside its config file, and
that the update process involves a file rename, you need to mount the
whole host rclone config dir, not just the single host rclone config
file.
.IP \(bu 2
You need to mount a host data dir at \f[CR]/data\f[R] into the Docker
container.
.IP \(bu 2
By default, the rclone binary inside a Docker container runs with UID=0
(root).
As a result, all files created in a run will have UID=0.
If your config and data files reside on the host with a non\-root
UID:GID, you need to pass these on the container start command line.
.IP \(bu 2
If you want to access the RC interface (either via the API or the Web
UI), it is required to set the \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R] to
\f[CR]:5572\f[R] in order to connect to it from outside the container.
An explanation about why this is necessary can be found in an old \c
.UR https://web.archive.org/web/20200808071950/https://pythonspeed.com/articles/docker-connection-refused/
pythonspeed.com
.UE \c
\ article.
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
NOTE: Users running this container with the docker network set to
\f[CR]host\f[R] should probably set it to listen to localhost only, with
\f[CR]127.0.0.1:5572\f[R] as the value for \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
It is possible to use \f[CR]rclone mount\f[R] inside a userspace Docker
container, and expose the resulting fuse mount to the host.
The exact \f[CR]docker run\f[R] options to do that might vary slightly
between hosts.
See, e.g.
the discussion in this \c
.UR https://github.com/moby/moby/issues/9448
thread
.UE \c
\&.
.RS 2
.PP
You also need to mount the host \f[CR]/etc/passwd\f[R] and
\f[CR]/etc/group\f[R] for fuse to work inside the container.
.RE
.PP
Here are some commands tested on an Ubuntu 18.04.3 host:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# config on host at \(ti/.config/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R]
\f[I]# data on host at \(ti/data\f[R]

\f[I]# add a remote interactively\f[R]
docker run \-\-rm \-it \(rs
    \-\-volume \(ti/.config/rclone:/config/rclone \(rs
    \-\-user $(id \-u):$(id \-g) \(rs
    rclone/rclone \(rs
    config

\f[I]# make sure the config is ok by listing the remotes\f[R]
docker run \-\-rm \(rs
    \-\-volume \(ti/.config/rclone:/config/rclone \(rs
    \-\-user $(id \-u):$(id \-g) \(rs
    rclone/rclone \(rs
    listremotes

\f[I]# perform mount inside Docker container, expose result to host\f[R]
mkdir \-p \(ti/data/mount
docker run \-\-rm \(rs
    \-\-volume \(ti/.config/rclone:/config/rclone \(rs
    \-\-volume \(ti/data:/data:shared \(rs
    \-\-user $(id \-u):$(id \-g) \(rs
    \-\-volume /etc/passwd:/etc/passwd:ro \-\-volume /etc/group:/etc/group:ro \(rs
    \-\-device /dev/fuse \-\-cap\-add SYS_ADMIN \-\-security\-opt apparmor:unconfined \(rs
    rclone/rclone \(rs
    mount dropbox:Photos /data/mount \f[B]&\f[R]
ls \(ti/data/mount
kill %1
.EE
