.SS Source installation
Make sure you have git and \c
.UR https://golang.org/
Go
.UE \c
\ installed.
Go version 1.26 or newer is required, the latest release is recommended.
You can get it from your package manager, or download it from \c
.UR https://golang.org/dl/
golang.org/dl
.UE \c
\&.
Then you can run the following:
.IP
.EX
git clone https://github.com/rclone/rclone.git
cd rclone
go build
.EE
.PP
This will check out the rclone source in subfolder rclone, which you can
later modify and send pull requests with.
Then it will build the rclone executable in the same folder.
As an initial check you can now run \f[CR]./rclone version\f[R]
(\f[CR].\(rsrclone version\f[R] on Windows).
.PP
Note that on macOS and Windows the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_mount/
mount
.UE \c
\ command will not be available unless you specify an additional build
tag \f[CR]cmount\f[R].
.IP
.EX
go build \-tags cmount
.EE
.PP
This assumes you have a GCC compatible C compiler (GCC or Clang) in your
PATH, as it uses \c
.UR https://pkg.go.dev/cmd/cgo
cgo
.UE \c
\&.
But on Windows, the \c
.UR https://github.com/winfsp/cgofuse
cgofuse
.UE \c
\ library that the cmount implementation is based on, also supports
building \c
.UR https://github.com/golang/go/wiki/WindowsDLLs
without cgo
.UE \c
, i.e.
by setting environment variable CGO_ENABLED to value 0 (static linking).
This is how the official Windows release of rclone is being built,
starting with version 1.59.
It is still possible to build with cgo on Windows as well, by using the
MinGW port of GCC, e.g.
by installing it in a \c
.UR https://www.msys2.org
MSYS2
.UE \c
\ distribution (make sure you install it in the classic mingw64
subsystem, the ucrt64 version is not compatible).
.PP
Additionally, to build with mount on Windows, you must install the third
party utility \c
.UR https://winfsp.dev/
WinFsp
.UE \c
, with the \(dqDeveloper\(dq feature selected.
If building with cgo, you must also set environment variable CPATH
pointing to the fuse include directory within the WinFsp installation
(normally
\f[CR]C:\(rsProgram Files (x86)\(rsWinFsp\(rsinc\(rsfuse\f[R]).
.PP
You may add arguments \f[CR]\-ldflags \-s\f[R] to omit symbol table and
debug information, making the executable file smaller, and
\f[CR]\-trimpath\f[R] to remove references to local file system paths.
The official rclone releases are built with both of these.
.IP
.EX
go build \-trimpath \-ldflags \-s \-tags cmount
.EE
.PP
If you want to customize the version string, as reported by the
\f[CR]rclone version\f[R] command, you can set one of the variables
\f[CR]fs.Version\f[R], \f[CR]fs.VersionTag\f[R] (to keep default suffix
but customize the number), or \f[CR]fs.VersionSuffix\f[R] (to keep
default number but customize the suffix).
This can be done from the build command, by adding to the
\f[CR]\-ldflags\f[R] argument value as shown below.
.IP
.EX
go build \-trimpath \-ldflags \(dq\-s \-X github.com/rclone/rclone/fs.Version=v9.9.9\-test\(dq \-tags cmount
.EE
.PP
On Windows, the official executables also have the version information,
as well as a file icon, embedded as binary resources.
To get that with your own build you need to run the following command
\f[B]before\f[R] the build command.
It generates a Windows resource system object file, with extension
\&.syso, e.g.
\f[CR]resource_windows_amd64.syso\f[R], that will be automatically
picked up by future build commands.
.IP
.EX
go run bin/resource_windows.go
.EE
.PP
The above command will generate a resource file containing version
information based on the fs.Version variable in source at the time you
run the command, which means if the value of this variable changes you
need to re\-run the command for it to be reflected in the version
information.
Also, if you override this version variable in the build command as
described above, you need to do that also when generating the resource
file, or else it will still use the value from the source.
.IP
.EX
go run bin/resource_windows.go \-version v9.9.9\-test
.EE
.PP
Instead of executing the \f[CR]go build\f[R] command directly, you can
run it via the Makefile.
The default target changes the version suffix from \(dq\-DEV\(dq to
\(dq\-beta\(dq followed by additional commit details, embeds version
information binary resources on Windows, and copies the resulting rclone
executable into your GOPATH bin folder (\f[CR]$(go env GOPATH)/bin\f[R],
which corresponds to \f[CR]\(ti/go/bin/rclone\f[R] by default).
.IP
.EX
make
.EE
.PP
To include mount command on macOS and Windows with Makefile build:
.IP
.EX
make GOTAGS=cmount
.EE
.PP
There are other make targets that can be used for more advanced builds,
such as cross\-compiling for all supported os/architectures, and
packaging results into release artifacts.
See \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/blob/master/Makefile
Makefile
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/blob/master/bin/cross-compile.go
cross\-compile.go
.UE \c
\ for details.
.PP
Another alternative method for source installation is to download the
source, build and install rclone \- all in one operation, as a regular
Go package.
The source will be stored it in the Go module cache, and the resulting
executable will be in your GOPATH bin folder
(\f[CR]$(go env GOPATH)/bin\f[R], which corresponds to
\f[CR]\(ti/go/bin/rclone\f[R] by default).
.IP
.EX
go install github.com/rclone/rclone\(atlatest
.EE
.PP
In some situations, rclone executable size might be too big for
deployment in very restricted environments when all backends with large
SDKs are included.
To limit binary size unused backends can be commented out in
\f[CR]backends/all/all.go\f[R] and unused commands in
\f[CR]cmd/all/all.go\f[R] before building with \f[CR]go build\f[R] or
\f[CR]make\f[R]
.SS Ansible installation
This can be done with \c
