.SS Basic syntax
Rclone syncs a directory tree from one storage system to another.
.PP
Its syntax is like this
.IP
.EX
rclone subcommand [options] <parameters> <parameters...>
.EE
.PP
A \f[CR]subcommand\f[R] is an rclone operation required (e.g.
\f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]copy\f[R], \f[CR]ls\f[R]).
.PP
An \f[CR]option\f[R] is a single letter flag (e.g.
\f[CR]\-v\f[R]) or a group of single letter flags (e.g.
\f[CR]\-Pv\f[R]) or a long flag (e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-progress\f[R]).
No options are required.
Options can come after the \f[CR]subcommand\f[R] or in between
parameters too or on the end, but only global options can be used before
the \f[CR]subcommand\f[R].
Anything after a \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] option will not be interpreted as an
option so if you need to add a parameter which starts with a
\f[CR]\-\f[R] then put a \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] on its own first, eg
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf \-\- \-directory\-starting\-with\-dash
.EE
.PP
A \f[CR]parameter\f[R] is usually a file path or rclone remote, eg
\f[CR]/path/to/file\f[R] or \f[CR]remote:path/to/file\f[R] but it can be
other things \- the \f[CR]subcommand\f[R] help will tell you what.
.PP
Source and destination paths are specified by the name you gave the
storage system in the config file then the sub path, e.g.
\(dqdrive:myfolder\(dq to look at \(dqmyfolder\(dq in Google drive.
.PP
You can define as many storage paths as you like in the config file.
.PP
Please use the \f[CR]\-\-interactive\f[R]/\f[CR]\-i\f[R] flag while
learning rclone to avoid accidental data loss.
.SS Subcommands
rclone uses a system of subcommands.
For example
.IP
.EX
rclone ls remote:path # lists a remote
rclone copy /local/path remote:path # copies /local/path to the remote
rclone sync \-\-interactive /local/path remote:path # syncs /local/path to the remote
.EE
