.SH rclone sync
Make source and dest identical, modifying destination only.
.SS Synopsis
Sync the source to the destination, changing the destination only.
Doesn\(aqt transfer files that are identical on source and destination,
testing by size and modification time or MD5SUM.
Destination is updated to match source, including deleting files if
necessary (except duplicate objects, see below).
If you don\(aqt want to delete files from destination, use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ command instead.
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: Since this can cause data loss, test first with the
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] or the \f[CR]\-\-interactive\f[R]/\f[CR]i\f[R]
flag.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive SOURCE remote:DESTINATION
.EE
.PP
Files in the destination won\(aqt be deleted if there were any errors at
any point.
Duplicate objects (files with the same name, on those providers that
support it) are not yet handled.
Files that are excluded won\(aqt be deleted unless
\f[CR]\-\-delete\-excluded\f[R] is used.
Symlinks won\(aqt be transferred or deleted from local file systems
unless \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] is used.
.PP
It is always the contents of the directory that is synced, not the
directory itself.
So when source:path is a directory, it\(aqs the contents of source:path
that are copied, not the directory name and contents.
See extended explanation in the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ command if unsure.
.PP
If dest:path doesn\(aqt exist, it is created and the source:path
contents go there.
.PP
It is not possible to sync overlapping remotes.
However, you may exclude the destination from the sync with a filter
rule or by putting an exclude\-if\-present file inside the destination
directory and sync to a destination that is inside the source directory.
.PP
Rclone will sync the modification times of files and directories if the
backend supports it.
If metadata syncing is required then use the \f[CR]\-\-metadata\f[R]
flag.
.PP
Note that the modification time and metadata for the root directory will
\f[B]not\f[R] be synced.
See \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/7652
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Use the \f[CR]\-P\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-progress\f[R] flag to
view real\-time transfer statistics
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R]: Use the \f[CR]rclone dedupe\f[R] command to deal with
\(dqDuplicate object/directory found in source/destination \-
ignoring\(dq errors.
See \c
.UR https://forum.rclone.org/t/sync-not-clearing-duplicates/14372
this forum post
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.SS Logger Flags
The \f[CR]\-\-differ\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-missing\-on\-dst\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-missing\-on\-src\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-match\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-error\f[R] flags write paths, one per line, to the file name
(or stdout if it is \f[CR]\-\f[R]) supplied.
What they write is described in the help below.
For example \f[CR]\-\-differ\f[R] will write all paths which are present
on both the source and destination but different.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-combined\f[R] flag will write a file (or stdout) which
contains all file paths with a symbol and then a space and then the path
to tell you what happened to it.
These are reminiscent of diff files.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]= path\f[R] means path was found in source and destination and was
identical
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\- path\f[R] means path was missing on the source, so only in the
destination
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]+ path\f[R] means path was missing on the destination, so only in
the source
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]* path\f[R] means path was present in source and destination but
different.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]! path\f[R] means there was an error reading or hashing the source
or dest.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-dest\-after\f[R] flag writes a list file using the same
format flags as \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_lsf/#synopsis
\f[CR]lsf\f[R]
.UE \c
\ (including \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_lsf/#synopsis
customizable options for hash, modtime, etc.
.UE \c
) Conceptually it is similar to rsync\(aqs
\f[CR]\-\-itemize\-changes\f[R], but not identical \-\- it should output
an accurate list of what will be on the destination after the command is
finished.
.PP
When the \f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] flag is set, all logs involving
files that exist only on the destination will be incomplete or
completely missing.
.PP
Note that these logger flags have a few limitations, and certain
scenarios are not currently supported:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-max\-duration\f[R] / \f[CR]CutoffModeHard\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-compare\-dest\f[R] / \f[CR]\-\-copy\-dest\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
server\-side moves of an entire dir at once
.IP \(bu 2
High\-level retries, because there would be duplicates (use
\f[CR]\-\-retries 1\f[R] to disable)
.IP \(bu 2
Possibly some unusual error scenarios
.PP
Note also that each file is logged during execution, as opposed to
after, so it is most useful as a predictor of what SHOULD happen to each
file (which may or may not match what actually DID).
.IP
.EX
rclone sync source:path dest:path [flags]
.EE
