.SH rclone delete
Remove the files in path.
.SS Synopsis
Remove the files in path.
Unlike \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_purge/
purge
.UE \c
\ it obeys include/exclude filters so can be used to selectively delete
files.
.PP
\f[CR]rclone delete\f[R] only deletes files but leaves the directory
structure alone.
If you want to delete a directory and all of its contents use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_purge/
purge
.UE \c
\ command.
.PP
If you supply the \f[CR]\-\-rmdirs\f[R] flag, it will remove all empty
directories along with it.
You can also use the separate command \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_rmdir/
rmdir
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_rmdirs/
rmdirs
.UE \c
\ to delete empty directories only.
.PP
For example, to delete all files bigger than 100 MiB, you may first want
to check what would be deleted (use either):
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-min\-size 100M lsl remote:path
rclone \-\-dry\-run \-\-min\-size 100M delete remote:path
.EE
.PP
Then proceed with the actual delete:
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-min\-size 100M delete remote:path
.EE
.PP
That reads \(dqdelete everything with a minimum size of 100 MiB\(dq,
hence delete all files bigger than 100 MiB.
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: Since this can cause data loss, test first with the
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] or the \f[CR]\-\-interactive\f[R]/\f[CR]\-i\f[R]
flag.
.IP
.EX
rclone delete remote:path [flags]
.EE
.SS Options
.IP
.EX
  \-h, \-\-help     help for delete
      \-\-rmdirs   rmdirs removes empty directories but leaves root intact
.EE
