.SH rclone cat
Concatenates any files and sends them to stdout.
.SS Synopsis
Sends any files to standard output.
.PP
You can use it like this to output a single file
.IP
.EX
rclone cat remote:path/to/file
.EE
.PP
Or like this to output any file in dir or its subdirectories.
.IP
.EX
rclone cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE
.PP
Or like this to output any .txt files in dir or its subdirectories.
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-include \(dq*.txt\(dq cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE
.PP
Use the \f[CR]\-\-head\f[R] flag to print characters only at the start,
\f[CR]\-\-tail\f[R] for the end and \f[CR]\-\-offset\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-count\f[R] to print a section in the middle.
Note that if offset is negative it will count from the end, so
\f[CR]\-\-offset \-1 \-\-count 1\f[R] is equivalent to
\f[CR]\-\-tail 1\f[R].
.PP
Use the \f[CR]\-\-separator\f[R] flag to print a separator value between
files.
Be sure to shell\-escape special characters.
For example, to print a newline between files, use:
.IP \(bu 2
bash:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-include \(dq*.txt\(dq \-\-separator $\(aq\(rsn\(aq cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
powershell:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-include \(dq*.txt\(dq \-\-separator \(dq\(gan\(dq cat remote:path/to/dir
.EE
.RE
.IP
.EX
rclone cat remote:path [flags]
.EE
