.SH rclone copyurl
Copy the contents of the URL supplied content to dest:path.
.SS Synopsis
Download a URL\(aqs content and copy it to the destination without
saving it in temporary storage.
.PP
Setting \f[CR]\-\-auto\-filename\f[R] will attempt to automatically
determine the filename from the URL (after any redirections) and used in
the destination path.
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-header\-filename\f[R] in addition, if a specific filename
is set in HTTP headers, it will be used instead of the name from the
URL.
With \f[CR]\-\-print\-filename\f[R] in addition, the resulting file name
will be printed.
.PP
Setting \f[CR]\-\-no\-clobber\f[R] will prevent overwriting file on the
destination if there is one with the same name.
.PP
Setting \f[CR]\-\-stdout\f[R] or making the output file name
\f[CR]\-\f[R] will cause the output to be written to standard output.
.PP
Setting \f[CR]\-\-urls\f[R] allows you to input a CSV file of URLs in
format: URL, FILENAME.
If \f[CR]\-\-urls\f[R] is in use then replace the URL in the arguments
with the file containing the URLs, e.g.:
.IP
.EX
rclone copyurl \-\-urls myurls.csv remote:dir
.EE
.PP
Missing filenames will be autogenerated equivalent to using
\f[CR]\-\-auto\-filename\f[R].
Note that \f[CR]\-\-stdout\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-print\-filename\f[R] are
incompatible with \f[CR]\-\-urls\f[R].
This will do \f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] copies in parallel.
Note that if \f[CR]\-\-auto\-filename\f[R] is desired for all URLs then
a file with only URLs and no filename can be used.
.PP
Each FILENAME in the CSV file can start with a relative path which will
be appended to the destination path provided at the command line.
For example, running the command shown above with the following CSV file
will write two files to the destination:
\f[CR]remote:dir/local/path/bar.json\f[R] and
\f[CR]remote:dir/another/local/directory/qux.json\f[R]
.IP
.EX
https://example.org/foo/bar.json,local/path/bar.json
https://example.org/qux/baz.json,another/local/directory/qux.json
.EE
.SS Troubleshooting
If you can\(aqt get \f[CR]rclone copyurl\f[R] to work then here are some
things you can try:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-disable\-http2\f[R] rclone will use HTTP2 if available \- try
disabling it
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-bind 0.0.0.0\f[R] rclone will use IPv6 if available \- try
disabling it
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-bind ::0\f[R] to disable IPv4
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-user agent curl\f[R] \- some sites have whitelists for
curl\(aqs user\-agent \- try that
.IP \(bu 2
Make sure the site works with \f[CR]curl\f[R] directly
.IP
.EX
rclone copyurl https://example.com dest:path [flags]
.EE
.SS Options
.IP
.EX
  \-a, \-\-auto\-filename     Get the file name from the URL and use it for destination file path
      \-\-header\-filename   Get the file name from the Content\-Disposition header
  \-h, \-\-help              help for copyurl
      \-\-no\-clobber        Prevent overwriting file with same name
  \-p, \-\-print\-filename    Print the resulting name from \-\-auto\-filename
      \-\-stdout            Write the output to stdout rather than a file
      \-\-urls              Use a CSV file of links to process multiple URLs
.EE
