.SH rclone rc
Run a command against a running rclone.
.SS Synopsis
This runs a command against a running rclone.
Use the \f[CR]\-\-url\f[R] flag to specify an non default URL to connect
on.
This can be either a \(dq:port\(dq which is taken to mean
http://localhost:port or a \(dqhost:port\(dq which is taken to mean
http://host:port.
.PP
A username and password can be passed in with \f[CR]\-\-user\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-pass\f[R].
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-rc\-user\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-pass\f[R] will be read also for \f[CR]\-\-url\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-user\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-pass\f[R].
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-unix\-socket\f[R] flag can be used to connect over a unix
socket like this
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# start server on /tmp/my.socket\f[R]
rclone rcd \-\-rc\-addr unix:///tmp/my.socket
\f[I]# Connect to it\f[R]
rclone rc \-\-unix\-socket /tmp/my.socket core/stats
.EE
.PP
Arguments should be passed in as parameter=value.
.PP
The result will be returned as a JSON object by default.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] parameter can be used to pass in a JSON blob as
an input instead of key=value arguments.
This is the only way of passing in more complicated values.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-o\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-opt\f[R] option can be used to set a key
\(dqopt\(dq with key, value options in the form \f[CR]\-o key=value\f[R]
or \f[CR]\-o key\f[R].
It can be repeated as many times as required.
This is useful for rc commands which take the \(dqopt\(dq parameter
which by convention is a dictionary of strings.
.IP
.EX
\-o key=value \-o key2
.EE
.PP
Will place this in the \(dqopt\(dq value
.IP
.EX
{\(dqkey\(dq:\(dqvalue\(dq, \(dqkey2\(dq\f[B],\f[R]\(dq\(dq\f[B])\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-a\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-arg\f[R] option can be used to set strings
in the \(dqarg\(dq value.
It can be repeated as many times as required.
This is useful for rc commands which take the \(dqarg\(dq parameter
which by convention is a list of strings.
.IP
.EX
\-a value \-a value2
.EE
.PP
Will place this in the \(dqarg\(dq value
.IP
.EX
[\(dqvalue\(dq, \(dqvalue2\(dq]
.EE
.PP
Use \f[CR]\-\-loopback\f[R] to connect to the rclone instance running
\f[CR]rclone rc\f[R].
This is very useful for testing commands without having to run an rclone
rc server, e.g.:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-loopback operations/about fs=/
.EE
.PP
Use \f[CR]rclone rc\f[R] to see a list of all possible commands.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc commands parameter [flags]
.EE
.SS Options
.IP
.EX
  \-a, \-\-arg stringArray      Argument placed in the \(dqarg\(dq array
  \-h, \-\-help                 help for rc
      \-\-json string          Input JSON \- use instead of key=value args
      \-\-loopback             If set connect to this rclone instance not via HTTP
      \-\-no\-output            If set, don\(aqt output the JSON result
  \-o, \-\-opt stringArray      Option in the form name=value or name placed in the \(dqopt\(dq array
      \-\-pass string          Password to use to connect to rclone remote control
      \-\-unix\-socket string   Path to a unix domain socket to dial to, instead of opening a TCP connection directly
      \-\-url string           URL to connect to rclone remote control (default \(dqhttp://localhost:5572/\(dq)
      \-\-user string          Username to use to rclone remote control
.EE
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/flags/
global flags page
.UE \c
\ for global options not listed here.
