.SH rclone rcd
Run rclone listening to remote control commands only.
.SS Synopsis
This runs rclone so that it only listens to remote control commands.
.PP
This is useful if you are controlling rclone via the rc API.
.PP
If you pass in a path to a directory, rclone will serve that directory
for GET requests on the URL passed in.
It will also open the URL in the browser when rclone is run.
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/rc/
rc documentation
.UE \c
\ for more info on the rc flags.
.SS Server options
Use \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R] to specify which IP address and port the
server should listen on, eg \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr 1.2.3.4:8000\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr :8080\f[R] to listen to all IPs.
By default it only listens on localhost.
You can use port :0 to let the OS choose an available port.
.PP
If you set \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R] to listen on a public or LAN
accessible IP address then using Authentication is advised \- see the
next section for info.
.PP
You can use a unix socket by setting the url to
\f[CR]unix:///path/to/socket\f[R] or just by using an absolute path
name.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R] may be repeated to listen on multiple
IPs/ports/sockets.
Socket activation, described further below, can also be used to
accomplish the same.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-server\-read\-timeout\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-server\-write\-timeout\f[R] can be used to control the
timeouts on the server.
Note that this is the total time for a transfer.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-max\-header\-bytes\f[R] controls the maximum number of
bytes the server will accept in the HTTP header.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-response\-header\f[R] can be used to set an HTTP header
for all responses, will overriding existing values.
The flag may be repeated to add multiple headers.
Use the format \f[CR]Header\-Name: value\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-baseurl\f[R] controls the URL prefix that rclone serves
from.
By default rclone will serve from the root.
If you used \f[CR]\-\-rc\-baseurl \(dq/rclone\(dq\f[R] then rclone would
serve from a URL starting with \(dq/rclone/\(dq.
This is useful if you wish to proxy rclone serve.
Rclone automatically inserts leading and trailing \(dq/\(dq on
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-baseurl\f[R], so
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-baseurl \(dqrclone\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-baseurl \(dq/rclone\(dq\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-baseurl \(dq/rclone/\(dq\f[R] are all treated identically.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-disable\-zip\f[R] may be set to disable the zipping
download option.
.SS Protocol
The server supports HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2.
HTTP/2 is used automatically for TLS connections.
For non\-TLS connections, HTTP/2 cleartext (h2c) is supported, allowing
HTTP/2 without encryption.
.SS TLS (SSL)
By default this will serve over http.
If you want you can serve over https.
You will need to supply the \f[CR]\-\-rc\-cert\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-key\f[R] flags.
If you wish to do client side certificate validation then you will need
to supply \f[CR]\-\-rc\-client\-ca\f[R] also.
.PP
When TLS is configured every listener given with \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R]
serves TLS.
An individual listener can be prefixed with \f[CR]http://\f[R] to serve
unencrypted HTTP on that address, or with \f[CR]tls://\f[R] to state
explicitly that it must serve TLS.
Using a \f[CR]tls://\f[R] address without \f[CR]\-\-rc\-cert\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-key\f[R] is an error.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-cert\f[R] must be set to the path of a file containing
either a PEM encoded certificate, or a concatenation of that with the CA
certificate.
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-key\f[R] must be set to the path of a file with the PEM
encoded private key.
If setting \f[CR]\-\-rc\-client\-ca\f[R], it should be set to the path
of a file with PEM encoded client certificate authority certificates.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-min\-tls\-version\f[R] is minimum TLS version that is
acceptable.
Valid values are \(dqtls1.0\(dq, \(dqtls1.1\(dq, \(dqtls1.2\(dq and
\(dqtls1.3\(dq (default \(dqtls1.0\(dq).
.SS Socket activation
Instead of the listening addresses specified above, rclone will listen
to all FDs passed by the service manager, if any (and ignore any
arguments passed by \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R]).
.PP
This allows rclone to be a socket\-activated service.
It can be configured with .socket and .service unit files as described
in \c
.UR https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.socket.html
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Socket activation can be tested ad\-hoc with the
\f[CR]systemd\-socket\-activate\f[R]command
.IP
.EX
systemd\-socket\-activate \-l 8000 \-\- rclone serve
.EE
.PP
This will socket\-activate rclone on the first connection to port 8000
over TCP.
.SS Template
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-template\f[R] allows a user to specify a custom markup
template for HTTP and WebDAV serve functions.
The server exports the following markup to be used within the template
to server pages:
