.SH rclone selfupdate
Update the rclone binary.
.SS Synopsis
This command downloads the latest release of rclone and replaces the
currently running binary.
The download is verified with a hashsum and cryptographically signed
signature; see \c
.UR https://rclone.org/release_signing/
the release signing docs
.UE \c
\ for details.
.PP
If used without flags (or with implied \f[CR]\-\-stable\f[R] flag), this
command will install the latest stable release.
However, some issues may be fixed (or features added) only in the latest
beta release.
In such cases you should run the command with the \f[CR]\-\-beta\f[R]
flag, i.e.
\f[CR]rclone selfupdate \-\-beta\f[R].
You can check in advance what version would be installed by adding the
\f[CR]\-\-check\f[R] flag, then repeat the command without it when you
are satisfied.
.PP
Sometimes the rclone team may recommend you a concrete beta or stable
rclone release to troubleshoot your issue or add a bleeding edge
feature.
The \f[CR]\-\-version VER\f[R] flag, if given, will update to the
concrete version instead of the latest one.
If you omit micro version from \f[CR]VER\f[R] (for example
\f[CR]1.53\f[R]), the latest matching micro version will be used.
.PP
Upon successful update rclone will print a message that contains a
previous version number.
You will need it if you later decide to revert your update for some
reason.
Then you\(aqll have to note the previous version and run the following
command: \f[CR]rclone selfupdate [\-\-beta] OLDVER\f[R].
If the old version contains only dots and digits (for example
\f[CR]v1.54.0\f[R]) then it\(aqs a stable release so you won\(aqt need
the \f[CR]\-\-beta\f[R] flag.
Beta releases have an additional information similar to
\f[CR]v1.54.0\-beta.5111.06f1c0c61\f[R].
(if you are a developer and use a locally built rclone, the version
number will end with \f[CR]\-DEV\f[R], you will have to rebuild it as it
obviously can\(aqt be distributed).
.PP
If you previously installed rclone via a package manager, the package
may include local documentation or configure services.
You may wish to update with the flag \f[CR]\-\-package deb\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-package rpm\f[R] (whichever is correct for your OS) to update
these too.
This command with the default \f[CR]\-\-package zip\f[R] will update
only the rclone executable so the local manual may become inaccurate
after it.
.PP
The \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_mount/
rclone mount
.UE \c
\ command may or may not support extended FUSE options depending on the
build and OS.
\f[CR]selfupdate\f[R] will refuse to update if the capability would be
discarded.
.PP
Note: Windows forbids deletion of a currently running executable so this
command will rename the old executable to \(aqrclone.old.exe\(aq upon
success.
.PP
Please note that this command was not available before rclone version
1.55.
If it fails for you with the message
\f[CR]unknown command \(dqselfupdate\(dq\f[R] then you will need to
update manually following the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/install/
install documentation
.UE \c
\&.
.IP
.EX
rclone selfupdate [flags]
.EE
.SS Options
.IP
.EX
      \-\-beta             Install beta release
      \-\-check            Check for latest release, do not download
  \-h, \-\-help             help for selfupdate
      \-\-output string    Save the downloaded binary at a given path (default: replace running binary)
      \-\-package string   Package format: zip|deb|rpm (default: zip)
      \-\-stable           Install stable release (this is the default)
      \-\-version string   Install the given rclone version (default: latest)
.EE
