\ \- Show help for rclone commands, flags and backends.
.SS Copying single files
rclone normally syncs or copies directories.
However, if the source remote points to a file, rclone will just copy
that file.
The destination remote must point to a directory \- rclone will give the
error
\f[CR]Failed to create file system for \(dqremote:file\(dq: is a file not a directory\f[R]
if it isn\(aqt.
.PP
For example, suppose you have a remote with a file in called
\f[CR]test.jpg\f[R], then you could copy just that file like this
.IP
.EX
rclone copy remote:test.jpg /tmp/download
.EE
.PP
The file \f[CR]test.jpg\f[R] will be placed inside
\f[CR]/tmp/download\f[R].
.PP
This is equivalent to specifying
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-files\-from /tmp/files remote: /tmp/download
.EE
.PP
Where \f[CR]/tmp/files\f[R] contains the single line
.IP
.EX
test.jpg
.EE
.PP
It is recommended to use \f[CR]copy\f[R] when copying individual files,
not \f[CR]sync\f[R].
They have pretty much the same effect but \f[CR]copy\f[R] will use a lot
less memory.
.SS Syntax of remote paths
The syntax of the paths passed to the rclone command are as follows.
.SS /path/to/dir
This refers to the local file system.
.PP
On Windows \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] may be used instead of \f[CR]/\f[R] in local
paths \f[B]only\f[R], non local paths must use \f[CR]/\f[R].
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/local/#paths-on-windows
local filesystem
.UE \c
\ documentation for more about Windows\-specific paths.
.PP
These paths needn\(aqt start with a leading \f[CR]/\f[R] \- if they
don\(aqt then they will be relative to the current directory.
.SS remote:path/to/dir
This refers to a directory \f[CR]path/to/dir\f[R] on \f[CR]remote:\f[R]
as defined in the config file (configured with
\f[CR]rclone config\f[R]).
.SS remote:/path/to/dir
On most backends this is refers to the same directory as
\f[CR]remote:path/to/dir\f[R] and that format should be preferred.
On a very small number of remotes (FTP, SFTP, Dropbox for business) this
will refer to a different directory.
On these, paths without a leading \f[CR]/\f[R] will refer to your
\(dqhome\(dq directory and paths with a leading \f[CR]/\f[R] will refer
to the root.
.SS :backend:path/to/dir
This is an advanced form for creating remotes on the fly.
\f[CR]backend\f[R] should be the name or prefix of a backend (the
\f[CR]type\f[R] in the config file) and all the configuration for the
backend should be provided on the command line (or in environment
variables).
.PP
Here are some examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd \-\-http\-url https://pub.rclone.org :http:
.EE
.PP
To list all the directories in the root of
\f[CR]https://pub.rclone.org/\f[R].
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf \-\-http\-url https://example.com :http:path/to/dir
.EE
.PP
To list files and directories in
\f[CR]https://example.com/path/to/dir/\f[R]
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-http\-url https://example.com :http:path/to/dir /tmp/dir
.EE
.PP
To copy files and directories in
\f[CR]https://example.com/path/to/dir\f[R] to \f[CR]/tmp/dir\f[R].
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-sftp\-host example.com :sftp:path/to/dir /tmp/dir
.EE
.PP
To copy files and directories from \f[CR]example.com\f[R] in the
relative directory \f[CR]path/to/dir\f[R] to \f[CR]/tmp/dir\f[R] using
sftp.
.SS Connection strings
The above examples can also be written using a connection string syntax,
so instead of providing the arguments as command line parameters
\f[CR]\-\-http\-url https://pub.rclone.org\f[R] they are provided as
part of the remote specification as a kind of connection string.
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd \(dq:http,url=\(aqhttps://pub.rclone.org\(aq:\(dq
rclone lsf \(dq:http,url=\(aqhttps://example.com\(aq:path/to/dir\(dq
rclone copy \(dq:http,url=\(aqhttps://example.com\(aq:path/to/dir\(dq /tmp/dir
rclone copy :sftp,host=example.com:path/to/dir /tmp/dir
.EE
.PP
These can apply to modify existing remotes as well as create new remotes
with the on the fly syntax.
This example is equivalent to adding the
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-shared\-with\-me\f[R] parameter to the remote
\f[CR]gdrive:\f[R].
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf \(dqgdrive,shared_with_me:path/to/dir\(dq
.EE
.PP
The major advantage to using the connection string style syntax is that
it only applies to the remote, not to all the remotes of that type of
the command line.
A common confusion is this attempt to copy a file shared on google drive
to the normal drive which \f[B]does not work\f[R] because the
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-shared\-with\-me\f[R] flag applies to both the source
and the destination.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-drive\-shared\-with\-me gdrive:shared\-file.txt gdrive:
.EE
.PP
However using the connection string syntax, this does work.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(dqgdrive,shared_with_me:shared\-file.txt\(dq gdrive:
.EE
.PP
Note that the connection string only affects the options of the
immediate backend.
If for example gdriveCrypt is a crypt based on gdrive, then the
following command \f[B]will not work\f[R] as intended, because
\f[CR]shared_with_me\f[R] is ignored by the crypt backend:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(dqgdriveCrypt,shared_with_me:shared\-file.txt\(dq gdriveCrypt:
.EE
.PP
The connection strings have the following syntax
.IP
.EX
remote,parameter=value,parameter2=value2:path/to/dir
:backend,parameter=value,parameter2=value2:path/to/dir
.EE
.PP
If the \f[CR]parameter\f[R] has a \f[CR]:\f[R] or \f[CR],\f[R] then it
must be placed in quotes \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] or \f[CR]\(aq\f[R], so
.IP
.EX
remote,parameter=\(dqcolon:value\(dq,parameter2=\(dqcomma,value\(dq:path/to/dir
:backend,parameter=\(aqcolon:value\(aq,parameter2=\(aqcomma,value\(aq:path/to/dir
.EE
.PP
If a quoted value needs to include that quote, then it should be
doubled, so
.IP
.EX
remote,parameter=\(dqwith\(dq\(dqquote\(dq,parameter2=\(aqwith\(aq\(aqquote\(aq:path/to/dir
.EE
.PP
This will make \f[CR]parameter\f[R] be \f[CR]with\(dqquote\f[R] and
\f[CR]parameter2\f[R] be \f[CR]with\(aqquote\f[R].
.PP
If you leave off the \f[CR]=parameter\f[R] then rclone will substitute
\f[CR]=true\f[R] which works very well with flags.
For example, to use s3 configured in the environment you could use:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd :s3,env_auth:
.EE
.PP
Which is equivalent to
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd :s3,env_auth=true:
.EE
.PP
Note that on the command line you might need to surround these
connection strings with \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] or \f[CR]\(aq\f[R] to stop the
shell interpreting any special characters within them.
.PP
If you are a shell master then you\(aqll know which strings are OK and
which aren\(aqt, but if you aren\(aqt sure then enclose them in
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R] and use \f[CR]\(aq\f[R] as the inside quote.
This syntax works on all OSes.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(dq:http,url=\(aqhttps://example.com\(aq:path/to/dir\(dq /tmp/dir
.EE
.PP
On Linux/macOS some characters are still interpreted inside
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R] strings in the shell (notably \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] and
\f[CR]$\f[R] and \f[CR]\(dq\f[R]) so if your strings contain those you
can swap the roles of \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] and \f[CR]\(aq\f[R] thus.
(This syntax does not work on Windows.)
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(aq:http,url=\(dqhttps://example.com\(dq:path/to/dir\(aq /tmp/dir
.EE
.PP
You can use \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_string/
rclone config string
.UE \c
\ to convert a remote into a connection string.
.SS Connection strings, config and logging
If you supply extra configuration to a backend by command line flag,
environment variable or connection string then rclone will add a suffix
based on the hash of the config to the name of the remote, eg
.IP
.EX
rclone \-vv lsf \-\-s3\-chunk\-size 20M s3:
.EE
.PP
Has the log message
.IP
.EX
DEBUG : s3: detected overridden config \- adding \(dq{Srj1p}\(dq suffix to name
.EE
.PP
This is so rclone can tell the modified remote apart from the unmodified
remote when caching the backends.
.PP
This should only be noticeable in the logs.
.PP
This means that on the fly backends such as
.IP
.EX
rclone \-vv lsf :s3,env_auth:
.EE
.PP
Will get their own names
.IP
.EX
DEBUG : :s3: detected overridden config \- adding \(dq{YTu53}\(dq suffix to name
.EE
.SS Valid remote names
Remote names are case sensitive, and must adhere to the following rules:
.IP \(bu 2
May contain number, letter, \f[CR]_\f[R], \f[CR]\-\f[R], \f[CR].\f[R],
\f[CR]+\f[R], \f[CR]\(at\f[R] and space.
.IP \(bu 2
May not start with \f[CR]\-\f[R] or space.
.IP \(bu 2
May not end with space.
.PP
Starting with rclone version 1.61, any Unicode numbers and letters are
allowed, while in older versions it was limited to plain ASCII (0\-9,
A\-Z, a\-z).
If you use the same rclone configuration from different shells, which
may be configured with different character encoding, you must be
cautious to use characters that are possible to write in all of them.
This is mostly a problem on Windows, where the console traditionally
uses a non\-Unicode character set \- defined by the so\-called \(dqcode
page\(dq.
.PP
Do not use single character names on Windows as it creates ambiguity
with Windows drives\(aq names, e.g.: remote called \f[CR]C\f[R] is
indistinguishable from \f[CR]C\f[R] drive.
Rclone will always assume that single letter name refers to a drive.
.SS Adding global configuration to a remote
It is possible to add global configuration to the remote configuration
which will be applied just before the remote is created.
.PP
This can be done in two ways.
The first is to use \f[CR]override.var = value\f[R] in the config file
or the connection string for a temporary change, and the second is to
use \f[CR]global.var = value\f[R] in the config file or connection
string for a permanent change.
.PP
This is explained fully below.
.SS override.var
This is used to override a global variable \f[B]just\f[R] for the
duration of the remote creation.
It won\(aqt affect other remotes even if they are created at the same
time.
.PP
This is very useful for overriding networking config needed for just for
that remote.
For example, say you have a remote which needs
\f[CR]\-\-no\-check\-certificate\f[R] as it is running on test
infrastructure without a proper certificate.
You could supply the \f[CR]\-\-no\-check\-certificate\f[R] flag to
rclone, but this will affect \f[B]all\f[R] the remotes.
To make it just affect this remote you use an override.
You could put this in the config file:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][remote]\f[R]
type = XXX
\&...
override.no_check_certificate = \f[B]true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
or use it in the connection string
\f[CR]remote,override.no_check_certificate=true:\f[R] (or just
\f[CR]remote,override.no_check_certificate:\f[R]).
.PP
Note how the global flag name loses its initial \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] and gets
\f[CR]\-\f[R] replaced with \f[CR]_\f[R] and gets an
\f[CR]override.\f[R] prefix.
.PP
Not all global variables make sense to be overridden like this as the
config is only applied during the remote creation.
Here is a non exhaustive list of ones which might be useful:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]bind_addr\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ca_cert\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_cert\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_key\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]connect_timeout\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]disable_http2\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]disable_http_keep_alives\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]dump\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]expect_continue_timeout\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]headers\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]http_proxy\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]low_level_retries\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]max_connections\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no_check_certificate\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no_gzip\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]timeout\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]traffic_class\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]use_cookies\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]use_server_modtime\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]user_agent\f[R]
.PP
An \f[CR]override.var\f[R] will override all other config methods, but
\f[B]just\f[R] for the duration of the creation of the remote.
.SS global.var
This is used to set a global variable \f[B]for everything\f[R].
The global variable is set just before the remote is created.
.PP
This is useful for parameters (eg sync parameters) which can\(aqt be set
as an \f[CR]override\f[R].
For example, say you have a remote where you would always like to use
the \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] flag.
You could supply the \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] flag to rclone on every
command line, but instead you could put this in the config file:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][remote]\f[R]
type = XXX
\&...
global.checksum = \f[B]true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
or use it in the connection string
\f[CR]remote,global.checksum=true:\f[R] (or just
\f[CR]remote,global.checksum:\f[R]).
This is equivalent to using the \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] flag.
.PP
Note how the global flag name loses its initial \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] and gets
\f[CR]\-\f[R] replaced with \f[CR]_\f[R] and gets a \f[CR]global.\f[R]
prefix.
.PP
Any global variable can be set like this and it is exactly equivalent to
using the equivalent flag on the command line.
This means it will affect all uses of rclone.
.PP
If two remotes set the same global variable then the first one
instantiated will be overridden by the second one.
A \f[CR]global.var\f[R] will override all other config methods when the
remote is created.
.SS Quoting and the shell
When you are typing commands to your computer you are using something
called the command line shell.
This interprets various characters in an OS specific way.
.PP
Here are some gotchas which may help users unfamiliar with the shell
rules
.SS Linux / macOS
If your names have spaces or shell metacharacters (e.g.
\f[CR]*\f[R], \f[CR]?\f[R], \f[CR]$\f[R], \f[CR]\(aq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R], etc.)
then you must quote them.
Use single quotes \f[CR]\(aq\f[R] by default.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(aqImportant files?\(aq remote:backup
.EE
.PP
If you want to send a \f[CR]\(aq\f[R] you will need to use
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R], e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(dqO\(aqReilly Reviews\(dq remote:backup
.EE
.PP
The rules for quoting metacharacters are complicated and if you want the
full details you\(aqll have to consult the manual page for your shell.
.SS Windows
If your names have spaces in you need to put them in \f[CR]\(dq\f[R],
e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \(dqE:\(rsfolder name\(rsfolder name\(rsfolder name\(dq remote:backup
.EE
.PP
If you are using the root directory on its own then don\(aqt quote it
(see \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/464
#464
.UE \c
\ for why), e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy E:\(rs remote:backup
.EE
.SS Copying files or directories with \f[CR]:\f[R] in the names
rclone uses \f[CR]:\f[R] to mark a remote name.
This is, however, a valid filename component in non\-Windows OSes.
The remote name parser will only search for a \f[CR]:\f[R] up to the
first \f[CR]/\f[R] so if you need to act on a file or directory like
this then use the full path starting with a \f[CR]/\f[R], or use
\f[CR]./\f[R] as a current directory prefix.
.PP
So to sync a directory called \f[CR]sync:me\f[R] to a remote called
\f[CR]remote:\f[R] use
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive ./sync:me remote:path
.EE
.PP
or
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive /full/path/to/sync:me remote:path
.EE
.SS Server\-side copy
Most remotes (but not all \- see \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#optional-features
the overview
.UE \c
) support server\-side copy.
.PP
This means if you want to copy one folder to another then rclone
won\(aqt download all the files and re\-upload them; it will instruct
the server to copy them in place.
.PP
Eg
.IP
.EX
rclone copy s3:oldbucket s3:newbucket
.EE
.PP
Will copy the contents of \f[CR]oldbucket\f[R] to \f[CR]newbucket\f[R]
without downloading and re\-uploading.
.PP
Remotes which don\(aqt support server\-side copy \f[B]will\f[R] download
and re\-upload in this case.
.PP
Server\-side copies are used with \f[CR]sync\f[R] and \f[CR]copy\f[R]
and will be identified in the log when using the \f[CR]\-v\f[R] flag.
The \f[CR]move\f[R] command may also use them if remote doesn\(aqt
support server\-side move directly.
This is done by issuing a server\-side copy then a delete which is much
quicker than a download and re\-upload.
.PP
Server\-side copies will only be attempted if the remote names are the
same.
.PP
This can be used when scripting to make aged backups efficiently, e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive remote:current\-backup remote:previous\-backup
rclone sync \-\-interactive /path/to/files remote:current\-backup
.EE
.SS Metadata support
Metadata is data about a file (or directory) which isn\(aqt the contents
of the file (or directory).
Normally rclone only preserves the modification time and the content
(MIME) type where possible.
.PP
Rclone supports preserving all the available metadata on files and
directories when using the \f[CR]\-\-metadata\f[R] or \f[CR]\-M\f[R]
flag.
.PP
Exactly what metadata is supported and what that support means depends
on the backend.
Backends that support metadata have a metadata section in their docs and
are listed in the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#features
features table
.UE \c
\ (Eg \c
.UR https://rclone.org/local/#metadata
local
.UE \c
, s3)
.PP
Some backends don\(aqt support metadata, some only support metadata on
files and some support metadata on both files and directories.
.PP
Rclone only supports a one\-time sync of metadata.
This means that metadata will be synced from the source object to the
destination object only when the source object has changed and needs to
be re\-uploaded.
If the metadata subsequently changes on the source object without
changing the object itself then it won\(aqt be synced to the destination
object.
This is in line with the way rclone syncs \f[CR]Content\-Type\f[R]
without the \f[CR]\-\-metadata\f[R] flag.
.PP
Using \f[CR]\-\-metadata\f[R] when syncing from local to local will
preserve file attributes such as file mode, owner, extended attributes
(not Windows).
.PP
Note that arbitrary metadata may be added to objects using the
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-set key=value\f[R] flag when the object is first
uploaded.
This flag can be repeated as many times as necessary.
.PP
The \-\-metadata\-mapper flag can be used to pass the name of a program
in which can transform metadata when it is being copied from source to
destination.
.PP
Rclone supports \f[CR]\-\-metadata\-set\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-mapper\f[R] when doing server\-side \f[CR]Move\f[R]
and server\-side \f[CR]Copy\f[R], but not when doing server side
\f[CR]DirMove\f[R] (renaming a directory) as this would involve
recursing into the directory.
Note that you can disable \f[CR]DirMove\f[R] with
\f[CR]\-\-disable DirMove\f[R] and rclone will revert back to using
\f[CR]Move\f[R] for each individual object where
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-set\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-metadata\-mapper\f[R] are
supported.
.SS Types of metadata
Metadata is divided into two type.
System metadata and User metadata.
.PP
Metadata which the backend uses itself is called system metadata.
For example on the local backend the system metadata \f[CR]uid\f[R] will
store the user ID of the file when used on a unix based platform.
.PP
Arbitrary metadata is called user metadata and this can be set however
is desired.
.PP
When objects are copied from backend to backend, they will attempt to
interpret system metadata if it is supplied.
Metadata may change from being user metadata to system metadata as
objects are copied between different backends.
For example copying an object from s3 sets the \f[CR]content\-type\f[R]
metadata.
In a backend which understands this (like \f[CR]azureblob\f[R]) this
will become the Content\-Type of the object.
In a backend which doesn\(aqt understand this (like the \f[CR]local\f[R]
backend) this will become user metadata.
However should the local object be copied back to s3, the Content\-Type
will be set correctly.
.SS Metadata framework
Rclone implements a metadata framework which can read metadata from an
object and write it to the object when (and only when) it is being
uploaded.
.PP
This metadata is stored as a dictionary with string keys and string
values.
.PP
There are some limits on the names of the keys (these may be clarified
further in the future).
.IP \(bu 2
must be lower case
.IP \(bu 2
may be \f[CR]a\-z\f[R] \f[CR]0\-9\f[R] containing \f[CR].\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\f[R] or \f[CR]_\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
length is backend dependent
.PP
Each backend can provide system metadata that it understands.
Some backends can also store arbitrary user metadata.
.PP
Where possible the key names are standardized, so, for example, it is
possible to copy object metadata from s3 to azureblob for example and
metadata will be translated appropriately.
.PP
Some backends have limits on the size of the metadata and rclone will
give errors on upload if they are exceeded.
.SS Metadata preservation
The goal of the implementation is to
.IP "1." 3
Preserve metadata if at all possible
.IP "2." 3
Interpret metadata if at all possible
.PP
The consequences of 1 is that you can copy an S3 object to a local disk
then back to S3 losslessly.
Likewise you can copy a local file with file attributes and xattrs from
local disk to s3 and back again losslessly.
.PP
The consequence of 2 is that you can copy an S3 object with metadata to
Azureblob (say) and have the metadata appear on the Azureblob object
also.
