.PP
The metadata keys \f[CR]mtime\f[R] and \f[CR]content\-type\f[R] will
take precedence if supplied in the metadata over reading the
\f[CR]Content\-Type\f[R] or modification time of the source object.
.PP
Hashes are not included in system metadata as there is a well defined
way of reading those already.
.SS Options
Rclone has a number of options to control its behaviour.
These are documented below, and in the flags page.
.PP
Options that take parameters can have the values passed in two ways,
\f[CR]\-\-option=value\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-option value\f[R].
However boolean (true/false) options behave slightly differently to the
other options in that \f[CR]\-\-boolean\f[R] sets the option to
\f[CR]true\f[R] and the absence of the flag sets it to \f[CR]false\f[R].
It is also possible to specify \f[CR]\-\-boolean=false\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-boolean=true\f[R].
Note that \f[CR]\-\-boolean false\f[R] is not valid \- this is parsed as
\f[CR]\-\-boolean\f[R] and the \f[CR]false\f[R] is parsed as an extra
command line argument for rclone.
.PP
String values that are recognized as special identifiers, e.g.
the name of the log level to set with option \f[CR]\-\-log\-level\f[R],
are case insensitive, e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-log\-level ERROR\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-log\-level error\f[R] are
identical.
.PP
Options documented to take a \f[CR]stringArray\f[R] parameter accept
multiple values.
To pass more than one value, repeat the option; for example:
\f[CR]\-\-include value1 \-\-include value2\f[R].
Other options may only accept a single value, and should only be
specified once, but where the specified parameter may indicate a list of
values separated by space or comma.
Such options are documented to take a \f[CR]CommaSepList\f[R] parameter,
if comma separated, and \f[CR]SpaceSepList\f[R] if space separated,
although some may also have different parameters such as
\f[CR]DumpFlags\f[R] or just \f[CR]string\f[R] and the help text
explains that this will be interpreted as a list.
.PP
Floating\-point values with fractional part must use period
(\f[CR].\f[R]) as decimal separator, common in English\-speaking
countries, regardless of your configured system locale.
Parameter type \f[CR]float\f[R] accepts decimal and hexadecimal
floating\-point numbers as defined by the Go syntax for \c
.UR https://go.dev/ref/spec#Floating-point_literals
floating\-point literals
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Time and duration options
Options that take a \f[CR]Time\f[R] or \f[CR]Duration\f[R] parameter
must be specified as a formatted string describing an absolute or
relative time.
Note that both \f[CR]Time\f[R] and \f[CR]Duration\f[R] parameter types
can be expressed as either absolute or relative time, just with
different interpretations, e.g.
a relative time will be treated as an offset from the current time when
passed as a \f[CR]Time\f[R] value.
.PP
An absolute time can be specified as a string in one of the following
formats:
.IP \(bu 2
RFC3339 \- e.g.
\f[CR]2006\-01\-02T15:04:05Z\f[R] or
\f[CR]2006\-01\-02T15:04:05+07:00\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
ISO8601 Date and time, local timezone \-
\f[CR]2006\-01\-02T15:04:05\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
ISO8601 Date and time, local timezone \-
\f[CR]2006\-01\-02 15:04:05\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
ISO8601 Date \- \f[CR]2006\-01\-02\f[R] (YYYY\-MM\-DD)
.PP
A relative time is a string with a, possibly signed, sequence of decimal
numbers, each with optional fraction, and each with a unit suffix.
If the string only contains a single number, then the unit suffix is
optional and will default to seconds, i.e.
a plain decimal value will be treated as a number of seconds.
The following suffixes are valid:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ms\f[R] \- Milliseconds
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]s\f[R] \- Seconds
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]m\f[R] \- Minutes
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]h\f[R] \- Hours
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]d\f[R] \- Days
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]w\f[R] \- Weeks
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]M\f[R] \- Months
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]y\f[R] \- Years
.PP
Examples: \(dq10\(dq, \(dq300ms\(dq, \(dq\-1.5h\(dq, \(dq2h45m\(dq or
\(dq1d12h\(dq.
.SS Size options
Options that take a SizeSuffix parameter can be specified as an integer
value, which will then be assumed to represent a KiB value (multiples of
1024 bytes) by default.
The interpretation can be changed by appending a suffix: \f[CR]B\f[R]
for Byte, \f[CR]K\f[R] for KiB, \f[CR]M\f[R] for MiB, \f[CR]G\f[R] for
GiB, \f[CR]T\f[R] for TiB and \f[CR]P\f[R] for PiB.
These are the binary units, e.g.
1, 2**10, 2**20, 2**30 respectively.
.PP
See also \-\-human\-readable.
.SS Main options
.SS \-\-backup\-dir string
When using \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
, any files which would have been overwritten or deleted are moved in
their original hierarchy into this directory.
.PP
If \f[CR]\-\-suffix\f[R] is set, then the moved files will have the
suffix added to them.
If there is a file with the same path (after the suffix has been added)
in the directory, then it will be overwritten.
.PP
The remote in use must support server\-side move or copy and you must
use the same remote as the destination of the sync.
The backup directory must not overlap the destination directory without
it being excluded by a filter rule.
.PP
For example
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive /path/to/local remote:current \-\-backup\-dir remote:old
.EE
.PP
will sync \f[CR]/path/to/local\f[R] to \f[CR]remote:current\f[R], but
for any files which would have been updated or deleted will be stored in
\f[CR]remote:old\f[R].
.PP
If running rclone from a script you might want to use today\(aqs date as
the directory name passed to \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] to store the old
files, or you might want to pass \f[CR]\-\-suffix\f[R] with today\(aqs
date.
This can be done with \f[CR]\-\-suffix $(date +%F)\f[R] in bash, and
\f[CR]\-\-suffix $(Get\-Date \-Format \(aqyyyy\-MM\-dd\(aq)\f[R] in
PowerShell.
.PP
See \f[CR]\-\-compare\-dest\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-copy\-dest\f[R].
.SS \-\-bind string
Local address to bind to for outgoing connections.
This can be an IPv4 address (1.2.3.4), an IPv6 address (1234::789A) or
host name.
If the host name doesn\(aqt resolve or resolves to more than one IP
address it will give an error.
.PP
You can use \f[CR]\-\-bind 0.0.0.0\f[R] to force rclone to use IPv4
addresses and \f[CR]\-\-bind ::0\f[R] to force rclone to use IPv6
addresses.
.SS \-\-bwlimit BwTimetable
This option controls the bandwidth limit.
For example
.IP
.EX
\-\-bwlimit 10M
.EE
.PP
would mean limit the upload and download bandwidth to 10 MiB/s.
\f[B]NB\f[R] this is \f[B]bytes\f[R] per second not \f[B]bits\f[R] per
second.
To use a single limit, specify the desired bandwidth in KiB/s, or use a
suffix B|K|M|G|T|P.
The default is \f[CR]0\f[R] which means to not limit bandwidth.
.PP
The upload and download bandwidth can be specified separately, as
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit UP:DOWN\f[R], so
.IP
.EX
\-\-bwlimit 10M:100k
.EE
.PP
would mean limit the upload bandwidth to 10 MiB/s and the download
bandwidth to 100 KiB/s.
Either limit can be \(dqoff\(dq meaning no limit, so to just limit the
upload bandwidth you would use
.IP
.EX
\-\-bwlimit 10M:off
.EE
.PP
this would limit the upload bandwidth to 10 MiB/s but the download
bandwidth would be unlimited.
.PP
When specified as above the bandwidth limits last for the duration of
run of the rclone binary.
.PP
It is also possible to specify a \(dqtimetable\(dq of limits, which will
cause certain limits to be applied at certain times.
To specify a timetable, format your entries as
\f[CR]WEEKDAY\-HH:MM,BANDWIDTH WEEKDAY\-HH:MM,BANDWIDTH...\f[R] where:
\f[CR]WEEKDAY\f[R] is optional element.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]BANDWIDTH\f[R] can be a single number, e.g.\f[CR]100k\f[R] or a
pair of numbers for upload:download, e.g.\f[CR]10M:1M\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]WEEKDAY\f[R] can be written as the whole word or only using the
first 3 characters.
It is optional.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HH:MM\f[R] is an hour from 00:00 to 23:59.
.PP
Entries can be separated by spaces or semicolons.
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Semicolons can be used as separators instead of spaces
to avoid parsing issues in environments like Docker.
.PP
An example of a typical timetable to avoid link saturation during
daytime working hours could be:
.PP
Using spaces as separators:
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit \(dq08:00,512k 12:00,10M 13:00,512k 18:00,30M 23:00,off\(dq\f[R]
.PP
Using semicolons as separators:
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit \(dq08:00,512k;12:00,10M;13:00,512k;18:00,30M;23:00,off\(dq\f[R]
.PP
In these examples, the transfer bandwidth will be set to 512 KiB/s at
8am every day.
At noon, it will rise to 10 MiB/s, and drop back to 512 KiB/sec at 1pm.
At 6pm, the bandwidth limit will be set to 30 MiB/s, and at 11pm it will
be completely disabled (full speed).
Anything between 11pm and 8am will remain unlimited.
.PP
An example of timetable with \f[CR]WEEKDAY\f[R] could be:
.PP
Using spaces as separators:
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit \(dqMon\-00:00,512 Fri\-23:59,10M Sat\-10:00,1M Sun\-20:00,off\(dq\f[R]
.PP
Using semicolons as separators:
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit \(dqMon\-00:00,512;Fri\-23:59,10M;Sat\-10:00,1M;Sun\-20:00,off\(dq\f[R]
.PP
It means that, the transfer bandwidth will be set to 512 KiB/s on
Monday.
It will rise to 10 MiB/s before the end of Friday.
At 10:00 on Saturday it will be set to 1 MiB/s.
From 20:00 on Sunday it will be unlimited.
.PP
Timeslots without \f[CR]WEEKDAY\f[R] are extended to the whole week.
So this example:
.IP
.EX
\-\-bwlimit \(dqMon\-00:00,512 12:00,1M Sun\-20:00,off\(dq
.EE
.PP
Is equivalent to this:
.IP
.EX
\-\-bwlimit \(dqMon\-00:00,512 Mon\-12:00,1M Tue\-12:00,1M Wed\-12:00,1M Thu\-12:00,1M Fri\-12:00,1M Sat\-12:00,1M Sun\-12:00,1M Sun\-20:00,off\(dq
.EE
.PP
Bandwidth limit apply to the data transfer for all backends.
For most backends the directory listing bandwidth is also included
(exceptions being the non HTTP backends, \f[CR]ftp\f[R], \f[CR]sftp\f[R]
and \f[CR]storj\f[R]).
.PP
Note that the units are \f[B]Byte/s\f[R], not \f[B]bit/s\f[R].
Typically connections are measured in bit/s \- to convert divide by 8.
For example, let\(aqs say you have a 10 Mbit/s connection and you wish
rclone to use half of it \- 5 Mbit/s.
This is 5/8 = 0.625 MiB/s so you would use a
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit 0.625M\f[R] parameter for rclone.
.PP
On Unix systems (Linux, macOS, \&...)
the bandwidth limiter can be toggled by sending a \f[CR]SIGUSR2\f[R]
signal to rclone.
This allows to remove the limitations of a long running rclone transfer
and to restore it back to the value specified with
\f[CR]\-\-bwlimit\f[R] quickly when needed.
Assuming there is only one rclone instance running, you can toggle the
limiter like this:
.IP
.EX
kill \-SIGUSR2 $(pidof rclone)
.EE
.PP
If you configure rclone with a remote control then you can use change
the bwlimit dynamically:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc core/bwlimit rate=1M
.EE
.SS \-\-bwlimit\-file BwTimetable
This option controls per file bandwidth limit.
For the options see the \f[CR]\-\-bwlimit\f[R] flag.
.PP
For example use this to allow no transfers to be faster than 1 MiB/s
.IP
.EX
\-\-bwlimit\-file 1M
.EE
.PP
This can be used in conjunction with \f[CR]\-\-bwlimit\f[R].
.PP
Note that if a schedule is provided the file will use the schedule in
effect at the start of the transfer.
.SS \-\-buffer\-size SizeSuffix
Use this sized buffer to speed up file transfers.
Each \f[CR]\-\-transfer\f[R] will use this much memory for buffering.
.PP
When using \f[CR]mount\f[R] or \f[CR]cmount\f[R] each open file
descriptor will use this much memory for buffering.
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_mount/#vfs-file-buffering
mount
.UE \c
\ documentation for more details.
.PP
Set to \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable the buffering for the minimum memory
usage.
.PP
Note that the memory allocation of the buffers is influenced by the
\-\-use\-mmap flag.
.SS \-\-cache\-dir string
Specify the directory rclone will use for caching, to override the
default.
.PP
Default value is depending on operating system:
.IP \(bu 2
Windows \f[CR]%LocalAppData%\(rsrclone\f[R], if \f[CR]LocalAppData\f[R]
is defined.
.IP \(bu 2
macOS \f[CR]$HOME/Library/Caches/rclone\f[R] if \f[CR]HOME\f[R] is
defined.
.IP \(bu 2
Unix \f[CR]$XDG_CACHE_HOME/rclone\f[R] if \f[CR]XDG_CACHE_HOME\f[R] is
defined, else \f[CR]$HOME/.cache/rclone\f[R] if \f[CR]HOME\f[R] is
defined.
.IP \(bu 2
Fallback (on all OS) to \f[CR]$TMPDIR/rclone\f[R], where
\f[CR]TMPDIR\f[R] is the value from \-\-temp\-dir.
.PP
You can use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_paths/
config paths
.UE \c
\ command to see the current value.
.PP
Cache directory is heavily used by the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_mount/#vfs-file-caching
VFS File Caching
.UE \c
\ mount feature, but also by \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_serve/
serve
.UE \c
, GUI and other parts of rclone.
.SS \-\-check\-first
If this flag is set then in a \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
, rclone will do all the checks to see whether files need to be
transferred before doing any of the transfers.
Normally rclone would start running transfers as soon as possible.
.PP
This flag can be useful on IO limited systems where transfers interfere
with checking.
.PP
It can also be useful to ensure perfect ordering when using
\f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R].
.PP
If both \f[CR]\-\-check\-first\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R] are set
when doing \f[CR]rclone move\f[R] then rclone will use the transfer
thread to delete source files which don\(aqt need transferring.
This will enable perfect ordering of the transfers and deletes but will
cause the transfer stats to have more items in than expected.
.PP
Using this flag can use more memory as it effectively sets
\f[CR]\-\-max\-backlog\f[R] to infinite.
This means that all the info on the objects to transfer is held in
memory before the transfers start.
.SS \-\-checkers int
Originally controlling just the number of file checkers to run in
parallel, e.g.
by \f[CR]rclone copy\f[R].
Now a fairly universal parallelism control used by \f[CR]rclone\f[R] in
several places.
.PP
Note: checkers do the equality checking of files during a sync.
For some storage systems (e.g.
S3, Swift, Dropbox) this can take a significant amount of time so they
are run in parallel.
.PP
The default is to run 8 checkers in parallel.
However, in case of slow\-reacting backends you may need to lower
(rather than increase) this default by setting \f[CR]\-\-checkers\f[R]
to 4 or less threads.
This is especially advised if you are experiencing backend server
crashes during file checking phase (e.g.
on subsequent or top\-up backups where little or no file copying is done
and checking takes up most of the time).
Increase this setting only with utmost care, while monitoring your
server health and file checking throughput.
.SS \-c, \-\-checksum
Normally rclone will look at modification time and size of files to see
if they are equal.
If you set this flag then rclone will check the file hash and size to
determine if files are equal.
.PP
This is useful when the remote doesn\(aqt support setting modified time
and a more accurate sync is desired than just checking the file size.
.PP
This is very useful when transferring between remotes which store the
same hash type on the object, e.g.
Drive and Swift.
For details of which remotes support which hash type see the table in
the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/
overview section
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Eg \f[CR]rclone \-\-checksum sync s3:/bucket swift:/bucket\f[R] would
run much quicker than without the \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] flag.
.PP
When using this flag, rclone won\(aqt update mtimes of remote files if
they are incorrect as it would normally.
.SS \-\-color AUTO|NEVER|ALWAYS
Specify when colors (and other ANSI codes) should be added to the
output.
.PP
\f[CR]AUTO\f[R] only allows ANSI codes when the output is a terminal.
This is the default.
.PP
\f[CR]NEVER\f[R] never allow ANSI codes.
.PP
\f[CR]ALWAYS\f[R] always add ANSI codes, regardless of the output format
(terminal or file).
.SS \-\-compare\-dest stringArray
When using \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
, the specified paths are checked in addition to the destination for
files.
If a file identical to the source is found, that file is \f[B]not\f[R]
copied from source.
This is useful to copy just files that have changed since the last
backup.
.PP
You must use the same remote as the destination of the sync.
The compare directory must not overlap the destination directory.
.PP
See \f[CR]\-\-copy\-dest\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R].
.SS \-\-config string
Specify the location of the rclone configuration file, to override the
default.
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone config \-\-config=\(dqrclone.conf\(dq\f[R].
.PP
The exact default is a bit complex to describe, due to changes
introduced through different versions of rclone while preserving
backwards compatibility, but in most cases it is as simple as:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]%APPDATA%/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R] on Windows
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(ti/.config/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R] on other
.PP
The complete logic is as follows: Rclone will look for an existing
configuration file in any of the following locations, in priority order:
.IP "1." 3
\f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] (in program directory, where rclone executable
is)
.IP "2." 3
\f[CR]%APPDATA%/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R] (only on Windows)
.IP "3." 3
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R] (on all systems,
including Windows)
.IP "4." 3
\f[CR]\(ti/.config/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R] (see below for explanation of
\(ti symbol)
.IP "5." 3
\f[CR]\(ti/.rclone.conf\f[R]
.PP
If no existing configuration file is found, then a new one will be
created in the following location:
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows: Location 2 listed above, except in the unlikely event that
\f[CR]APPDATA\f[R] is not defined, then location 4 is used instead.
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix: Location 3 if \f[CR]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is defined, else
location 4.
.IP \(bu 2
Fallback to location 5 (on all OS), when the rclone directory cannot be
created, but if also a home directory was not found then path
\f[CR].rclone.conf\f[R] relative to current working directory will be
used as a final resort.
.PP
The \f[CR]\(ti\f[R] symbol in paths above represent the home directory
of the current user on any OS, and the value is defined as following:
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows: \f[CR]%HOME%\f[R] if defined, else \f[CR]%USERPROFILE%\f[R],
or else \f[CR]%HOMEDRIVE%\(rs%HOMEPATH%\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix: \f[CR]$HOME\f[R] if defined, else by looking up current user in
OS\-specific user database (e.g.
passwd file), or else use the result from shell command
\f[CR]cd && pwd\f[R].
.PP
If you run \f[CR]rclone config file\f[R] you will see where the default
location is for you.
Running \f[CR]rclone config touch\f[R] will ensure a configuration file
exists, creating an empty one in the default location if there is none.
.PP
The fact that an existing file \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] in the same
directory as the rclone executable is always preferred, means that it is
easy to run in \(dqportable\(dq mode by downloading rclone executable to
a writable directory and then create an empty file
\f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] in the same directory.
.PP
If the location is set to empty string \f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R] or path to a
file with name \f[CR]notfound\f[R], or the os null device represented by
value \f[CR]NUL\f[R] on Windows and \f[CR]/dev/null\f[R] on Unix
systems, then rclone will keep the configuration file in memory only.
.PP
You may see a log message \(dqConfig file not found \- using
defaults\(dq if there is no configuration file.
This can be suppressed, e.g.
if you are using rclone entirely with \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#backend-path-to-dir
on the fly remotes
.UE \c
, by using memory\-only configuration file or by creating an empty
configuration file, as described above.
.PP
The file format is basic \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/INI_file#Format
INI
.UE \c
: Sections of text, led by a \f[CR][section]\f[R] header and followed by
\f[CR]key=value\f[R] entries on separate lines.
In rclone each remote is represented by its own section, where the
section name defines the name of the remote.
Options are specified as the \f[CR]key=value\f[R] entries, where the key
is the option name without the \f[CR]\-\-backend\-\f[R] prefix, in
lowercase and with \f[CR]_\f[R] instead of \f[CR]\-\f[R].
E.g.
option \f[CR]\-\-mega\-hard\-delete\f[R] corresponds to key
\f[CR]hard_delete\f[R].
Only backend options can be specified.
A special, and required, key \f[CR]type\f[R] identifies the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/
storage system
.UE \c
, where the value is the internal lowercase name as returned by command
\f[CR]rclone help backends\f[R].
Comments are indicated by \f[CR];\f[R] or \f[CR]#\f[R] at the beginning
of a line.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][megaremote]\f[R]
type = mega
user = you\(atexample.com
pass = PDPcQVVjVtzFY\-GTdDFozqBhTdsPg3qH
.EE
.PP
Note that passwords are in \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_obscure/
obscured
.UE \c
\ form.
Also, many storage systems uses token\-based authentication instead of
passwords, and this requires additional steps.
It is easier, and safer, to use the interactive command
\f[CR]rclone config\f[R] instead of manually editing the configuration
file.
.PP
The configuration file will typically contain login information, and
should therefore have restricted permissions so that only the current
user can read it.
Rclone tries to ensure this when it writes the file.
You may also choose to encrypt the file.
.PP
When token\-based authentication are used, the configuration file must
be writable, because rclone needs to update the tokens inside it.
.PP
To reduce risk of corrupting an existing configuration file, rclone will
not write directly to it when saving changes.
Instead it will first write to a new, temporary, file.
If a configuration file already existed, it will (on Unix systems) try
to mirror its permissions to the new file.
Then it will rename the existing file to a temporary name as backup.
Next, rclone will rename the new file to the correct name, before
finally cleaning up by deleting the backup file.
.PP
If the configuration file path used by rclone is a symbolic link, then
this will be evaluated and rclone will write to the resolved path,
instead of overwriting the symbolic link.
Temporary files used in the process (described above) will be written to
the same parent directory as that of the resolved configuration file,
but if this directory is also a symbolic link it will not be resolved
and the temporary files will be written to the location of the directory
symbolic link.
.SS \-\-contimeout Duration
Set the connection timeout.
This should be in go time format which looks like \f[CR]5s\f[R] for 5
seconds, \f[CR]10m\f[R] for 10 minutes, or \f[CR]3h30m\f[R].
.PP
The connection timeout is the amount of time rclone will wait for a
connection to go through to a remote object storage system.
It is \f[CR]1m\f[R] by default.
.SS \-\-copy\-dest stringArray
When using \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
, the specified paths are checked in addition to the destination for
files.
This part is the same as \f[CR]\-\-compare\-dest\f[R], but the
difference is that with \f[CR]\-\-copy\-dest\f[R], if a file identical
to the source is found, that file is server\-side copied from the
specified paths to the destination.
This is useful for incremental backup.
.PP
The remote in use must support server\-side copy and you must use the
same remote as the destination of the sync.
The compare directory must not overlap the destination directory.
.PP
See \f[CR]\-\-compare\-dest\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R].
.SS \-\-dedupe\-mode interactive|skip|first|newest|oldest|largest|smallest|rename|list
Mode to run dedupe command in.
One of \f[CR]interactive\f[R], \f[CR]skip\f[R], \f[CR]first\f[R],
\f[CR]newest\f[R], \f[CR]oldest\f[R], \f[CR]largest\f[R],
\f[CR]smallest\f[R], \f[CR]rename\f[R] \f[CR]list\f[R].
The default is \f[CR]interactive\f[R].
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_dedupe/
dedupe
.UE \c
\ command for more information as to what these options mean.
.SS \-\-default\-time Time
If a file or directory does have a modification time rclone can read
then rclone will display this fixed time instead.
.PP
The default is \f[CR]2000\-01\-01 00:00:00 UTC\f[R].
This can be configured in any of the ways shown in time options.
.PP
For example \f[CR]\-\-default\-time 2020\-06\-01\f[R] to set the default
time to the 1st of June 2020 or \f[CR]\-\-default\-time 0s\f[R] to set
the default time to the time rclone started up.
.SS \-\-disable string
This disables a comma separated list of optional features.
For example to disable server\-side move and server\-side copy use:
.IP
.EX
\-\-disable move,copy
.EE
.PP
The features can be put in any case.
.PP
To see a list of which features can be disabled use:
.IP
.EX
\-\-disable help
.EE
.PP
The features a remote has can be seen in JSON format with:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend features remote:
.EE
.PP
See the overview \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#features
features
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#optional-features
optional features
.UE \c
\ to get an idea of which feature does what.
.PP
Note that some features can be set to \f[CR]true\f[R] if they are
\f[CR]true\f[R]/\f[CR]false\f[R] feature flag features by prefixing them
with \f[CR]!\f[R].
For example the \f[CR]CaseInsensitive\f[R] feature can be forced to
\f[CR]false\f[R] with \f[CR]\-\-disable CaseInsensitive\f[R] and forced
to \f[CR]true\f[R] with \f[CR]\-\-disable \(aq!CaseInsensitive\(aq\f[R].
In general it isn\(aqt a good idea doing this but it may be useful in
extremis.
.PP
(Note that \f[CR]!\f[R] is a shell command which you will need to escape
with single quotes or a backslash on unix like platforms.)
.PP
This flag can be useful for debugging and in exceptional circumstances
(e.g.
Google Drive limiting the total volume of server\-side copies to 100
GiB/day).
.SS \-\-disable\-http2
This stops rclone from trying to use HTTP/2 if available.
This can sometimes speed up transfers due to a \c
.UR https://github.com/golang/go/issues/37373
problem in the Go standard library
.UE \c
\&.
.SS \-\-dscp string
Specify a DSCP value or name to use in connections.
This could help QoS system to identify traffic class.
BE, EF, DF, LE, CSx and AFxx are allowed.
.PP
See the description of \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differentiated_services
differentiated services
.UE \c
\ to get an idea of this field.
Setting this to 1 (LE) to identify the flow to SCAVENGER class can avoid
occupying too much bandwidth in a network with DiffServ support (\c
.UR https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8622
RFC 8622
.UE \c
).
.PP
For example, if you configured QoS on router to handle LE properly.
Running:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-dscp LE from:/from to:/to
.EE
.PP
would make the priority lower than usual internet flows.
.PP
This option has no effect on Windows (see \c
.UR https://github.com/golang/go/issues/42728
golang/go#42728
.UE \c
).
.SS \-n, \-\-dry\-run
Do a trial run with no permanent changes.
Use this to see what rclone would do without actually doing it.
Useful when setting up the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
\ command which deletes files in the destination.
.SS \-\-expect\-continue\-timeout Duration
This specifies the amount of time to wait for a server\(aqs first
response headers after fully writing the request headers if the request
has an \(dqExpect: 100\-continue\(dq header.
Not all backends support using this.
.PP
Zero means no timeout and causes the body to be sent immediately,
without waiting for the server to approve.
This time does not include the time to send the request header.
.PP
The default is \f[CR]1s\f[R].
Set to \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable.
.SS \-\-error\-on\-no\-transfer
By default, rclone will exit with return code 0 if there were no errors.
.PP
This option allows rclone to return exit code 9 if no files were
transferred between the source and destination.
This allows using rclone in scripts, and triggering follow\-on actions
if data was copied, or skipping if not.
.PP
NB: Enabling this option turns a usually non\-fatal error into a
potentially fatal one \- please check and adjust your scripts
accordingly!
.SS \-\-fix\-case
Normally, a sync to a case insensitive dest (such as macOS / Windows)
will not result in a matching filename if the source and dest filenames
have casing differences but are otherwise identical.
For example, syncing \f[CR]hello.txt\f[R] to \f[CR]HELLO.txt\f[R] will
normally result in the dest filename remaining \f[CR]HELLO.txt\f[R].
If \f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R] is set, then \f[CR]HELLO.txt\f[R] will be
renamed to \f[CR]hello.txt\f[R] to match the source.
.PP
NB:
.IP \(bu 2
directory names with incorrect casing will also be fixed
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R] will be ignored if \f[CR]\-\-immutable\f[R] is
set
.IP \(bu 2
using \f[CR]\-\-local\-case\-sensitive\f[R] instead is not advisable; it
will cause \f[CR]HELLO.txt\f[R] to get deleted!
.IP \(bu 2
the old dest filename must not be excluded by filters.
Be especially careful with \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/#files-from-read-list-of-source-file-names
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R]
.UE \c
, which does not respect \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/#ignore-case-make-searches-case-insensitive
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R]
.UE \c
!
.IP \(bu 2
on remotes that do not support server\-side move,
\f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R] will require downloading the file and
re\-uploading it.
To avoid this, do not use \f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R].
.SS \-\-fs\-cache\-expire\-duration Duration
When using rclone via the API rclone caches created remotes for 5
minutes by default in the \(dqfs cache\(dq.
This means that if you do repeated actions on the same remote then
rclone won\(aqt have to build it again from scratch, which makes it more
efficient.
.PP
This flag sets the time that the remotes are cached for.
If you set it to \f[CR]0\f[R] (or negative) then rclone won\(aqt cache
the remotes at all.
.PP
Note that if you use some flags, eg \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] and if
this is set to \f[CR]0\f[R] rclone may build two remotes (one for the
source or destination and one for the \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] where
it may have only built one before.
.SS \-\-fs\-cache\-expire\-interval Duration
This controls how often rclone checks for cached remotes to expire.
See the \f[CR]\-\-fs\-cache\-expire\-duration\f[R] documentation above
for more info.
The default is 60s, set to 0 to disable expiry.
.SS \-\-header stringArray
Add an HTTP header for all transactions.
The flag can be repeated to add multiple headers.
.PP
If you want to add headers only for uploads use
\f[CR]\-\-header\-upload\f[R] and if you want to add headers only for
downloads use \f[CR]\-\-header\-download\f[R].
.PP
This flag is supported for all HTTP based backends even those not
supported by \f[CR]\-\-header\-upload\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-header\-download\f[R] so may be used as a workaround for those
with care.
.PP
The headers are only sent to the host of the original request.
If the server redirects to another host (including a subdomain or a
different port), or redirects an \f[CR]https\f[R] request to
\f[CR]http\f[R], the headers are not sent to it, or to any further hop
in that redirect chain.
Rclone logs this at debug level (\f[CR]\-vv\f[R]).
.IP
.EX
rclone ls remote:test \-\-header \(dqX\-Rclone: Foo\(dq \-\-header \(dqX\-LetMeIn: Yes\(dq
.EE
.SS \-\-header\-download stringArray
Add an HTTP header for all download transactions.
The flag can be repeated to add multiple headers.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive s3:test/src \(ti/dst \-\-header\-download \(dqX\-Amz\-Meta\-Test: Foo\(dq \-\-header\-download \(dqX\-Amz\-Meta\-Test2: Bar\(dq
.EE
.PP
See GitHub issue \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/59
#59
.UE \c
\ for currently supported backends.
.SS \-\-header\-upload stringArray
Add an HTTP header for all upload transactions.
The flag can be repeated to add multiple headers.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive \(ti/src s3:test/dst \-\-header\-upload \(dqContent\-Disposition: attachment; filename=\(aqcool.html\(aq\(dq \-\-header\-upload \(dqX\-Amz\-Meta\-Test: FooBar\(dq
.EE
.PP
See GitHub issue \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/59
#59
.UE \c
\ for currently supported backends.
.SS \-\-http\-proxy string
Use this option to set an HTTP proxy for all HTTP based services to use.
.PP
Rclone also supports the standard HTTP proxy environment variables which
it will pick up automatically.
The is the way the HTTP proxy will normally be set but this flag can be
used to override it.
.SS \-\-human\-readable
Rclone commands output values for sizes (e.g.
number of bytes) and counts (e.g.
number of files) either as \f[I]raw\f[R] numbers, or in
\f[I]human\-readable\f[R] format.
.PP
In human\-readable format the values are scaled to larger units,
indicated with a suffix shown after the value, and rounded to three
decimals.
Rclone consistently uses binary units (powers of 2) for sizes and
decimal units (powers of 10) for counts.
The unit prefix for size is according to IEC standard notation, e.g.
\f[CR]Ki\f[R] for kibi.
Used with byte unit, \f[CR]1 KiB\f[R] means 1024 Byte.
In list type of output, only the unit prefix appended to the value (e.g.
\f[CR]9.762Ki\f[R]), while in more textual output the full unit is shown
(e.g.
\f[CR]9.762 KiB\f[R]).
For counts the SI standard notation is used, e.g.
prefix \f[CR]k\f[R] for kilo.
Used with file counts, \f[CR]1k\f[R] means 1000 files.
.PP
The various \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_ls/
list
.UE \c
\ commands output raw numbers by default.
Option \f[CR]\-\-human\-readable\f[R] will make them output values in
human\-readable format instead (with the short unit prefix).
.PP
The \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_about/
about
.UE \c
\ command outputs human\-readable by default, with a command\-specific
option \f[CR]\-\-full\f[R] to output the raw numbers instead.
.PP
Command \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_size/
size
.UE \c
\ outputs both human\-readable and raw numbers in the same output.
.PP
The \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_tree/
tree
.UE \c
\ command also considers \f[CR]\-\-human\-readable\f[R], but it will not
use the exact same notation as the other commands: It rounds to one
decimal, and uses single letter suffix, e.g.
\f[CR]K\f[R] instead of \f[CR]Ki\f[R].
The reason for this is that it relies on an external library.
.PP
The interactive command \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_ncdu/
ncdu
.UE \c
\ shows human\-readable by default, and responds to key \f[CR]u\f[R] for
toggling human\-readable format.
.SS \-\-ignore\-case\-sync
Using this option will cause rclone to ignore the case of the files when
synchronizing so files will not be copied/synced when the existing
filenames are the same, even if the casing is different.
.SS \-\-ignore\-checksum
Normally rclone will check that the checksums of transferred files
match, and give an error \(dqcorrupted on transfer\(dq if they don\(aqt.
.PP
You can use this option to skip that check.
You should only use it if you have had the \(dqcorrupted on transfer\(dq
error message and you are sure you might want to transfer potentially
corrupted data.
.SS \-\-ignore\-existing
Using this option will make rclone unconditionally skip all files that
exist on the destination, no matter the content of these files.
.PP
While this isn\(aqt a generally recommended option, it can be useful in
cases where your files change due to encryption.
However, it cannot correct partial transfers in case a transfer was
interrupted.
.PP
When performing a \f[CR]move\f[R]/\f[CR]moveto\f[R] command, this flag
will leave skipped files in the source location unchanged when a file
with the same name exists on the destination.
.SS \-\-ignore\-size
Normally rclone will look at modification time and size of files to see
if they are equal.
If you set this flag then rclone will check only the modification time.
If \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] is set then it only checks the checksum.
.PP
It will also cause rclone to skip verifying the sizes are the same after
transfer.
.PP
This can be useful for transferring files to and from OneDrive which
occasionally misreports the size of image files (see \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/399
#399
.UE \c
\ for more info).
.SS \-I, \-\-ignore\-times
Using this option will cause rclone to unconditionally upload all files
regardless of the state of files on the destination.
.PP
Normally rclone would skip any files that have the same modification
time and are the same size (or have the same checksum if using
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R]).
.SS \-\-immutable
Treat source and destination files as immutable and disallow
modification.
.PP
With this option set, files will be created and deleted as requested,
but existing files will never be updated.
If an existing file does not match between the source and destination,
rclone will give the error
\f[CR]Source and destination exist but do not match: immutable file modified\f[R].
.PP
Note that only commands which transfer files (e.g.
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
) are affected by this behavior, and only modification is disallowed.
Files may still be deleted explicitly (e.g.
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_delete/
delete
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_purge/
purge
.UE \c
) or implicitly (e.g.
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
).
Use \f[CR]copy \-\-immutable\f[R] if it is desired to avoid deletion as
well as modification.
.PP
This can be useful as an additional layer of protection for immutable or
append\-only data sets (notably backup archives), where modification
implies corruption and should not be propagated.
.SS \-\-inplace
The \f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R] flag changes the behaviour of rclone when
uploading files to some backends (backends with the
\f[CR]PartialUploads\f[R] feature flag set) such as:
.IP \(bu 2
local
.IP \(bu 2
ftp
.IP \(bu 2
sftp
.IP \(bu 2
pcloud
.PP
Without \f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R] (the default) rclone will first upload to
a temporary file with an extension like this, where \f[CR]XXXXXX\f[R]
represents a hash of the source file\(aqs fingerprint and
\f[CR].partial\f[R] is \-\-partial\-suffix value (\f[CR].partial\f[R] by
default).
.IP
.EX
original\-file\-name.XXXXXX.partial
.EE
.PP
(rclone will make sure the final name is no longer than 100 characters
by truncating the \f[CR]original\-file\-name\f[R] part if necessary).
.PP
When the upload is complete, rclone will rename the \f[CR].partial\f[R]
file to the correct name, overwriting any existing file at that point.
If the upload fails then the \f[CR].partial\f[R] file will be deleted.
.PP
This prevents other users of the backend from seeing partially uploaded
files in their new names and prevents overwriting the old file until the
new one is completely uploaded.
.PP
If the \f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R] flag is supplied, rclone will upload
directly to the final name without creating a \f[CR].partial\f[R] file.
.PP
This means that an incomplete file will be visible in the directory
listings while the upload is in progress and any existing files will be
overwritten as soon as the upload starts.
If the transfer fails then the file will be deleted.
This can cause data loss of the existing file if the transfer fails.
.PP
Note that on the local file system if you don\(aqt use
\f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R] hard links (Unix only) will be broken.
And if you do use \f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R] you won\(aqt be able to update
in use executables.
.PP
Note also that versions of rclone prior to v1.63.0 behave as if the
\f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R] flag is always supplied.
.SS \-i, \-\-interactive
This flag can be used to tell rclone that you wish a manual confirmation
before destructive operations.
.PP
It is \f[B]recommended\f[R] that you use this flag while learning rclone
especially with \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R].
.PP
For example
.IP
.EX
$ rclone delete \-\-interactive /tmp/dir
rclone: delete \(dqimportant\-file.txt\(dq?
y) Yes, this is OK (default)
n) No, skip this
s) Skip all delete operations with no more questions
!) Do all delete operations with no more questions
q) Exit rclone now.
y/n/s/!/q> n
.EE
.PP
The options mean
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]y\f[R]: \f[B]Yes\f[R], this operation should go ahead.
You can also press Return for this to happen.
You\(aqll be asked every time unless you choose \f[CR]s\f[R] or
\f[CR]!\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]n\f[R]: \f[B]No\f[R], do not do this operation.
You\(aqll be asked every time unless you choose \f[CR]s\f[R] or
\f[CR]!\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]s\f[R]: \f[B]Skip\f[R] all the following operations of this type
with no more questions.
This takes effect until rclone exits.
If there are any different kind of operations you\(aqll be prompted for
them.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]!\f[R]: \f[B]Do all\f[R] the following operations with no more
questions.
Useful if you\(aqve decided that you don\(aqt mind rclone doing that
kind of operation.
This takes effect until rclone exits .
If there are any different kind of operations you\(aqll be prompted for
them.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]q\f[R]: \f[B]Quit\f[R] rclone now, just in case!
.SS \-\-leave\-root
During rmdirs it will not remove root directory, even if it\(aqs empty.
.SS \-l, \-\-links
Normally rclone will ignore symlinks or junction points (which behave
like symlinks under Windows).
Ignored files won\(aqt be copied, moved or deleted in a sync.
.PP
If you supply this flag then rclone will copy symbolic links from any
supported backend, and store them as text files, with a
\f[CR].rclonelink\f[R] suffix in the destination.
.PP
The text file will contain the target of the symbolic link.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] / \f[CR]\-l\f[R] flag enables this feature for
all supported backends and the VFS.
There are individual flags for just enabling it for the VFS
\f[CR]\-\-vfs\-links\f[R] and the local backend
\f[CR]\-\-local\-links\f[R] if required.
.SS \-\-list\-cutoff int
When syncing rclone needs to sort directory entries before comparing
them.
Below this threshold (1,000,000) by default, rclone will store the
directory entries in memory.
1,000,000 entries will take approx 1GB of RAM to store.
Above this threshold rclone will store directory entries on disk and
sort them without using a lot of memory.
.PP
Doing this is slightly less efficient then sorting them in memory and
will only work well for the bucket based backends (eg s3, b2, azureblob,
swift) but these are the only backends likely to have millions of
entries in a directory.
.SS \-\-log\-file string
Log all of rclone\(aqs output to a file.
This is not active by default.
This can be useful for tracking down problems with syncs in combination
with the \f[CR]\-v\f[R] flag.
See the logging section for more info.
.PP
If the file exists, then rclone will append to it.
.PP
Note that if you are using the \f[CR]logrotate\f[R] program to manage
rclone\(aqs logs, then you should use the \f[CR]copytruncate\f[R] option
as rclone doesn\(aqt have a signal to rotate logs.
.PP
Alternatively you can use the options below to manage rclone\(aqs built
in log rotation.
.SS \-\-log\-file\-max\-size SizeSuffix
Maximum size of the log file before it\(aqs rotated (eg \f[CR]10M\f[R]).
This SizeSuffix is rounded to the nearest MiB or 1 MiB if lower.
.PP
If \f[CR]\-\-log\-file\f[R] is not set then this option will be ignored.
.PP
If this option is not set, then the other log rotation options will be
ignored.
.PP
For example if the following flags are in use
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-log\-file rclone.log \-\-log\-file\-max\-size 1M \-\-log\-file\-max\-backups 3
.EE
.PP
Then this will create log files which look like this
.IP
.EX
$ ls \-l
\-rw\-\-\-\-\-\-\-  1 user user  1048491 Apr 11 17:15 rclone\-2025\-04\-11T17\-15\-29.998.log
\-rw\-\-\-\-\-\-\-  1 user user  1048511 Apr 11 17:15 rclone\-2025\-04\-11T17\-15\-30.467.log
\-rw\-\-\-\-\-\-\-  1 user user  1048559 Apr 11 17:15 rclone\-2025\-04\-11T17\-15\-30.543.log
\-rw\-\-\-\-\-\-\-  1 user user   521602 Apr 11 17:15 rclone.log
.EE
.PP
The file \f[CR]rclone.log\f[R] being the current one.
.SS \-\-log\-file\-compress
If set, compress rotated log files using gzip.
This changes the extension of the old log files to \f[CR].log.gz\f[R].
.PP
Defaults to false \- don\(aqt compress log files.
.SS \-\-log\-file\-max\-age Duration
Maximum duration to retain old log files (eg \f[CR]7d\f[R]).
This is rounded to the dearest day, or 1 day if lower.
.PP
The default is to retain all old log files.
.SS \-\-log\-file\-max\-backups int
Maximum number of old log files to retain
.PP
The default is to retain all old log files.
.SS \-\-log\-format string
Comma separated list of log format options.
The accepted options are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]date\f[R] \- Add a date in the format YYYY/MM/YY to the log.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]time\f[R] \- Add a time to the log in format HH:MM:SS.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]microseconds\f[R] \- Add microseconds to the time in format
HH:MM:SS.SSSSSS.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]UTC\f[R] \- Make the logs in UTC not localtime.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]longfile\f[R] \- Adds the source file and line number of the log
statement.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]shortfile\f[R] \- Adds the source file and line number of the log
statement.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]pid\f[R] \- Add the process ID to the log \- useful with
\f[CR]rclone mount \-\-daemon\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nolevel\f[R] \- Don\(aqt add the level to the log.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]json\f[R] \- Equivalent to adding \f[CR]\-\-use\-json\-log\f[R]
.PP
They are added to the log line in the order above.
.PP
The default log format is \f[CR]\(dqdate,time\(dq\f[R].
.SS \-\-log\-level LogLevel
This sets the log level for rclone.
The default log level is \f[CR]NOTICE\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]DEBUG\f[R] is equivalent to \f[CR]\-vv\f[R].
It outputs lots of debug info \- useful for bug reports and really
finding out what rclone is doing.
.PP
\f[CR]INFO\f[R] is equivalent to \f[CR]\-v\f[R].
It outputs information about each transfer and prints stats once a
minute by default.
.PP
\f[CR]NOTICE\f[R] is the default log level if no logging flags are
supplied.
It outputs very little when things are working normally.
It outputs warnings and significant events.
.PP
\f[CR]ERROR\f[R] is equivalent to \f[CR]\-q\f[R].
It only outputs error messages.
.PP
See also the logging section.
.SS \-\-windows\-event\-log\-level LogLevel
If this is configured (the default is \f[CR]OFF\f[R]) then logs of this
level and above will be logged to the Windows event log in
\f[B]addition\f[R] to the normal logs.
These will be logged in JSON format as described below regardless of
what format the main logs are configured for.
.PP
The Windows event log only has 3 levels of severity \f[CR]Info\f[R],
\f[CR]Warning\f[R] and \f[CR]Error\f[R].
If enabled we map rclone levels like this.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Error\f[R] ← \f[CR]ERROR\f[R] (and above)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Warning\f[R] ← \f[CR]WARNING\f[R] (note that this level is defined
but not currently used).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Info\f[R] ← \f[CR]NOTICE\f[R], \f[CR]INFO\f[R] and
\f[CR]DEBUG\f[R].
.PP
Rclone will declare its log source as \(dqrclone\(dq if it is has enough
permissions to create the registry key needed.
If not then logs will appear as \(dqApplication\(dq.
You can run
\f[CR]rclone version \-\-windows\-event\-log\-level DEBUG\f[R] once as
administrator to create the registry key in advance.
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R] that the \f[CR]\-\-windows\-event\-log\-level\f[R] level
must be greater (more severe) than or equal to the
\f[CR]\-\-log\-level\f[R].
For example to log DEBUG to a log file but ERRORs to the event log you
would use
.IP
.EX
\-\-log\-file rclone.log \-\-log\-level DEBUG \-\-windows\-event\-log\-level ERROR
.EE
.PP
This option is only supported Windows platforms.
.SS \-\-use\-json\-log
This switches the log format to JSON.
The log messages are then streamed as individual JSON objects, with
fields: \f[CR]level\f[R], \f[CR]msg\f[R], \f[CR]source\f[R], and
\f[CR]time\f[R].
The resulting format is what is sometimes referred to as \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON_streaming#Newline-delimited_JSON
newline\-delimited JSON
.UE \c
\ (NDJSON), or JSON Lines (JSONL).
This is well suited for processing by traditional line\-oriented tools
and shell pipelines, but a complete log file is not strictly valid JSON
and needs a parser that can handle it.
.PP
The JSON logs will be printed on a single line, but are shown expanded
here for clarity.
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtime\(dq: \(dq2025\-05\-13T17:30:51.036237518+01:00\(dq,
  \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqdebug\(dq,
  \(dqmsg\(dq: \(dq4 go routines active\(rsn\(dq,
  \(dqsource\(dq: \(dqcmd/cmd.go:298\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
Completed data transfer logs will have extra \f[CR]size\f[R]
information.
Logs which are about a particular object will have \f[CR]object\f[R] and
\f[CR]objectType\f[R] fields also.
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtime\(dq: \(dq2025\-05\-13T17:38:05.540846352+01:00\(dq,
  \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqinfo\(dq,
  \(dqmsg\(dq: \(dqCopied (new) to: file2.txt\(dq,
  \(dqsize\(dq: 6,
  \(dqobject\(dq: \(dqfile.txt\(dq,
  \(dqobjectType\(dq: \(dq*local.Object\(dq,
  \(dqsource\(dq: \(dqoperations/copy.go:368\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
Stats logs will contain a \f[CR]stats\f[R] field which is the same as
returned from the rc call \c
.UR https://rclone.org/rc/#core-stats
core/stats
.UE \c
\&.
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtime\(dq: \(dq2025\-05\-13T17:38:05.540912847+01:00\(dq,
  \(dqlevel\(dq: \(dqinfo\(dq,
  \(dqmsg\(dq: \(dq...text version of the stats...\(dq,
  \(dqstats\(dq: {
    \(dqbytes\(dq: 6,
    \(dqchecks\(dq: 0,
    \(dqdeletedDirs\(dq: 0,
    \(dqdeletes\(dq: 0,
    \(dqelapsedTime\(dq: 0.000904825,
    \f[B]...truncated\f[R] \f[B]for\f[R] \f[B]clarity...\f[R]
    \(dqtotalBytes\(dq: 6,
    \(dqtotalChecks\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtotalTransfers\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtransferTime\(dq: 0.000882794,
    \(dqtransfers\(dq: 1
  },
  \(dqsource\(dq: \(dqaccounting/stats.go:569\(dq
}
.EE
.SS \-\-low\-level\-retries int
This controls the number of low level retries rclone does.
.PP
A low level retry is used to retry a failing operation \- typically one
HTTP request.
This might be uploading a chunk of a big file for example.
You will see low level retries in the log with the \f[CR]\-v\f[R] flag.
.PP
This shouldn\(aqt need to be changed from the default in normal
operations.
However, if you get a lot of low level retries you may wish to reduce
the value so rclone moves on to a high level retry (see the
\f[CR]\-\-retries\f[R] flag) quicker.
.PP
Disable low level retries with \f[CR]\-\-low\-level\-retries 1\f[R].
.SS \-\-max\-backlog int
This is the maximum allowable backlog of files in a sync/copy/move
queued for being checked or transferred.
.PP
This can be set arbitrarily large.
It will only use memory when the queue is in use.
Note that it will use in the order of N KiB of memory when the backlog
is in use.
.PP
Setting this large allows rclone to calculate how many files are pending
more accurately, give a more accurate estimated finish time and make
\f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R] work more accurately.
.PP
Setting this small will make rclone more synchronous to the listings of
the remote which may be desirable.
.PP
Setting this to a negative number will make the backlog as large as
possible.
.SS \-\-max\-buffer\-memory SizeSuffix
If set, don\(aqt allocate more than given amount of memory as buffers.
If not set or set to \f[CR]0\f[R] or \f[CR]off\f[R] this will not limit
the amount of memory in use.
.PP
This includes memory used by buffers created by the
\f[CR]\-\-buffer\f[R] flag and buffers used by multi\-thread transfers.
.PP
Most multi\-thread transfers do not take additional memory, but some do
depending on the backend (eg the s3 backend for uploads).
This means there is a tension between total setting
\f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] as high as possible and memory use.
.PP
Setting \f[CR]\-\-max\-buffer\-memory\f[R] allows the buffer memory to
be controlled so that it doesn\(aqt overwhelm the machine and allows
\f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] to be set large.
.SS \-\-max\-connections int
This sets the maximum number of concurrent calls to the backend API.
It may not map 1:1 to TCP or HTTP connections depending on the backend
in use and the use of HTTP1 vs HTTP2.
.PP
When downloading files, backends only limit the initial opening of the
stream.
The bulk data download is not counted as a connection.
This means that the \f[CR]\-\-max\-\-connections\f[R] flag won\(aqt
limit the total number of downloads.
.PP
Note that it is possible to cause deadlocks with this setting so it
should be used with care.
.PP
If you are doing a sync or copy then make sure
\f[CR]\-\-max\-connections\f[R] is one more than the sum of
\f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-checkers\f[R].
.PP
If you use \f[CR]\-\-check\-first\f[R] then
\f[CR]\-\-max\-connections\f[R] just needs to be one more than the
maximum of \f[CR]\-\-checkers\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R].
.PP
So for \f[CR]\-\-max\-connections 3\f[R] you\(aqd use
\f[CR]\-\-checkers 2 \-\-transfers 2 \-\-check\-first\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-checkers 1 \-\-transfers 1\f[R].
.PP
Setting this flag can be useful for backends which do multipart uploads
to limit the number of simultaneous parts being transferred.
.SS \-\-max\-delete int
This tells rclone not to delete more than N files.
If that limit is exceeded then a fatal error will be generated and
rclone will stop the operation in progress.
.SS \-\-max\-delete\-size SizeSuffix
Rclone will stop deleting files when the total size of deletions has
reached the size specified.
It defaults to off.
.PP
If that limit is exceeded then a fatal error will be generated and
rclone will stop the operation in progress.
.SS \-\-max\-depth int
This modifies the recursion depth for all the commands except purge.
.PP
So if you do \f[CR]rclone \-\-max\-depth 1 ls remote:path\f[R] you will
see only the files in the top level directory.
Using \f[CR]\-\-max\-depth 2\f[R] means you will see all the files in
first two directory levels and so on.
.PP
For historical reasons the \f[CR]lsd\f[R] command defaults to using a
\f[CR]\-\-max\-depth\f[R] of 1 \- you can override this with the command
line flag.
.PP
You can use this command to disable recursion (with
\f[CR]\-\-max\-depth 1\f[R]).
.PP
Note that if you use this with \f[CR]sync\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-delete\-excluded\f[R] the files not recursed through are
considered excluded and will be deleted on the destination.
Test first with \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] if you are not sure what will
happen.
.SS \-\-max\-duration Duration
Rclone will stop transferring when it has run for the duration
specified.
Defaults to off.
.PP
When the limit is reached all transfers will stop immediately.
Use \f[CR]\-\-cutoff\-mode\f[R] to modify this behaviour.
.PP
Rclone will exit with exit code 10 if the duration limit is reached.
.SS \-\-max\-transfer SizeSuffix
Rclone will stop transferring when it has reached the size specified.
Defaults to off.
.PP
When the limit is reached all transfers will stop immediately.
Use \f[CR]\-\-cutoff\-mode\f[R] to modify this behaviour.
.PP
Rclone will exit with exit code 8 if the transfer limit is reached.
.SS \-\-cutoff\-mode HARD|SOFT|CAUTIOUS
Configure the behavior of \f[CR]\-\-max\-transfer\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-max\-duration\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]HARD\f[R] will stop transferring immediately when rclone reaches
the limit.
This is the default.
.PP
\f[CR]SOFT\f[R] will stop starting new transfers when rclone reaches the
limit.
.PP
\f[CR]CAUTIOUS\f[R] will try to prevent rclone from reaching the limit.
Only applicable for \f[CR]\-\-max\-transfer\f[R].
.SS \-M, \-\-metadata
Setting this flag enables rclone to copy the metadata from the source to
the destination.
For local backends this is ownership, permissions, xattr etc.
See the metadata section for more info.
.SS \-\-metadata\-mapper SpaceSepList
If you supply the parameter
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-mapper /path/to/program\f[R] then rclone will use
that program to map metadata from source object to destination object.
.PP
The argument to this flag should be a command with an optional space
separated list of arguments.
If one of the arguments has a space in then enclose it in
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R], if you want a literal \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] in an argument
then enclose the argument in \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] and double the
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R].
See \c
.UR https://godoc.org/encoding/csv
CSV encoding
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.IP
.EX
\-\-metadata\-mapper \(dqpython bin/test_metadata_mapper.py\(dq
\-\-metadata\-mapper \(aqpython bin/test_metadata_mapper.py \(dqargument with a space\(dq\(aq
\-\-metadata\-mapper \(aqpython bin/test_metadata_mapper.py \(dqargument with \(dq\(dqtwo\(dq\(dq quotes\(dq\(aq
.EE
.PP
This uses a simple JSON based protocol with input on STDIN and output on
STDOUT.
This will be called for every file and directory copied and may be
called concurrently.
.PP
The program\(aqs job is to take a metadata blob on the input and turn it
into a metadata blob on the output suitable for the destination backend.
.PP
Input to the program (via STDIN) might look like this.
This provides some context for the \f[CR]Metadata\f[R] which may be
important.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SrcFs\f[R] is the config string for the remote that the object is
currently on.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SrcFsType\f[R] is the name of the source backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]DstFs\f[R] is the config string for the remote that the object is
being copied to
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]DstFsType\f[R] is the name of the destination backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Remote\f[R] is the path of the object relative to the root.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Size\f[R], \f[CR]MimeType\f[R], \f[CR]ModTime\f[R] are attributes
of the object.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]IsDir\f[R] is \f[CR]true\f[R] if this is a directory (not yet
implemented).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ID\f[R] is the source \f[CR]ID\f[R] of the object if known.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Metadata\f[R] is the backend specific metadata as described in the
backend docs.
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqSrcFs\(dq: \(dqgdrive:\(dq,
  \(dqSrcFsType\(dq: \(dqdrive\(dq,
  \(dqDstFs\(dq: \(dqnewdrive:user\(dq,
  \(dqDstFsType\(dq: \(dqonedrive\(dq,
  \(dqRemote\(dq: \(dqtest.txt\(dq,
  \(dqSize\(dq: 6,
  \(dqMimeType\(dq: \(dqtext/plain; charset=utf\-8\(dq,
  \(dqModTime\(dq: \(dq2022\-10\-11T17:53:10.286745272+01:00\(dq,
  \(dqIsDir\(dq: \f[B]false\f[R],
  \(dqID\(dq: \(dqxyz\(dq,
  \(dqMetadata\(dq: {
    \(dqbtime\(dq: \(dq2022\-10\-11T16:53:11Z\(dq,
    \(dqcontent\-type\(dq: \(dqtext/plain; charset=utf\-8\(dq,
    \(dqmtime\(dq: \(dq2022\-10\-11T17:53:10.286745272+01:00\(dq,
    \(dqowner\(dq: \(dquser1\(atdomain1.com\(dq,
    \(dqpermissions\(dq: \(dq...\(dq,
    \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqmy nice file\(dq,
    \(dqstarred\(dq: \(dqfalse\(dq
  }
}
.EE
.PP
The program should then modify the input as desired and send it to
STDOUT.
The returned \f[CR]Metadata\f[R] field will be used in its entirety for
the destination object.
Any other fields will be ignored.
Note in this example we translate user names and permissions and add
something to the description:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqMetadata\(dq: {
    \(dqbtime\(dq: \(dq2022\-10\-11T16:53:11Z\(dq,
    \(dqcontent\-type\(dq: \(dqtext/plain; charset=utf\-8\(dq,
    \(dqmtime\(dq: \(dq2022\-10\-11T17:53:10.286745272+01:00\(dq,
    \(dqowner\(dq: \(dquser1\(atdomain2.com\(dq,
    \(dqpermissions\(dq: \(dq...\(dq,
    \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqmy nice file [migrated from domain1]\(dq,
    \(dqstarred\(dq: \(dqfalse\(dq
  }
}
.EE
.PP
Metadata can be removed here too.
.PP
An example python program might look something like this to implement
the above transformations.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] sys, json

i = json.load(sys.stdin)
metadata = i[\(dqMetadata\(dq]
\f[I]# Add tag to description\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] \(dqdescription\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] metadata:
    metadata[\(dqdescription\(dq] += \(dq [migrated from domain1]\(dq
\f[B]else\f[R]:
    metadata[\(dqdescription\(dq] = \(dq[migrated from domain1]\(dq
\f[I]# Modify owner\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] \(dqowner\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] metadata:
    metadata[\(dqowner\(dq] = metadata[\(dqowner\(dq].replace(\(dqdomain1.com\(dq, \(dqdomain2.com\(dq)
o = { \(dqMetadata\(dq: metadata }
json.dump(o, sys.stdout, indent=\(dq\(rst\(dq)
.EE
.PP
You can find this example (slightly expanded) in the rclone source code
at \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/blob/master/bin/test_metadata_mapper.py
bin/test_metadata_mapper.py
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
If you want to see the input to the metadata mapper and the output
returned from it in the log you can use \f[CR]\-vv \-\-dump mapper\f[R].
.PP
See the metadata section for more info.
.SS \-\-metadata\-set stringArray
Specify value as string in format \f[CR]key=value\f[R] to add metadata
\f[CR]key\f[R] with value \f[CR]value\f[R] when uploading.
This can be repeated as many times as required.
See the metadata section for more info.
.SS \-\-modify\-window Duration
When checking whether a file has been modified, this is the maximum
allowed time difference that a file can have and still be considered
equivalent.
.PP
The default is \f[CR]1ns\f[R] unless this is overridden by a remote.
For example OS X only stores modification times to the nearest second so
if you are reading and writing to an OS X filing system this will be
\f[CR]1s\f[R] by default.
.PP
This command line flag allows you to override that computed default.
.SS \-\-multi\-thread\-write\-buffer\-size SizeSuffix
When transferring with multiple threads, rclone will buffer the
specified number of bytes in memory before writing to disk for each
thread.
.PP
This can improve performance if the underlying filesystem does not deal
well with a lot of small writes in different positions of the file, so
if you see transfers being limited by disk write speed, you might want
to experiment with different values.
Specially for magnetic drives and remote file systems a higher value can
be useful.
.PP
Nevertheless, the default of \f[CR]128k\f[R] should be fine for almost
all use cases, so before changing it ensure that network is not really
your bottleneck.
.PP
As a final hint, size is not the only factor: block size (or similar
concept) can have an impact.
In one case, we observed that exact multiples of 16k performed much
better than other values.
.SS \-\-multi\-thread\-chunk\-size SizeSuffix
Normally the chunk size for multi thread transfers is set by the
backend.
However some backends such as \f[CR]local\f[R] and \f[CR]smb\f[R] (which
implement \f[CR]OpenWriterAt\f[R] but not \f[CR]OpenChunkWriter\f[R])
don\(aqt have a natural chunk size.
.PP
In this case the value of this option is used (default 64Mi).
.SS \-\-multi\-thread\-cutoff SizeSuffix
When transferring files above specified size to capable backends, rclone
will use multiple threads to transfer the file (default 256M).
.PP
Capable backends are marked in the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#optional-features
overview
.UE \c
\ as \f[CR]MultithreadUpload\f[R].
(They need to implement either the \f[CR]OpenWriterAt\f[R] or
\f[CR]OpenChunkWriter\f[R] internal interfaces).
These include \f[CR]local\f[R], \f[CR]s3\f[R], \f[CR]azureblob\f[R],
\f[CR]b2\f[R], \f[CR]oracleobjectstorage\f[R] and \f[CR]smb\f[R] at the
time of writing.
.PP
On the local disk, rclone preallocates the file (using
\f[CR]fallocate(FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE)\f[R] on unix or
\f[CR]NTSetInformationFile\f[R] on Windows both of which takes no time)
then each thread writes directly into the file at the correct place.
This means that rclone won\(aqt create fragmented or sparse files and
there won\(aqt be any assembly time at the end of the transfer.
.PP
The number of threads used to transfer is controlled by
\f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-streams\f[R].
.PP
Use \f[CR]\-vv\f[R] if you wish to see info about the threads.
.PP
This will work with the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
\ commands, and friends \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copyto/
copyto
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_moveto/
moveto
.UE \c
\&.
Multi thread transfers will be used with \f[CR]rclone mount\f[R] and
\f[CR]rclone serve\f[R] if \f[CR]\-\-vfs\-cache\-mode\f[R] is set to
\f[CR]writes\f[R] or above.
.PP
Most multi\-thread transfers do not take additional memory, but some do
(for example uploading to s3).
In the worst case memory usage can be at maximum
\f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] * \f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-chunk\-size\f[R] *
\f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-streams\f[R] or specifically for the s3 backend
\f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] * \f[CR]\-\-s3\-chunk\-size\f[R] *
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-concurrency\f[R].
However you can use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#max-buffer-memory
\-\-max\-buffer\-memory
.UE \c
\ flag to control the maximum memory used here.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] that this \f[B]only\f[R] works with supported backends as
the destination but will work with any backend as the source.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] that multi\-thread copies are disabled for local to local
copies as they are faster without unless
\f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-streams\f[R] is set explicitly.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] on Windows using multi\-thread transfers to the local disk
will cause the resulting files to be \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sparse_file
sparse
.UE \c
\&.
Use \f[CR]\-\-local\-no\-sparse\f[R] to disable sparse files (which may
cause long delays at the start of transfers) or disable multi\-thread
transfers with \f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-streams 0\f[R]
.SS \-\-multi\-thread\-streams int
When using multi thread transfers (see above
\f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-cutoff\f[R]) this sets the number of streams to
use.
Set to \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable multi thread transfers (Default 4).
.PP
If the backend has a \f[CR]\-\-backend\-upload\-concurrency\f[R] setting
(eg \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-concurrency\f[R]) then this setting will be
used as the number of transfers instead if it is larger than the value
of \f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-streams\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-multi\-thread\-streams\f[R] isn\(aqt set.
.SS \-\-name\-transform stringArray
\f[CR]\-\-name\-transform\f[R] introduces path name transformations for
\f[CR]rclone copy\f[R], \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R], and
\f[CR]rclone move\f[R].
These transformations enable modifications to source and destination
file names by applying prefixes, suffixes, and other alterations during
transfer operations.
For detailed docs and examples, see \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_convmv/
\f[CR]convmv\f[R]
.UE \c
\&.
.SS \-\-no\-check\-dest
The \f[CR]\-\-no\-check\-dest\f[R] can be used with \f[CR]move\f[R] or
\f[CR]copy\f[R] and it causes rclone not to check the destination at all
when copying files.
.PP
This means that:
.IP \(bu 2
the destination is not listed minimising the API calls
.IP \(bu 2
files are always transferred
.IP \(bu 2
this can cause duplicates on remotes which allow it (e.g.
Google Drive)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-retries 1\f[R] is recommended otherwise you\(aqll transfer
everything again on a retry
.PP
This flag is useful to minimise the transactions if you know that none
of the files are on the destination.
.PP
This is a specialized flag which should be ignored by most users!
.SS \-\-no\-gzip\-encoding
Don\(aqt set \f[CR]Accept\-Encoding: gzip\f[R].
This means that rclone won\(aqt ask the server for compressed files
automatically.
Useful if you\(aqve set the server to return files with
\f[CR]Content\-Encoding: gzip\f[R] but you uploaded compressed files.
.PP
There is no need to set this in normal operation, and doing so will
decrease the network transfer efficiency of rclone.
.SS \-\-no\-traverse
The \f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] flag controls whether the destination
file system is traversed when using the \f[CR]copy\f[R] or
\f[CR]move\f[R] commands.
\f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] is not compatible with \f[CR]sync\f[R] and
will be ignored if you supply it with \f[CR]sync\f[R].
.PP
If you are only copying a small number of files (or are filtering most
of the files) and/or have a large number of files on the destination
then \f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] will stop rclone listing the
destination and save time.
.PP
However, if you are copying a large number of files, especially if you
are doing a copy where lots of the files under consideration haven\(aqt
changed and won\(aqt need copying then you shouldn\(aqt use
\f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R].
.PP
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
rclone copy
.UE \c
\ for an example of how to use it.
.SS \-\-no\-unicode\-normalization
Don\(aqt normalize unicode characters in filenames during the sync
routine.
.PP
Sometimes, an operating system will store filenames containing unicode
parts in their decomposed form (particularly macOS).
Some cloud storage systems will then recompose the unicode, resulting in
duplicate files if the data is ever copied back to a local filesystem.
.PP
Using this flag will disable that functionality, treating each unicode
character as unique.
For example, by default é and é will be normalized into the same
character.
With \f[CR]\-\-no\-unicode\-normalization\f[R] they will be treated as
unique characters.
.SS \-\-no\-update\-modtime
When using this flag, rclone won\(aqt update modification times of
remote files if they are incorrect as it would normally.
.PP
This can be used if the remote is being synced with another tool also
(e.g.
the Google Drive client).
.SS \-\-no\-update\-dir\-modtime
When using this flag, rclone won\(aqt update modification times of
remote directories if they are incorrect as it would normally.
.SS \-\-order\-by string
The \f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R] flag controls the order in which files in
the backlog are processed in \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R],
\f[CR]rclone copy\f[R] and \f[CR]rclone move\f[R].
.PP
The order by string is constructed like this.
The first part describes what aspect is being measured:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]size\f[R] \- order by the size of the files
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]name\f[R] \- order by the full path of the files
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]modtime\f[R] \- order by the modification date of the files
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]pattern\f[R] \- order by a list of path glob patterns
.PP
The \f[CR]size\f[R], \f[CR]name\f[R] and \f[CR]modtime\f[R] comparisons
can have a modifier appended with a comma:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ascending\f[R] or \f[CR]asc\f[R] \- order so that the smallest (or
oldest) is processed first
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]descending\f[R] or \f[CR]desc\f[R] \- order so that the largest
(or newest) is processed first
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]mixed\f[R] \- order so that the smallest is processed first for
some threads and the largest for others
.PP
If the modifier is \f[CR]mixed\f[R] then it can have an optional
percentage (which defaults to \f[CR]50\f[R]), e.g.
\f[CR]size,mixed,25\f[R] which means that 25% of the threads should be
taking the smallest items and 75% the largest.
The threads which take the smallest first will always take the smallest
first and likewise the largest first threads.
The \f[CR]mixed\f[R] mode can be useful to minimise the transfer time
when you are transferring a mixture of large and small files \- the
large files are guaranteed upload threads and bandwidth and the small
files will be processed continuously.
.PP
If no modifier is supplied then the order is \f[CR]ascending\f[R].
.PP
For \f[CR]pattern\f[R], append one or more comma\-separated patterns.
Patterns use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/#patterns
filter pattern syntax
.UE \c
\&.
Files matching the first pattern are processed first, followed by files
matching the second pattern, and so on.
Files which do not match a pattern are processed last.
Within each group, files are ordered by full path.
Patterns cannot contain a comma.
.PP
For example
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-order\-by size,desc\f[R] \- send the largest files first
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-order\-by modtime,ascending\f[R] \- send the oldest files
first
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-order\-by name\f[R] \- send the files with alphabetically by
path first
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-order\-by \(aqpattern,*.tar.gz,*.md5\(aq\f[R] \- send archives
before checksum files
.PP
If the \f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R] flag is not supplied or it is supplied
with an empty string then the default ordering will be used which is as
scanned.
With \f[CR]\-\-checkers 1\f[R] this is mostly alphabetical, however with
the default \f[CR]\-\-checkers 8\f[R] it is somewhat random.
.SS Limitations
The \f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R] flag does not do a separate pass over the
data.
This means that it may transfer some files out of the order specified if
.IP \(bu 2
there are no files in the backlog or the source has not been fully
scanned yet
.IP \(bu 2
there are more than \-\-max\-backlog files in the backlog
.PP
Rclone will do its best to transfer the best file it has so in practice
this should not cause a problem.
Think of \f[CR]\-\-order\-by\f[R] as being more of a best efforts flag
rather than a perfect ordering.
.PP
If you want perfect ordering then you will need to specify
\-\-check\-first which will find all the files which need transferring
first before transferring any.
.SS \-\-partial\-suffix string
When \-\-inplace is not used, it causes rclone to use the
\f[CR]\-\-partial\-suffix\f[R] as suffix for temporary files.
.PP
Suffix length limit is 16 characters.
.PP
The default is \f[CR].partial\f[R].
.SS \-\-password\-command SpaceSepList
This flag supplies a program which should supply the config password
when run.
This is an alternative to rclone prompting for the password or setting
the \f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\f[R] variable.
It is also used when setting the config password for the first time.
.PP
The argument to this should be a command with a space separated list of
arguments.
If one of the arguments has a space in then enclose it in
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R], if you want a literal \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] in an argument
then enclose the argument in \f[CR]\(dq\f[R] and double the
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R].
This includes the command itself: if the path to the executable contains
a space, it must be quoted too.
See \c
.UR https://godoc.org/encoding/csv
CSV encoding
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.PP
Eg
.IP
.EX
\-\-password\-command \(dqecho hello\(dq
\-\-password\-command \(aqecho \(dqhello with space\(dq\(aq
\-\-password\-command \(aqecho \(dqhello with \(dq\(dqquotes\(dq\(dq and space\(dq\(aq
\-\-password\-command \(aq\(dq/path with a space/get\-password.sh\(dq\(aq
.EE
.PP
Note that when changing the configuration password the environment
variable \f[CR]RCLONE_PASSWORD_CHANGE=1\f[R] will be set.
This can be used to distinguish initial decryption of the config file
from the new password.
.PP
See the Configuration Encryption for more info.
.PP
See a \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/wiki/Windows-Powershell-use-rclone-password-command-for-Config-file-password
Windows PowerShell example on the Wiki
.UE \c
\&.
.SS \-P, \-\-progress
This flag makes rclone update the stats in a static block in the
terminal providing a realtime overview of the transfer.
.PP
Any log messages will scroll above the static block.
Log messages will push the static block down to the bottom of the
terminal where it will stay.
.PP
Normally this is updated every 500mS but this period can be overridden
with the \f[CR]\-\-stats\f[R] flag.
.PP
This can be used with the \f[CR]\-\-stats\-one\-line\f[R] flag for a
simpler display.
.PP
To change the display length of filenames (for different terminal
widths), see the \f[CR]\-\-stats\-file\-name\-length\f[R] option.
The default output is formatted for 80 character wide terminals.
.PP
Note: On Windows until \c
.UR https://github.com/Azure/go-ansiterm/issues/26
this bug
.UE \c
\ is fixed all non\-ASCII characters will be replaced with \f[CR].\f[R]
when \f[CR]\-\-progress\f[R] is in use.
.SS \-\-progress\-terminal\-title
This flag, when used with \f[CR]\-P/\-\-progress\f[R], will print the
string \f[CR]ETA: %s\f[R] to the terminal title.
.SS \-q, \-\-quiet
This flag will limit rclone\(aqs output to error messages only.
.SS \-\-refresh\-times
The \f[CR]\-\-refresh\-times\f[R] flag can be used to update
modification times of existing files when they are out of sync on
backends which don\(aqt support hashes.
.PP
This is useful if you uploaded files with the incorrect timestamps and
you now wish to correct them.
.PP
This flag is \f[B]only\f[R] useful for destinations which don\(aqt
support hashes (e.g.
\f[CR]crypt\f[R]).
.PP
This can be used any of the sync commands \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
To use this flag you will need to be doing a modification time sync (so
not using \f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R]).
The flag will have no effect when using \f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R].
.PP
If this flag is used when rclone comes to upload a file it will check to
see if there is an existing file on the destination.
If this file matches the source with size (and checksum if available)
but has a differing timestamp then instead of re\-uploading it, rclone
will update the timestamp on the destination file.
If the checksum does not match rclone will upload the new file.
If the checksum is absent (e.g.
on a \f[CR]crypt\f[R] backend) then rclone will update the timestamp.
.PP
Note that some remotes can\(aqt set the modification time without
re\-uploading the file so this flag is less useful on them.
.PP
Normally if you are doing a modification time sync rclone will update
modification times without \f[CR]\-\-refresh\-times\f[R] provided that
the remote supports checksums \f[B]and\f[R] the checksums match on the
file.
However if the checksums are absent then rclone will upload the file
rather than setting the timestamp as this is the safe behaviour.
.SS \-\-retries int
Retry the entire sync if it fails this many times it fails (default 3).
.PP
Some remotes can be unreliable and a few retries help pick up the files
which didn\(aqt get transferred because of errors.
.PP
Disable retries with \f[CR]\-\-retries 1\f[R].
.SS \-\-retries\-sleep Duration
This sets the interval between each retry specified by
\f[CR]\-\-retries\f[R]
.PP
The default is \f[CR]0\f[R].
Use \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable.
.SS \-\-server\-side\-across\-configs
Allow server\-side operations (e.g.
copy or move) to work across different configurations.
.PP
This can be useful if you wish to do a server\-side copy or move between
two remotes which use the same backend but are configured differently.
.PP
Note that this isn\(aqt enabled by default because it isn\(aqt easy for
rclone to tell if it will work between any two configurations.
.SS \-\-size\-only
Normally rclone will look at modification time and size of files to see
if they are equal.
If you set this flag then rclone will check only the size.
.PP
This can be useful transferring files from Dropbox which have been
modified by the desktop sync client which doesn\(aqt set checksums of
modification times in the same way as rclone.
.SS \-\-stats Duration
Commands which transfer data (\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copyto/
copyto
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_moveto/
moveto
.UE \c
) will print data transfer stats at regular intervals to show their
progress.
.PP
This sets the interval.
.PP
The default is \f[CR]1m\f[R].
Use \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable.
.PP
If you set the stats interval then all commands can show stats.
This can be useful when running other commands, \f[CR]check\f[R] or
\f[CR]mount\f[R] for example.
.PP
Stats are logged at \f[CR]INFO\f[R] level by default which means they
won\(aqt show at default log level \f[CR]NOTICE\f[R].
Use \f[CR]\-\-stats\-log\-level NOTICE\f[R] or \f[CR]\-v\f[R] to make
them show.
See the logging section for more info on log levels.
.PP
Note that on macOS you can send a SIGINFO (which is normally ctrl\-T in
the terminal) to make the stats print immediately.
.SS \-\-stats\-file\-name\-length int
By default, the \f[CR]\-\-stats\f[R] output will truncate file names and
paths longer than 40 characters.
This is equivalent to providing
\f[CR]\-\-stats\-file\-name\-length 40\f[R].
Use \f[CR]\-\-stats\-file\-name\-length 0\f[R] to disable any truncation
of file names printed by stats.
.SS \-\-stats\-log\-level LogLevel
Log level to show \f[CR]\-\-stats\f[R] output at.
This can be \f[CR]DEBUG\f[R], \f[CR]INFO\f[R], \f[CR]NOTICE\f[R], or
\f[CR]ERROR\f[R].
The default is \f[CR]INFO\f[R].
This means at the default level of logging, which is \f[CR]NOTICE\f[R],
the stats won\(aqt show \- if you want them to then use
\f[CR]\-\-stats\-log\-level NOTICE\f[R].
See the logging section for more details on log levels.
.SS \-\-stats\-one\-line
When this is specified, rclone condenses the stats into a single line
showing the most important stats only.
.SS \-\-stats\-one\-line\-date
When this is specified, rclone enables the single\-line stats and
prepends  the display with a date string.
The default is \f[CR]2006/01/02 15:04:05 \-\f[R]
.SS \-\-stats\-one\-line\-date\-format string
When this is specified, rclone enables the single\-line stats and
prepends the display with a user\-supplied date string.
The date string MUST be enclosed in quotes.
Follow \c
.UR https://golang.org/pkg/time/#Time.Format
golang specs
.UE \c
\ for date formatting syntax.
.SS \-\-stats\-unit string
By default, data transfer rates will be printed in bytes per second,
corresponding to \f[CR]\-\-stats\-unit bytes\f[R].
.PP
This option allows the data rate to be printed in bits per second, by
specifying \f[CR]\-\-stats\-unit bits\f[R].
Data transfer volume will still be reported in bytes.
.PP
The rate is reported as a binary unit, not SI unit.
So 1 Mbit/s equals 1,048,576 bit/s and not 1,000,000 bit/s.
.SS \-\-suffix string
When using \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
sync
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
copy
.UE \c
\ or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_move/
move
.UE \c
\ any files which would have been overwritten or deleted will have the
suffix added to them.
If there is a file with the same path (after the suffix has been added),
then it will be overwritten.
.PP
The remote in use must support server\-side move or copy and you must
use the same remote as the destination of the sync.
.PP
This is for use with files to add the suffix in the current directory or
with \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R].
See \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] for more info.
.PP
For example
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-interactive /path/to/local/file remote:current \-\-suffix .bak
.EE
.PP
will copy \f[CR]/path/to/local\f[R] to \f[CR]remote:current\f[R], but
for any files which would have been updated or deleted have .bak added.
.PP
If using \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R] with \f[CR]\-\-suffix\f[R] and without
\f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] then it is recommended to put a filter rule
in excluding the suffix otherwise the \f[CR]sync\f[R] will delete the
backup files.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive /path/to/local/file remote:current \-\-suffix .bak \-\-exclude \(dq*.bak\(dq
.EE
.SS \-\-suffix\-keep\-extension
When using \f[CR]\-\-suffix\f[R], setting this causes rclone put the
SUFFIX before the extension of the files that it backs up rather than
after.
.PP
So let\(aqs say we had \f[CR]\-\-suffix \-2019\-01\-01\f[R], without the
flag \f[CR]file.txt\f[R] would be backed up to
\f[CR]file.txt\-2019\-01\-01\f[R] and with the flag it would be backed
up to \f[CR]file\-2019\-01\-01.txt\f[R].
This can be helpful to make sure the suffixed files can still be opened.
.PP
If a file has two (or more) extensions and the second (or subsequent)
extension is recognised as a valid mime type, then the suffix will go
before that extension.
So \f[CR]file.tar.gz\f[R] would be backed up to
\f[CR]file\-2019\-01\-01.tar.gz\f[R] whereas
\f[CR]file.badextension.gz\f[R] would be backed up to
\f[CR]file.badextension\-2019\-01\-01.gz\f[R].
.SS \-\-syslog
On capable OSes (not Windows or Plan9) send all log output to syslog.
.PP
This can be useful for running rclone in a script or
\f[CR]rclone mount\f[R].
.SS \-\-syslog\-facility string
If using \f[CR]\-\-syslog\f[R] this sets the syslog facility (e.g.
\f[CR]KERN\f[R], \f[CR]USER\f[R]).
See \f[CR]man syslog\f[R] for a list of possible facilities.
The default facility is \f[CR]DAEMON\f[R].
.SS \-\-temp\-dir string
Specify the directory rclone will use for temporary files, to override
the default.
Make sure the directory exists and have accessible permissions.
.PP
By default the operating system\(aqs temp directory will be used:
.IP \(bu 2
On Unix systems, \f[CR]$TMPDIR\f[R] if non\-empty, else \f[CR]/tmp\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows, the first non\-empty value from \f[CR]%TMP%\f[R],
\f[CR]%TEMP%\f[R], \f[CR]%USERPROFILE%\f[R], or the Windows directory.
.PP
When overriding the default with this option, the specified path will be
set as value of environment variable \f[CR]TMPDIR\f[R] on Unix systems
and \f[CR]TMP\f[R] and \f[CR]TEMP\f[R] on Windows.
.PP
You can use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_paths/
config paths
.UE \c
\ command to see the current value.
.SS \-\-tpslimit float
Limit transactions per second to this number.
Default is 0 which is used to mean unlimited transactions per second.
.PP
A transaction is roughly defined as an API call; its exact meaning will
depend on the backend.
For HTTP based backends it is an HTTP PUT/GET/POST/etc and its response.
For FTP/SFTP it is a round trip transaction over TCP.
.PP
For example, to limit rclone to 10 transactions per second use
\f[CR]\-\-tpslimit 10\f[R], or to 1 transaction every 2 seconds use
\f[CR]\-\-tpslimit 0.5\f[R].
.PP
Use this when the number of transactions per second from rclone is
causing a problem with the cloud storage provider (e.g.
getting you banned or rate limited).
.PP
This can be very useful for \f[CR]rclone mount\f[R] to control the
behaviour of applications using it.
.PP
This limit applies to all HTTP based backends and to the FTP and SFTP
backends.
It does not apply to the local backend or the Storj backend.
.PP
See also \f[CR]\-\-tpslimit\-burst\f[R].
.SS \-\-tpslimit\-burst int
Max burst of transactions for \f[CR]\-\-tpslimit\f[R] (default
\f[CR]1\f[R]).
.PP
Normally \f[CR]\-\-tpslimit\f[R] will do exactly the number of
transaction per second specified.
However if you supply \f[CR]\-\-tps\-burst\f[R] then rclone can save up
some transactions from when it was idle giving a burst of up to the
parameter supplied.
.PP
For example if you provide \f[CR]\-\-tpslimit\-burst 10\f[R] then if
rclone has been idle for more than 10*\f[CR]\-\-tpslimit\f[R] then it
can do 10 transactions very quickly before they are limited again.
.PP
This may be used to increase performance of \f[CR]\-\-tpslimit\f[R]
without changing the long term average number of transactions per
second.
.SS \-\-track\-renames
By default, rclone doesn\(aqt keep track of renamed files, so if you
rename a file locally then sync it to a remote, rclone will delete the
old file on the remote and upload a new copy.
.PP
An rclone sync with \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] runs like a normal
sync, but keeps track of objects which exist in the destination but not
in the source (which would normally be deleted), and which objects exist
in the source but not the destination (which would normally be
transferred).
These objects are then candidates for renaming.
.PP
After the sync, rclone matches up the source only and destination only
objects using the \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\-strategy\f[R] specified and
either renames the destination object or transfers the source and
deletes the destination object.
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] is stateless like all of rclone\(aqs
syncs.
.PP
To use this flag the destination must support server\-side copy or
server\-side move, and to use a hash based
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\-strategy\f[R] (the default) the source and the
destination must have a compatible hash.
.PP
If the destination does not support server\-side copy or move, rclone
will fall back to the default behaviour and log an error level message
to the console.
.PP
Encrypted destinations are not currently supported by
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] if \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\-strategy\f[R]
includes \f[CR]hash\f[R].
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] is incompatible with
\f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] and that it uses extra memory to keep track
of all the rename candidates.
.PP
Note also that \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] is incompatible with
\f[CR]\-\-delete\-before\f[R] and will select
\f[CR]\-\-delete\-after\f[R] instead of \f[CR]\-\-delete\-during\f[R].
.SS \-\-track\-renames\-strategy string
This option changes the file matching criteria for
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R].
.PP
The matching is controlled by a comma separated selection of these
tokens:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]modtime\f[R] \- the modification time of the file \- not supported
on all backends
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]hash\f[R] \- the hash of the file contents \- not supported on all
backends
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]leaf\f[R] \- the name of the file not including its directory name
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]size\f[R] \- the size of the file (this is always enabled)
.PP
The default option is \f[CR]hash\f[R].
.PP
Using \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\-strategy modtime,leaf\f[R] would match
files based on modification time, the leaf of the file name and the size
only.
.PP
Using \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\-strategy modtime\f[R] or \f[CR]leaf\f[R]
can enable \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] support for encrypted
destinations.
.PP
Note that the \f[CR]hash\f[R] strategy is not supported with encrypted
destinations.
.SS \-\-delete\-(before,during,after)
This option allows you to specify when files on your destination are
deleted when you sync folders.
.PP
Specifying the value \f[CR]\-\-delete\-before\f[R] will delete all files
present on the destination, but not on the source \f[I]before\f[R]
starting the transfer of any new or updated files.
This uses two passes through the file systems, one for the deletions and
one for the copies.
.PP
Specifying \f[CR]\-\-delete\-during\f[R] will delete files while
checking and uploading files.
This is the fastest option and uses the least memory.
.PP
Specifying \f[CR]\-\-delete\-after\f[R] (the default value) will delay
deletion of files until all new/updated files have been successfully
transferred.
The files to be deleted are collected in the copy pass then deleted
after the copy pass has completed successfully.
The files to be deleted are held in memory so this mode may use more
memory.
This is the safest mode as it will only delete files if there have been
no errors subsequent to that.
If there have been errors before the deletions start then you will get
the message \f[CR]not deleting files as there were IO errors\f[R].
.SS \-\-fast\-list
When doing anything which involves a directory listing (e.g.
\f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]copy\f[R], \f[CR]ls\f[R] \- in fact nearly every
command), rclone has different strategies to choose from.
.PP
The basic strategy is to list one directory and processes it before
using more directory lists to process any subdirectories.
This is a mandatory backend feature, called \f[CR]List\f[R], which means
it is supported by all backends.
This strategy uses small amount of memory, and because it can be
parallelised it is fast for operations involving processing of the list
results.
.PP
Some backends provide the support for an alternative strategy, where all
files beneath a directory can be listed in one (or a small number) of
transactions.
Rclone supports this alternative strategy through an optional backend
feature called \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#listr
\f[CR]ListR\f[R]
.UE \c
\&.
You can see in the storage system overview documentation\(aqs \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#optional-features
optional features
.UE \c
\ section which backends it is enabled for (these tend to be the
bucket\-based ones, e.g.
S3, B2, GCS, Swift).
This strategy requires fewer transactions for highly recursive
operations, which is important on backends where this is charged or
heavily rate limited.
It may be faster (due to fewer transactions) or slower (because it
can\(aqt be parallelized) depending on different parameters, and may
require more memory if rclone has to keep the whole listing in memory.
.PP
Which listing strategy rclone picks for a given operation is
complicated, but in general it tries to choose the best possible.
It will prefer \f[CR]ListR\f[R] in situations where it doesn\(aqt need
to store the listed files in memory, e.g.
for unlimited recursive \f[CR]ls\f[R] command variants.
In other situations it will prefer \f[CR]List\f[R], e.g.
for \f[CR]sync\f[R] and \f[CR]copy\f[R], where it needs to keep the
listed files in memory, and is performing operations on them where
parallelization may be a huge advantage.
.PP
Rclone is not able to take all relevant parameters into account for
deciding the best strategy, and therefore allows you to influence the
choice in two ways: You can stop rclone from using \f[CR]ListR\f[R] by
disabling the feature, using the \-\-disable option
(\f[CR]\-\-disable ListR\f[R]), or you can allow rclone to use
\f[CR]ListR\f[R] where it would normally choose not to do so due to
higher memory usage, using the \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] option.
Rclone should always produce identical results either way.
Using \f[CR]\-\-disable ListR\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] on a
remote which doesn\(aqt support \f[CR]ListR\f[R] does nothing, rclone
will just ignore it.
.PP
A rule of thumb is that if you pay for transactions and can fit your
entire sync listing into memory, then \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] is
recommended.
If you have a very big sync to do, then don\(aqt use
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R], otherwise you will run out of memory.
Run some tests and compare before you decide, and if in doubt then just
leave the default, let rclone decide, i.e.
not use \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R].
.SS \-\-timeout Duration
This sets the IO idle timeout.
If a transfer has started but then becomes idle for this long it is
considered broken and disconnected.
.PP
The default is \f[CR]5m\f[R].
Set to \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable.
.SS \-\-transfers int
The number of file transfers to run in parallel.
It can sometimes be useful to set this to a smaller number if the remote
is giving a lot of timeouts or bigger if you have lots of bandwidth and
a fast remote.
.PP
The default is to run 4 file transfers in parallel.
.PP
Look at \-\-multi\-thread\-streams if you would like to control single
file transfers.
.SS \-u, \-\-update
This forces rclone to skip any files which exist on the destination and
have a modified time that is newer than the source file.
.PP
This can be useful in avoiding needless transfers when transferring to a
remote which doesn\(aqt support modification times directly (or when
using \f[CR]\-\-use\-server\-modtime\f[R] to avoid extra API calls) as
it is more accurate than a \f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R] check and faster
than using \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R].
On such remotes (or when using \f[CR]\-\-use\-server\-modtime\f[R]) the
time checked will be the uploaded time.
.PP
If an existing destination file has a modification time older than the
source file\(aqs, it will be updated if the sizes are different.
If the sizes are the same, it will be updated if the checksum is
different or not available.
.PP
If an existing destination file has a modification time equal (within
the computed modify window) to the source file\(aqs, it will be updated
if the sizes are different.
The checksum will not be checked in this case unless the
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] flag is provided.
.PP
In all other cases the file will not be updated.
.PP
Consider using the \f[CR]\-\-modify\-window\f[R] flag to compensate for
time skews between the source and the backend, for backends that do not
support mod times, and instead use uploaded times.
However, if the backend does not support checksums, note that syncing or
copying within the time skew window may still result in additional
transfers for safety.
.SS \-\-use\-mmap
If this flag is set then rclone will use anonymous memory allocated by
mmap on Unix based platforms and VirtualAlloc on Windows for its
transfer buffers (size controlled by \f[CR]\-\-buffer\-size\f[R]).
Memory allocated like this does not go on the Go heap and can be
returned to the OS immediately when it is finished with.
.PP
If this flag is not set then rclone will allocate and free the buffers
using the Go memory allocator which may use more memory as memory pages
are returned less aggressively to the OS.
.PP
It is possible this does not work well on all platforms so it is
disabled by default; in the future it may be enabled by default.
.SS \-\-use\-server\-modtime
Some object\-store backends (e.g, Swift, S3) do not preserve file
modification times (modtime).
On these backends, rclone stores the original modtime as additional
metadata on the object.
By default it will make an API call to retrieve the metadata when the
modtime is needed by an operation.
.PP
Use this flag to disable the extra API call and rely instead on the
server\(aqs modified time.
In cases such as a local to remote sync using \f[CR]\-\-update\f[R],
knowing the local file is newer than the time it was last uploaded to
the remote is sufficient.
In those cases, this flag can speed up the process and reduce the number
of API calls necessary.
.PP
This flag is only supported on certain backends and will be silently
ignored on unsupported backends.
Supported backends include \f[CR]azureblob\f[R],
\f[CR]oracleobjectstorage\f[R], \f[CR]s3\f[R], \f[CR]swift\f[R].
.PP
Using this flag on a sync operation without also using
\f[CR]\-\-update\f[R] would cause all files modified at any time other
than the last upload time to be uploaded again, which is probably not
what you want.
.SS \-v, \-vv, \-\-verbose
With \f[CR]\-v\f[R] rclone will tell you about each file that is
transferred and a small number of significant events.
.PP
With \f[CR]\-vv\f[R] rclone will become very verbose telling you about
every file it considers and transfers.
Please send bug reports with a log with this setting.
.PP
When setting verbosity as an environment variable, use
\f[CR]RCLONE_VERBOSE=1\f[R] or \f[CR]RCLONE_VERBOSE=2\f[R] for
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] and \f[CR]\-vv\f[R] respectively.
.SS \-V, \-\-version
Prints the version number
.SS SSL/TLS options
The outgoing SSL/TLS connections rclone makes can be controlled with
these options.
For example this can be very useful with the HTTP or WebDAV backends.
Rclone HTTP servers have their own set of configuration for SSL/TLS
which you can find in their documentation.
.SS \-\-ca\-cert stringArray
This loads the PEM encoded certificate authority certificates and uses
it to verify the certificates of the servers rclone connects to.
.PP
If you have generated certificates signed with a local CA then you will
need this flag to connect to servers using those certificates.
.SS \-\-client\-cert string
This loads the PEM encoded client side certificate.
.PP
This is used for \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutual_authentication
mutual TLS authentication
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-client\-key\f[R] flag is required too when using this.
.SS \-\-client\-key string
This loads the PEM encoded client side private key used for mutual TLS
authentication.
Used in conjunction with \f[CR]\-\-client\-cert\f[R].
.PP
Supported types are:
.IP \(bu 2
Unencrypted PKCS#1 (\(dqBEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY\(dq)
.IP \(bu 2
Unencrypted PKCS#8 (\(dqBEGIN PRIVATE KEY\(dq)
.IP \(bu 2
Encrypted PKCS#8 (\(dqBEGIN ENCRYPTED PRIVATE KEY\(dq)
.IP \(bu 2
Legacy PEM encryption (e.g., DEK\-Info headers), which are automatically
detected.
.SS \-\-client\-pass string
This can be used to supply an optional password to decrypt the client
key file.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] the password should be obscured so it should be the output
of \f[CR]rclone obscure YOURPASSWORD\f[R].
.SS \-\-no\-check\-certificate
\f[CR]\-\-no\-check\-certificate\f[R] controls whether a client verifies
the server\(aqs certificate chain and host name.
If \f[CR]\-\-no\-check\-certificate\f[R] is true, TLS accepts any
certificate presented by the server and any host name in that
certificate.
In this mode, TLS is susceptible to man\-in\-the\-middle attacks.
.PP
This option defaults to \f[CR]false\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]This should be used only for testing.\f[R]
.SS Configuration encryption
Your configuration file contains information for logging in to your
cloud services.
This means that you should keep your \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] file in a
secure location.
.PP
If you are in an environment where that isn\(aqt possible, you can add a
password to your configuration.
This means that you will have to supply the password every time you
start rclone.
.PP
To add a password to your rclone configuration, execute
\f[CR]rclone config\f[R].
.IP
.EX
$ rclone config
Current remotes:

e) Edit existing remote
n) New remote
d) Delete remote
s) Set configuration password
q) Quit config
e/n/d/s/q>
.EE
.PP
Go into \f[CR]s\f[R], Set configuration password:
.IP
.EX
e/n/d/s/q> s
Your configuration is not encrypted.
If you add a password, you will protect your login information to cloud services.
a) Add Password
q) Quit to main menu
a/q> a
Enter NEW configuration password:
password:
Confirm NEW password:
password:
Password set
Your configuration is encrypted.
c) Change Password
u) Unencrypt configuration
q) Quit to main menu
c/u/q>
.EE
.PP
Your configuration is now encrypted, and every time you start rclone you
will have to supply the password.
See below for details.
In the same menu, you can change the password or completely remove
encryption from your configuration.
.PP
There is no way to recover the configuration if you lose your password.
.PP
You can also use
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_encryption_set/
rclone config encryption set
.UE \c
\ to set the config encryption directly
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_encryption_remove/
rclone config encryption remove
.UE \c
\ to remove it
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_encryption_check/
rclone config encryption check
.UE \c
\ to check that it is encrypted properly.
.PP
rclone uses \c
.UR https://godoc.org/golang.org/x/crypto/nacl/secretbox
nacl secretbox
.UE \c
\ which in turn uses XSalsa20 and Poly1305 to encrypt and authenticate
your configuration with secret\-key cryptography.
The password is SHA\-256 hashed, which produces the key for secretbox.
The hashed password is not stored.
.PP
While this provides very good security, we do not recommend storing your
encrypted rclone configuration in public if it contains sensitive
information, maybe except if you use a very strong password.
.PP
If it is safe in your environment, you can set the
\f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\f[R] environment variable to contain your
password, in which case it will be used for decrypting the
configuration.
.PP
You can set this for a session from a script.
For unix like systems save this to a file called
\f[CR]set\-rclone\-password\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]#!/bin/echo Source this file don\(aqt run it\f[R]

read \-s RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS
export RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS
.EE
.PP
Then source the file when you want to use it.
From the shell you would do \f[CR]source set\-rclone\-password\f[R].
It will then ask you for the password and set it in the environment
variable.
.PP
An alternate means of supplying the password is to provide a script
which will retrieve the password and print on standard output.
This script should have a fully specified path name and not rely on any
environment variables.
The script is supplied either via
\f[CR]\-\-password\-command=\(dq...\(dq\f[R] command line argument or
via the \f[CR]RCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND\f[R] environment variable.
.PP
One useful example of this is using the \f[CR]passwordstore\f[R]
application to retrieve the password:
.IP
.EX
export RCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND=\(dqpass rclone/config\(dq
.EE
.PP
If the \f[CR]passwordstore\f[R] password manager holds the password for
the rclone configuration, using the script method means the password is
primarily protected by the \f[CR]passwordstore\f[R] system, and is never
embedded in the clear in scripts, nor available for examination using
the standard commands available.
It is quite possible with long running rclone sessions for copies of
passwords to be innocently captured in log files or terminal scroll
buffers, etc.
Using the script method of supplying the password enhances the security
of the config password considerably.
.PP
If you are running rclone inside a script, unless you are using the
\f[CR]\-\-password\-command\f[R] method, you might want to disable
password prompts.
To do that, pass the parameter \f[CR]\-\-ask\-password=false\f[R] to
rclone.
This will make rclone fail instead of asking for a password if
\f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\f[R] doesn\(aqt contain a valid password, and
\f[CR]\-\-password\-command\f[R] has not been supplied.
.PP
Whenever running commands that may be affected by options in a
configuration file, rclone will look for an existing file according to
the rules described above, and load any it finds.
If an encrypted file is found, this includes decrypting it, with the
possible consequence of a password prompt.
When executing a command line that you know are not actually using
anything from such a configuration file, you can avoid it being loaded
by overriding the location, e.g.
with one of the documented special values for memory\-only
configuration.
Since only backend options can be stored in configuration files, this is
normally unnecessary for commands that do not operate on backends, e.g.
\f[CR]completion\f[R].
However, it will be relevant for commands that do operate on backends in
general, but are used without referencing a stored remote, e.g.
listing local filesystem paths, or connection strings:
\f[CR]rclone \-\-config=\(dq\(dq ls .\f[R]
.SS Configuration encryption cheatsheet
You can quickly apply a configuration encryption without plain\-text at
rest or transfer.
Detailed instructions for popular OSes:
.SS Mac
.IP \(bu 2
Generate and store a password
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
security add\-generic\-password \-a rclone \-s config \-w $(openssl rand \-base64 40)
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Add the retrieval instruction to your \f[CR].zprofile\f[R] /
\f[CR].profile\f[R]
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
export RCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND=\(dq/usr/bin/security find\-generic\-password \-a rclone \-s config \-w\(dq
.EE
.RE
.SS Linux
.IP \(bu 2
Prerequisite: Linux doesn\(aqt come with a default password manager.
Let\(aqs install the \(dqpass\(dq utility using a package manager, e.g.
\f[CR]apt install pass\f[R], \f[CR]yum install pass\f[R], \c
.UR https://www.passwordstore.org/#download
etc.
.UE \c
; then initialize a password store: \f[CR]pass init rclone\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Generate and store a password
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
echo $(openssl rand \-base64 40) | pass insert \-m rclone/config
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Add the retrieval instruction
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
export RCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND=\(dq/usr/bin/pass rclone/config\(dq
.EE
.RE
.SS Windows
.IP \(bu 2
Generate and store a password
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
New\-Object \-TypeName PSCredential \-ArgumentList \(dqrclone\(dq, (ConvertTo\-SecureString \-String ([System.Web.Security.Membership]::GeneratePassword(40, 10)) \-AsPlainText \-Force) | Export\-Clixml \-Path \(dqrclone\-credential.xml\(dq
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Add the password retrieval instruction
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(\(dqRCLONE_PASSWORD_COMMAND\(dq, \(dq[System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::PtrToStringAuto([System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::SecureStringToBSTR((Import\-Clixml \-Path \(dqrclone\-credential.xml\(dq).Password))\(dq)
.EE
.RE
.SS Encrypt the config file (all systems)
.IP \(bu 2
Execute \f[CR]rclone config\f[R], and select option
\f[CR]s) Set configuration password\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Add/update the password from previous steps
.SS Developer options
These options are useful when developing or debugging rclone.
There are also some more remote specific options which aren\(aqt
documented here which are used for testing.
These start with remote name e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-test\-option\f[R] \- see the docs for the remote in
question.
.SS \-\-cpuprofile string
Write CPU profile to a file.
This can be analysed with \f[CR]go tool pprof\f[R].
.SS \-\-memprofile string
Write memory profile to a file.
This can be analysed with \f[CR]go tool pprof\f[R].
.SS \-\-dump DumpFlags
The \f[CR]\-\-dump\f[R] flag takes a comma separated list of flags to
dump info about.
.PP
Note that some headers, such as \f[CR]Accept\-Encoding\f[R], may not be
correct as shown in the request, and the response may not show
\f[CR]Content\-Encoding\f[R] if the go standard libraries auto gzip
encoding was in effect.
In this case the body of the request will be gunzipped before showing
it.
.PP
The available flags are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]headers\f[R] dumps HTTP headers.
Any \f[CR]Authorization:\f[R] headers will be excluded, but output may
still contain sensitive information.
Can be very verbose.
Useful for debugging only.
Use \f[CR]auth\f[R] if you do want the \f[CR]Authorization:\f[R]
headers.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]auth\f[R] dumps HTTP headers like \f[CR]headers\f[R], but also
includes any \f[CR]Authorization:\f[R] headers.
This means the output will probably contain sensitive information.
Use \f[CR]headers\f[R] to dump without \f[CR]Authorization:\f[R]
headers.
Can be very verbose.
Useful for debugging only.
This flag also makes the debug log of the config process (e.g.
\f[CR]rclone config \-vv\f[R]) show answers to questions, passwords and
tokens which are otherwise redacted.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]bodies\f[R] dumps HTTP headers and bodies.
May contain sensitive info.
Can be very verbose.
Useful for debugging only.
Note that the bodies are buffered in memory so don\(aqt use this for
enormous files.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]requests\f[R] is similar to \f[CR]bodies\f[R], but dumps the
request bodies and the response headers.
Useful for debugging download problems.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]responses\f[R] is similar to \f[CR]bodies\f[R], but dumps the
response bodies and the request headers.
Useful for debugging upload problems.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]filters\f[R] dumps the filters.
Useful to see exactly what include and exclude options are filtering on.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]goroutines\f[R] dumps a list of the running go\-routines at the
end of the command.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]openfiles\f[R] dumps a list of the open files at the end of the
command.
It uses the \f[CR]lsof\f[R] Unix command to do that, so you\(aqll need
that installed to use it.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]mapper\f[R] dumps the JSON blobs being sent to the program
supplied with \f[CR]\-\-metadata\-mapper\f[R] and received from it.
It can be useful for debugging the metadata mapper interface.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]curl\f[R] dumps the HTTP request as a \f[CR]curl\f[R] command.
Can be used with the other HTTP debugging flags (e.g.
\f[CR]requests\f[R], \f[CR]bodies\f[R]).
By default the auth will be masked \- use with \f[CR]auth\f[R] to have
the curl commands with authentication too.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]errors\f[R] only dumps the HTTP transactions which fail with a
retryable error \- that is a transport error, an HTTP 429 (too many
requests) or an HTTP 5xx server error.
The other dump flags control what is dumped, so for example use
\f[CR]\-\-dump errors,bodies\f[R] to dump the headers and bodies of
failed transactions only.
On its own \f[CR]\-\-dump errors\f[R] dumps the headers.
This lets you capture first\-failure diagnostics without the noise of
dumping every transaction.
May contain sensitive info.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]trace\f[R] logs connection level events for each HTTP transaction
using Go\(aqs \f[CR]net/http/httptrace\f[R] \- DNS resolution, TCP
connect, TLS handshake (including the negotiated TLS version, cipher,
ALPN protocol and server certificate), connection reuse, request write
and time to first response byte.
Each line is tagged with the time elapsed since the start of the
transaction so you can see where the time goes.
This is complementary to the other dump flags: it shows how the
connection behaved rather than what was sent, so it is useful for
debugging connectivity, DNS, TLS, proxy and keep\-alive problems.
It does not dump headers or bodies \- combine it with \f[CR]headers\f[R]
or \f[CR]bodies\f[R] for that.
