.SS Logging
rclone has 4 levels of logging, \f[CR]ERROR\f[R], \f[CR]NOTICE\f[R],
\f[CR]INFO\f[R] and \f[CR]DEBUG\f[R].
.PP
By default, rclone logs to standard error.
This means you can redirect standard error and still see the normal
output of rclone commands (e.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls\f[R]).
.PP
By default, rclone will produce \f[CR]Error\f[R] and \f[CR]Notice\f[R]
level messages.
.PP
If you use the \f[CR]\-q\f[R] flag, rclone will only produce
\f[CR]Error\f[R] messages.
.PP
If you use the \f[CR]\-v\f[R] flag, rclone will produce
\f[CR]Error\f[R], \f[CR]Notice\f[R] and \f[CR]Info\f[R] messages.
.PP
If you use the \f[CR]\-vv\f[R] flag, rclone will produce
\f[CR]Error\f[R], \f[CR]Notice\f[R], \f[CR]Info\f[R] and
\f[CR]Debug\f[R] messages.
.PP
You can also control the log levels with the \f[CR]\-\-log\-level\f[R]
flag.
.PP
If you use the \f[CR]\-\-log\-file\f[R] option, rclone will redirect
\f[CR]Error\f[R], \f[CR]Info\f[R] and \f[CR]Debug\f[R] messages along
with standard error to a file.
.PP
If you use the \f[CR]\-\-syslog\f[R] flag then rclone will log to syslog
and the \f[CR]\-\-syslog\-facility\f[R] control which facility it uses.
.PP
Rclone prefixes all log messages with their level in capitals, e.g.
INFO which makes it easy to grep the log file for different kinds of
information.
.SS Metrics
Rclone can publish metrics in the OpenMetrics/Prometheus format.
.PP
To enable the metrics endpoint, use the \f[CR]\-\-metrics\-addr\f[R]
flag.
Metrics can also be published on the \f[CR]\-\-rc\-addr\f[R] port if the
\f[CR]\-\-rc\f[R] flag and \f[CR]\-\-rc\-enable\-metrics\f[R] flags are
supplied or if using rclone rcd \f[CR]\-\-rc\-enable\-metrics\f[R]
.PP
Rclone provides extensive configuration options for the metrics HTTP
endpoint.
These settings are grouped under the Metrics section and have a prefix
\f[CR]\-\-metrics\-*\f[R].
.PP
When metrics are enabled with \f[CR]\-\-rc\-enable\-metrics\f[R], they
will be published on the same port as the rc API.
In this case, the \f[CR]\-\-metrics\-*\f[R] flags will be ignored, and
the HTTP endpoint configuration will be managed by the
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-*\f[R] parameters.
.SS Exit code
If any errors occur during the command execution, rclone will exit with
a non\-zero exit code.
This allows scripts to detect when rclone operations have failed.
.PP
During the startup phase, rclone will exit immediately if an error is
detected in the configuration.
There will always be a log message immediately before exiting.
.PP
When rclone is running it will accumulate errors as it goes along, and
only exit with a non\-zero exit code if (after retries) there were still
failed transfers.
For every error counted there will be a high priority log message
(visible with \f[CR]\-q\f[R]) showing the message and which file caused
the problem.
A high priority message is also shown when starting a retry so the user
can see that any previous error messages may not be valid after the
retry.
If rclone has done a retry it will log a high priority message if the
retry was successful.
.SS List of exit codes
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]0\f[R] \- Success
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]1\f[R] \- Error not otherwise categorised
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]2\f[R] \- Syntax or usage error
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]3\f[R] \- Directory not found
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]4\f[R] \- File not found
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]5\f[R] \- Temporary error (one that more retries might fix) (Retry
errors)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]6\f[R] \- Less serious errors (like 461 errors from dropbox)
(NoRetry errors)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]7\f[R] \- Fatal error (one that more retries won\(aqt fix, like
account suspended) (Fatal errors)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]8\f[R] \- Transfer exceeded \- limit set by \-\-max\-transfer
reached
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]9\f[R] \- Operation successful, but no files transferred (Requires
\f[CR]\-\-error\-on\-no\-transfer\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]10\f[R] \- Duration exceeded \- limit set by \-\-max\-duration
reached
.SS Environment variables
Rclone can be configured entirely using environment variables.
These can be used to set defaults for options or config file entries.
.SS Options
Every option in rclone can have its default set by environment variable.
.PP
To find the name of the environment variable, first, take the long
option name, strip the leading \f[CR]\-\-\f[R], change \f[CR]\-\f[R] to
\f[CR]_\f[R], make upper case and prepend \f[CR]RCLONE_\f[R].
.PP
For example, to always set \f[CR]\-\-stats 5s\f[R], set the environment
variable \f[CR]RCLONE_STATS=5s\f[R].
If you set stats on the command line this will override the environment
variable setting.
.PP
Or to always use the trash in drive \f[CR]\-\-drive\-use\-trash\f[R],
set \f[CR]RCLONE_DRIVE_USE_TRASH=true\f[R].
.PP
Verbosity is slightly different, the environment variable equivalent of
\f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R] or \f[CR]\-v\f[R] is \f[CR]RCLONE_VERBOSE=1\f[R],
or for \f[CR]\-vv\f[R], \f[CR]RCLONE_VERBOSE=2\f[R].
.PP
The same parser is used for the options and the environment variables so
they take exactly the same form.
.PP
The options set by environment variables can be seen with the
\f[CR]\-vv\f[R] flag, e.g.
\f[CR]rclone version \-vv\f[R].
.PP
Options that can appear multiple times (type \f[CR]stringArray\f[R]) are
treated slightly differently as environment variables can only be
defined once.
In order to allow a simple mechanism for adding one or many items, the
input is treated as a \c
.UR https://godoc.org/encoding/csv
CSV encoded
.UE \c
\ string.
For example
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(36.7n) lw(33.3n).
T{
Environment variable
T}@T{
Equivalent options
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]RCLONE_EXCLUDE=\(dq*.jpg\(dq\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-exclude \(dq*.jpg\(dq\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]RCLONE_EXCLUDE=\(dq*.jpg,*.png\(dq\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-exclude \(dq*.jpg\(dq\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-exclude \(dq*.png\(dq\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]RCLONE_EXCLUDE=\(aq\(dq*.jpg\(dq,\(dq*.png\(dq\(aq\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-exclude \(dq*.jpg\(dq\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-exclude \(dq*.png\(dq\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]RCLONE_EXCLUDE=\(aq\(dq/directory with comma , in it /**\(dq\(aq\f[R]
T}@T{
\(ga\-\-exclude \(dq/directory with comma , in it /**\(dq
T}
.TE
.PP
If \f[CR]stringArray\f[R] options are defined as environment variables
\f[B]and\f[R] options on the command line then all the values will be
used.
.SS Config file
You can set defaults for values in the config file on an individual
remote basis.
The names of the config items are documented in the page for each
backend.
.PP
To find the name of the environment variable, you need to set, take
\f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_\f[R] + name of remote + \f[CR]_\f[R] + name of
config file option and make it all uppercase.
Note one implication here is the remote\(aqs name must be convertible
into a valid environment variable name, so it can only contain letters,
digits, or the \f[CR]_\f[R] (underscore) character.
.PP
For example, to configure an S3 remote named \f[CR]mys3:\f[R] without a
config file (using unix ways of setting environment variables):
.IP
.EX
$ export RCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3_TYPE=s3
$ export RCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=XXX
$ export RCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=XXX
$ rclone lsd mys3:
          \-1 2016\-09\-21 12:54:21        \-1 my\-bucket
$ rclone listremotes | grep mys3
mys3:
.EE
.PP
Note that if you want to create a remote using environment variables you
must create the \f[CR]..._TYPE\f[R] variable as above.
.PP
Note that the name of a remote created using environment variable is
case insensitive, in contrast to regular remotes stored in config file
as documented above.
You must write the name in uppercase in the environment variable, but as
seen from example above it will be listed and can be accessed in
lowercase, while you can also refer to the same remote in uppercase:
.IP
.EX
$ rclone lsd mys3:
          \-1 2016\-09\-21 12:54:21        \-1 my\-bucket
$ rclone lsd MYS3:
          \-1 2016\-09\-21 12:54:21        \-1 my\-bucket
.EE
.PP
Note that you can only set the options of the immediate backend, so
RCLONE_CONFIG_MYS3CRYPT_ACCESS_KEY_ID has no effect, if myS3Crypt is a
crypt remote based on an S3 remote.
However RCLONE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID will set the access key of all remotes
using S3, including myS3Crypt.
.PP
Note also that now rclone has connection strings, it is probably easier
to use those instead which makes the above example
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd :s3,access_key_id=XXX,secret_access_key=XXX:
.EE
.SS Precedence
The various different methods of backend configuration are read in this
order and the first one with a value is used.
.IP \(bu 2
Parameters in connection strings, e.g.
\f[CR]myRemote,skip_links:\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Flag values as supplied on the command line, e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-skip\-links\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Remote specific environment vars, e.g.
\f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_MYREMOTE_SKIP_LINKS\f[R] (see above).
.IP \(bu 2
Backend\-specific environment vars, e.g.
\f[CR]RCLONE_LOCAL_SKIP_LINKS\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Backend generic environment vars, e.g.
\f[CR]RCLONE_SKIP_LINKS\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Config file, e.g.
\f[CR]skip_links = true\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Default values, e.g.
\f[CR]false\f[R] \- these can\(aqt be changed.
.PP
So if both \f[CR]\-\-skip\-links\f[R] is supplied on the command line
and an environment variable \f[CR]RCLONE_LOCAL_SKIP_LINKS\f[R] is set,
the command line flag will take preference.
.PP
The backend configurations set by environment variables can be seen with
the \f[CR]\-vv\f[R] flag, e.g.
\f[CR]rclone about myRemote: \-vv\f[R].
.PP
For non backend configuration the order is as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
Flag values as supplied on the command line, e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-stats 5s\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Environment vars, e.g.
\f[CR]RCLONE_STATS=5s\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Default values, e.g.
\f[CR]1m\f[R] \- these can\(aqt be changed.
.SS Other environment variables
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS\f[R] set to contain your config file password
(see Configuration Encryption section)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTTP_PROXY\f[R], \f[CR]HTTPS_PROXY\f[R] and \f[CR]NO_PROXY\f[R]
(or the lowercase versions thereof).
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTTPS_PROXY\f[R] takes precedence over \f[CR]HTTP_PROXY\f[R] for
https requests.
.IP \(bu 2
The environment values may be either a complete URL or a
\(dqhost[:port]\(dq for, in which case the \(dqhttp\(dq scheme is
assumed.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]USER\f[R] and \f[CR]LOGNAME\f[R] values are used as fallbacks for
current username.
The primary method for looking up username is OS\-specific: Windows API
on Windows, real user ID in /etc/passwd on Unix systems.
In the documentation the current username is simply referred to as
\f[CR]$USER\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]RCLONE_CONFIG_DIR\f[R] \- rclone \f[B]sets\f[R] this variable for
use in config files and sub processes to point to the directory holding
the config file.
.PP
The options set by environment variables can be seen with the
\f[CR]\-vv\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-log\-level=DEBUG\f[R] flags, e.g.
\f[CR]rclone version \-vv\f[R].
.SH Configuring rclone on a remote / headless machine
Some of the configurations (those involving oauth2) require an
internet\-connected web browser.
.PP
If you are trying to set rclone up on a remote or headless machine with
no browser available on it (e.g.
a NAS or a server in a datacenter), then you will need to use an
alternative means of configuration.
There are three ways of doing it, described below.
.SS Configuring using rclone authorize
On the headless machine run rclone config, but answer \f[CR]N\f[R] to
the question
\f[CR]Use web browser to automatically authenticate rclone with remote?\f[R].
.IP
.EX
Use web browser to automatically authenticate rclone with remote?
 * Say Y if the machine running rclone has a web browser you can use
 * Say N if running rclone on a (remote) machine without web browser access
If not sure try Y. If Y failed, try N.

y) Yes (default)
n) No
y/n> n

Option config_token.
For this to work, you will need rclone available on a machine that has
a web browser available.
For more help and alternate methods see: https://rclone.org/remote_setup/
Execute the following on the machine with the web browser (same rclone
version recommended):
        rclone authorize \(dqonedrive\(dq
Then paste the result.
Enter a value.
config_token>
.EE
.PP
Then on your main desktop machine, run \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_authorize/
rclone authorize
.UE \c
\&.
.IP
.EX
rclone authorize \(dqonedrive\(dq
NOTICE: Make sure your Redirect URL is set to \(dqhttp://localhost:53682/\(dq in your custom config.
NOTICE: If your browser doesn\(aqt open automatically go to the following link: http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
NOTICE: Log in and authorize rclone for access
NOTICE: Waiting for code...

Got code
Paste the following into your remote machine \-\-\->
SECRET_TOKEN
<\-\-\-End paste
.EE
.PP
Then back to the headless machine, paste in the code.
.IP
.EX
config_token> SECRET_TOKEN
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
[acd12]
client_id =
client_secret =
token = SECRET_TOKEN
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d>
.EE
.SS Configuring by copying the config file
Rclone stores all of its configuration in a single file.
This can easily be copied to configure a remote rclone (although some
backends do not support reusing the same configuration, consult your
backend documentation to be sure).
.PP
Start by running rclone config to create the configuration file on your
desktop machine.
.IP
.EX
rclone config
.EE
.PP
Then locate the file by running rclone config file.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone config file
Configuration file is stored at:
/home/user/.rclone.conf
.EE
.PP
Finally, transfer the file to the remote machine (scp, cut paste, ftp,
sftp, etc.)
and place it in the correct location (use rclone config file on the
remote machine to find out where).
.SS Configuring using SSH Tunnel
If you have an SSH client installed on your local machine, you can set
up an SSH tunnel to redirect the port 53682 into the headless machine by
using the following command:
.IP
.EX
ssh \-L localhost:53682:localhost:53682 username\(atremote_server
.EE
.PP
Then on the headless machine run rclone config and answer \f[CR]Y\f[R]
to the question
\f[CR]Use web browser to automatically authenticate rclone with remote?\f[R].
.IP
.EX
Use web browser to automatically authenticate rclone with remote?
 * Say Y if the machine running rclone has a web browser you can use
 * Say N if running rclone on a (remote) machine without web browser access
If not sure try Y. If Y failed, try N.

y) Yes (default)
n) No
y/n> y
NOTICE: Make sure your Redirect URL is set to \(dqhttp://localhost:53682/\(dq in your custom config.
NOTICE: If your browser doesn\(aqt open automatically go to the following link: http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
NOTICE: Log in and authorize rclone for access
NOTICE: Waiting for code...
.EE
.PP
Finally, copy and paste the presented URL
\f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\f[R] to
the browser on your local machine, complete the auth and you are done.
.SH Filtering, includes and excludes
Filter flags determine which files rclone \f[CR]sync\f[R],
\f[CR]move\f[R], \f[CR]ls\f[R], \f[CR]lsl\f[R], \f[CR]md5sum\f[R],
\f[CR]sha1sum\f[R], \f[CR]size\f[R], \f[CR]delete\f[R], \f[CR]check\f[R]
and similar commands apply to.
.PP
They are specified in terms of path/file name patterns; path/file lists;
file age and size, or presence of a file in a directory.
Bucket based remotes without the concept of directory apply filters to
object key, age and size in an analogous way.
.PP
Rclone \f[CR]purge\f[R] does not obey filters.
.PP
To test filters without risk of damage to data, apply them to
\f[CR]rclone ls\f[R], or with the \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-vv\f[R] flags.
.PP
Rclone filter patterns can only be used in filter command line options,
not in the specification of a remote.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone copy \(dqremote:dir*.jpg\(dq /path/to/dir\f[R] does not
have a filter effect.
\f[CR]rclone copy remote:dir /path/to/dir \-\-include \(dq*.jpg\(dq\f[R]
does.
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R] Avoid mixing any two of \f[CR]\-\-include...\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-exclude...\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-filter...\f[R] flags in an rclone
command.
The results might not be what you expect.
Instead use a \f[CR]\-\-filter...\f[R] flag.
.SS Patterns for matching path/file names
.SS Pattern syntax
Here is a formal definition of the pattern syntax, examples are below.
.PP
Rclone matching rules follow a glob style:
.IP
.EX
*         matches any sequence of non\-separator (/) characters
**        matches any sequence of characters including / separators
?         matches any single non\-separator (/) character
[ [ ! ] { character\-range } ]
          character class (must be non\-empty)
{ pattern\-list }
          pattern alternatives
{{ regexp }}
          regular expression to match
c         matches character c (c != *, **, ?, \(rs, [, {, })
\(rsc        matches reserved character c (c = *, **, ?, \(rs, [, {, }) or character class
.EE
.PP
character\-range:
.IP
.EX
c         matches character c (c != \(rs, \-, ])
\(rsc        matches reserved character c (c = \(rs, \-, ])
lo \- hi   matches character c for lo <= c <= hi
.EE
.PP
pattern\-list:
.IP
.EX
pattern { , pattern }
          comma\-separated (without spaces) patterns
.EE
.PP
character classes (see \c
.UR https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/
Go regular expression reference
.UE \c
) include:
.IP
.EX
Named character classes (e.g. [\(rsd], [\(ha\(rsd], [\(rsD], [\(ha\(rsD])
Perl character classes (e.g. \(rss, \(rsS, \(rsw, \(rsW)
ASCII character classes (e.g. [[:alnum:]], [[:alpha:]], [[:punct:]], [[:xdigit:]])
.EE
.PP
regexp for advanced users to insert a regular expression \- see below
for more info:
.IP
.EX
Any re2 regular expression not containing \(ga}}\(ga
.EE
.PP
If the filter pattern starts with a \f[CR]/\f[R] then it only matches at
the top level of the directory tree, \f[B]relative to the root of the
remote\f[R] (not necessarily the root of the drive).
If it does not start with \f[CR]/\f[R] then it is matched starting at
the \f[B]end of the path/file name\f[R] but it only matches a complete
path element \- it must match from a \f[CR]/\f[R] separator or the
beginning of the path/file.
.IP
.EX
file.jpg   \- matches \(dqfile.jpg\(dq
           \- matches \(dqdirectory/file.jpg\(dq
           \- doesn\(aqt match \(dqafile.jpg\(dq
           \- doesn\(aqt match \(dqdirectory/afile.jpg\(dq
/file.jpg  \- matches \(dqfile.jpg\(dq in the root directory of the remote
           \- doesn\(aqt match \(dqafile.jpg\(dq
           \- doesn\(aqt match \(dqdirectory/file.jpg\(dq
.EE
.PP
The top level of the remote might not be the top level of the drive.
.PP
E.g.
for a Microsoft Windows local directory structure
.IP
.EX
F:
├── bkp
├── data
│   ├── excl
│   │   ├── 123.jpg
│   │   └── 456.jpg
│   ├── incl
│   │   └── document.pdf
.EE
.PP
To copy the contents of folder \f[CR]data\f[R] into folder
\f[CR]bkp\f[R] excluding the contents of subfolder \f[CR]excl\f[R]the
following command treats \f[CR]F:\(rsdata\f[R] and \f[CR]F:\(rsbkp\f[R]
as top level for filtering.
.PP
\f[CR]rclone copy F:\(rsdata\(rs F:\(rsbkp\(rs \-\-exclude=/excl/**\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R] Use \f[CR]/\f[R] in path/file name patterns and not
\f[CR]\(rs\f[R] even if running on Microsoft Windows.
.PP
Simple patterns are case sensitive unless the
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R] flag is used.
.PP
Without \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R] (default)
.IP
.EX
potato \- matches \(dqpotato\(dq
       \- doesn\(aqt match \(dqPOTATO\(dq
.EE
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R]
.IP
.EX
potato \- matches \(dqpotato\(dq
       \- matches \(dqPOTATO\(dq
.EE
.SS Using regular expressions in filter patterns
The syntax of filter patterns is glob style matching (like
\f[CR]bash\f[R] uses) to make things easy for users.
However this does not provide absolute control over the matching, so for
advanced users rclone also provides a regular expression syntax.
.PP
Rclone generally accepts Perl\-style regular expressions, the exact
syntax is defined in the \c
.UR https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/
Go regular expression reference
.UE \c
\&.
Regular expressions should be enclosed in \f[CR]{{\f[R] \f[CR]}}\f[R].
They will match only the last path segment if the glob doesn\(aqt start
with \f[CR]/\f[R] or the whole path name if it does.
Note that rclone does not attempt to parse the supplied regular
expression, meaning that using any regular expression filter will
prevent rclone from using directory filter rules, as it will instead
check every path against the supplied regular expression(s).
.PP
Here is how the \f[CR]{{regexp}}\f[R] is transformed into an full
regular expression to match the entire path:
.IP
.EX
{{regexp}}  becomes (\(ha|/)(regexp)$
/{{regexp}} becomes \(ha(regexp)$
.EE
.PP
Regexp syntax can be mixed with glob syntax, for example
.IP
.EX
*.{{jpe?g}} to match file.jpg, file.jpeg but not file.png
.EE
.PP
You can also use regexp flags \- to set case insensitive, for example
.IP
.EX
*.{{(?i)jpg}} to match file.jpg, file.JPG but not file.png
.EE
.PP
Be careful with wildcards in regular expressions \- you don\(aqt want
them to match path separators normally.
To match any file name starting with \f[CR]start\f[R] and ending with
\f[CR]end\f[R] write
.IP
.EX
{{start[\(ha/]*end\(rs.jpg}}
.EE
.PP
Not
.IP
.EX
{{start.*end\(rs.jpg}}
.EE
.PP
Which will match a directory called \f[CR]start\f[R] with a file called
\f[CR]end.jpg\f[R] in it as the \f[CR].*\f[R] will match \f[CR]/\f[R]
characters.
.PP
Note that you can use \f[CR]\-vv \-\-dump filters\f[R] to show the
filter patterns in regexp format \- rclone implements the glob patterns
by transforming them into regular expressions.
.SS Filter pattern examples
.PP
