.TE
.SS How filter rules are applied to files
Rclone path/file name filters are made up of one or more of the
following flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R]
.PP
There can be more than one instance of individual flags.
.PP
Rclone internally uses a combined list of all the include and exclude
rules.
The order in which rules are processed can influence the result of the
filter.
.PP
All flags of the same type are processed together in the order above,
regardless of what order the different types of flags are included on
the command line.
.PP
Multiple instances of the same flag are processed from left to right
according to their position in the command line.
.PP
To mix up the order of processing includes and excludes use
\f[CR]\-\-filter...\f[R] flags.
.PP
Within \f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R] flags rules are processed from top to bottom
of the referenced file.
.PP
If there is an \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R]
flag specified, rclone implies a \f[CR]\- **\f[R] rule which it adds to
the bottom of the internal rule list.
Specifying a \f[CR]+\f[R] rule with a \f[CR]\-\-filter...\f[R] flag does
not imply that rule.
.PP
Each path/file name passed through rclone is matched against the
combined filter list.
At first match to a rule the path/file name is included or excluded and
no further filter rules are processed for that path/file.
.PP
If rclone does not find a match, after testing against all rules
(including the implied rule if appropriate), the path/file name is
included.
.PP
Any path/file included at that stage is processed by the rclone command.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] flags over\-ride and cannot be combined with
other filter options.
.PP
To see the internal combined rule list, in regular expression form, for
a command add the \f[CR]\-\-dump filters\f[R] flag.
Running an rclone command with \f[CR]\-\-dump filters\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-vv\f[R] flags lists the internal filter elements and shows how
they are applied to each source path/file.
There is not currently a means provided to pass regular expression
filter options into rclone directly though character class filter rules
contain character classes.
\c
.UR https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/
Go regular expression reference
.UE \c
.SS How filter rules are applied to directories
Rclone commands are applied to path/file names not directories.
The entire contents of a directory can be matched to a filter by the
pattern \f[CR]directory/*\f[R] or recursively by
\f[CR]directory/**\f[R].
.PP
Directory filter rules are defined with a closing \f[CR]/\f[R]
separator.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]/directory/subdirectory/\f[R] is an rclone directory filter rule.
.PP
Rclone commands can use directory filter rules to determine whether they
recurse into subdirectories.
This potentially optimises access to a remote by avoiding listing
unnecessary directories.
Whether optimisation is desirable depends on the specific filter rules
and source remote content.
.PP
If any regular expression filters are in use, then no directory
recursion optimisation is possible, as rclone must check every path
against the supplied regular expression(s).
.PP
Directory recursion optimisation occurs if either:
.IP \(bu 2
A source remote does not support the rclone \f[CR]ListR\f[R] primitive.
local, sftp, Microsoft OneDrive and WebDAV do not support
\f[CR]ListR\f[R].
Google Drive and most bucket type storage do.
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#optional-features
Full list
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
On other remotes (those that support \f[CR]ListR\f[R]), if the rclone
command is not naturally recursive, and provided it is not run with the
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] flag.
\f[CR]ls\f[R], \f[CR]lsf \-R\f[R] and \f[CR]size\f[R] are naturally
recursive but \f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]copy\f[R] and \f[CR]move\f[R] are
not.
.IP \(bu 2
Whenever the \f[CR]\-\-disable ListR\f[R] flag is applied to an rclone
command.
.PP
Rclone commands imply directory filter rules from path/file filter
rules.
To view the directory filter rules rclone has implied for a command
specify the \f[CR]\-\-dump filters\f[R] flag.
.PP
E.g.
for an include rule
.IP
.EX
/a/*.jpg
.EE
.PP
Rclone implies the directory include rule
.IP
.EX
/a/
.EE
.PP
Directory filter rules specified in an rclone command can limit the
scope of an rclone command but path/file filters still have to be
specified.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-include /directory/\f[R] will not match any
files.
Because it is an \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] option the
\f[CR]\-\-exclude **\f[R] rule is implied, and the
\f[CR]/directory/\f[R] pattern serves only to optimise access to the
remote by ignoring everything outside of that directory.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-filter\-from filter\-list.txt\f[R] with a
file \f[CR]filter\-list.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\- /dir1/
\- /dir2/
+ *.pdf
\- **
.EE
.PP
All files in directories \f[CR]dir1\f[R] or \f[CR]dir2\f[R] or their
subdirectories are completely excluded from the listing.
Only files of suffix \f[CR]pdf\f[R] in the root of \f[CR]remote:\f[R] or
its subdirectories are listed.
The \f[CR]\- **\f[R] rule prevents listing of any path/files not
previously matched by the rules above.
.PP
Option \f[CR]exclude\-if\-present\f[R] creates a directory exclude rule
based on the presence of a file in a directory and takes precedence over
other rclone directory filter rules.
.PP
When using pattern list syntax, if a pattern item contains either
\f[CR]/\f[R] or \f[CR]**\f[R], then rclone will not able to imply a
directory filter rule from this pattern list.
.PP
E.g.
for an include rule
.IP
.EX
{dir1/**,dir2/**}
.EE
.PP
Rclone will match files below directories \f[CR]dir1\f[R] or
\f[CR]dir2\f[R] only, but will not be able to use this filter to exclude
a directory \f[CR]dir3\f[R] from being traversed.
.PP
Directory recursion optimisation may affect performance, but normally
not the result.
One exception to this is sync operations with option
\f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R], where any traversed empty
directories will be created.
With the pattern list example \f[CR]{dir1/**,dir2/**}\f[R] above, this
would create an empty directory \f[CR]dir3\f[R] on destination (when it
exists on source).
Changing the filter to \f[CR]{dir1,dir2}/**\f[R], or splitting it into
two include rules \f[CR]\-\-include dir1/** \-\-include dir2/**\f[R],
will match the same files while also filtering directories, with the
result that an empty directory \f[CR]dir3\f[R] will no longer be
created.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R] \- Exclude files matching pattern
Excludes path/file names from an rclone command based on a single
exclude rule.
.PP
This flag can be repeated.
See above for the order filter flags are processed in.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R] should not be used with \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R] flags.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R] has no effect when combined with
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] flags.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-exclude *.bak\f[R] excludes all .bak files
from listing.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone size remote: \-\-exclude \(dq/dir/**\(dq\f[R] returns the
total size of all files on \f[CR]remote:\f[R] excluding those in root
directory \f[CR]dir\f[R] and sub directories.
.PP
E.g.
on Microsoft Windows
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-exclude \(dq*\(rs[{JP,KR,HK}\(rs]*\(dq\f[R]
lists the files in \f[CR]remote:\f[R] without \f[CR][JP]\f[R] or
\f[CR][KR]\f[R] or \f[CR][HK]\f[R] in their name.
Quotes prevent the shell from interpreting the \f[CR]\(rs\f[R]
characters.\f[CR]\(rs\f[R] characters escape the \f[CR][\f[R] and
\f[CR]]\f[R] so an rclone filter treats them literally rather than as a
character\-range.
The \f[CR]{\f[R] and \f[CR]}\f[R] define an rclone pattern list.
For other operating systems single quotes are required ie
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-exclude \(aq*\(rs[{JP,KR,HK}\(rs]*\(aq\f[R]
.SS \f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] \- Read exclude patterns from file
Excludes path/file names from an rclone command based on rules in a
named file.
The file contains a list of remarks and pattern rules.
.PP
For an example \f[CR]exclude\-file.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
# a sample exclude rule file
*.bak
file2.jpg
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-exclude\-from exclude\-file.txt\f[R] lists
the files on \f[CR]remote:\f[R] except those named \f[CR]file2.jpg\f[R]
or with a suffix \f[CR].bak\f[R].
That is equivalent to
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-exclude file2.jpg \-\-exclude \(dq*.bak\(dq\f[R].
.PP
This flag can be repeated.
See above for the order filter flags are processed in.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] flag is useful where multiple exclude
filter rules are applied to an rclone command.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] should not be used with
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R] flags.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] has no effect when combined with
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] flags.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] followed by \f[CR]\-\f[R] reads filter
rules from standard input.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] \- Include files matching pattern
Adds a single include rule based on path/file names to an rclone
command.
.PP
This flag can be repeated.
See above for the order filter flags are processed in.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] has no effect when combined with
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] flags.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] implies \f[CR]\-\-exclude **\f[R] at the end of
an rclone internal filter list.
Therefore if you mix \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] flags with \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R], you must use include rules for all the
files you want in the include statement.
For more flexibility use the \f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R] flag.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-include \(dq*.{png,jpg}\(dq\f[R] lists the
files on \f[CR]remote:\f[R] with suffix \f[CR].png\f[R] and
\f[CR].jpg\f[R].
All other files are excluded.
.PP
E.g.
multiple rclone copy commands can be combined with
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] and a pattern\-list.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy /vol1/A remote:A
rclone copy /vol1/B remote:B
.EE
.PP
is equivalent to:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy /vol1 remote: \-\-include \(dq{A,B}/**\(dq
.EE
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote:/wheat \-\-include \(dq??[\(ha[:punct:]]*\(dq\f[R]
lists the files \f[CR]remote:\f[R] directory \f[CR]wheat\f[R] (and
subdirectories) whose third character is not punctuation.
This example uses an \c
.UR https://golang.org/pkg/regexp/syntax/
ASCII character class
.UE \c
\&.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] \- Read include patterns from file
Adds path/file names to an rclone command based on rules in a named
file.
The file contains a list of remarks and pattern rules.
.PP
For an example \f[CR]include\-file.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
# a sample include rule file
*.jpg
file2.avi
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-include\-from include\-file.txt\f[R] lists
the files on \f[CR]remote:\f[R] with name \f[CR]file2.avi\f[R] or suffix
\f[CR].jpg\f[R].
That is equivalent to
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-include file2.avi \-\-include \(dq*.jpg\(dq\f[R].
.PP
This flag can be repeated.
See above for the order filter flags are processed in.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] flag is useful where multiple include
filter rules are applied to an rclone command.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] implies \f[CR]\-\-exclude **\f[R] at the
end of an rclone internal filter list.
Therefore if you mix \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] flags with \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R], you must use include rules for all the
files you want in the include statement.
For more flexibility use the \f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R] flag.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] has no effect when combined with
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] flags.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R] followed by \f[CR]\-\f[R] reads filter
rules from standard input.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] \- Add a file\-filtering rule
Specifies path/file names to an rclone command, based on a single
include or exclude rule, in \f[CR]+\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\f[R] format.
.PP
This flag can be repeated.
See above for the order filter flags are processed in.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-filter +\f[R] differs from \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R].
In the case of \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] rclone implies an
\f[CR]\-\-exclude *\f[R] rule which it adds to the bottom of the
internal rule list.
\f[CR]\-\-filter...+\f[R] does not imply that rule.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] has no effect when combined with
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] flags.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] should not be used with \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R] flags.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-filter \(dq\- *.bak\(dq\f[R] excludes all
\f[CR].bak\f[R] files from a list of \f[CR]remote:\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R] \- Read filtering patterns from a file
Adds path/file names to an rclone command based on rules in a named
file.
The file contains a list of remarks and pattern rules.
Include  rules start with \f[CR]+\f[R] and exclude rules with
\f[CR]\-\f[R].
\f[CR]!\f[R] clears existing rules.
Rules are processed in the order they are defined.
.PP
This flag can be repeated.
See above for the order filter flags are processed in.
.PP
Arrange the order of filter rules with the most restrictive first and
work down.
.PP
Lines starting with # or ; are ignored, and can be used to write
comments.
Inline comments are not supported.
\f[I]Use \f[CI]\-vv \-\-dump filters\f[I] to see how they appear in the
final regexp.\f[R]
.PP
E.g.
for \f[CR]filter\-file.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
# a sample filter rule file
\- secret*.jpg
+ *.jpg
+ *.png
+ file2.avi
\- /dir/tmp/** # WARNING! This text will be treated as part of the path.
\- /dir/Trash/**
+ /dir/**
# exclude everything else
\- *
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-filter\-from filter\-file.txt\f[R] lists the
path/files on \f[CR]remote:\f[R] including all \f[CR]jpg\f[R] and
\f[CR]png\f[R] files, excluding any matching \f[CR]secret*.jpg\f[R] and
including \f[CR]file2.avi\f[R].
It also includes everything in the directory \f[CR]dir\f[R] at the root
of \f[CR]remote\f[R], except \f[CR]remote:dir/Trash\f[R] which it
excludes.
Everything else is excluded.
.PP
E.g.
for an alternative \f[CR]filter\-file.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\- secret*.jpg
+ *.jpg
+ *.png
+ file2.avi
\- *
.EE
.PP
Files \f[CR]file1.jpg\f[R], \f[CR]file3.png\f[R] and
\f[CR]file2.avi\f[R] are listed whilst \f[CR]secret17.jpg\f[R] and files
without the suffix \f[CR].jpg\f[R] or \f[CR].png\f[R] are excluded.
.PP
E.g.
for an alternative \f[CR]filter\-file.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
+ *.jpg
+ *.gif
!
+ 42.doc
\- *
.EE
.PP
Only file 42.doc is listed.
Prior rules are cleared by the \f[CR]!\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] \- Read list of source\-file names
Adds path/files to an rclone command from a list in a named file.
Rclone processes the path/file names in the order of the list, and no
others.
.PP
Other filter flags (\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-include\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-filter\-from\f[R]) are ignored when
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] is used.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] expects a list of files as its input.
Leading or trailing whitespace is stripped from the input lines.
Lines starting with \f[CR]#\f[R] or \f[CR];\f[R] are ignored.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] followed by \f[CR]\-\f[R] reads the list of
files from standard input.
.PP
Rclone commands with a \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] flag traverse the
remote, treating the names in \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] as a set of
filters.
.PP
If the \f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] flags
are used together an rclone command does not traverse the remote.
Instead it addresses each path/file named in the file individually.
For each path/file name, that requires typically 1 API call.
This can be efficient for a short \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] list and a
remote containing many files.
.PP
Rclone commands do not error if any names in the
\f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] file are missing from the source remote.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] flag can be repeated in a single rclone
command to read path/file names from more than one file.
The files are read from left to right along the command line.
.PP
Paths within the \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] file are interpreted as
starting with the root specified in the rclone command.
Leading \f[CR]/\f[R] separators are ignored.
See \-\-files\-from\-raw if you need the input to be processed in a raw
manner.
.PP
E.g.
for a file \f[CR]files\-from.txt\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
# comment
file1.jpg
subdir/file2.jpg
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]rclone copy \-\-files\-from files\-from.txt /home/me/pics remote:pics\f[R]
copies the following, if they exist, and only those files.
.IP
.EX
/home/me/pics/file1.jpg        → remote:pics/file1.jpg
/home/me/pics/subdir/file2.jpg → remote:pics/subdir/file2.jpg
.EE
.PP
E.g.
to copy the following files referenced by their absolute paths:
.IP
.EX
/home/user1/42
/home/user1/dir/ford
/home/user2/prefect
.EE
.PP
First find a common subdirectory \- in this case \f[CR]/home\f[R] and
put the remaining files in \f[CR]files\-from.txt\f[R] with or without
leading \f[CR]/\f[R], e.g.
.IP
.EX
user1/42
user1/dir/ford
user2/prefect
.EE
.PP
Then copy these to a remote:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-files\-from files\-from.txt /home remote:backup
.EE
.PP
The three files are transferred as follows:
.IP
.EX
/home/user1/42       → remote:backup/user1/important
/home/user1/dir/ford → remote:backup/user1/dir/file
/home/user2/prefect  → remote:backup/user2/stuff
.EE
.PP
Alternatively if \f[CR]/\f[R] is chosen as root
\f[CR]files\-from.txt\f[R] will be:
.IP
.EX
/home/user1/42
/home/user1/dir/ford
/home/user2/prefect
.EE
.PP
The copy command will be:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-files\-from files\-from.txt / remote:backup
.EE
.PP
Then there will be an extra \f[CR]home\f[R] directory on the remote:
.IP
.EX
/home/user1/42       → remote:backup/home/user1/42
/home/user1/dir/ford → remote:backup/home/user1/dir/ford
/home/user2/prefect  → remote:backup/home/user2/prefect
.EE
.SS \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] \- Read list of source\-file names without any processing
This flag is the same as \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R] except that input is
read in a raw manner.
Lines with leading / trailing whitespace, and lines starting with
\f[CR];\f[R] or \f[CR]#\f[R] are read without any processing.
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_lsf/
rclone lsf
.UE \c
\ has a compatible format that can be used to export file lists from
remotes for input to \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] \- Read NUL separated list of source\-file names
This flag is the same as \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R] except that
input is split on NUL (\f[CR]\(rs0\f[R]) characters instead of newlines.
This allows handling filenames that contain embedded newline characters.
.PP
It is similar to the \f[CR]\-print0\f[R] option of \f[CR]find\f[R] and
the \f[CR]\-0\f[R] option of \f[CR]xargs\f[R].
.PP
E.g.
to copy files listed by \f[CR]find\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
find /path \-print0 | rclone copy \-\-files\-from0 \- / remote:path
.EE
.SS \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R] \- make searches case insensitive
By default, rclone filter patterns are case sensitive.
The \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R] flag makes all of the filters patterns
on the command line case insensitive.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]\-\-include \(dqzaphod.txt\(dq\f[R] does not match a file
\f[CR]Zaphod.txt\f[R].
With \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R] a match is made.
.SS Quoting shell metacharacters
Rclone commands with filter patterns containing shell metacharacters may
not as work as expected in your shell and may require quoting.
.PP
E.g.
linux, OSX (\f[CR]*\f[R] metacharacter)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-include \(rs*.jpg\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-include \(aq*.jpg\(aq\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-include=\(aq*.jpg\(aq\f[R]
.PP
Microsoft Windows expansion is done by the command, not shell, so
\f[CR]\-\-include *.jpg\f[R] does not require quoting.
.PP
If the rclone error
\f[CR]Command .... needs .... arguments maximum: you provided .... non flag arguments:\f[R]
is encountered, the cause is commonly spaces within the name of a remote
or flag value.
The fix then is to quote values containing spaces.
.SS Other filters
.SS \f[CR]\-\-min\-size\f[R] \- Don\(aqt transfer any file smaller than this
Controls the minimum size file within the scope of an rclone command.
Default units are \f[CR]KiB\f[R] but abbreviations \f[CR]B\f[R],
\f[CR]K\f[R], \f[CR]M\f[R], \f[CR]G\f[R], \f[CR]T\f[R] or \f[CR]P\f[R]
are valid.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-min\-size 50k\f[R] lists files on
\f[CR]remote:\f[R] of 50 KiB size or larger.
.PP
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#size-options
the size option docs
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-max\-size\f[R] \- Don\(aqt transfer any file larger than this
Controls the maximum size file within the scope of an rclone command.
Default units are \f[CR]KiB\f[R] but abbreviations \f[CR]B\f[R],
\f[CR]K\f[R], \f[CR]M\f[R], \f[CR]G\f[R], \f[CR]T\f[R] or \f[CR]P\f[R]
are valid.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-max\-size 1G\f[R] lists files on
\f[CR]remote:\f[R] of 1 GiB size or smaller.
.PP
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#size-options
the size option docs
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-max\-age\f[R] \- Don\(aqt transfer any file older than this
Controls the maximum age of files within the scope of an rclone command.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-max\-age\f[R] applies only to files and not to directories.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-max\-age 2d\f[R] lists files on
\f[CR]remote:\f[R] of 2 days old or less.
.PP
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#time-options
the time option docs
.UE \c
\ for valid formats.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-min\-age\f[R] \- Don\(aqt transfer any file younger than this
Controls the minimum age of files within the scope of an rclone command.
(see \f[CR]\-\-max\-age\f[R] for valid formats)
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-min\-age\f[R] applies only to files and not to directories.
.PP
E.g.
\f[CR]rclone ls remote: \-\-min\-age 2d\f[R] lists files on
\f[CR]remote:\f[R] of 2 days old or more.
.PP
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#time-options
the time option docs
.UE \c
\ for valid formats.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-hash\-filter\f[R] \- Deterministically select a subset of files
The \f[CR]\-\-hash\-filter\f[R] flag enables selecting a deterministic
subset of files, useful for:
.IP "1." 3
Running large sync operations across multiple machines.
.IP "2." 3
Checking a subset of files for bitrot.
.IP "3." 3
Any other operations where a sample of files is required.
.SS Syntax
The flag takes two parameters expressed as a fraction:
.IP
.EX
\-\-hash\-filter K/N
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]N\f[R]: The total number of partitions (must be a positive
integer).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]K\f[R]: The specific partition to select (an integer from
\f[CR]0\f[R] to \f[CR]N\f[R]).
.PP
For example:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-hash\-filter 1/3\f[R]: Selects the first third of the files.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-hash\-filter 2/3\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-hash\-filter 3/3\f[R]:
Select the second and third partitions, respectively.
.PP
Each partition is non\-overlapping, ensuring all files are covered
without duplication.
.SS Random Partition Selection
Use \f[CR]\(at\f[R] as \f[CR]K\f[R] to randomly select a partition:
.IP
.EX
\-\-hash\-filter \(at/M
.EE
.PP
For example, \f[CR]\-\-hash\-filter \(at/3\f[R] will randomly select a
number between 0 and 2.
This will stay constant across retries.
.SS How It Works
.IP \(bu 2
Rclone takes each file\(aqs full path, normalizes it to lowercase, and
applies Unicode normalization.
.IP \(bu 2
It then hashes the normalized path into a 64 bit number.
.IP \(bu 2
The hash result is reduced modulo \f[CR]N\f[R] to assign the file to a
partition.
.IP \(bu 2
If the calculated partition does not match \f[CR]K\f[R] the file is
excluded.
.IP \(bu 2
Other filters may apply if the file is not excluded.
.PP
\f[B]Important:\f[R] Rclone will traverse all directories to apply the
filter.
.SS Usage Notes
.IP \(bu 2
Safe to use with \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R]; source and destination
selections will match.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Do not\f[R] use with \f[CR]\-\-delete\-excluded\f[R], as this could
delete unselected files.
.IP \(bu 2
Ignored if \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-files\-from\-raw\f[R]
or \f[CR]\-\-files\-from0\f[R] is used.
.SS Examples
.SS Dividing files into 4 partitions
Assuming the current directory contains \f[CR]file1.jpg\f[R] through
\f[CR]file9.jpg\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ rclone lsf \-\-hash\-filter 0/4 .
file1.jpg
file5.jpg

$ rclone lsf \-\-hash\-filter 1/4 .
file3.jpg
file6.jpg
file9.jpg

$ rclone lsf \-\-hash\-filter 2/4 .
file2.jpg
file4.jpg

$ rclone lsf \-\-hash\-filter 3/4 .
file7.jpg
file8.jpg

$ rclone lsf \-\-hash\-filter 4/4 . # the same as \-\-hash\-filter 0/4
file1.jpg
file5.jpg
.EE
.SS Syncing the first quarter of files
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-hash\-filter 1/4 source:path destination:path
.EE
.SS Checking a random 1% of files for integrity
.IP
.EX
rclone check \-\-download \-\-hash\-filter \(at/100 source:path destination:path
.EE
.SS Other flags
.SS \f[CR]\-\-delete\-excluded\f[R] \- Delete files on dest excluded from sync
\f[B]Important\f[R] this flag is dangerous to your data \- use with
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] and \f[CR]\-v\f[R] first.
.PP
In conjunction with \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-delete\-excluded\f[R] deletes any files on the destination
which are excluded from the command.
.PP
E.g.
the scope of \f[CR]rclone sync \-\-interactive A: B:\f[R] can be
restricted:
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-min\-size 50k \-\-delete\-excluded sync A: B:
.EE
.PP
All files on \f[CR]B:\f[R] which are less than 50 KiB are deleted
because they are excluded from the rclone sync command.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-dump filters\f[R] \- dump the filters to the output
Dumps the defined filters to standard output in regular expression
format.
.PP
Useful for debugging.
.SS Exclude directory based on a file
The \f[CR]\-\-exclude\-if\-present\f[R] flag controls whether a
directory is within the scope of an rclone command based on the presence
of a named file within it.
The flag can be repeated to check for multiple file names, presence of
any of them will exclude the directory.
.PP
This flag has a priority over other filter flags.
.PP
E.g.
for the following directory structure:
.IP
.EX
dir1/file1
dir1/dir2/file2
dir1/dir2/dir3/file3
dir1/dir2/dir3/.ignore
.EE
.PP
The command \f[CR]rclone ls \-\-exclude\-if\-present .ignore dir1\f[R]
does not list \f[CR]dir3\f[R], \f[CR]file3\f[R] or \f[CR].ignore\f[R].
.SS Metadata filters
The metadata filters work in a very similar way to the normal file name
filters, except they match \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#metadata
metadata
.UE \c
\ on the object.
.PP
The metadata should be specified as \f[CR]key=value\f[R] patterns.
This may be wildcarded using the normal filter patterns or regular
expressions.
.PP
For example if you wished to list only local files with a mode of
\f[CR]100664\f[R] you could do that with:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf \-M \-\-files\-only \-\-metadata\-include \(dqmode=100664\(dq .
.EE
.PP
Or if you wished to show files with an \f[CR]atime\f[R],
\f[CR]mtime\f[R] or \f[CR]btime\f[R] at a given date:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf \-M \-\-files\-only \-\-metadata\-include \(dq[abm]time=2022\-12\-16*\(dq .
.EE
.PP
Like file filtering, metadata filtering only applies to files not to
directories.
.PP
The filters can be applied using these flags.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-include\f[R] \- Include metadatas matching pattern
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-include\-from\f[R] \- Read metadata include patterns
from file (use \- to read from stdin)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-exclude\f[R] \- Exclude metadatas matching pattern
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-exclude\-from\f[R] \- Read metadata exclude patterns
from file (use \- to read from stdin)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-filter\f[R] \- Add a metadata filtering rule
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-metadata\-filter\-from\f[R] \- Read metadata filtering
patterns from a file (use \- to read from stdin)
.PP
Each flag can be repeated.
See the section on how filter rules are applied for more details \-
these flags work in an identical way to the file name filtering flags,
but instead of file name patterns have metadata patterns.
.SS Common pitfalls
The most frequent filter support issues on the \c
.UR https://forum.rclone.org/
rclone forum
.UE \c
\ are:
.IP \(bu 2
Not using paths relative to the root of the remote
.IP \(bu 2
Not using \f[CR]/\f[R] to match from the root of a remote
.IP \(bu 2
Not using \f[CR]**\f[R] to match the contents of a directory
.SH rclone gui
The \f[CR]rclone gui\f[R] command starts the official Web GUI that comes
bundled with rclone.
.PP
With this command, rclone can serve a web\-based GUI (graphical user
interface) that is accessible from a normal web browser.
.PP
Run it in a terminal and rclone will initialize and then start the GUI.
.IP
.EX
rclone gui
.EE
.PP
This will produce logs like this.
The terminal window needs to stay open to continue to run the GUI:
.IP
.EX
2026/04/14 11:36:04 NOTICE: Serving remote control on http://127.0.0.1:50803/
2026/04/14 11:36:04 NOTICE: Serving GUI on http://127.0.0.1:50802/
2026/04/14 11:36:04 NOTICE: GUI available at http://127.0.0.1:50802/login?pass=XXX&url=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A50803%2F&user=gui
.EE
.PP
You can also add debugging flags when running the GUI, such as
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], which will show more logging output from the rc server.
.SS Using the GUI
Once the browser opens, you will be presented with the Dashboard, the
main screen where you can see the status of your remotes and system.
.PP
At the top, starting from the left, you will see a series of tabs you
can click on.
On the right side you will have the logout button and potentially an
\(dqUpdate available\(dq message if a new rclone version has been
released.
.SS Dashboard
See live metrics, learn if your remotes are near capacity, and read the
changelog for the current version.
.SS Explorer
Explore and manage both local disks and remotes, download files and
directories and start transfers.
.SS Remotes
Scroll the list of remotes and tap on it to navigate to the explorer.
There you can navigate the contents of your remotes, transfer, download,
and even upload files.
.SS Mounts
Mount remotes as local drives on your computer and check on your
existing mounts.
.SS Serves
Get quick info about your active serve instances, and start new ones.
.SS Settings
Edit your \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] file directly, set logging flags, and
performance parameters.
.SS How it works
When you run \f[CR]rclone gui\f[R] this is what happens
.IP \(bu 2
Rclone starts the remote control API (\(dqrc\(dq).
.IP \(bu 2
Rclone starts a second server to serve the Web GUI.
.IP \(bu 2
If a port, username or password is not specified, then missing values
will be auto\-generated.
.IP \(bu 2
Unless \f[CR]\-\-no\-open\-browser\f[R] is passed, a browser window will
open.
.IP \(bu 2
The URL already contains the username & password, in which case the GUI
will use those values and log you in automatically.
.PP
The rc address is passed to the GUI via the \f[CR]url\f[R] query
parameter (the URL printed in the terminal); append
\f[CR]?url=http://...\f[R] to point it at a different rc server.
.SS Security
It\(aqs important to think first about what rclone has access to and
what you might be sharing.
.PP
A few good measures:
.IP \(bu 2
Don\(aqt use \f[CR]\-\-no\-auth\f[R] (this is for testing only).
.IP \(bu 2
Do not expose to the local network (eg with
\f[CR]\-\-api\-addr :5572 \-\-addr :8080\f[R]) unless you trust all
devices on your local network.
Prefer \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R] or \f[CR]localhost\f[R] (the default).
.IP \(bu 2
Use a strong password and non\-obvious usernames like \(dqadmin\(dq or
\(dqrclone\(dq if you are using \f[CR]\-\-user\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-pass\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
If you want to host it on a server and access it remotely, make sure
you\(aqre only exposing the GUI and not the RC API.
They listen on different ports.
.PP
If you want to access it remotely but want to avoid running a proxy and
exposing ports, you can use Cloudflare Tunnels or localhost.run or
Tailscale (all free).
.SS Options
.IP
.EX
      \-\-addr stringArray       IPaddress:Port for the GUI server (default auto\-chosen localhost port)
      \-\-api\-addr stringArray   IPaddress:Port for the RC API server (default auto\-chosen localhost port)
      \-\-enable\-metrics         Enable OpenMetrics/Prometheus compatible endpoint at /metrics
  \-h, \-\-help                   help for gui
      \-\-no\-auth                Don\(aqt require auth for the RC API
      \-\-no\-open\-browser        Skip opening the browser automatically
      \-\-pass string            Password for RC authentication
      \-\-user string            User name for RC authentication
.EE
.SS History
In v1.74 the GUI was redone and embedded within rclone for ease of use.
The GUI bundle ships as a compressed zip embedded in the rclone binary
and is served from the zip at runtime.
.SH Remote controlling rclone with its API
If rclone is run with the \f[CR]\-\-rc\f[R] flag then it starts an HTTP
server which can be used to remote control rclone using its API.
.PP
You can either use the rc command to access the API or use HTTP
directly.
.PP
If you just want to run a remote control then see the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_rcd/
rcd
.UE \c
\ command.
.SS Security
\f[B]Access to the rc API is equivalent to shell access as the user
running rclone.\f[R] Treat the rc port as you would an interactive login
on the host.
.PP
Any caller who can reach the API (and pass authentication, if it is
enabled) can, among other things:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Run OS commands\f[R] as the rclone user.
\f[CR]core/command\f[R] re\-executes the rclone binary with arbitrary
arguments, and several backend options shell out to programs, so even
creating a remote with \f[CR]config/create\f[R] can lead to command
execution.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Read and write any file\f[R] reachable by the rclone process, by
pointing \f[CR]operations/*\f[R] or \f[CR]sync/*\f[R] at a
\f[CR]local\f[R] remote (or via \f[CR]\-\-rc\-files\f[R] /
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-serve\f[R]).
Writing arbitrary files as the rclone user is itself a route to code
execution.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Read back stored credentials.\f[R] rclone configs routinely hold
cloud\-provider secrets.
\f[CR]config/dump\f[R] and friends expose them, so a compromise of the
rc reaches every configured backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Change rclone\(aqs runtime behaviour\f[R] with
\f[CR]options/set\f[R], \f[B]manage remotes\f[R] with
\f[CR]config/*\f[R], and \f[B]stop the process\f[R] with
\f[CR]core/quit\f[R].
.PP
There is currently no per\-endpoint capability or scope system:
authentication is all\-or\-nothing.
Granting any access grants all of the above.
.PP
Consequently:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Do not bind the rc to a network address you do not control.\f[R]
The default bind is loopback (\f[CR]localhost:5572\f[R]); keep it there
unless you have a specific reason to change it.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Do not use \f[CB]\-\-rc\-no\-auth\f[B] on a non\-loopback
bind.\f[R] It disables authentication on the endpoints that access
remotes \(em see \f[CR]\-\-rc\-no\-auth\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Use authentication and TLS\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-rc\-user\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-rc\-pass\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-htpasswd\f[R], plus
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-cert\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-rc\-key\f[R]) whenever the port is
reachable by anyone you do not fully trust, and raise
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-min\-tls\-version\f[R].
.SS Supported parameters
.SS \-\-rc
Flag to start the http server listen on remote requests.
.SS \-\-rc\-addr=IP
IPaddress:Port or :Port to bind server to.
(default \(dqlocalhost:5572\(dq).
.SS \-\-rc\-cert=KEY
SSL PEM key (concatenation of certificate and CA certificate).
.SS \-\-rc\-client\-ca=PATH
Client certificate authority to verify clients with.
.SS \-\-rc\-htpasswd=PATH
htpasswd file \- if not provided no authentication is done.
.SS \-\-rc\-key=PATH
TLS PEM private key file.
.SS \-\-rc\-max\-header\-bytes=VALUE
Maximum size of request header (default 4096).
.SS \-\-rc\-min\-tls\-version=VALUE
The minimum TLS version that is acceptable.
Valid values are \(dqtls1.0\(dq, \(dqtls1.1\(dq, \(dqtls1.2\(dq and
\(dqtls1.3\(dq (default \(dqtls1.0\(dq).
.SS \-\-rc\-user=VALUE
User name for authentication.
.SS \-\-rc\-pass=VALUE
Password for authentication.
.SS \-\-rc\-realm=VALUE
Realm for authentication (default \(dqrclone\(dq).
.SS \-\-rc\-server\-read\-timeout=DURATION
Timeout for server reading data (default 1h0m0s).
.SS \-\-rc\-server\-write\-timeout=DURATION
Timeout for server writing data (default 1h0m0s).
.SS \-\-rc\-serve
Enable the serving of remote objects via the HTTP interface.
This means objects will be accessible at
\f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:5572/\f[R] by default, so you can browse to
\f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:5572/\f[R] or \f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:5572/*\f[R]
to see a listing of the remotes.
Objects may be requested from remotes using this syntax
\f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:5572/[remote:path]/path/to/object\f[R]
.PP
Unless the rc server has authentication configured
(\f[CR]\-\-rc\-user\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-rc\-pass\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-htpasswd\f[R]) or the \f[CR]\-\-rc\-no\-auth\f[R] flag is
set, only remotes already present in the config file may be served this
way.
Inline remotes (e.g.
\f[CR][:webdav,url=...:]\f[R]), connection string parameters and bare
local paths are rejected, since instantiating them from an
unauthenticated request could run commands or read arbitrary local
files.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS global.* connection string options and the rc
Remotes instantiated by the rc do not let \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#connection-strings
connection string
.UE \c
\ \f[CR]global.*\f[R] options change rclone\(aqs process\-wide
configuration.
Remotes created directly on the command line or defined in the config
file are unaffected.
.PP
A \f[CR]global.*\f[R] option still takes effect for the individual
backend it is set on (exactly like an \f[CR]override.*\f[R] option), it
just does not leak into the global config for the rest of the process.
.SS \-\-rc\-serve\-no\-modtime
Set this flag to skip reading the modification time (can speed things
up).
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-files /path/to/directory
Path to local files to serve on the HTTP server.
.PP
If this is set then rclone will serve the files in that directory.
It will also open the root in the web browser if specified.
This is for implementing browser based GUIs for rclone functions.
.PP
If \f[CR]\-\-rc\-user\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-rc\-pass\f[R] is set then the
URL that is opened will have the authorization in the URL in the
\f[CR]http://user:pass\(atlocalhost/\f[R] style.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-enable\-metrics
Enable OpenMetrics/Prometheus compatible endpoint at
\f[CR]/metrics\f[R].
If more control over the metrics is desired (for example running it on a
different port or with different auth) then endpoint can be enabled with
the \f[CR]\-\-metrics\-*\f[R] flags instead.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-web\-gui
Set this flag to serve the default web gui on the same port as rclone.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-allow\-origin
Set the allowed Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin for rc requests.
.PP
This enables cross\-origin (CORS) requests from browsers on the origin
given, for example
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-allow\-origin https://app.example.com\f[R].
Browser CORS preflight OPTIONS requests are answered by rclone without
authentication as browsers never send credentials with them.
All other requests, including other OPTIONS requests, must be
authenticated when authentication is enabled.
.PP
The \f[CR]rclone gui\f[R] command sets this automatically to the origin
the GUI is served from if it is not set explicitly.
.PP
Setting this to \f[CR]*\f[R] allows any origin which is a security risk
on an authenticated server.
.PP
Default is off (no CORS).
.SS \-\-rc\-web\-fetch\-url
Set the URL to fetch the rclone\-web\-gui files from.
.PP
Default \c
.UR https://api.github.com/repos/rclone/rclone-webui-react/releases/latest
.UE \c
\&.
.SS \-\-rc\-web\-gui\-update
Set this flag to check and update rclone\-webui\-react from the
rc\-web\-fetch\-url.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-web\-gui\-force\-update
Set this flag to force update rclone\-webui\-react from the
rc\-web\-fetch\-url.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-web\-gui\-no\-open\-browser
Set this flag to disable opening browser automatically when using
web\-gui.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-job\-expire\-duration=DURATION
Expire finished async jobs older than DURATION (default 60s).
.SS \-\-rc\-job\-expire\-interval=DURATION
Interval duration to check for expired async jobs (default 10s).
.SS \-\-rc\-no\-auth
By default rclone will require authorisation to have been set up on the
rc interface in order to use any methods which access any rclone
remotes.
Eg \f[CR]operations/list\f[R] is denied as it involved creating a remote
as is \f[CR]sync/copy\f[R].
.PP
If this is set then no authorisation will be required on the server to
use these methods.
The alternative is to use \f[CR]\-\-rc\-user\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-pass\f[R] and use these credentials in the request.
.PP
Default Off.
.SS \-\-rc\-baseurl
Prefix for URLs.
.PP
Default is root
.SS \-\-rc\-template
User\-specified template.
.SS Accessing the remote control via the rclone rc command
Rclone itself implements the remote control protocol in its
\f[CR]rclone rc\f[R] command.
.PP
You can use it like this:
.IP
.EX
$ rclone rc rc/noop param1=one param2=two
{
    \(dqparam1\(dq: \(dqone\(dq,
    \(dqparam2\(dq: \(dqtwo\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
If the remote is running on a different URL than the default
\f[CR]http://localhost:5572/\f[R], use the \f[CR]\-\-url\f[R] option to
specify it:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-url http://some.remote:1234/ rc/noop
.EE
.PP
Or, if the remote is listening on a Unix socket, use the
\f[CR]\-\-unix\-socket\f[R] option instead:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-unix\-socket /tmp/rclone.sock rc/noop
.EE
.PP
Run \f[CR]rclone rc\f[R] on its own, without any commands, to see the
help for the installed remote control commands.
Note that this also needs to connect to the remote server.
.SS JSON input
\f[CR]rclone rc\f[R] also supports a \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] flag which can
be used to send more complicated input parameters.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone rc \-\-json \(aq{ \(dqp1\(dq: [1,\(dq2\(dq,null,4], \(dqp2\(dq: { \(dqa\(dq:1, \(dqb\(dq:2 } }\(aq rc/noop
{
    \(dqp1\(dq: [
        1,
        \(dq2\(dq,
        null,
        4
    ],
    \(dqp2\(dq: {
        \(dqa\(dq: 1,
        \(dqb\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.PP
If the parameter being passed is an object then it can be passed as a
JSON string rather than using the \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] flag which
simplifies the command line.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc operations/list fs=/tmp remote=test opt=\(aq{\(dqshowHash\(dq: true}\(aq
.EE
.PP
Rather than
.IP
.EX
rclone rc operations/list \-\-json \(aq{\(dqfs\(dq: \(dq/tmp\(dq, \(dqremote\(dq: \(dqtest\(dq, \(dqopt\(dq: {\(dqshowHash\(dq: true}}\(aq
.EE
.SS Special parameters
The rc interface supports some special parameters which apply to
\f[B]all\f[R] commands.
These start with \f[CR]_\f[R] to show they are different.
.SS Running asynchronous jobs with _async = true
Each rc call is classified as a job and it is assigned its own id.
By default jobs are executed immediately as they are created or
synchronously.
.PP
If \f[CR]_async\f[R] has a true value when supplied to an rc call then
it will return immediately with a job id and execute id, and the task
will be run in the background.
The \f[CR]job/status\f[R] call can be used to get information of the
background job.
The job can be queried for up to 1 minute after it has finished.
.PP
It is recommended that potentially long running jobs, e.g.
\f[CR]sync/sync\f[R], \f[CR]sync/copy\f[R], \f[CR]sync/move\f[R],
\f[CR]operations/purge\f[R] are run with the \f[CR]_async\f[R] flag to
avoid any potential problems with the HTTP request and response timing
out.
.PP
As an alternative to \f[CR]_async\f[R], you can send the HTTP header
\f[CR]Prefer: respond\-async\f[R] (RFC 7240).
This has the same effect as \f[CR]_async = true\f[R] but additionally
returns HTTP status 202 (Accepted) instead of 200, and includes a
\f[CR]Preference\-Applied: respond\-async\f[R] response header.
The 202 status code makes it easy for clients to distinguish an async
response from a completed one without inspecting the body.
.PP
Starting a job with the \f[CR]_async\f[R] flag:
.IP
.EX
$ rclone rc \-\-json \(aq{ \(dqp1\(dq: [1,\(dq2\(dq,null,4], \(dqp2\(dq: { \(dqa\(dq:1, \(dqb\(dq:2 }, \(dq_async\(dq: true }\(aq rc/noop
{
    \(dqjobid\(dq: 2,
    \(dqexecuteId\(dq: \(dqd794c33c\-463e\-4acf\-b911\-f4b23e4f40b7\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]jobid\f[R] is a unique identifier for the job within this
rclone instance.
The \f[CR]executeId\f[R] identifies the rclone process instance and
changes after rclone restart.
Together, the pair (\f[CR]executeId\f[R], \f[CR]jobid\f[R]) uniquely
identifies a job across rclone restarts.
.PP
Query the status to see if the job has finished.
For more information on the meaning of these return parameters see the
\f[CR]job/status\f[R] call.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone rc \-\-json \(aq{ \(dqjobid\(dq:2 }\(aq job/status
{
    \(dqduration\(dq: 0.000124163,
    \(dqendTime\(dq: \(dq2018\-10\-27T11:38:07.911245881+01:00\(dq,
    \(dqerror\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
    \(dqexecuteId\(dq: \(dqd794c33c\-463e\-4acf\-b911\-f4b23e4f40b7\(dq,
    \(dqfinished\(dq: true,
    \(dqid\(dq: 2,
    \(dqoutput\(dq: {
        \(dq_async\(dq: true,
        \(dqp1\(dq: [
            1,
            \(dq2\(dq,
            null,
            4
        ],
        \(dqp2\(dq: {
            \(dqa\(dq: 1,
            \(dqb\(dq: 2
        }
    },
    \(dqstartTime\(dq: \(dq2018\-10\-27T11:38:07.911121728+01:00\(dq,
    \(dqsuccess\(dq: true
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]job/list\f[R] can be used to show running or recently completed
jobs along with their status
.IP
.EX
$ rclone rc job/list
{
    \(dqexecuteId\(dq: \(dqd794c33c\-463e\-4acf\-b911\-f4b23e4f40b7\(dq,
    \(dqfinished_ids\(dq: [
        1
    ],
    \(dqjobids\(dq: [
        1,
        2
    ],
    \(dqrunning_ids\(dq: [
        2
    ]
}
.EE
.PP
This shows: \- \f[CR]executeId\f[R] \- the current rclone instance ID
(same for all jobs, changes after restart) \- \f[CR]jobids\f[R] \- array
of all job IDs (both running and finished) \- \f[CR]running_ids\f[R] \-
array of currently running job IDs \- \f[CR]finished_ids\f[R] \- array
of finished job IDs
.SS Setting config flags with _config
If you wish to set config (the equivalent of the global flags) for the
duration of an rc call only then pass in the \f[CR]_config\f[R]
parameter.
.PP
Alternatively, you can pass config options flat at the top level of the
parameter map.
The option names are the same as their CLI flags without \f[CR]\-\-\f[R]
and with \f[CR]\-\f[R] replaced by \f[CR]_\f[R] (e.g.
\f[CR]transfers\f[R] instead of \f[CR]Transfers\f[R] inside
\f[CR]_config\f[R]).
.PP
This should be in the same format as the \f[CR]main\f[R] key returned by
options/get.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-loopback options/get blocks=main
.EE
.PP
You can see more help on these options with this command (see the
options blocks section for more info).
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-loopback options/info blocks=main
.EE
.PP
For example, if you wished to run a sync with the
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] parameter, you would pass this parameter in your
JSON blob.
.IP
.EX
\(dq_config\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]{\(dqCheckSum\(dq: \f[B]true\f[R]}
.EE
.PP
Or pass it flat at the top level:
.IP
.EX
\(dqchecksum\(dq\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
If using \f[CR]rclone rc\f[R] this could be passed as
.IP
.EX
rclone rc sync/sync ... _config=\(aq{\(dqCheckSum\(dq: true}\(aq
.EE
.PP
Or simply flat:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc sync/sync ... checksum=true
.EE
.PP
If both flat parameters and \f[CR]_config\f[R] are supplied, the
parameters in the legacy \f[CR]_config\f[R] block will take precedence.
.PP
Any config parameters you don\(aqt set will inherit the global defaults
which were set with command line flags or environment variables.
.PP
Note that it is possible to set some values as strings or integers \-
see data types for more info.
Here is an example setting the equivalent of \f[CR]\-\-buffer\-size\f[R]
in string or integer format.
.IP
.EX
\(dq_config\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]{\(dqBufferSize\(dq: \(dq42M\(dq}
\(dq_config\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]{\(dqBufferSize\(dq: 44040192}
.EE
.PP
Or flat:
.IP
.EX
\(dqbuffer_size\(dq\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq42M\(dq
\(dqbuffer_size\(dq\f[B]:\f[R] 44040192
.EE
.PP
If you wish to check the \f[CR]_config\f[R] assignment has worked
properly then calling \f[CR]options/local\f[R] will show what the value
got set to.
.SS Setting filter flags with _filter
If you wish to set filters for the duration of an rc call only then pass
in the \f[CR]_filter\f[R] parameter.
.PP
Alternatively, you can pass filter options flat at the top level of the
parameter map.
The option names are the same as their CLI flags without \f[CR]\-\-\f[R]
and with \f[CR]\-\f[R] replaced by \f[CR]_\f[R] (e.g.
\f[CR]exclude\f[R] instead of \f[CR]Exclude\f[R] inside
\f[CR]_filter\f[R]).
.PP
This should be in the same format as the \f[CR]filter\f[R] key returned
by options/get.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-loopback options/get blocks=filter
.EE
.PP
You can see more help on these options with this command (see the
options blocks section for more info).
.IP
.EX
rclone rc \-\-loopback options/info blocks=filter
.EE
.PP
For example, if you wished to run a sync with these flags
.IP
.EX
\-\-max\-size 1M \-\-max\-age 42s \-\-include \(dqa\(dq \-\-include \(dqb\(dq
.EE
.PP
you would pass this parameter in your JSON blob.
.IP
.EX
\(dq_filter\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]{\(dqMaxSize\(dq:\(dq1M\(dq, \(dqIncludeRule\(dq:[\(dqa\(dq,\(dqb\(dq], \(dqMaxAge\(dq:\(dq42s\(dq}
.EE
.PP
Or pass them flat at the top level:
.IP
.EX
\(dqmax_size\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]\(dq1M\(dq\f[B],\f[R] \(dqinclude\(dq\f[B]:\f[R][\(dqa\(dq,\(dqb\(dq]\f[B],\f[R] \(dqmax_age\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]\(dq42s\(dq
.EE
.PP
If using \f[CR]rclone rc\f[R] this could be passed as
.IP
.EX
rclone rc ... _filter=\(aq{\(dqMaxSize\(dq:\(dq1M\(dq, \(dqIncludeRule\(dq:[\(dqa\(dq,\(dqb\(dq], \(dqMaxAge\(dq:\(dq42s\(dq}\(aq
.EE
.PP
Or simply flat:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc ... max_size=1M include=\(dqa,b\(dq max_age=42s
.EE
.PP
If both flat parameters and \f[CR]_filter\f[R] are supplied, the
parameters in the legacy \f[CR]_filter\f[R] block will take precedence.
.PP
Any filter parameters you don\(aqt set will inherit the global defaults
which were set with command line flags or environment variables.
.PP
Note that it is possible to set some values as strings or integers \-
see data types for more info.
Here is an example setting the equivalent of \f[CR]\-\-buffer\-size\f[R]
in string or integer format.
.IP
.EX
\(dq_filter\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]{\(dqMinSize\(dq: \(dq42M\(dq}
\(dq_filter\(dq\f[B]:\f[R]{\(dqMinSize\(dq: 44040192}
.EE
.PP
Or flat:
.IP
.EX
\(dqmin_size\(dq\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq42M\(dq
.EE
.PP
If you wish to check the \f[CR]_filter\f[R] assignment has worked
properly then calling \f[CR]options/local\f[R] will show what the value
got set to.
.SS Assigning operations to groups with _group = value
Each rc call has its own stats group for tracking its metrics.
By default grouping is done by the composite group name from prefix
\f[CR]job/\f[R] and id of the job like so \f[CR]job/1\f[R].
.PP
If \f[CR]_group\f[R] has a value then stats for that request will be
grouped under that value.
This allows caller to group stats under their own name.
.PP
Stats for specific group can be accessed by passing \f[CR]group\f[R] to
\f[CR]core/stats\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ rclone rc \-\-json \(aq{ \(dqgroup\(dq: \(dqjob/1\(dq }\(aq core/stats
{
    \(dqspeed\(dq: 12345
    ...
}
.EE
.SS Data types
When the API returns types, these will mostly be straight forward
integer, string or boolean types.
.PP
However some of the types returned by the options/get call and taken by
the options/set calls as well as the \f[CR]vfsOpt\f[R],
\f[CR]mountOpt\f[R] and the \f[CR]_config\f[R] parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Duration\f[R] \- these are returned as an integer duration in
nanoseconds.
They may be set as an integer, or they may be set with time string, eg
\(dq5s\(dq.
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#options
options section
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Size\f[R] \- these are returned as an integer number of bytes.
They may be set as an integer or they may be set with a size suffix
string, eg \(dq10M\(dq.
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#options
options section
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.IP \(bu 2
Enumerated type (such as \f[CR]CutoffMode\f[R], \f[CR]DumpFlags\f[R],
\f[CR]LogLevel\f[R], \f[CR]VfsCacheMode\f[R] \- these will be returned
as an integer and may be set as an integer but more conveniently they
can be set as a string, eg \(dqHARD\(dq for \f[CR]CutoffMode\f[R] or
\f[CR]DEBUG\f[R] for \f[CR]LogLevel\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]BandwidthSpec\f[R] \- this will be set and returned as a string,
eg \(dq1M\(dq.
.SS Option blocks
The calls options/info (for the main config) and config/providers (for
the backend config) may be used to get information on the rclone
configuration options.
This can be used to build user interfaces for displaying and setting any
rclone option.
.PP
These consist of arrays of \f[CR]Option\f[R] blocks.
These have the following format.
Each block describes a single option.
.PP
