.TE
.PP
An example of this might be the \f[CR]\-\-log\-level\f[R] flag.
Note that the \f[CR]Name\f[R] of the option becomes the command line
flag with \f[CR]_\f[R] replaced with \f[CR]\-\f[R].
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqAdvanced\(dq: \f[B]false\f[R],
    \(dqDefault\(dq: 5,
    \(dqDefaultStr\(dq: \(dqNOTICE\(dq,
    \(dqExamples\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqHelp\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
            \(dqValue\(dq: \(dqEMERGENCY\(dq
        },
        {
            \(dqHelp\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
            \(dqValue\(dq: \(dqALERT\(dq
        },
        \f[B]...\f[R]
    ],
    \(dqExclusive\(dq: \f[B]true\f[R],
    \(dqFieldName\(dq: \(dqLogLevel\(dq,
    \(dqGroups\(dq: \(dqLogging\(dq,
    \(dqHelp\(dq: \(dqLog level DEBUG|INFO|NOTICE|ERROR\(dq,
    \(dqHide\(dq: 0,
    \(dqIsPassword\(dq: \f[B]false\f[R],
    \(dqName\(dq: \(dqlog_level\(dq,
    \(dqNoPrefix\(dq: \f[B]true\f[R],
    \(dqRequired\(dq: \f[B]true\f[R],
    \(dqSensitive\(dq: \f[B]false\f[R],
    \(dqType\(dq: \(dqLogLevel\(dq,
    \(dqValue\(dq: \f[B]null\f[R],
    \(dqValueStr\(dq: \(dqNOTICE\(dq
}\f[B],\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Note that the \f[CR]Help\f[R] may be multiple lines separated by
\f[CR]\(rsn\f[R].
The first line will always be a short sentence and this is the sentence
shown when running \f[CR]rclone help flags\f[R].
.SS Specifying remotes to work on
Remotes are specified with the \f[CR]fs=\f[R], \f[CR]srcFs=\f[R],
\f[CR]dstFs=\f[R] parameters depending on the command being used.
.PP
The parameters can be a string as per the rest of rclone, eg
\f[CR]s3:bucket/path\f[R] or \f[CR]:sftp:/my/dir\f[R].
They can also be specified as JSON blobs.
.PP
If specifying a JSON blob it should be a object mapping strings to
strings.
These values will be used to configure the remote.
There are 3 special values which may be set:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]type\f[R] \- set to \f[CR]type\f[R] to specify a remote called
\f[CR]:type:\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]_name\f[R] \- set to \f[CR]name\f[R] to specify a remote called
\f[CR]name:\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]_root\f[R] \- sets the root of the remote \- may be empty
.PP
One of \f[CR]_name\f[R] or \f[CR]type\f[R] should normally be set.
If the \f[CR]local\f[R] backend is desired then \f[CR]type\f[R] should
be set to \f[CR]local\f[R].
If \f[CR]_root\f[R] isn\(aqt specified then it defaults to the root of
the remote.
.PP
For example this JSON is equivalent to \f[CR]remote:/tmp\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dq_name\(dq: \(dqremote\(dq,
    \(dq_root\(dq: \(dq/tmp\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
And this is equivalent to \f[CR]:sftp,host=\(aqexample.com\(aq:/tmp\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqsftp\(dq,
    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqexample.com\(dq,
    \(dq_root\(dq: \(dq/tmp\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
And this is equivalent to \f[CR]/tmp/dir\f[R]
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqlocal\(dq,
    \(dq_root\(dq: \(dq/tmp/dir\(dq
}
.EE
.SS Supported commands
.SS backend/command: Runs a backend command.
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
command \- a string with the command name
.IP \(bu 2
fs \- a remote name string e.g.
\(dqdrive:\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
arg \- a list of arguments for the backend command
.IP \(bu 2
opt \- a map of string to string of options
.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
result \- result from the backend command
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc backend/command command=noop fs=. \-o echo=yes \-o blue \-a path1 \-a path2
.EE
.PP
Returns
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: {
        \(dqarg\(dq: [
            \(dqpath1\(dq,
            \(dqpath2\(dq
        ],
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqnoop\(dq,
        \(dqopt\(dq: {
            \(dqblue\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
            \(dqecho\(dq: \(dqyes\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.PP
Note that this is the direct equivalent of using this \(dqbackend\(dq
command:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend noop . \-o echo=yes \-o blue path1 path2
.EE
.PP
Note that arguments must be preceded by the \(dq\-a\(dq flag
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_backend/
backend
.UE \c
\ command for more information.
.SS cache/expire: Purge a remote from cache
Purge a remote from the cache backend.
Supports either a directory or a file.
Params: \- remote = path to remote (required) \- withData = true/false
to delete cached data (chunks) as well (optional)
.PP
Eg
.IP
.EX
rclone rc cache/expire remote=path/to/sub/folder/
rclone rc cache/expire remote=/ withData=true
.EE
.SS cache/fetch: Fetch file chunks
Ensure the specified file chunks are cached on disk.
.PP
The chunks= parameter specifies the file chunks to check.
It takes a comma separated list of array slice indices.
The slice indices are similar to Python slices: start[:end]
.PP
start is the 0 based chunk number from the beginning of the file to
fetch inclusive.
end is 0 based chunk number from the beginning of the file to fetch
exclusive.
Both values can be negative, in which case they count from the back of
the file.
The value \(dq\-5:\(dq represents the last 5 chunks of a file.
.PP
Some valid examples are: \(dq:5,\-5:\(dq \-> the first and last five
chunks \(dq0,\-2\(dq \-> the first and the second last chunk
\(dq0:10\(dq \-> the first ten chunks
.PP
Any parameter with a key that starts with \(dqfile\(dq can be used to
specify files to fetch, e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc cache/fetch chunks=0 file=hello file2=home/goodbye
.EE
.PP
File names will automatically be encrypted when the a crypt remote is
used on top of the cache.
.SS cache/stats: Get cache stats
Show statistics for the cache remote.
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS config/create: create the config for a remote.
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name \- name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
parameters \- a map of { \(dqkey\(dq: \(dqvalue\(dq } pairs
.IP \(bu 2
type \- type of the new remote
.IP \(bu 2
opt \- a dictionary of options to control the configuration
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
obscure \- declare passwords are plain and need obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noObscure \- declare passwords are already obscured and don\(aqt need
obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noOutput \- don\(aqt print anything to stdout
.IP \(bu 2
nonInteractive \- don\(aqt interact with a user, return questions
.IP \(bu 2
continue \- continue the config process with an answer
.IP \(bu 2
all \- ask all the config questions not just the post config ones
.IP \(bu 2
state \- state to restart with \- used with continue
.IP \(bu 2
result \- result to restart with \- used with continue
.RE
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_create/
config create
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/delete: Delete a remote in the config file.
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name \- name of remote to delete
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_delete/
config delete
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/dump: Dumps the config file.
Returns a JSON object: \- key: value
.PP
Where keys are remote names and values are the config parameters.
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_dump/
config dump
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/get: Get a remote in the config file.
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name \- name of remote to get
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_dump/
config dump
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/listremotes: Lists the remotes in the config file and defined in environment variables.
Returns \- remotes \- array of remote names
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_listremotes/
listremotes
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/oauthstatus: Get the status of the OAuth authentication server.
Returns the current status of the OAuth authentication server.
.PP
Returns a JSON object: \- status \- \(dqrunning\(dq or \(dqstopped\(dq
\- authUrl \- URL for the authorization (only if status is
\(dqrunning\(dq)
.PP
Eg
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqrunning\(dq,
    \(dqauthUrl\(dq: \(dqhttp://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=...\(dq
}
.EE
.SS config/oauthstop: Stop any running OAuth authentication server.
Stops the OAuth authentication server if one is running.
.PP
This can be used to recover from an interrupted OAuth flow without
restarting rclone.
If no OAuth authentication is in progress, an error is returned.
.SS config/password: password the config for a remote.
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name \- name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
parameters \- a map of { \(dqkey\(dq: \(dqvalue\(dq } pairs
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_password/
config password
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/paths: Reads the config file path and other important paths.
Returns a JSON object with the following keys:
.IP \(bu 2
config: path to config file
.IP \(bu 2
cache: path to root of cache directory
.IP \(bu 2
temp: path to root of temporary directory
.PP
Eg
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqcache\(dq: \(dq/home/USER/.cache/rclone\(dq,
    \(dqconfig\(dq: \(dq/home/USER/.rclone.conf\(dq,
    \(dqtemp\(dq: \(dq/tmp\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_paths/
config paths
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/providers: Shows how providers are configured in the config file.
Returns a JSON object: \- providers \- array of objects
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_providers/
config providers
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.PP
Note that the Options blocks are in the same format as returned by
\(dqoptions/info\(dq.
They are described in the option blocks section.
.SS config/setpath: Set the path of the config file
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
path \- path to the config file to use
.SS config/unlock: Unlock the config file.
Unlocks the config file if it is locked.
.PP
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\(aqconfigPassword\(aq \- password to unlock the config file
.PP
A good idea is to disable AskPassword before making this call
.SS config/unset: Unset keys in a remote in the config file.
Parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name \- name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
keys \- a list of key names to remove
.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
removed \- a list of the keys that were actually removed
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_unset/
config unset
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS config/update: update the config for a remote.
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
name \- name of remote
.IP \(bu 2
parameters \- a map of { \(dqkey\(dq: \(dqvalue\(dq } pairs
.IP \(bu 2
opt \- a dictionary of options to control the configuration
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
obscure \- declare passwords are plain and need obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noObscure \- declare passwords are already obscured and don\(aqt need
obscuring
.IP \(bu 2
noOutput \- don\(aqt print anything to stdout
.IP \(bu 2
nonInteractive \- don\(aqt interact with a user, return questions
.IP \(bu 2
continue \- continue the config process with an answer
.IP \(bu 2
all \- ask all the config questions not just the post config ones
.IP \(bu 2
state \- state to restart with \- used with continue
.IP \(bu 2
result \- result to restart with \- used with continue
.RE
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_update/
config update
.UE \c
\ command for more information on the above.
.SS core/bwlimit: Set the bandwidth limit.
This sets the bandwidth limit to the string passed in.
This should be a single bandwidth limit entry or a pair of
upload:download bandwidth.
.PP
Eg
.IP
.EX
rclone rc core/bwlimit rate=off
{
    \(dqbytesPerSecond\(dq: \-1,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondTx\(dq: \-1,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondRx\(dq: \-1,
    \(dqrate\(dq: \(dqoff\(dq
}
rclone rc core/bwlimit rate=1M
{
    \(dqbytesPerSecond\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondTx\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondRx\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqrate\(dq: \(dq1M\(dq
}
rclone rc core/bwlimit rate=1M:100k
{
    \(dqbytesPerSecond\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondTx\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondRx\(dq: 131072,
    \(dqrate\(dq: \(dq1M\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
If the rate parameter is not supplied then the bandwidth is queried
.IP
.EX
rclone rc core/bwlimit
{
    \(dqbytesPerSecond\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondTx\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqbytesPerSecondRx\(dq: 1048576,
    \(dqrate\(dq: \(dq1M\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
The format of the parameter is exactly the same as passed to \-\-bwlimit
except only one bandwidth may be specified.
.PP
In either case \(dqrate\(dq is returned as a human\-readable string, and
\(dqbytesPerSecond\(dq is returned as a number.
.SS core/command: Run a rclone terminal command over rc.
This takes the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
command \- a string with the command name.
.IP \(bu 2
arg \- a list of arguments for the backend command.
.IP \(bu 2
opt \- a map of string to string of options.
.IP \(bu 2
returnType \- one of (\(dqCOMBINED_OUTPUT\(dq, \(dqSTREAM\(dq,
\(dqSTREAM_ONLY_STDOUT\(dq, \(dqSTREAM_ONLY_STDERR\(dq).
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Defaults to \(dqCOMBINED_OUTPUT\(dq if not set.
.IP \(bu 2
The STREAM returnTypes will write the output to the body of the HTTP
message.
.IP \(bu 2
The COMBINED_OUTPUT will write the output to the \(dqresult\(dq
parameter.
.RE
.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
result \- result from the backend command.
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Only set when using returnType \(dqCOMBINED_OUTPUT\(dq.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
error \- set if rclone exits with an error code.
.IP \(bu 2
returnType \- one of (\(dqCOMBINED_OUTPUT\(dq, \(dqSTREAM\(dq,
\(dqSTREAM_ONLY_STDOUT\(dq, \(dqSTREAM_ONLY_STDERR\(dq).
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc core/command command=ls \-a mydrive:/ \-o max\-depth=1
rclone rc core/command \-a ls \-a mydrive:/ \-o max\-depth=1
.EE
.PP
Returns:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqerror\(dq: false,
    \(dqresult\(dq: \(dq<Raw command line output>\(dq
}

OR
{
    \(dqerror\(dq: true,
    \(dqresult\(dq: \(dq<Raw command line output>\(dq
}
.EE
.SS core/disks: List the local disks
This does not take any parameters
.PP
This call is for rclone GUI programs to enumerate local disks and
important directories for doing transfers to and from.
The list returned will include the root directory and the user\(aqs home
directory and any mounted disks.
The returned items should be usable directly as remotes.
.PP
Returns:
.IP \(bu 2
disks
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
This is an array of strings of local disk names
.RE
.SS core/du: Returns disk usage of a locally attached disk.
This returns the disk usage for the local directory passed in as dir.
.PP
If the directory is not passed in, it defaults to the directory pointed
to by \-\-cache\-dir.
.IP \(bu 2
dir \- string (optional)
.PP
Returns:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqdir\(dq: \(dq/\(dq,
    \(dqinfo\(dq: {
        \(dqAvailable\(dq: 361769115648,
        \(dqFree\(dq: 361785892864,
        \(dqTotal\(dq: 982141468672
    }
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS core/gc: Runs a garbage collection.
This tells the go runtime to do a garbage collection run.
It isn\(aqt necessary to call this normally, but it can be useful for
debugging memory problems.
.SS core/group\-list: Returns list of stats.
This returns list of stats groups currently in memory.
.PP
Returns the following values:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqgroups\(dq:  an array of group names:
        [
            \(dqgroup1\(dq,
            \(dqgroup2\(dq,
            ...
        ]
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS core/memstats: Returns the memory statistics
This returns the memory statistics of the running program.
What the values mean are explained in the go docs:
https://golang.org/pkg/runtime/#MemStats
.PP
The most interesting values for most people are:
.IP \(bu 2
HeapAlloc \- this is the amount of memory rclone is actually using
.IP \(bu 2
HeapSys \- this is the amount of memory rclone has obtained from the OS
.IP \(bu 2
Sys \- this is the total amount of memory requested from the OS
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
It is virtual memory so may include unused memory
.RE
.SS core/obscure: Obscures a string passed in.
Pass a clear string and rclone will obscure it for the config file: \-
clear \- string
.PP
Returns: \- obscured \- string
.SS core/pid: Return PID of current process
This returns PID of current process.
Useful for stopping rclone process.
.SS core/quit: Terminates the app.
(Optional) Pass an exit code to be used for terminating the app: \-
exitCode \- int
.SS core/stats: Returns stats about current transfers.
This returns all available stats:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc core/stats
.EE
.PP
If group is not provided then summed up stats for all groups will be
returned.
.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group \- name of the stats group (string, optional)
.IP \(bu 2
short \- if true will not return the transferring and checking arrays
(boolean, optional)
.PP
Returns the following values:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqbytes\(dq: total transferred bytes since the start of the group,
    \(dqchecks\(dq: number of files checked,
    \(dqdeletedDirs\(dq: number of directories deleted,
    \(dqdeletes\(dq : number of files deleted,
    \(dqelapsedTime\(dq: time in floating point seconds since rclone was started,
    \(dqerrors\(dq: number of errors,
    \(dqeta\(dq: estimated time in seconds until the group completes,
    \(dqfatalError\(dq: boolean whether there has been at least one fatal error,
    \(dqlastError\(dq: last error string,
    \(dqrenames\(dq : number of files renamed,
    \(dqlisted\(dq : number of directory entries listed,
    \(dqretryError\(dq: boolean showing whether there has been at least one non\-NoRetryError,
        \(dqserverSideCopies\(dq: number of server side copies done,
        \(dqserverSideCopyBytes\(dq: number bytes server side copied,
        \(dqserverSideMoves\(dq: number of server side moves done,
        \(dqserverSideMoveBytes\(dq: number bytes server side moved,
    \(dqspeed\(dq: average speed in bytes per second since start of the group,
    \(dqtotalBytes\(dq: total number of bytes in the group,
    \(dqtotalChecks\(dq: total number of checks in the group,
    \(dqtotalTransfers\(dq: total number of transfers in the group,
    \(dqtransferTime\(dq : total time spent on running jobs,
    \(dqtransfers\(dq: number of transferred files,
    \(dqtransferring\(dq: an array of currently active file transfers:
        [
            {
                \(dqbytes\(dq: total transferred bytes for this file,
                \(dqeta\(dq: estimated time in seconds until file transfer completion
                \(dqname\(dq: name of the file,
                \(dqpercentage\(dq: progress of the file transfer in percent,
                \(dqspeed\(dq: average speed over the whole transfer in bytes per second,
                \(dqspeedAvg\(dq: current speed in bytes per second as an exponentially weighted moving average,
                \(dqsize\(dq: size of the file in bytes
            }
        ],
    \(dqchecking\(dq: an array of names of currently active file checks
        []
}
.EE
.PP
Values for \(dqtransferring\(dq, \(dqchecking\(dq and \(dqlastError\(dq
are only assigned if data is available.
The value for \(dqeta\(dq is null if an eta cannot be determined.
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS core/stats\-delete: Delete stats group.
This deletes entire stats group.
.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group \- name of the stats group (string)
.SS core/stats\-reset: Reset stats.
This clears counters, errors and finished transfers for all stats or
specific stats group if group is provided.
.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group \- name of the stats group (string)
.SS core/transferred: Returns stats about completed transfers.
This returns stats about completed transfers:
.IP
.EX
rclone rc core/transferred
.EE
.PP
If group is not provided then completed transfers for all groups will be
returned.
.PP
Note only the last 100 completed transfers are returned.
.PP
Parameters
.IP \(bu 2
group \- name of the stats group (string)
.PP
Returns the following values:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqtransferred\(dq:  an array of completed transfers (including failed ones):
        [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: name of the file,
                \(dqsize\(dq: size of the file in bytes,
                \(dqbytes\(dq: total transferred bytes for this file,
                \(dqchecked\(dq: if the transfer is only checked (skipped, deleted),
                \(dqwhat\(dq: the purpose of the transfer (transferring, deleting, checking, importing, hashing, merging, listing, moving, renaming),
                \(dqtimestamp\(dq: integer representing millisecond unix epoch,
                \(dqerror\(dq: string description of the error (empty if successful),
                \(dqjobid\(dq: id of the job that this transfer belongs to
            }
        ]
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS core/version: Shows the current version of rclone, Go and the OS.
This shows the current versions of rclone, Go and the OS:
.IP \(bu 2
version \- rclone version, e.g.
\(dqv1.71.2\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
decomposed \- version number as [major, minor, patch]
.IP \(bu 2
isGit \- boolean \- true if this was compiled from the git version
.IP \(bu 2
isBeta \- boolean \- true if this is a beta version
.IP \(bu 2
os \- OS in use as according to Go GOOS (e.g.
\(dqlinux\(dq)
.IP \(bu 2
osKernel \- OS Kernel version (e.g.
\(dq6.8.0\-86\-generic (x86_64)\(dq)
.IP \(bu 2
osVersion \- OS Version (e.g.
\(dqubuntu 24.04 (64 bit)\(dq)
.IP \(bu 2
