.SS vfs/forget: Forget files or directories in the directory cache.
This forgets the paths in the directory cache causing them to be
re\-read from the remote when needed.
.PP
If no paths are passed in then it will forget all the paths in the
directory cache.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc vfs/forget
.EE
.PP
Otherwise pass files or dirs in as file=path or dir=path.
Any parameter key starting with file will forget that file and any
starting with dir will forget that dir, e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc vfs/forget file=hello file2=goodbye dir=home/junk
.EE
.PP
This command takes an \(dqfs\(dq parameter.
If this parameter is not supplied and if there is only one VFS in use
then that VFS will be used.
If there is more than one VFS in use then the \(dqfs\(dq parameter must
be supplied.
.SS vfs/list: List active VFSes.
This lists the active VFSes.
.PP
It returns a list under the key \(dqvfses\(dq where the values are the
VFS names that could be passed to the other VFS commands in the
\(dqfs\(dq parameter.
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS vfs/poll\-interval: Get the status or update the value of the poll\-interval option.
Without any parameter given this returns the current status of the
poll\-interval setting.
.PP
When the interval=duration parameter is set, the poll\-interval value is
updated and the polling function is notified.
Setting interval=0 disables poll\-interval.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc vfs/poll\-interval interval=5m
.EE
.PP
The timeout=duration parameter can be used to specify a time to wait for
the current poll function to apply the new value.
If timeout is less or equal 0, which is the default, wait indefinitely.
.PP
The new poll\-interval value will only be active when the timeout is not
reached.
.PP
If poll\-interval is updated or disabled temporarily, some changes might
not get picked up by the polling function, depending on the used remote.
.PP
This command takes an \(dqfs\(dq parameter.
If this parameter is not supplied and if there is only one VFS in use
then that VFS will be used.
If there is more than one VFS in use then the \(dqfs\(dq parameter must
be supplied.
.SS vfs/queue: Queue info for a VFS.
This returns info about the upload queue for the selected VFS.
.PP
This is only useful if \f[CR]\-\-vfs\-cache\-mode\f[R] > off.
If you call it when the \f[CR]\-\-vfs\-cache\-mode\f[R] is off, it will
return an empty result.
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqqueue\(dq: // an array of files queued for upload
    [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq:      \(dqfile\(dq,   // string: name (full path) of the file,
            \(dqid\(dq:        123,      // integer: id of this item in the queue,
            \(dqsize\(dq:      79,       // integer: size of the file in bytes
            \(dqexpiry\(dq:    1.5       // float: time until file is eligible for transfer, lowest goes first
            \(dqtries\(dq:     1,        // integer: number of times we have tried to upload
            \(dqdelay\(dq:     5.0,      // float: seconds between upload attempts
            \(dquploading\(dq: false,    // boolean: true if item is being uploaded
        },
   ],
}
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]expiry\f[R] time is the time until the file is eligible for
being uploaded in floating point seconds.
This may go negative.
As rclone only transfers \f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] files at once, only
the lowest \f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] expiry times will have
\f[CR]uploading\f[R] as \f[CR]true\f[R].
So there may be files with negative expiry times for which
\f[CR]uploading\f[R] is \f[CR]false\f[R].
.PP
This command takes an \(dqfs\(dq parameter.
If this parameter is not supplied and if there is only one VFS in use
then that VFS will be used.
If there is more than one VFS in use then the \(dqfs\(dq parameter must
be supplied.
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS vfs/queue\-set\-expiry: Set the expiry time for an item queued for upload.
Use this to adjust the \f[CR]expiry\f[R] time for an item in the upload
queue.
You will need to read the \f[CR]id\f[R] of the item using
\f[CR]vfs/queue\f[R] before using this call.
.PP
You can then set \f[CR]expiry\f[R] to a floating point number of seconds
from now when the item is eligible for upload.
If you want the item to be uploaded as soon as possible then set it to a
large negative number (eg \-1000000000).
If you want the upload of the item to be delayed for a long time then
set it to a large positive number.
.PP
Setting the \f[CR]expiry\f[R] of an item which has already has started
uploading will have no effect \- the item will carry on being uploaded.
.PP
This will return an error if called with \f[CR]\-\-vfs\-cache\-mode\f[R]
off or if the \f[CR]id\f[R] passed is not found.
.PP
This takes the following parameters
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]fs\f[R] \- select the VFS in use (optional)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]id\f[R] \- a numeric ID as returned from \f[CR]vfs/queue\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]expiry\f[R] \- a new expiry time as floating point seconds
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]relative\f[R] \- if set, expiry is to be treated as relative to
the current expiry (optional, boolean)
.PP
This returns an empty result on success, or an error.
.PP
This command takes an \(dqfs\(dq parameter.
If this parameter is not supplied and if there is only one VFS in use
then that VFS will be used.
If there is more than one VFS in use then the \(dqfs\(dq parameter must
be supplied.
.SS vfs/refresh: Refresh the directory cache.
This reads the directories for the specified paths and freshens the
directory cache.
.PP
If no paths are passed in then it will refresh the root directory.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc vfs/refresh
.EE
.PP
Otherwise pass directories in as dir=path.
Any parameter key starting with dir will refresh that directory, e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone rc vfs/refresh dir=home/junk dir2=data/misc
.EE
.PP
If the parameter recursive=true is given the whole directory tree will
get refreshed.
This refresh will use \-\-fast\-list if enabled.
.PP
This command takes an \(dqfs\(dq parameter.
If this parameter is not supplied and if there is only one VFS in use
then that VFS will be used.
If there is more than one VFS in use then the \(dqfs\(dq parameter must
be supplied.
.SS vfs/stats: Stats for a VFS.
This returns stats for the selected VFS.
.IP
.EX
{
    // Status of the disk cache \- only present if \-\-vfs\-cache\-mode > off
    \(dqdiskCache\(dq: {
        \(dqbytesUsed\(dq: 0,
        \(dqerroredFiles\(dq: 0,
        \(dqfiles\(dq: 0,
        \(dqhashType\(dq: 1,
        \(dqoutOfSpace\(dq: false,
        \(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/home/user/.cache/rclone/vfs/local/mnt/a\(dq,
        \(dqpathMeta\(dq: \(dq/home/user/.cache/rclone/vfsMeta/local/mnt/a\(dq,
        \(dquploadsInProgress\(dq: 0,
        \(dquploadsQueued\(dq: 0
    },
    \(dqfs\(dq: \(dq/mnt/a\(dq,
    \(dqinUse\(dq: 1,
    // Status of the in memory metadata cache
    \(dqmetadataCache\(dq: {
        \(dqdirs\(dq: 1,
        \(dqfiles\(dq: 0
    },
    // Options as returned by options/get
    \(dqopt\(dq: {
        \(dqCacheMaxAge\(dq: 3600000000000,
        // ...
        \(dqWriteWait\(dq: 1000000000
    }
}
.EE
.PP
This command takes an \(dqfs\(dq parameter.
If this parameter is not supplied and if there is only one VFS in use
then that VFS will be used.
If there is more than one VFS in use then the \(dqfs\(dq parameter must
be supplied.
.PP
\f[B]Authentication is not required for this call.\f[R]
.SS Accessing the remote control via HTTP
Rclone implements a simple HTTP based protocol.
.PP
Each endpoint takes an JSON object and returns a JSON object or an
error.
The JSON objects are essentially a map of string names to values.
.PP
All calls must made using POST.
.PP
The input objects can be supplied using URL parameters, POST parameters
or by supplying \(dqContent\-Type: application/json\(dq and a JSON blob
in the body.
There are examples of these below using \f[CR]curl\f[R].
.PP
The response will be a JSON blob in the body of the response.
This is formatted to be reasonably human\-readable.
.SS Error returns
If an error occurs then there will be an HTTP error status (e.g.
500) and the body of the response will contain a JSON encoded error
object, e.g.
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqerror\(dq: \(dqExpecting string value for key \(rs\(dqremote\(rs\(dq (was float64)\(dq,
    \(dqinput\(dq: {
        \(dqfs\(dq: \(dq/tmp\(dq,
        \(dqremote\(dq: 3
    },
    \(dqstatus\(dq: 400,
    \(dqpath\(dq: \(dqoperations/rmdir\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
The keys in the error response are:
.IP \(bu 2
error \- error string
.IP \(bu 2
input \- the input parameters to the call
.IP \(bu 2
status \- the HTTP status code
.IP \(bu 2
path \- the path of the call
.SS CORS
The server implements basic CORS support when
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-allow\-origin\f[R] is set.
Browser CORS preflight OPTIONS requests are then answered by rclone
without authentication with the \f[CR]Access\-Control\-*\f[R] headers.
Without \f[CR]\-\-rc\-allow\-origin\f[R] no CORS headers are sent and a
preflight request is authenticated like any other request.
.SS Using POST with URL parameters only
.IP
.EX
curl \-X POST \(aqhttp://localhost:5572/rc/noop?potato=1&sausage=2\(aq
.EE
.PP
Response
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqpotato\(dq: \(dq1\(dq,
    \(dqsausage\(dq: \(dq2\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
Here is what an error response looks like:
.IP
.EX
curl \-X POST \(aqhttp://localhost:5572/rc/error?potato=1&sausage=2\(aq
.EE
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqerror\(dq: \(dqarbitrary error on input map[potato:1 sausage:2]\(dq,
    \(dqinput\(dq: {
        \(dqpotato\(dq: \(dq1\(dq,
        \(dqsausage\(dq: \(dq2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.PP
Note that curl doesn\(aqt return errors to the shell unless you use the
\f[CR]\-f\f[R] option
.IP
.EX
$ curl \-f \-X POST \(aqhttp://localhost:5572/rc/error?potato=1&sausage=2\(aq
curl: (22) The requested URL returned error: 400 Bad Request
$ echo $?
22
.EE
.SS Using POST with a form
.IP
.EX
curl \-\-data \(dqpotato=1\(dq \-\-data \(dqsausage=2\(dq http://localhost:5572/rc/noop
.EE
.PP
Response
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqpotato\(dq: \(dq1\(dq,
    \(dqsausage\(dq: \(dq2\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
Note that you can combine these with URL parameters too with the POST
parameters taking precedence.
.IP
.EX
curl \-\-data \(dqpotato=1\(dq \-\-data \(dqsausage=2\(dq \(dqhttp://localhost:5572/rc/noop?rutabaga=3&sausage=4\(dq
.EE
.PP
Response
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqpotato\(dq: \(dq1\(dq,
    \(dqrutabaga\(dq: \(dq3\(dq,
    \(dqsausage\(dq: \(dq4\(dq
}
.EE
.SS Using POST with a JSON blob
.IP
.EX
curl \-H \(dqContent\-Type: application/json\(dq \-X POST \-d \(aq{\(dqpotato\(dq:2,\(dqsausage\(dq:1}\(aq http://localhost:5572/rc/noop
.EE
.PP
response
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dqxyz\(dq,
    \(dqusername\(dq: \(dqxyz\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
This can be combined with URL parameters too if required.
The JSON blob takes precedence.
.IP
.EX
curl \-H \(dqContent\-Type: application/json\(dq \-X POST \-d \(aq{\(dqpotato\(dq:2,\(dqsausage\(dq:1}\(aq \(aqhttp://localhost:5572/rc/noop?rutabaga=3&potato=4\(aq
.EE
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqpotato\(dq: 2,
    \(dqrutabaga\(dq: \(dq3\(dq,
    \(dqsausage\(dq: 1
}
.EE
.SS Debugging rclone with pprof
If you use the \f[CR]\-\-rc\f[R] flag this will also enable the use of
the go profiling tools on the same port.
.PP
The profiling endpoints follow the same authentication rules as the rest
of the rc: they are only served if authentication has been set up
(\f[CR]\-\-rc\-user\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-rc\-pass\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-htpasswd\f[R]) or the \f[CR]\-\-rc\-no\-auth\f[R] flag is
in use.
For debugging on the default localhost port the easiest thing is to use
\f[CR]\-\-rc \-\-rc\-no\-auth\f[R] (but see the warning about using
\f[CR]\-\-rc\-no\-auth\f[R] on a non\-loopback bind).
.PP
To use these, first \c
.UR https://golang.org/doc/install
install go
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Debugging memory use
To profile rclone\(aqs memory use you can run:
.IP
.EX
go tool pprof \-web http://localhost:5572/debug/pprof/heap
.EE
.PP
This should open a page in your browser showing what is using what
memory.
.PP
You can also use the \f[CR]\-text\f[R] flag to produce a textual summary
.IP
.EX
$ go tool pprof \-text http://localhost:5572/debug/pprof/heap
