.PP
¹ Encoding from NUL 0x00 to ␀ is always implicit except when using Raw.
It was previously incorrectly documented as disabling encoding, and to
maintain backward compatibility, its behavior has not been changed.
.SS Encoding example: FTP
To take a specific example, the FTP backend\(aqs default encoding is
.IP
.EX
\-\-ftp\-encoding \(dqSlash,Del,Ctl,RightSpace,Dot\(dq
.EE
.PP
However, let\(aqs say the FTP server is running on Windows and can\(aqt
have any of the invalid Windows characters in file names.
You are backing up Linux servers to this FTP server which do have those
characters in file names.
So you would add the Windows set which are
.IP
.EX
Slash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Colon,Question,Asterisk,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot
.EE
.PP
to the existing ones, giving:
.IP
.EX
Slash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Colon,Question,Asterisk,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot,Del,RightSpace
.EE
.PP
This can be specified using the \f[CR]\-\-ftp\-encoding\f[R] flag or
using an \f[CR]encoding\f[R] parameter in the config file.
.SS Encoding example: Windows
As another example, take a Windows system where there is a file with
name \f[CR]Test：1.jpg\f[R], where \f[CR]：\f[R] is the Unicode
fullwidth colon symbol.
When using rclone to copy this to a remote which supports \f[CR]:\f[R],
the regular (halfwidth) colon (such as Google Drive), you will notice
that the file gets renamed to \f[CR]Test:1.jpg\f[R].
.PP
To avoid this you can change the set of characters rclone should convert
for the local filesystem, using command\-line argument
\f[CR]\-\-local\-encoding\f[R].
Rclone\(aqs default behavior on Windows corresponds to
.IP
.EX
\-\-local\-encoding \(dqSlash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Colon,Question,Asterisk,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot\(dq
.EE
.PP
If you want to use fullwidth characters \f[CR]：\f[R], \f[CR]＊\f[R] and
\f[CR]？\f[R] in your filenames without rclone changing them when
uploading to a remote, then set the same as the default value but
without \f[CR]Colon,Question,Asterisk\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\-\-local\-encoding \(dqSlash,LtGt,DoubleQuote,Pipe,BackSlash,Ctl,RightSpace,RightPeriod,InvalidUtf8,Dot\(dq
.EE
.PP
Alternatively, you can disable the conversion of any characters with
\f[CR]\-\-local\-encoding Raw\f[R].
.PP
Instead of using command\-line argument \f[CR]\-\-local\-encoding\f[R],
you may also set it as \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#environment-variables
environment variable
.UE \c
\ \f[CR]RCLONE_LOCAL_ENCODING\f[R], or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#configure
configure
.UE \c
\ a remote of type \f[CR]local\f[R] in your config, and set the
\f[CR]encoding\f[R] option there.
.PP
The risk by doing this is that if you have a filename with the regular
(halfwidth) \f[CR]:\f[R], \f[CR]*\f[R] and \f[CR]?\f[R] in your cloud
storage, and you try to download it to your Windows filesystem, this
will fail.
These characters are not valid in filenames on Windows, and you have
told rclone not to work around this by converting them to valid
fullwidth variants.
.SS MIME Type
MIME types (also known as media types) classify types of documents using
a simple text classification, e.g.
\f[CR]text/html\f[R] or \f[CR]application/pdf\f[R].
.PP
Some cloud storage systems support reading (\f[CR]R\f[R]) the MIME type
of objects and some support writing (\f[CR]W\f[R]) the MIME type of
objects.
.PP
The MIME type can be important if you are serving files directly to HTTP
from the storage system.
.PP
If you are copying from a remote which supports reading (\f[CR]R\f[R])
to a remote which supports writing (\f[CR]W\f[R]) then rclone will
preserve the MIME types.
Otherwise they will be guessed from the extension, or the remote itself
may assign the MIME type.
.SS Metadata
Backends may or may support reading or writing metadata.
They may support reading and writing system metadata (metadata intrinsic
to that backend) and/or user metadata (general purpose metadata).
.PP
The levels of metadata support are
