.SH Docker Volume Plugin
.SS Introduction
Docker 1.9 has added support for creating \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/storage/volumes/
named volumes
.UE \c
\ via \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/volume_create/
command\-line interface
.UE \c
\ and mounting them in containers as a way to share data between them.
Since Docker 1.10 you can create named volumes with \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/compose/
Docker Compose
.UE \c
\ by descriptions in \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/compose-file-v2/#volume-configuration-reference
docker\-compose.yml
.UE \c
\ files for use by container groups on a single host.
As of Docker 1.12 volumes are supported by \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/key-concepts/
Docker Swarm
.UE \c
\ included with Docker Engine and created from descriptions in \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/compose-file-v3/#volume-configuration-reference
swarm compose v3
.UE \c
\ files for use with \f[I]swarm stacks\f[R] across multiple cluster
nodes.
.PP
\c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/extend/plugins_volume/
Docker Volume Plugins
.UE \c
\ augment the default \f[CR]local\f[R] volume driver included in Docker
with stateful volumes shared across containers and hosts.
Unlike local volumes, your data will \f[I]not\f[R] be deleted when such
volume is removed.
Plugins can run managed by the docker daemon, as a native system service
(under systemd, \f[I]sysv\f[R] or \f[I]upstart\f[R]) or as a standalone
executable.
Rclone can run as docker volume plugin in all these modes.
It interacts with the local docker daemon via \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/extend/plugin_api/
plugin API
.UE \c
\ and handles mounting of remote file systems into docker containers so
it must run on the same host as the docker daemon or on every Swarm
node.
.SS Getting started
In the first example we will use the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/sftp/
SFTP
.UE \c
\ rclone volume with Docker engine on a standalone Ubuntu machine.
.PP
Start from \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/
installing Docker
.UE \c
\ on the host.
.PP
The \f[I]FUSE\f[R] driver is a prerequisite for rclone mounting and
should be installed on host:
.IP
.EX
sudo apt\-get \-y install fuse3
.EE
.PP
Create two directories required by rclone docker plugin:
.IP
.EX
sudo mkdir \-p /var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/config
sudo mkdir \-p /var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/cache
.EE
.PP
Install the managed rclone docker plugin for your architecture (here
\f[CR]amd64\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
docker plugin install rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:amd64 args=\(dq\-v\(dq \-\-alias rclone \-\-grant\-all\-permissions
docker plugin list
.EE
.PP
Create your \c
.UR https://rclone.org/sftp/#standard-options
SFTP volume
.UE \c
:
.IP
.EX
docker volume create firstvolume \-d rclone \-o type=sftp \-o sftp\-host=_hostname_ \-o sftp\-user=_username_ \-o sftp\-pass=_password_ \-o allow\-other=true
.EE
.PP
Note that since all options are static, you don\(aqt even have to run
\f[CR]rclone config\f[R] or create the \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] file (but
the \f[CR]config\f[R] directory should still be present).
In the simplest case you can use \f[CR]localhost\f[R] as
\f[I]hostname\f[R] and your SSH credentials as \f[I]username\f[R] and
\f[I]password\f[R].
You can also change the remote path to your home directory on the host,
for example \f[CR]\-o path=/home/username\f[R].
.PP
Time to create a test container and mount the volume into it:
.IP
.EX
docker run \-\-rm \-it \-v firstvolume:/mnt \-\-workdir /mnt ubuntu:latest bash
.EE
.PP
If all goes well, you will enter the new container and change right to
the mounted SFTP remote.
You can type \f[CR]ls\f[R] to list the mounted directory or otherwise
play with it.
Type \f[CR]exit\f[R] when you are done.
The container will stop but the volume will stay, ready to be reused.
When it\(aqs not needed anymore, remove it:
.IP
.EX
docker volume list
docker volume remove firstvolume
.EE
.PP
Now let us try \f[B]something more elaborate\f[R]: \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/
Google Drive
.UE \c
\ volume on multi\-node Docker Swarm.
.PP
You should start from installing Docker and FUSE, creating plugin
directories and installing rclone plugin on \f[I]every\f[R] swarm node.
Then \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/swarm/swarm-mode/
setup the Swarm
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Google Drive volumes need an access token which can be setup via web
browser and will be periodically renewed by rclone.
The managed plugin cannot run a browser so we will use a technique
similar to the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/remote_setup/
rclone setup on a headless box
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Run \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_config_create/
rclone config
.UE \c
\ on \f[I]another\f[R] machine equipped with \f[I]web browser\f[R] and
graphical user interface.
Create the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#standard-options
Google Drive remote
.UE \c
\&.
When done, transfer the resulting \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] to the Swarm
cluster and save as
\f[CR]/var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/config/rclone.conf\f[R] on
\f[I]every\f[R] node.
By default this location is accessible only to the root user so you will
need appropriate privileges.
The resulting config will look like this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][gdrive]\f[R]
type = drive
scope = drive
drive_id = 1234567...
root_folder_id = 0Abcd...
token = {\(dqaccess_token\(dq:...}
.EE
.PP
Now create the file named \f[CR]example.yml\f[R] with a swarm stack
description like this:
.IP
.EX
version\f[B]:\f[R] \(aq3\(aq
services\f[B]:\f[R]
  heimdall\f[B]:\f[R]
    image\f[B]:\f[R] linuxserver/heimdall:latest
    ports\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B][\f[R]8080:80\f[B]]\f[R]
    volumes\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B][\f[R]configdata:/config\f[B]]\f[R]
volumes\f[B]:\f[R]
  configdata\f[B]:\f[R]
    driver\f[B]:\f[R] rclone
    driver_opts\f[B]:\f[R]
      remote\f[B]:\f[R] \(aqgdrive:heimdall\(aq
      allow_other\f[B]:\f[R] \(aqtrue\(aq
      vfs_cache_mode\f[B]:\f[R] full
      poll_interval\f[B]:\f[R] 0
.EE
.PP
and run the stack:
.IP
.EX
docker stack deploy example \-c ./example.yml
.EE
.PP
After a few seconds docker will spread the parsed stack description over
cluster, create the \f[CR]example_heimdall\f[R] service on port
\f[I]8080\f[R], run service containers on one or more cluster nodes and
request the \f[CR]example_configdata\f[R] volume from rclone plugins on
the node hosts.
You can use the following commands to confirm results:
.IP
.EX
docker service ls
docker service ps example_heimdall
docker volume ls
.EE
.PP
Point your browser to \f[CR]http://cluster.host.address:8080\f[R] and
play with the service.
Stop it with \f[CR]docker stack remove example\f[R] when you are done.
Note that the \f[CR]example_configdata\f[R] volume(s) created on demand
at the cluster nodes will not be automatically removed together with the
stack but stay for future reuse.
You can remove them manually by invoking the
\f[CR]docker volume remove example_configdata\f[R] command on every
node.
.SS Creating Volumes via CLI
Volumes can be created with \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/volume_create/
docker volume create
.UE \c
\&.
Here are a few examples:
.IP
.EX
docker volume create vol1 \-d rclone \-o remote=storj: \-o vfs\-cache\-mode=full
docker volume create vol2 \-d rclone \-o remote=:storj,access_grant=xxx:heimdall
docker volume create vol3 \-d rclone \-o type=storj \-o path=heimdall \-o storj\-access\-grant=xxx \-o poll\-interval=0
.EE
.PP
Note the \f[CR]\-d rclone\f[R] flag that tells docker to request volume
from the rclone driver.
This works even if you installed managed driver by its full name
\f[CR]rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone\f[R] because you provided the
\f[CR]\-\-alias rclone\f[R] option.
.PP
Volumes can be inspected as follows:
.IP
.EX
docker volume list
docker volume inspect vol1
.EE
.SS Volume Configuration
Rclone flags and volume options are set via the \f[CR]\-o\f[R] flag to
the \f[CR]docker volume create\f[R] command.
They include backend\-specific parameters as well as mount and
\f[I]VFS\f[R] options.
Also there are a few special \f[CR]\-o\f[R] options: \f[CR]remote\f[R],
\f[CR]fs\f[R], \f[CR]type\f[R], \f[CR]path\f[R], \f[CR]mount\-type\f[R]
and \f[CR]persist\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]remote\f[R] determines an existing remote name from the config
file, with trailing colon and optionally with a remote path.
See the full syntax in the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#syntax-of-remote-paths
rclone documentation
.UE \c
\&.
This option can be aliased as \f[CR]fs\f[R] to prevent confusion with
the \f[I]remote\f[R] parameter of such backends as \f[I]crypt\f[R] or
\f[I]alias\f[R].
.PP
The \f[CR]remote=:backend:dir/subdir\f[R] syntax can be used to create
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#backend-path-to-dir
on\-the\-fly (config\-less) remotes
.UE \c
, while the \f[CR]type\f[R] and \f[CR]path\f[R] options provide a
simpler alternative for this.
Using two split options
.IP
.EX
\-o type=backend \-o path=dir/subdir
.EE
.PP
is equivalent to the combined syntax
.IP
.EX
\-o remote=:backend:dir/subdir
.EE
.PP
but is arguably easier to parameterize in scripts.
The \f[CR]path\f[R] part is optional.
.PP
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_serve_docker/#options
Mount and VFS options
.UE \c
\ as well as \c
.UR https://rclone.org/flags/#backend
backend parameters
.UE \c
\ are named like their twin command\-line flags without the
\f[CR]\-\-\f[R] CLI prefix.
Optionally you can use underscores instead of dashes in option names.
For example, \f[CR]\-\-vfs\-cache\-mode full\f[R] becomes
\f[CR]\-o vfs\-cache\-mode=full\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-o vfs_cache_mode=full\f[R].
Boolean CLI flags without value will gain the \f[CR]true\f[R] value,
e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-allow\-other\f[R] becomes \f[CR]\-o allow\-other=true\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-o allow_other=true\f[R].
.PP
Please note that you can provide parameters only for the backend
immediately referenced by the backend type of mounted \f[CR]remote\f[R].
If this is a wrapping backend like \f[I]alias, chunker or crypt\f[R],
you cannot provide options for the referred to remote or backend.
This limitation is imposed by the rclone connection string parser.
The only workaround is to feed plugin with \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] or
configure plugin arguments (see below).
.SS Special Volume Options
\f[CR]mount\-type\f[R] determines the mount method and in general can be
one of: \f[CR]mount\f[R], \f[CR]cmount\f[R], or \f[CR]mount2\f[R].
This can be aliased as \f[CR]mount_type\f[R].
It should be noted that the managed rclone docker plugin currently does
not support the \f[CR]cmount\f[R] method and \f[CR]mount2\f[R] is rarely
needed.
This option defaults to the first found method, which is usually
\f[CR]mount\f[R] so you generally won\(aqt need it.
.PP
\f[CR]persist\f[R] is a reserved boolean (true/false) option.
In future it will allow to persist on\-the\-fly remotes in the plugin
\f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] file.
.SS Connection Strings
The \f[CR]remote\f[R] value can be extended with \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#connection-strings
connection strings
.UE \c
\ as an alternative way to supply backend parameters.
This is equivalent to the \f[CR]\-o\f[R] backend options with one
\f[I]syntactic difference\f[R].
Inside connection string the backend prefix must be dropped from
parameter names but in the \f[CR]\-o param=value\f[R] array it must be
present.
For instance, compare the following option array
.IP
.EX
\-o remote=:sftp:/home \-o sftp\-host=localhost
.EE
.PP
with equivalent connection string:
.IP
.EX
\-o remote=:sftp,host=localhost:/home
.EE
.PP
This difference exists because flag options \f[CR]\-o key=val\f[R]
include not only backend parameters but also mount/VFS flags and
possibly other settings.
Also it allows to discriminate the \f[CR]remote\f[R] option from the
\f[CR]crypt\-remote\f[R] (or similarly named backend parameters) and
arguably simplifies scripting due to clearer value substitution.
.SS Using with Swarm or Compose
Both \f[I]Docker Swarm\f[R] and \f[I]Docker Compose\f[R] use \c
.UR http://yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html
YAML
.UE \c
\-formatted text files to describe groups (stacks) of containers, their
properties, networks and volumes.
\f[I]Compose\f[R] uses the \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/compose-file-v2/#volume-configuration-reference
compose v2
.UE \c
\ format, \f[I]Swarm\f[R] uses the \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/compose-file-v3/#volume-configuration-reference
compose v3
.UE \c
\ format.
They are mostly similar, differences are explained in the \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/compose/compose-file/compose-versioning/#upgrading
docker documentation
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Volumes are described by the children of the top\-level
\f[CR]volumes:\f[R] node.
Each of them should be named after its volume and have at least two
elements, the self\-explanatory \f[CR]driver: rclone\f[R] value and the
\f[CR]driver_opts:\f[R] structure playing the same role as
\f[CR]\-o key=val\f[R] CLI flags:
.IP
.EX
volumes\f[B]:\f[R]
  volume_name_1\f[B]:\f[R]
    driver\f[B]:\f[R] rclone
    driver_opts\f[B]:\f[R]
      remote\f[B]:\f[R] \(aqgdrive:\(aq
      allow_other\f[B]:\f[R] \(aqtrue\(aq
      vfs_cache_mode\f[B]:\f[R] full
      token\f[B]:\f[R] \(aq{\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqborrower\(dq, \(dqexpires\(dq: \(dq2021\-12\-31\(dq}\(aq
      poll_interval\f[B]:\f[R] 0
.EE
.PP
Notice a few important details:
.IP \(bu 2
YAML prefers \f[CR]_\f[R] in option names instead of \f[CR]\-\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
YAML treats single and double quotes interchangeably.
Simple strings and integers can be left unquoted.
.IP \(bu 2
Boolean values must be quoted like \f[CR]\(aqtrue\(aq\f[R] or
\f[CR]\(dqfalse\(dq\f[R] because these two words are reserved by YAML.
.IP \(bu 2
The filesystem string is keyed with \f[CR]remote\f[R] (or with
\f[CR]fs\f[R]).
Normally you can omit quotes here, but if the string ends with colon,
you \f[B]must\f[R] quote it like
\f[CR]remote: \(dqstorage_box:\(dq\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
YAML is picky about surrounding braces in values as this is in fact
another \c
.UR http://yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html#id2790832
syntax for key/value mappings
.UE \c
\&.
For example, JSON access tokens usually contain double quotes and
surrounding braces, so you must put them in single quotes.
.SS Installing as Managed Plugin
Docker daemon can install plugins from an image registry and run them
managed.
We maintain the \c
.UR https://hub.docker.com/p/rclone/docker-volume-rclone/
docker\-volume\-rclone
.UE \c
\ plugin image on \c
.UR https://hub.docker.com
Docker Hub
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Rclone volume plugin requires \f[B]Docker Engine >= 19.03.15\f[R]
.PP
The plugin requires presence of two directories on the host before it
can be installed.
Note that plugin will \f[B]not\f[R] create them automatically.
By default they must exist on host at the following locations (though
you can tweak the paths):
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/config\f[R] is reserved for the
\f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] config file and \f[B]must\f[R] exist even if
it\(aqs empty and the config file is not present.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/cache\f[R] holds the plugin state
file as well as optional VFS caches.
.PP
You can \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/plugin_install/
install managed plugin
.UE \c
\ with default settings as follows:
.IP
.EX
docker plugin install rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:amd64 \-\-grant\-all\-permissions \-\-alias rclone
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]:amd64\f[R] part of the image specification after colon is
called a \f[I]tag\f[R].
Usually you will want to install the latest plugin for your
architecture.
In this case the tag will just name it, like \f[CR]amd64\f[R] above.
The following plugin architectures are currently available:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]amd64\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]arm64\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]arm\-v7\f[R]
.PP
Sometimes you might want a concrete plugin version, not the latest one.
Then you should use image tag in the form
\f[CR]:ARCHITECTURE\-VERSION\f[R].
For example, to install plugin version \f[CR]v1.56.2\f[R] on
architecture \f[CR]arm64\f[R] you will use tag \f[CR]arm64\-1.56.2\f[R]
(note the removed \f[CR]v\f[R]) so the full image specification becomes
\f[CR]rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:arm64\-1.56.2\f[R].
.PP
We also provide the \f[CR]latest\f[R] plugin tag, but since docker does
not support multi\-architecture plugins as of the time of this writing,
this tag is currently an \f[B]alias for \f[CB]amd64\f[B]\f[R].
By convention the \f[CR]latest\f[R] tag is the default one and can be
omitted, thus both \f[CR]rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:latest\f[R] and
just \f[CR]rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone\f[R] will refer to the latest
plugin release for the \f[CR]amd64\f[R] platform.
.PP
Also the \f[CR]amd64\f[R] part can be omitted from the versioned rclone
plugin tags.
For example, rclone image reference
\f[CR]rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:amd64\-1.56.2\f[R] can be
abbreviated as \f[CR]rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:1.56.2\f[R] for
convenience.
However, for non\-intel architectures you still have to use the full tag
as \f[CR]amd64\f[R] or \f[CR]latest\f[R] will fail to start.
.PP
Managed plugin is in fact a special container running in a namespace
separate from normal docker containers.
Inside it runs the \f[CR]rclone serve docker\f[R] command.
The config and cache directories are bind\-mounted into the container at
start.
The docker daemon connects to a unix socket created by the command
inside the container.
The command creates on\-demand remote mounts right inside, then docker
machinery propagates them through kernel mount namespaces and
bind\-mounts into requesting user containers.
.PP
You can tweak a few plugin settings after installation when it\(aqs
disabled (not in use), for instance:
.IP
.EX
docker plugin disable rclone
docker plugin set rclone RCLONE_VERBOSE=2 config=/etc/rclone args=\(dq\-\-vfs\-cache\-mode=writes \-\-allow\-other\(dq
docker plugin enable rclone
docker plugin inspect rclone
.EE
.PP
Note that if docker refuses to disable the plugin, you should find and
remove all active volumes connected with it as well as containers and
swarm services that use them.
This is rather tedious so please carefully plan in advance.
.PP
You can tweak the following settings: \f[CR]args\f[R],
\f[CR]config\f[R], \f[CR]cache\f[R], \f[CR]HTTP_PROXY\f[R],
\f[CR]HTTPS_PROXY\f[R], \f[CR]NO_PROXY\f[R] and
\f[CR]RCLONE_VERBOSE\f[R].
It\(aqs \f[I]your\f[R] task to keep plugin settings in sync across swarm
cluster nodes.
.PP
\f[CR]args\f[R] sets command\-line arguments for the
\f[CR]rclone serve docker\f[R] command (\f[I]none\f[R] by default).
Arguments should be separated by space so you will normally want to put
them in quotes on the \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/commandline/plugin_set/
docker plugin set
.UE \c
\ command line.
Both \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_serve_docker/#options
serve docker flags
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/flags/
generic rclone flags
.UE \c
\ are supported, including backend parameters that will be used as
defaults for volume creation.
Note that plugin will fail (due to \c
.UR https://github.com/moby/moby/blob/v20.10.7/plugin/v2/plugin.go#L195
this docker bug
.UE \c
) if the \f[CR]args\f[R] value is empty.
Use e.g.
\f[CR]args=\(dq\-v\(dq\f[R] as a workaround.
.PP
\f[CR]config=/host/dir\f[R] sets alternative host location for the
config directory.
Plugin will look for \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R] here.
It\(aqs not an error if the config file is not present but the directory
must exist.
Please note that plugin can periodically rewrite the config file, for
example when it renews storage access tokens.
Keep this in mind and try to avoid races between the plugin and other
instances of rclone on the host that might try to change the config
simultaneously resulting in corrupted \f[CR]rclone.conf\f[R].
You can also put stuff like private key files for SFTP remotes in this
directory.
Just note that it\(aqs bind\-mounted inside the plugin container at the
predefined path \f[CR]/data/config\f[R].
For example, if your key file is named \f[CR]sftp\-box1.key\f[R] on the
host, the corresponding volume config option should read
\f[CR]\-o sftp\-key\-file=/data/config/sftp\-box1.key\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]cache=/host/dir\f[R] sets alternative host location for the
\f[I]cache\f[R] directory.
The plugin will keep VFS caches here.
Also it will create and maintain the \f[CR]docker\-plugin.state\f[R]
file in this directory.
When the plugin is restarted or reinstalled, it will look in this file
to recreate any volumes that existed previously.
However, they will not be re\-mounted into consuming containers after
restart.
Usually this is not a problem as the docker daemon normally will restart
affected user containers after failures, daemon restarts or host
reboots.
.PP
\f[CR]RCLONE_VERBOSE\f[R] sets plugin verbosity from \f[CR]0\f[R]
(errors only, by default) to \f[CR]2\f[R] (debugging).
Verbosity can be also tweaked via \f[CR]args=\(dq\-v [\-v] ...\(dq\f[R].
Since arguments are more generic, you will rarely need this setting.
The plugin output by default feeds the docker daemon log on local host.
Log entries are reflected as \f[I]errors\f[R] in the docker log but
retain their actual level assigned by rclone in the encapsulated message
string.
.PP
\f[CR]HTTP_PROXY\f[R], \f[CR]HTTPS_PROXY\f[R], \f[CR]NO_PROXY\f[R]
customize the plugin proxy settings.
.PP
You can set custom plugin options right when you install it, \f[I]in one
go\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
docker plugin remove rclone
docker plugin install rclone/docker\-volume\-rclone:amd64 \(rs
       \-\-alias rclone \-\-grant\-all\-permissions \(rs
       args=\(dq\-v \-\-allow\-other\(dq config=/etc/rclone
docker plugin inspect rclone
.EE
.SS Healthchecks
The docker plugin volume protocol doesn\(aqt provide a way for plugins
to inform the docker daemon that a volume is (un\-)available.
As a workaround you can setup a healthcheck to verify that the mount is
responding, for example:
.IP
.EX
services\f[B]:\f[R]
  my_service\f[B]:\f[R]
    image\f[B]:\f[R] my_image
    healthcheck\f[B]:\f[R]
      test\f[B]:\f[R] ls /path/to/rclone/mount || exit 1
      interval\f[B]:\f[R] 1m
      timeout\f[B]:\f[R] 15s
      retries\f[B]:\f[R] 3
      start_period\f[B]:\f[R] 15s
.EE
.SS Running Plugin under Systemd
In most cases you should prefer managed mode.
Moreover, MacOS and Windows do not support native Docker plugins.
Please use managed mode on these systems.
Proceed further only if you are on Linux.
.PP
First, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/install/
install rclone
.UE \c
\&.
You can just run it (type \f[CR]rclone serve docker\f[R] and hit enter)
for the test.
.PP
Install \f[I]FUSE\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
sudo apt\-get \-y install fuse
.EE
.PP
Download two systemd configuration files: \c
.UR https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rclone/rclone/master/contrib/docker-plugin/systemd/docker-volume-rclone.service
docker\-volume\-rclone.service
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rclone/rclone/master/contrib/docker-plugin/systemd/docker-volume-rclone.socket
docker\-volume\-rclone.socket
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Put them to the \f[CR]/etc/systemd/system/\f[R] directory:
.IP
.EX
cp docker\-volume\-plugin.service /etc/systemd/system/
cp docker\-volume\-plugin.socket  /etc/systemd/system/
.EE
.PP
Please note that all commands in this section must be run as
\f[I]root\f[R] but we omit \f[CR]sudo\f[R] prefix for brevity.
Now create directories required by the service:
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p /var/lib/docker\-volumes/rclone
mkdir \-p /var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/config
mkdir \-p /var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/cache
.EE
.PP
Run the docker plugin service in the socket activated mode:
.IP
.EX
systemctl daemon\-reload
systemctl start docker\-volume\-rclone.service
systemctl enable docker\-volume\-rclone.socket
systemctl start docker\-volume\-rclone.socket
systemctl restart docker
.EE
.PP
Or run the service directly:
.IP \(bu 2
run \f[CR]systemctl daemon\-reload\f[R] to let systemd pick up new
config
.IP \(bu 2
run \f[CR]systemctl enable docker\-volume\-rclone.service\f[R] to make
the new service start automatically when you power on your machine.
.IP \(bu 2
run \f[CR]systemctl start docker\-volume\-rclone.service\f[R] to start
the service now.
.IP \(bu 2
run \f[CR]systemctl restart docker\f[R] to restart docker daemon and let
it detect the new plugin socket.
Note that this step is not needed in managed mode where docker knows
about plugin state changes.
.PP
The two methods are equivalent from the user perspective, but I
personally prefer socket activation.
.SS Troubleshooting
You can \c
.UR https://docs.docker.com/engine/extend/#debugging-plugins
see managed plugin settings
.UE \c
\ with
.IP
.EX
docker plugin list
docker plugin inspect rclone
.EE
.PP
Note that docker (including latest 20.10.7) will not show actual values
of \f[CR]args\f[R], just the defaults.
.PP
Use \f[CR]journalctl \-\-unit docker\f[R] to see managed plugin output
as part of the docker daemon log.
Note that docker reflects plugin lines as \f[I]errors\f[R] but their
actual level can be seen from encapsulated message string.
.PP
You will usually install the latest version of managed plugin for your
platform.
Use the following commands to print the actual installed version:
.IP
.EX
PLUGID=$(docker plugin list \-\-no\-trunc | awk \(aq/rclone/{print$1}\(aq)
sudo runc \-\-root /run/docker/runtime\-runc/plugins.moby exec $PLUGID rclone version
.EE
.PP
You can even use \f[CR]runc\f[R] to run shell inside the plugin
container:
.IP
.EX
sudo runc \-\-root /run/docker/runtime\-runc/plugins.moby exec \-\-tty $PLUGID bash
.EE
.PP
Also you can use curl to check the plugin socket connectivity:
.IP
.EX
docker plugin list \-\-no\-trunc
PLUGID=123abc...
sudo curl \-H Content\-Type:application/json \-XPOST \-d {} \-\-unix\-socket /run/docker/plugins/$PLUGID/rclone.sock http://localhost/Plugin.Activate
.EE
.PP
though this is rarely needed.
.PP
If the plugin fails to work properly, and only as a last resort after
you tried diagnosing with the above methods, you can try clearing the
state of the plugin.
\f[B]Note that all existing rclone docker volumes will probably have to
be recreated.\f[R] This might be needed because a reinstall don\(aqt
cleanup existing state files to allow for easy restoration, as stated
above.
.IP
.EX
docker plugin disable rclone # disable the plugin to ensure no interference
sudo rm /var/lib/docker\-plugins/rclone/cache/docker\-plugin.state # removing the plugin state
docker plugin enable rclone # re\-enable the plugin afterward
.EE
.SS Caveats
Finally I\(aqd like to mention a \f[I]caveat with updating volume
settings\f[R].
Docker CLI does not have a dedicated command like
\f[CR]docker volume update\f[R].
It may be tempting to invoke \f[CR]docker volume create\f[R] with
updated options on existing volume, but there is a gotcha.
The command will do nothing, it won\(aqt even return an error.
I hope that docker maintainers will fix this some day.
In the meantime be aware that you must remove your volume before
recreating it with new settings:
.IP
.EX
docker volume remove my_vol
docker volume create my_vol \-d rclone \-o opt1=new_val1 ...
.EE
.PP
and verify that settings did update:
.IP
.EX
docker volume list
docker volume inspect my_vol
.EE
.PP
If docker refuses to remove the volume, you should find containers or
swarm services that use it and stop them first.
.SS Bisync
\f[CR]bisync\f[R] is considered an \f[B]advanced command\f[R], so use
with care.
Make sure you have read and understood the entire \c
.UR https://rclone.org/bisync
manual
.UE \c
\ (especially the Limitations section) before using, or data loss can
result.
Questions can be asked in the \c
.UR https://forum.rclone.org/
Rclone Forum
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Getting started
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/install/
Install rclone
.UE \c
\ and setup your remotes.
.IP \(bu 2
Bisync will create its working directory at
\f[CR]\(ti/.cache/rclone/bisync\f[R] on Linux,
\f[CR]/Users/yourusername/Library/Caches/rclone/bisync\f[R] on Mac, or
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsAppData\(rsLocal\(rsrclone\(rsbisync\f[R]
on Windows.
Make sure that this location is writable.
.IP \(bu 2
Run bisync with the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] flag, specifying the paths to
the local and remote sync directory roots.
.IP \(bu 2
For successive sync runs, leave off the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] flag.
(\f[B]Important!\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Consider using a filters file for excluding unnecessary files and
directories from the sync.
.IP \(bu 2
Consider setting up the \-\-check\-access feature for safety.
.IP \(bu 2
On Linux or Mac, consider setting up a crontab entry.
bisync can safely run in concurrent cron jobs thanks to lock files it
maintains.
.PP
For example, your first command might look like this:
.IP
.EX
rclone bisync remote1:path1 remote2:path2 \-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs \-\-compare size,modtime,checksum \-\-slow\-hash\-sync\-only \-\-resilient \-MvP \-\-drive\-skip\-gdocs \-\-fix\-case \-\-resync \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
If all looks good, run it again without \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R].
After that, remove \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] as well.
.PP
