.SS Command line syntax
.IP
.EX
$ rclone bisync \-\-help
Usage:
  rclone bisync remote1:path1 remote2:path2 [flags]

Positional arguments:
  Path1, Path2  Local path, or remote storage with \(aq:\(aq plus optional path.
                Type \(aqrclone listremotes\(aq for list of configured remotes.

Optional Flags:
      \-\-backup\-dir1 string                   \-\-backup\-dir for Path1. Must be a non\-overlapping path on the same remote.
      \-\-backup\-dir2 string                   \-\-backup\-dir for Path2. Must be a non\-overlapping path on the same remote.
      \-\-check\-access                         Ensure expected RCLONE_TEST files are found on both Path1 and Path2 filesystems, else abort.
      \-\-check\-filename string                Filename for \-\-check\-access (default: RCLONE_TEST)
      \-\-check\-sync string                    Controls comparison of final listings: true|false|only (default: true) (default \(dqtrue\(dq)
      \-\-compare string                       Comma\-separated list of bisync\-specific compare options ex. \(aqsize,modtime,checksum\(aq (default: \(aqsize,modtime\(aq)
      \-\-conflict\-loser ConflictLoserAction   Action to take on the loser of a sync conflict (when there is a winner) or on both files (when there is no winner): , num, pathname, delete (default: num)
      \-\-conflict\-resolve string              Automatically resolve conflicts by preferring the version that is: none, path1, path2, newer, older, larger, smaller (default: none) (default \(dqnone\(dq)
      \-\-conflict\-suffix string               Suffix to use when renaming a \-\-conflict\-loser. Can be either one string or two comma\-separated strings to assign different suffixes to Path1/Path2. (default: \(aqconflict\(aq)
      \-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs                Sync creation and deletion of empty directories. (Not compatible with \-\-remove\-empty\-dirs)
      \-\-download\-hash                        Compute hash by downloading when otherwise unavailable. (warning: may be slow and use lots of data!)
      \-\-filters\-file string                  Read filtering patterns from a file
      \-\-force                                Bypass \-\-max\-delete safety check and run the sync. Consider using with \-\-verbose
  \-h, \-\-help                                 help for bisync
      \-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum              Do not use checksums for listings (add \-\-ignore\-checksum to additionally skip post\-copy checksum checks)
      \-\-max\-delete\-renames\-aware             Exclude tracked renames from the \-\-max\-delete safety check (requires \-\-track\-renames)
      \-\-max\-lock Duration                    Consider lock files older than this to be expired (default: 0 (never expire)) (minimum: 2m) (default 0s)
      \-\-no\-cleanup                           Retain working files (useful for troubleshooting and testing).
      \-\-no\-slow\-hash                         Ignore listing checksums only on backends where they are slow
      \-\-recover                              Automatically recover from interruptions without requiring \-\-resync.
      \-\-remove\-empty\-dirs                    Remove ALL empty directories at the final cleanup step.
      \-\-resilient                            Allow future runs to retry after certain less\-serious errors, instead of requiring \-\-resync.
  \-1, \-\-resync                               Performs the resync run. Equivalent to \-\-resync\-mode path1. Consider using \-\-verbose or \-\-dry\-run first.
      \-\-resync\-mode string                   During resync, prefer the version that is: path1, path2, newer, older, larger, smaller (default: path1 if \-\-resync, otherwise none for no resync.) (default \(dqnone\(dq)
      \-\-retries int                          Retry operations this many times if they fail (requires \-\-resilient). (default 3)
      \-\-retries\-sleep Duration               Interval between retrying operations if they fail, e.g. 500ms, 60s, 5m (0 to disable) (default 0s)
      \-\-slow\-hash\-sync\-only                  Ignore slow checksums for listings and deltas, but still consider them during sync calls.
      \-\-workdir string                       Use custom working dir \- useful for testing. (default: {WORKDIR})
      \-\-max\-delete PERCENT                   Safety check on maximum percentage of deleted files allowed. If exceeded, the bisync run will abort. (default: 50%)
  \-n, \-\-dry\-run                              Go through the motions \- No files are copied/deleted.
  \-v, \-\-verbose                              Increases logging verbosity. May be specified more than once for more details.
.EE
.PP
Arbitrary rclone flags may be specified on the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_bisync/
bisync command line
.UE \c
, for example
\f[CR]rclone bisync ./testdir/path1/ gdrive:testdir/path2/ \-\-drive\-skip\-gdocs \-v \-v \-\-timeout 10s\f[R]
Note that interactions of various rclone flags with bisync process flow
has not been fully tested yet.
.SS Paths
Path1 and Path2 arguments may be references to any mix of local
directory paths (absolute or relative), UNC paths
(\f[CR]//server/share/path\f[R]), Windows drive paths (with a drive
letter and \f[CR]:\f[R]) or configured \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#syntax-of-remote-paths
remotes
.UE \c
\ with optional subdirectory paths.
Cloud references are distinguished by having a \f[CR]:\f[R] in the
argument (see Windows support below).
.PP
Path1 and Path2 are treated equally, in that neither has priority for
file changes (except during \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R]), and access
efficiency does not change whether a remote is on Path1 or Path2.
.PP
The listings in bisync working directory (default:
\f[CR]\(ti/.cache/rclone/bisync\f[R]) are named based on the Path1 and
Path2 arguments so that separate syncs to individual directories within
the tree may be set up, e.g.:
\f[CR]path_to_local_tree..dropbox_subdir.lst\f[R].
.PP
Any empty directories after the sync on both the Path1 and Path2
filesystems are not deleted by default, unless
\f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R] is specified.
If the \f[CR]\-\-remove\-empty\-dirs\f[R] flag is specified, then both
paths will have ALL empty directories purged as the last step in the
process.
.SS Command\-line flags
.SS \-\-resync
This will effectively make both Path1 and Path2 filesystems contain a
matching superset of all files.
By default, Path2 files that do not exist in Path1 will be copied to
Path1, and the process will then copy the Path1 tree to Path2.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] sequence is roughly equivalent to the
following (but see \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] for other options):
.IP
.EX
rclone copy Path2 Path1 \-\-ignore\-existing [\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs]
rclone copy Path1 Path2 [\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs]
.EE
.PP
The base directories on both Path1 and Path2 filesystems must exist or
bisync will fail.
This is required for safety \- that bisync can verify that both paths
are valid.
.PP
When using \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], a newer version of a file on the Path2
filesystem will (by default) be overwritten by the Path1 filesystem
version.
(Note that this is \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/5681#issuecomment-938761815
NOT entirely symmetrical
.UE \c
, and more symmetrical options can be specified with the
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] flag.)
Carefully evaluate deltas using \c
.UR https://rclone.org/flags/#non-backend-flags
\-\-dry\-run
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
For a resync run, one of the paths may be empty (no files in the path
tree).
The resync run should result in files on both paths, else a normal
non\-resync run will fail.
.PP
For a non\-resync run, either path being empty (no files in the tree)
fails with
\f[CR]Empty current PathN listing. Cannot sync to an empty directory: X.pathN.lst\f[R]
This is a safety check that an unexpected empty path does not result in
deleting \f[B]everything\f[R] in the other path.
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] implies
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode path1\f[R] unless a different
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] is explicitly specified.
It is not necessary to use both the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] flags \-\- either one is sufficient without
the other.
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] (including
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R]) should only be used under three specific
(rare) circumstances:
.IP "1." 3
It is your \f[I]first\f[R] bisync run (between these two paths)
.IP "2." 3
You\(aqve just made changes to your bisync settings (such as editing the
contents of your \f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R])
.IP "3." 3
There was an error on the prior run, and as a result, bisync now
requires \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] to recover
.PP
The rest of the time, you should \f[I]omit\f[R] \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
The reason is because \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] will only \f[I]copy\f[R]
(not \f[I]sync\f[R]) each side to the other.
Therefore, if you included \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] for every bisync run,
it would never be possible to delete a file \-\- the deleted file would
always keep reappearing at the end of every run (because it\(aqs being
copied from the other side where it still exists).
Similarly, renaming a file would always result in a duplicate copy (both
old and new name) on both sides.
.PP
If you find that frequent interruptions from #3 are an issue, rather
than automatically running \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], the recommended
alternative is to use the \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R] flags,
(along with Graceful Shutdown mode, when needed) for a very robust
\(dqset\-it\-and\-forget\-it\(dq bisync setup that can automatically
bounce back from almost any interruption it might encounter.
Consider adding something like the following:
.IP
.EX
\-\-resilient \-\-recover \-\-max\-lock 2m \-\-conflict\-resolve newer
.EE
.SS \-\-resync\-mode CHOICE
In the event that a file differs on both sides during a
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] controls which
version will overwrite the other.
The supported options are similar to \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R].
For all of the following options, the version that is kept is referred
to as the \(dqwinner\(dq, and the version that is overwritten (deleted)
is referred to as the \(dqloser\(dq.
The options are named after the \(dqwinner\(dq:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]path1\f[R] \- (the default) \- the version from Path1 is
unconditionally considered the winner (regardless of \f[CR]modtime\f[R]
and \f[CR]size\f[R], if any).
This can be useful if one side is more trusted or up\-to\-date than the
other, at the time of the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]path2\f[R] \- same as \f[CR]path1\f[R], except the path2 version
is considered the winner.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]newer\f[R] \- the newer file (by \f[CR]modtime\f[R]) is considered
the winner, regardless of which side it came from.
This may result in having a mix of some winners from Path1, and some
winners from Path2.
(The implementation is analogous to running
\f[CR]rclone copy \-\-update\f[R] in both directions.)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]older\f[R] \- same as \f[CR]newer\f[R], except the older file is
considered the winner, and the newer file is considered the loser.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]larger\f[R] \- the larger file (by \f[CR]size\f[R]) is considered
the winner (regardless of \f[CR]modtime\f[R], if any).
This can be a useful option for remotes without \f[CR]modtime\f[R]
support, or with the kinds of files (such as logs) that tend to grow but
not shrink, over time.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]smaller\f[R] \- the smaller file (by \f[CR]size\f[R]) is
considered the winner (regardless of \f[CR]modtime\f[R], if any).
.PP
For all of the above options, note the following:
.IP \(bu 2
If either of the underlying remotes lacks support for the chosen method,
it will be ignored and will fall back to the default of
\f[CR]path1\f[R].
(For example, if \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode newer\f[R] is set, but one of
the paths uses a remote that doesn\(aqt support \f[CR]modtime\f[R].)
.IP \(bu 2
If a winner can\(aqt be determined because the chosen method\(aqs
attribute is missing or equal, it will be ignored, and bisync will
instead try to determine whether the files differ by looking at the
other \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] methods in effect.
(For example, if \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode newer\f[R] is set, but the Path1
and Path2 modtimes are identical, bisync will compare the sizes.)
If bisync concludes that they differ, preference is given to whichever
is the \(dqsource\(dq at that moment.
(In practice, this gives a slight advantage to Path2, as the 2to1 copy
comes before the 1to2 copy.)
If the files \f[I]do not\f[R] differ, nothing is copied (as both sides
are already correct).
.IP \(bu 2
These options apply only to files that exist on both sides (with the
same name and relative path).
Files that exist \f[I]only\f[R] on one side and not the other are
\f[I]always\f[R] copied to the other, during \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] (this
is one of the main differences between resync and non\-resync runs.).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R] do not apply during
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], and unlike these flags, nothing is renamed during
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
When a file differs on both sides during \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], one
version always overwrites the other (much like in
\f[CR]rclone copy\f[R].)
(Consider using \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] to retain a backup of the
losing version.)
.IP \(bu 2
Unlike for \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode none\f[R] is not a valid option (or rather, it
will be interpreted as \(dqno resync\(dq, unless \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R]
has also been specified, in which case it will be ignored.)
.IP \(bu 2
Winners and losers are decided at the individual file\-level only (there
is not currently an option to pick an entire winning directory
atomically, although the \f[CR]path1\f[R] and \f[CR]path2\f[R] options
typically produce a similar result.)
.IP \(bu 2
To maintain backward\-compatibility, the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] flag
implies \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode path1\f[R] unless a different
\f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] is explicitly specified.
Similarly, all \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] options (except
\f[CR]none\f[R]) imply \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], so it is not necessary to
use both the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] flags
simultaneously \-\- either one is sufficient without the other.
.SS \-\-check\-access
Access check files are an additional safety measure against data loss.
bisync will ensure it can find matching \f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files in
the same places in the Path1 and Path2 filesystems.
\f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files are not generated automatically.
For \f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] to succeed, you must first either:
\f[B]A)\f[R] Place one or more \f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files in both
systems, or \f[B]B)\f[R] Set \f[CR]\-\-check\-filename\f[R] to a
filename already in use in various locations throughout your sync\(aqd
fileset.
Recommended methods for \f[B]A)\f[R] include:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rclone touch Path1/RCLONE_TEST\f[R] (create a new file)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rclone copyto Path1/RCLONE_TEST Path2/RCLONE_TEST\f[R] (copy an
existing file)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rclone copy Path1/RCLONE_TEST Path2/RCLONE_TEST  \-\-include \(dqRCLONE_TEST\(dq\f[R]
(copy multiple files at once, recursively)
.IP \(bu 2
create the files manually (outside of rclone)
.IP \(bu 2
run \f[CR]bisync\f[R] once \f[I]without\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] to set matching files on both filesystems
will also work, but is not preferred, due to potential for user error
(you are temporarily disabling the safety feature).
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] is still enforced on
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], so \f[CR]bisync \-\-resync \-\-check\-access\f[R]
will not work as a method of initially setting the files (this is to
ensure that bisync can\(aqt \c
.UR https://forum.rclone.org/t/bisync-bugs-and-feature-requests/37636#:~:text=3.%20%2D%2Dcheck%2Daccess%20doesn%27t%20always%20fail%20when%20it%20should
inadvertently circumvent its own safety switch
.UE \c
\&.)
.PP
Time stamps and file contents for \f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files are not
important, just the names and locations.
If you have symbolic links in your sync tree it is recommended to place
\f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files in the linked\-to directory tree to protect
against bisync assuming a bunch of deleted files if the linked\-to tree
should not be accessible.
See also the \-\-check\-filename flag.
.SS \-\-check\-filename
Name of the file(s) used in access health validation.
The default \f[CR]\-\-check\-filename\f[R] is \f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R].
One or more files having this filename must exist, synchronized between
your source and destination filesets, in order for
\f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] to succeed.
See \-\-check\-access for additional details.
.SS \-\-compare
As of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], bisync fully supports comparing based on any
combination of size, modtime, and checksum (lifting the prior
restriction on backends without modtime support.)
.PP
By default (without the \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] flag), bisync inherits
the same comparison options as \f[CR]sync\f[R] (that is: \f[CR]size\f[R]
and \f[CR]modtime\f[R] by default, unless modified with flags such as \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#c-checksum
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R]
.UE \c
\ or \f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R].)
.PP
If the \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] flag is set, it will override these
defaults.
This can be useful if you wish to compare based on combinations not
currently supported in \f[CR]sync\f[R], such as comparing all three of
\f[CR]size\f[R] AND \f[CR]modtime\f[R] AND \f[CR]checksum\f[R]
simultaneously (or just \f[CR]modtime\f[R] AND \f[CR]checksum\f[R]).
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] takes a comma\-separated list, with the currently
supported values being \f[CR]size\f[R], \f[CR]modtime\f[R], and
\f[CR]checksum\f[R].
For example, if you want to compare size and checksum, but not modtime,
you would do:
.IP
.EX
\-\-compare size,checksum
.EE
.PP
Or if you want to compare all three:
.IP
.EX
\-\-compare size,modtime,checksum
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] overrides any conflicting flags.
For example, if you set the conflicting flags
\f[CR]\-\-compare checksum \-\-size\-only\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R] will be ignored, and bisync will compare
checksum and not size.
To avoid confusion, it is recommended to use \f[I]either\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] or the normal \f[CR]sync\f[R] flags, but not
both.
.PP
If \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] includes \f[CR]checksum\f[R] and both remotes
support checksums but have no hash types in common with each other,
checksums will be considered \f[I]only\f[R] for comparisons within the
same side (to determine what has changed since the prior sync), but not
for comparisons against the opposite side.
If one side supports checksums and the other does not, checksums will
only be considered on the side that supports them.
.PP
When comparing with \f[CR]checksum\f[R] and/or \f[CR]size\f[R] without
\f[CR]modtime\f[R], bisync cannot determine whether a file is
\f[CR]newer\f[R] or \f[CR]older\f[R] \-\- only whether it is
\f[CR]changed\f[R] or \f[CR]unchanged\f[R].
(If it is \f[CR]changed\f[R] on both sides, bisync still does the
standard equality\-check to avoid declaring a sync conflict unless it
absolutely has to.)
.PP
It is recommended to do a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] when changing
\f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] settings, as otherwise your prior listing files
may not contain the attributes you wish to compare (for example, they
will not have stored checksums if you were not previously comparing
checksums.)
.SS \-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum
When \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-compare checksum\f[R] is set,
bisync will retrieve (or generate) checksums (for backends that support
them) when creating the listings for both paths, and store the checksums
in the listing files.
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R] will disable this behavior,
which may speed things up considerably, especially on backends (such as
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/local/
local
.UE \c
) where hashes must be computed on the fly instead of retrieved.
Please note the following:
.IP \(bu 2
As of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R] is now
automatically set when neither \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] nor
\f[CR]\-\-compare checksum\f[R] are in use (as the checksums would not
be used for anything.)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R] is NOT the same as \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#ignore-checksum
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-checksum\f[R]
.UE \c
, and you may wish to use one or the other, or both.
In a nutshell: \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R] controls whether
checksums are considered when scanning for diffs, while
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-checksum\f[R] controls whether checksums are
considered during the copy/sync operations that follow, if there ARE
diffs.
.IP \(bu 2
Unless \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R] is passed, bisync
currently computes hashes for one path \f[I]even when there\(aqs no
common hash with the other path\f[R] (for example, a \c
.UR https://rclone.org/crypt/#modification-times-and-hashes
crypt
.UE \c
\ remote.)
This can still be beneficial, as the hashes will still be used to detect
changes within the same side (if \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-compare checksum\f[R] is set), even if they can\(aqt be used
to compare against the opposite side.
.IP \(bu 2
If you wish to ignore listing checksums \f[I]only\f[R] on remotes where
they are slow to compute, consider using \f[CR]\-\-no\-slow\-hash\f[R]
(or \f[CR]\-\-slow\-hash\-sync\-only\f[R]) instead of
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
If \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R] is used simultaneously with
\f[CR]\-\-compare checksum\f[R] (or \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R]), checksums
will be ignored for bisync deltas, but still considered during the sync
operations that follow (if deltas are detected based on modtime and/or
size.)
.SS \-\-no\-slow\-hash
On some remotes (notably \f[CR]local\f[R]), checksums can dramatically
slow down a bisync run, because hashes cannot be stored and need to be
computed in real\-time when they are requested.
On other remotes (such as \f[CR]drive\f[R]), they add practically no
time at all.
The \f[CR]\-\-no\-slow\-hash\f[R] flag will automatically skip checksums
on remotes where they are slow, while still comparing them on others
(assuming \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] includes \f[CR]checksum\f[R].)
This can be useful when one of your bisync paths is slow but you still
want to check checksums on the other, for a more robust sync.
.SS \-\-slow\-hash\-sync\-only
Same as \f[CR]\-\-no\-slow\-hash\f[R], except slow hashes are still
considered during sync calls.
They are still NOT considered for determining deltas, nor or they
included in listings.
They are also skipped during \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
The main use case for this flag is when you have a large number of
files, but relatively few of them change from run to run \-\- so you
don\(aqt want to check your entire tree every time (it would take too
long), but you still want to consider checksums for the smaller group of
files for which a \f[CR]modtime\f[R] or \f[CR]size\f[R] change was
detected.
Keep in mind that this speed savings comes with a safety trade\-off: if
a file\(aqs content were to change without a change to its
\f[CR]modtime\f[R] or \f[CR]size\f[R], bisync would not detect it, and
it would not be synced.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-slow\-hash\-sync\-only\f[R] is only useful if both remotes
share a common hash type (if they don\(aqt, bisync will automatically
fall back to \f[CR]\-\-no\-slow\-hash\f[R].)
Both \f[CR]\-\-no\-slow\-hash\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-\-slow\-hash\-sync\-only\f[R] have no effect without
\f[CR]\-\-compare checksum\f[R] (or \f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R]).
.SS \-\-download\-hash
If \f[CR]\-\-download\-hash\f[R] is set, bisync will use best efforts to
obtain an MD5 checksum by downloading and computing on\-the\-fly, when
checksums are not otherwise available (for example, a remote that
doesn\(aqt support them.)
Note that since rclone has to download the entire file, this may
dramatically slow down your bisync runs, and is also likely to use a lot
of data, so it is probably not practical for bisync paths with a large
total file size.
However, it can be a good option for syncing small\-but\-important files
with maximum accuracy (for example, a source code repo on a
\f[CR]crypt\f[R] remote.)
An additional advantage over methods like \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_cryptcheck/
\f[CR]cryptcheck\f[R]
.UE \c
\ is that the original file is not required for comparison (for example,
\f[CR]\-\-download\-hash\f[R] can be used to bisync two different crypt
remotes with different passwords.)
.PP
When \f[CR]\-\-download\-hash\f[R] is set, bisync still looks for more
efficient checksums first, and falls back to downloading only when none
are found.
It takes priority over conflicting flags such as
\f[CR]\-\-no\-slow\-hash\f[R].
\f[CR]\-\-download\-hash\f[R] is not suitable for Google Docs and other
files of unknown size, as their checksums would change from run to run
(due to small variances in the internals of the generated export file.)
Therefore, bisync automatically skips \f[CR]\-\-download\-hash\f[R] for
files with a size less than 0.
.PP
See also: \c
.UR https://rclone.org/hasher/
\f[CR]Hasher\f[R]
.UE \c
\ backend, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_cryptcheck/
\f[CR]cryptcheck\f[R]
.UE \c
\ command, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_check/
\f[CR]rclone check \-\-download\f[R]
.UE \c
\ option, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_md5sum/
\f[CR]md5sum\f[R]
.UE \c
\ command
.SS \-\-max\-delete
As a safety check, if greater than the \f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\f[R]
percent of files were deleted on either the Path1 or Path2 filesystem,
then bisync will abort with a warning message, without making any
changes.
The default \f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\f[R] is \f[CR]50%\f[R].
One way to trigger this limit is to rename a directory that contains
more than half of your files.
This will appear to bisync as a bunch of deleted files and a bunch of
new files.
This safety check is intended to block bisync from deleting all of the
files on both filesystems due to a temporary network access issue, or if
the user had inadvertently deleted the files on one side or the other.
To force the sync, either set a different delete percentage limit, e.g.
\f[CR]\-\-max\-delete 75\f[R] (allows up to 75% deletion), or use
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] to bypass the check.
.PP
Also see the all files changed check.
.SS \-\-max\-delete\-renames\-aware
When \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] is in use,
\f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\-renames\-aware\f[R] can exclude files that are
guaranteed to match as tracked renames from the deletion count.
If the initial deletion count exceeds \f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\f[R], bisync
performs a rename matching preflight before starting either sync
direction.
The run still aborts if the remaining unmatched deletions exceed the
limit.
.PP
The preflight uses the selected \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#track-renames-strategy
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\-strategy\f[R]
.UE \c
\ and the attributes stored in the normal bisync listings.
A hash strategy forces both listings to use a common hash and is
incompatible with \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum\f[R].
The option only exempts matches when the destination supports
server\-side moves or copies and the selected strategy is supported by
both paths.
It requires \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] and is disabled by default
because hashing may add listing time.
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] bypasses both the safety check and this preflight.
The preflight is also disabled during \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], when rename
tracking is unavailable.
.SS \-\-filters\-file
By using rclone filter features you can exclude file types or directory
sub\-trees from the sync.
See the bisync filters section and generic \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/#filter-from-read-filtering-patterns-from-a-file
\-\-filter\-from
.UE \c
\ documentation.
An example filters file contains filters for non\-allowed files for
syncing with Dropbox.
.PP
If you make changes to your filters file then bisync requires a run with
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
This is a safety feature, which prevents existing files on the Path1
and/or Path2 side from seeming to disappear from view (since they are
excluded in the new listings), which would fool bisync into seeing them
as deleted (as compared to the prior run listings), and then bisync
would proceed to delete them for real.
.PP
To block this from happening, bisync calculates an MD5 hash of the
filters file and stores the hash in a \f[CR].md5\f[R] file in the same
place as your filters file.
On the next run with \f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R] set, bisync
re\-calculates the MD5 hash of the current filters file and compares it
to the hash stored in the \f[CR].md5\f[R] file.
If they don\(aqt match, the run aborts with a critical error and thus
forces you to do a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], likely avoiding a disaster.
.SS \-\-conflict\-resolve CHOICE
In bisync, a \(dqconflict\(dq is a file that is \f[I]new\f[R] or
\f[I]changed\f[R] on \f[I]both sides\f[R] (relative to the prior run)
AND is \f[I]not currently identical\f[R] on both sides.
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R] controls how bisync handles such a
scenario.
The currently supported options are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]none\f[R] \- (the default) \- do not attempt to pick a winner,
keep and rename both files according to \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser\f[R]
and \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R] settings.
For example, with the default settings, \f[CR]file.txt\f[R] on Path1 is
renamed \f[CR]file.txt.conflict1\f[R] and \f[CR]file.txt\f[R] on Path2
is renamed \f[CR]file.txt.conflict2\f[R].
Both are copied to the opposite path during the run, so both sides end
up with a copy of both files.
(As \f[CR]none\f[R] is the default, it is not necessary to specify
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve none\f[R] \-\- you can just omit the flag.)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]newer\f[R] \- the newer file (by \f[CR]modtime\f[R]) is considered
the winner and is copied without renaming.
The older file (the \(dqloser\(dq) is handled according to
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R]
settings (either renamed or deleted.)
For example, if \f[CR]file.txt\f[R] on Path1 is newer than
\f[CR]file.txt\f[R] on Path2, the result on both sides (with other
default settings) will be \f[CR]file.txt\f[R] (winner from Path1) and
\f[CR]file.txt.conflict1\f[R] (loser from Path2).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]older\f[R] \- same as \f[CR]newer\f[R], except the older file is
considered the winner, and the newer file is considered the loser.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]larger\f[R] \- the larger file (by \f[CR]size\f[R]) is considered
the winner (regardless of \f[CR]modtime\f[R], if any).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]smaller\f[R] \- the smaller file (by \f[CR]size\f[R]) is
considered the winner (regardless of \f[CR]modtime\f[R], if any).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]path1\f[R] \- the version from Path1 is unconditionally considered
the winner (regardless of \f[CR]modtime\f[R] and \f[CR]size\f[R], if
any).
This can be useful if one side is usually more trusted or up\-to\-date
than the other.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]path2\f[R] \- same as \f[CR]path1\f[R], except the path2 version
is considered the winner.
.PP
For all of the above options, note the following:
.IP \(bu 2
If either of the underlying remotes lacks support for the chosen method,
it will be ignored and fall back to \f[CR]none\f[R].
(For example, if \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve newer\f[R] is set, but one
of the paths uses a remote that doesn\(aqt support \f[CR]modtime\f[R].)
.IP \(bu 2
If a winner can\(aqt be determined because the chosen method\(aqs
attribute is missing or equal, it will be ignored and fall back to
\f[CR]none\f[R].
(For example, if \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve newer\f[R] is set, but the
Path1 and Path2 modtimes are identical, even if the sizes may differ.)
.IP \(bu 2
If the file\(aqs content is currently identical on both sides, it is not
considered a \(dqconflict\(dq, even if new or changed on both sides
since the prior sync.
(For example, if you made a change on one side and then synced it to the
other side by other means.)
Therefore, none of the conflict resolution flags apply in this scenario.
.IP \(bu 2
The conflict resolution flags do not apply during a
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], as there is no \(dqprior run\(dq to speak of (but
see \f[CR]\-\-resync\-mode\f[R] for similar options.)
.SS \-\-conflict\-loser CHOICE
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser\f[R] determines what happens to the
\(dqloser\(dq of a sync conflict (when \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R]
determines a winner) or to both files (when there is no winner.)
The currently supported options are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]num\f[R] \- (the default) \- auto\-number the conflicts by
automatically appending the next available number to the
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R], in chronological order.
For example, with the default settings, the first conflict for
\f[CR]file.txt\f[R] will be renamed \f[CR]file.txt.conflict1\f[R].
If \f[CR]file.txt.conflict1\f[R] already exists,
\f[CR]file.txt.conflict2\f[R] will be used instead (etc., up to a
maximum of 9223372036854775807 conflicts.)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]pathname\f[R] \- rename the conflicts according to which side they
came from, which was the default behavior prior to \f[CR]v1.66\f[R].
For example, with \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix path\f[R],
\f[CR]file.txt\f[R] from Path1 will be renamed
\f[CR]file.txt.path1\f[R], and \f[CR]file.txt\f[R] from Path2 will be
renamed \f[CR]file.txt.path2\f[R].
If two non\-identical suffixes are provided (ex.
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix cloud,local\f[R]), the trailing digit is
omitted.
Importantly, note that with \f[CR]pathname\f[R], there is no
auto\-numbering beyond \f[CR]2\f[R], so if \f[CR]file.txt.path2\f[R]
somehow already exists, it will be overwritten.
Using a dynamic date variable in your \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R]
(see below) is one possible way to avoid this.
Note also that conflicts\-of\-conflicts are possible, if the original
conflict is not manually resolved \-\- for example, if for some reason
you edited \f[CR]file.txt.path1\f[R] on both sides, and those edits were
different, the result would be \f[CR]file.txt.path1.path1\f[R] and
\f[CR]file.txt.path1.path2\f[R] (in addition to
\f[CR]file.txt.path2\f[R].)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]delete\f[R] \- keep the winner only and delete the loser, instead
of renaming it.
If a winner cannot be determined (see \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R]
for details on how this could happen), \f[CR]delete\f[R] is ignored and
the default \f[CR]num\f[R] is used instead (i.e.
both versions are kept and renamed, and neither is deleted.)
\f[CR]delete\f[R] is inherently the most destructive option, so use it
only with care.
.PP
For all of the above options, note that if a winner cannot be determined
(see \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R] for details on how this could
happen), or if \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R] is not in use,
\f[I]both\f[R] files will be renamed.
.SS \-\-conflict\-suffix STRING[,STRING]
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R] controls the suffix that is appended
when bisync renames a \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser\f[R] (default:
\f[CR]conflict\f[R]).
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R] will accept either one string or two
comma\-separated strings to assign different suffixes to Path1 vs.
Path2.
This may be helpful later in identifying the source of the conflict.
(For example,
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix dropboxconflict,laptopconflict\f[R])
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser num\f[R], a number is always appended to
the suffix.
With \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser pathname\f[R], a number is appended only
when one suffix is specified (or when two identical suffixes are
specified.)
i.e.
with \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser pathname\f[R], all of the following would
produce exactly the same result:
.IP
.EX
\-\-conflict\-suffix path
\-\-conflict\-suffix path,path
\-\-conflict\-suffix path1,path2
.EE
.PP
Suffixes may be as short as 1 character.
By default, the suffix is appended after any other extensions (ex.
\f[CR]file.jpg.conflict1\f[R]), however, this can be changed with the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#suffix-keep-extension
\f[CR]\-\-suffix\-keep\-extension\f[R]
.UE \c
\ flag (i.e.
to instead result in \f[CR]file.conflict1.jpg\f[R]).
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R] supports several \f[I]dynamic date
variables\f[R] when enclosed in curly braces as globs.
This can be helpful to track the date and/or time that each conflict was
handled by bisync.
For example:
.IP
.EX
\-\-conflict\-suffix {DateOnly}\-conflict
// result: myfile.txt.2006\-01\-02\-conflict1
.EE
.PP
All of the formats described \c
.UR https://pkg.go.dev/time#pkg-constants
here (go Time.Layout constants)
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://pkg.go.dev/time#example-Time.Format
here (go Time.Format examples)
.UE \c
\ are supported, but take care to ensure that your chosen format does
not use any characters that are illegal on your remotes (for example,
macOS does not allow colons in filenames, and slashes are also best
avoided as they are often interpreted as directory separators.)
To address this particular issue, an additional
\f[CR]{MacFriendlyTime}\f[R] (or just \f[CR]{mac}\f[R]) option is
supported, which results in \f[CR]2006\-01\-02 0304PM\f[R].
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R] is entirely separate from
rclone\(aqs main \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#suffix-string
\f[CR]\-\-sufix\f[R]
.UE \c
\ flag.
This is intentional, as users may wish to use both flags simultaneously,
if also using \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R].
.PP
Finally, note that the default in bisync prior to \f[CR]v1.66\f[R] was
to rename conflicts with \f[CR]..path1\f[R] and \f[CR]..path2\f[R] (with
two periods, and \f[CR]path\f[R] instead of \f[CR]conflict\f[R].)
Bisync now defaults to a single dot instead of a double dot, but
additional dots can be added by including them in the specified suffix
string.
For example, for behavior equivalent to the previous default, use:
.IP
.EX
[\-\-conflict\-resolve none] \-\-conflict\-loser pathname \-\-conflict\-suffix .path
.EE
.SS \-\-check\-sync
Enabled by default, the check\-sync function checks that all of the same
files exist in both the Path1 and Path2 history listings.
This \f[I]check\-sync\f[R] integrity check is performed at the end of
the sync run by default.
Any untrapped failing copy/deletes between the two paths might result in
differences between the two listings and in the untracked file content
differences between the two paths.
A resync run would correct the error.
.PP
Note that the default\-enabled integrity check locally executes a load
of both the final Path1 and Path2 listings, and thus adds to the run
time of a sync.
Using \f[CR]\-\-check\-sync=false\f[R] will disable it and may
significantly reduce the sync run times for very large numbers of files.
.PP
The check may be run manually with \f[CR]\-\-check\-sync=only\f[R].
It runs only the integrity check and terminates without actually
syncing.
.PP
Note that currently, \f[CR]\-\-check\-sync\f[R] \f[B]only checks listing
snapshots and NOT the actual files on the remotes.\f[R] Note also that
the listing snapshots will not know about any changes that happened
during or after the latest bisync run, as those will be discovered on
the next run.
Therefore, while listings should always match \f[I]each other\f[R] at
the end of a bisync run, it is \f[I]expected\f[R] that they will not
match the underlying remotes, nor will the remotes match each other, if
there were changes during or after the run.
This is normal, and any differences will be detected and synced on the
next run.
.PP
For a robust integrity check of the current state of the remotes (as
opposed to just their listing snapshots), consider using
\f[CR]check\f[R] (or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_cryptcheck/
\f[CR]cryptcheck\f[R]
.UE \c
, if at least one path is a \f[CR]crypt\f[R] remote) instead of
\f[CR]\-\-check\-sync\f[R], keeping in mind that differences are
expected if files changed during or after your last bisync run.
.PP
For example, a possible sequence could look like this:
.IP "1." 3
Normally scheduled bisync run:
.RS 4
.IP
.EX
rclone bisync Path1 Path2 \-MPc \-\-check\-access \-\-max\-delete 10 \-\-filters\-file /path/to/filters.txt \-v \-\-no\-cleanup \-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum \-\-disable ListR \-\-checkers=16 \-\-drive\-pacer\-min\-sleep=10ms \-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs \-\-resilient
.EE
.RE
.IP "2." 3
Periodic independent integrity check (perhaps scheduled nightly or
weekly):
.RS 4
.IP
.EX
rclone check \-MvPc Path1 Path2 \-\-filter\-from /path/to/filters.txt
.EE
.RE
.IP "3." 3
If diffs are found, you have some choices to correct them.
If one side is more up\-to\-date and you want to make the other side
match it, you could run:
.RS 4
.IP
.EX
rclone sync Path1 Path2 \-\-filter\-from /path/to/filters.txt \-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs \-MPc \-v
.EE
.RE
.PP
(or switch Path1 and Path2 to make Path2 the source\-of\-truth)
.PP
Or, if neither side is totally up\-to\-date, you could run a
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] to bring them back into agreement (but remember
that this could cause deleted files to re\-appear.)
.PP
*Note also that \f[CR]rclone check\f[R] does not currently include empty
directories, so if you want to know if any empty directories are out of
sync, consider alternatively running the above \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R]
command with \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] added.
.PP
See also: Concurrent modifications, \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R]
.SS \-\-resilient
By default, most errors or interruptions will cause bisync to abort and
require \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] to recover.
This is a safety feature, to prevent bisync from running again until a
user checks things out.
However, in some cases, bisync can go too far and enforce a lockout when
one isn\(aqt actually necessary, like for certain less\-serious errors
that might resolve themselves on the next run.
When \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] is specified, bisync tries its best to
recover and self\-correct, and only requires \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] as a
last resort when a human\(aqs involvement is absolutely necessary.
The intended use case is for running bisync as a background process
(such as via scheduled cron).
.PP
When using \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] mode, bisync will still report the
error and abort, however it will not lock out future runs \-\- allowing
the possibility of retrying at the next normally scheduled time, without
requiring a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] first.
Examples of such retryable errors include access test failures, missing
listing files, and filter change detections.
These safety features will still prevent the \f[I]current\f[R] run from
proceeding \-\- the difference is that if conditions have improved by
the time of the \f[I]next\f[R] run, that next run will be allowed to
proceed.
Certain more serious errors will still enforce a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R]
lockout, even in \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] mode, to prevent data loss.
.PP
Behavior of \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] may change in a future version.
(See also: \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R], Graceful
Shutdown)
.SS \-\-recover
If \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R] is set, in the event of a sudden interruption
or other un\-graceful shutdown, bisync will attempt to automatically
recover on the next run, instead of requiring \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
Bisync is able to recover robustly by keeping one \(dqbackup\(dq listing
at all times, representing the state of both paths after the last known
successful sync.
Bisync can then compare the current state with this snapshot to
determine which changes it needs to retry.
Changes that were synced after this snapshot (during the run that was
later interrupted) will appear to bisync as if they are \(dqnew or
changed on both sides\(dq, but in most cases this is not a problem, as
bisync will simply do its usual \(dqequality check\(dq and learn that no
action needs to be taken on these files, since they are already
identical on both sides.
.PP
In the rare event that a file is synced successfully during a run that
later aborts, and then that same file changes AGAIN before the next run,
bisync will think it is a sync conflict, and handle it accordingly.
(From bisync\(aqs perspective, the file has changed on both sides since
the last trusted sync, and the files on either side are not currently
identical.)
Therefore, \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R] carries with it a slightly increased
chance of having conflicts \-\- though in practice this is pretty rare,
as the conditions required to cause it are quite specific.
This risk can be reduced by using bisync\(aqs \(dqGraceful Shutdown\(dq
mode (triggered by sending \f[CR]SIGINT\f[R] or \f[CR]Ctrl+C\f[R]), when
you have the choice, instead of forcing a sudden termination.
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] are similar, but
distinct \-\- the main difference is that \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] is
about \f[I]retrying\f[R], while \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R] is about
\f[I]recovering\f[R].
Most users will probably want both.
\f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] allows retrying when bisync has chosen to abort
itself due to safety features such as failing
\f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] or detecting a filter change.
\f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] does not cover external interruptions such as a
user shutting down their computer in the middle of a sync \-\- that is
what \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R] is for.
.SS \-\-max\-lock
Bisync uses lock files as a safety feature to prevent interference from
other bisync runs while it is running.
Bisync normally removes these lock files at the end of a run, but if
bisync is abruptly interrupted, these files will be left behind.
By default, they will lock out all future runs, until the user has a
chance to manually check things out and remove the lock.
As an alternative, \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] can be used to make them
automatically expire after a certain period of time, so that future runs
are not locked out forever, and auto\-recovery is possible.
\f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] can be any duration \f[CR]2m\f[R] or greater
(or \f[CR]0\f[R] to disable).
If set, lock files older than this will be considered \(dqexpired\(dq,
and future runs will be allowed to disregard them and proceed.
(Note that the \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] duration must be set by the
process that left the lock file \-\- not the later one interpreting it.)
.PP
If set, bisync will also \(dqrenew\(dq these lock files every
\f[CR]\-\-max\-lock minus one minute\f[R] throughout a run, for extra
safety.
(For example, with \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock 5m\f[R], bisync would renew the
lock file (for another 5 minutes) every 4 minutes until the run has
completed.)
In other words, it should not be possible for a lock file to pass its
expiration time while the process that created it is still running \-\-
and you can therefore be reasonably sure that any \f[I]expired\f[R] lock
file you may find was left there by an interrupted run, not one that is
still running and just taking awhile.
.PP
If a lock file exists but its contents are unreadable (for example, due
to an incomplete write or disk error), it is treated as expired when
\f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] is set to a value greater than \f[CR]0\f[R].
If \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] is \f[CR]0\f[R] or not set, an unreadable
lock file will produce an error and block future runs until removed
manually.
.PP
If \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] is \f[CR]0\f[R] or not set, the default is
that lock files will never expire, and will block future runs (of these
same two bisync paths) indefinitely.
.PP
For maximum resilience from disruptions, consider setting a relatively
short duration like \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock 2m\f[R] along with
\f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R], and a relatively
frequent cron schedule.
The result will be a very robust \(dqset\-it\-and\-forget\-it\(dq bisync
run that can automatically bounce back from almost any interruption it
might encounter, without requiring the user to get involved and run a
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
(See also: Graceful Shutdown mode)
.SS \-\-backup\-dir1 and \-\-backup\-dir2
As of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#backup-dir-string
\f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R]
.UE \c
\ is supported in bisync.
Because \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] must be a non\-overlapping path on
the same remote, Bisync has introduced new \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir1\f[R]
and \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir2\f[R] flags to support separate backup\-dirs
for \f[CR]Path1\f[R] and \f[CR]Path2\f[R] (bisyncing between different
remotes with \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] would not otherwise be
possible.)
\f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir1\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir2\f[R] can use
different remotes from each other, but \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir1\f[R] must
use the same remote as \f[CR]Path1\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir2\f[R]
must use the same remote as \f[CR]Path2\f[R].
Each backup directory must not overlap its respective bisync Path
without being excluded by a filter rule.
.PP
The standard \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R] will also work, if both paths
use the same remote (but note that deleted files from both paths would
be mixed together in the same dir).
If either \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir1\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir2\f[R]
are set, they will override \f[CR]\-\-backup\-dir\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
rclone bisync /Users/someuser/some/local/path/Bisync gdrive:Bisync \-\-backup\-dir1 /Users/someuser/some/local/path/BackupDir \-\-backup\-dir2 gdrive:BackupDir \-\-suffix \-2023\-08\-26 \-\-suffix\-keep\-extension \-\-check\-access \-\-max\-delete 10 \-\-filters\-file /Users/someuser/some/local/path/bisync_filters.txt \-\-no\-cleanup \-\-ignore\-listing\-checksum \-\-checkers=16 \-\-drive\-pacer\-min\-sleep=10ms \-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs \-\-resilient \-MvP \-\-drive\-skip\-gdocs \-\-fix\-case
.EE
.PP
In this example, if the user deletes a file in
\f[CR]/Users/someuser/some/local/path/Bisync\f[R], bisync will propagate
the delete to the other side by moving the corresponding file from
\f[CR]gdrive:Bisync\f[R] to \f[CR]gdrive:BackupDir\f[R].
If the user deletes a file from \f[CR]gdrive:Bisync\f[R], bisync moves
it from \f[CR]/Users/someuser/some/local/path/Bisync\f[R] to
\f[CR]/Users/someuser/some/local/path/BackupDir\f[R].
.PP
In the event of a rename due to a sync conflict, the rename is not
considered a delete, unless a previous conflict with the same name
already exists and would get overwritten.
.PP
See also: \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#suffix-string
\f[CR]\-\-suffix\f[R]
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#suffix-keep-extension
\f[CR]\-\-suffix\-keep\-extension\f[R]
.UE \c
.SS Operation
.SS Runtime flow details
bisync retains the listings of the \f[CR]Path1\f[R] and \f[CR]Path2\f[R]
filesystems from the prior run.
On each successive run it will:
.IP \(bu 2
list files on \f[CR]path1\f[R] and \f[CR]path2\f[R], and check for
changes on each side.
Changes include \f[CR]New\f[R], \f[CR]Newer\f[R], \f[CR]Older\f[R], and
\f[CR]Deleted\f[R] files.
.IP \(bu 2
Propagate changes on \f[CR]path1\f[R] to \f[CR]path2\f[R], and
vice\-versa.
.SS Safety measures
.IP \(bu 2
Lock file prevents multiple simultaneous runs when taking a while.
This can be particularly useful if bisync is run by cron scheduler.
.IP \(bu 2
Handle change conflicts non\-destructively by creating
\f[CR].conflict1\f[R], \f[CR].conflict2\f[R], etc.
file versions, according to \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-resolve\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-conflict\-loser\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-conflict\-suffix\f[R]
settings.
.IP \(bu 2
File system access health check using \f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files (see
the \f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] flag).
.IP \(bu 2
Abort on excessive deletes \- protects against a failed listing being
interpreted as all the files were deleted.
See the \f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] flags.
.IP \(bu 2
If something evil happens, bisync goes into a safe state to block damage
by later runs.
(See Error Handling)
.SS Normal sync checks
.PP
