.PP
As of \f[CR]rclone v1.64\f[R], bisync is now better at detecting
\f[I]false positive\f[R] sync conflicts, which would previously have
resulted in unnecessary renames and duplicates.
Now, when bisync comes to a file that it wants to rename (because it is
new/changed on both sides), it first checks whether the Path1 and Path2
versions are currently \f[I]identical\f[R] (using the same underlying
function as \f[CR]check\f[R].)
If bisync concludes that the files are identical, it will skip them and
move on.
Otherwise, it will create renamed duplicates, as before.
This behavior also \c
.UR https://forum.rclone.org/t/bisync-bugs-and-feature-requests/37636#:~:text=Renamed%20directories
improves the experience of renaming directories
.UE \c
, as a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] is no longer required, so long as the same
change has been made on both sides.
.SS All files changed check
If \f[I]all\f[R] prior existing files on either of the filesystems have
changed (e.g.
timestamps have changed due to changing the system\(aqs timezone) then
bisync will abort without making any changes.
Any new files are not considered for this check.
You could use \f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] to force the sync (whichever side has
the changed timestamp files wins).
Alternately, a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] may be used (Path1 versions will be
pushed to Path2).
Consider the situation carefully and perhaps use \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R]
before you commit to the changes.
.SS Modification times
By default, bisync compares files by modification time and size.
If you or your application should change the content of a file without
changing the modification time and size, then bisync will \f[I]not\f[R]
notice the change, and thus will not copy it to the other side.
As an alternative, consider comparing by checksum (if your remotes
support it).
See \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] for details.
.SS Error handling
Certain bisync critical errors, such as file copy/move failing, will
result in a bisync lockout of following runs.
The lockout is asserted because the sync status and history of the Path1
and Path2 filesystems cannot be trusted, so it is safer to block any
further changes until someone checks things out.
The recovery is to do a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] again.
.PP
It is recommended to use \f[CR]\-\-resync \-\-dry\-run \-\-verbose\f[R]
initially and \f[I]carefully\f[R] review what changes will be made
before running the \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] without
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R].
.PP
Most of these events come up due to an error status from an internal
call.
On such a critical error the \f[CR]{...}.path1.lst\f[R] and
\f[CR]{...}.path2.lst\f[R] listing files are renamed to extension
\f[CR].lst\-err\f[R], which blocks any future bisync runs (since the
normal \f[CR].lst\f[R] files are not found).
Bisync keeps them under \f[CR]bisync\f[R] subdirectory of the rclone
cache directory, typically at \f[CR]${HOME}/.cache/rclone/bisync/\f[R]
on Linux.
.PP
Some errors are considered temporary and re\-running the bisync is not
blocked.
The \f[I]critical return\f[R] blocks further bisync runs.
.PP
See also: \f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R], Graceful Shutdown
.SS Lock file
When bisync is running, a lock file is created in the bisync working
directory, typically at
\f[CR]\(ti/.cache/rclone/bisync/PATH1..PATH2.lck\f[R] on Linux.
If bisync should crash or hang, the lock file will remain in place and
block any further runs of bisync \f[I]for the same paths\f[R].
Delete the lock file as part of debugging the situation.
The lock file effectively blocks follow\-on (e.g., scheduled by
\f[I]cron\f[R]) runs when the prior invocation is taking a long time.
The lock file contains \f[I]PID\f[R] of the blocking process, which may
help in debug.
Lock files can be set to automatically expire after a certain amount of
time, using the \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R] flag.
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R] that while concurrent bisync runs are allowed, \f[I]be
very cautious\f[R] that there is no overlap in the trees being synched
between concurrent runs, lest there be replicated files, deleted files
and general mayhem.
.SS Exit codes
\f[CR]rclone bisync\f[R] returns the following codes to calling program:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]0\f[R] on a successful run,
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]1\f[R] for a non\-critical failing run (a rerun may be
successful),
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]2\f[R] on syntax or usage error,
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]7\f[R] for a critically aborted run (requires a
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] to recover).
.PP
See also the section about \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#exit-code
exit codes
.UE \c
\ in main docs.
.SS Graceful Shutdown
Bisync has a \(dqGraceful Shutdown\(dq mode which is activated by
sending \f[CR]SIGINT\f[R] or pressing \f[CR]Ctrl+C\f[R] during a run.
Once triggered, bisync will use best efforts to exit cleanly before the
timer runs out.
If bisync is in the middle of transferring files, it will attempt to
cleanly empty its queue by finishing what it has started but not taking
more.
If it cannot do so within 30 seconds, it will cancel the in\-progress
transfers at that point and then give itself a maximum of 60 seconds to
wrap up, save its state for next time, and exit.
With the \f[CR]\-vP\f[R] flags you will see constant status updates and
a final confirmation of whether or not the graceful shutdown was
successful.
.PP
At any point during the \(dqGraceful Shutdown\(dq sequence, a second
\f[CR]SIGINT\f[R] or \f[CR]Ctrl+C\f[R] will trigger an immediate,
un\-graceful exit, which will leave things in a messier state.
Usually a robust recovery will still be possible if using
\f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R] mode, otherwise you will need to do a
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R].
.PP
If you plan to use Graceful Shutdown mode, it is recommended to use
\f[CR]\-\-resilient\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-recover\f[R], and it is important
to NOT use \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#inplace
\f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R]
.UE \c
, otherwise you risk leaving partially\-written files on one side, which
may be confused for real files on the next run.
Note also that in the event of an abrupt interruption, a lock file will
be left behind to block concurrent runs.
You will need to delete it before you can proceed with the next run (or
wait for it to expire on its own, if using \f[CR]\-\-max\-lock\f[R].)
.SS Limitations
.SS Supported backends
Bisync is integration\-tested against all rclone backends to verify
compatibility.
Most backends (including all backends not listed below) are deemed fully
supported with no known issues.
Occasionally, however, these tests reveal issues with particular
backends, usually related to a provider\-specific limitation beyond
rclone\(aqs control (for example, disallowed special characters and
filename encodings.)
.PP
