.PP
Early beta versions of \f[CR]rclone bisync\f[R] required both underlying
backends to support modification times, and refused to run otherwise.
This limitation has been lifted as of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], as bisync now
supports comparing checksum and/or size instead of (or in addition to)
modtime.
See \f[CR]\-\-compare\f[R] for details.
.SS Concurrent modifications
When using \f[B]Local, FTP or SFTP\f[R] remotes with \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#inplace
\f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R]
.UE \c
, rclone does not create \f[I]temporary\f[R] files at the destination
when copying, and thus if the connection is lost the created file may be
corrupt, which will likely propagate back to the original path on the
next sync, resulting in data loss.
It is therefore recommended to \f[I]omit\f[R] \f[CR]\-\-inplace\f[R].
.PP
Files that \f[B]change during\f[R] a bisync run may result in data loss.
Prior to \f[CR]rclone v1.66\f[R], this was commonly seen in highly
dynamic environments, where the filesystem was getting hammered by
running processes during the sync.
As of \f[CR]rclone v1.66\f[R], bisync was redesigned to use a
\(dqsnapshot\(dq model, greatly reducing the risks from changes during a
sync.
Changes that are not detected during the current sync will now be
detected during the following sync, and will no longer cause the entire
run to throw a critical error.
There is additionally a mechanism to mark files as needing to be
internally rechecked next time, for added safety.
It should therefore no longer be necessary to sync only at quiet times
\-\- however, note that an error can still occur if a file happens to
change at the exact moment it\(aqs being read/written by bisync (same as
would happen in \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R].)
(See also: \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#ignore-checksum
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-checksum\f[R]
.UE \c
, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/local/#local-no-check-updated
\f[CR]\-\-local\-no\-check\-updated\f[R]
.UE \c
)
.SS Empty directories
By default, new/deleted empty directories on one path are \f[I]not\f[R]
propagated to the other side.
This is because bisync (and rclone) natively works on files, not
directories.
However, this can be changed with the
\f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R] flag, which works in much the
same way as in \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_sync/
\f[CR]sync\f[R]
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copy/
\f[CR]copy\f[R]
.UE \c
\&.
When used, empty directories created or deleted on one side will also be
created or deleted on the other side.
The following should be noted:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R] is not compatible with
\f[CR]\-\-remove\-empty\-dirs\f[R].
Use only one or the other (or neither).
.IP \(bu 2
It is not recommended to switch back and forth between
\f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R] and the default (no
\f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R]) without running
\f[CR]\-\- resync\f[R].
This is because it may appear as though all directories (not just the
empty ones) were created/deleted, when actually you\(aqve just toggled
between making them visible/invisible to bisync.
It looks scarier than it is, but it\(aqs still probably best to stick to
one or the other, and use \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] when you need to switch.
.SS Renamed directories
By default, renaming a folder on the Path1 side results in deleting all
files on the Path2 side and then copying all files again from Path1 to
Path2.
Bisync sees this as all files in the old directory name as deleted and
all files in the new directory name as new.
.PP
A recommended solution is to use \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#track-renames
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R]
.UE \c
, which is now supported in bisync as of \f[CR]rclone v1.66\f[R].
Note that \f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] is not available during
\f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], as \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] does not delete anything
(\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] only supports \f[CR]sync\f[R], not
\f[CR]copy\f[R].)
.PP
Otherwise, the most effective and efficient method of renaming a
directory is to rename it to the same name on both sides.
(As of \f[CR]rclone v1.64\f[R], a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R] is no longer
required after doing so, as bisync will automatically detect that Path1
and Path2 are in agreement.)
.PP
By default, renamed or moved files are still counted as deleted files
for purposes of \f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\f[R], because the safety check
happens before rename detection.
Use \f[CR]\-\-max\-delete\-renames\-aware\f[R] together with
\f[CR]\-\-track\-renames\f[R] to run a rename preflight and exclude
guaranteed matches from this count.
.SS \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] used by default
Unlike most other rclone commands, bisync uses \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#fast-list
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R]
.UE \c
\ by default, for backends that support it.
In many cases this is desirable, however, there are some scenarios in
which bisync could be faster \f[I]without\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R], and there is also a \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/commit/cbf3d4356135814921382dd3285d859d15d0aa77
known issue concerning Google Drive users with many empty directories
.UE \c
\&.
For now, the recommended way to avoid using \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] is
to add \f[CR]\-\-disable ListR\f[R] to all bisync commands.
The default behavior may change in a future version.
.SS Case (and unicode) sensitivity
As of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], case and unicode form differences no longer
cause critical errors, and normalization (when comparing between
filesystems) is handled according to the same flags and defaults as
\f[CR]rclone sync\f[R].
See the following options (all of which are supported by bisync) to
control this behavior more granularly:
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#fix-case
\f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R]
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#ignore-case-sync
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-case\-sync\f[R]
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#no-unicode-normalization
\f[CR]\-\-no\-unicode\-normalization\f[R]
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/local/#local-unicode-normalization
\f[CR]\-\-local\-unicode\-normalization\f[R]
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/local/#local-case-sensitive
\f[CR]\-\-local\-case\-sensitive\f[R]
.UE \c
\ (caution: these are normally not what you want.)
.PP
Note that in the (probably rare) event that \f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R] is
used AND a file is new/changed on both sides AND the checksums match AND
the filename case does not match, the Path1 filename is considered the
winner, for the purposes of \f[CR]\-\-fix\-case\f[R] (Path2 will be
renamed to match it).
.SS Windows support
Bisync has been tested on Windows 8.1, Windows 10 Pro 64\-bit and on
Windows GitHub runners.
.PP
Drive letters are allowed, including drive letters mapped to network
drives (\f[CR]rclone bisync J:\(rslocalsync GDrive:\f[R]).
If a drive letter is omitted, the shell current drive is the default.
Drive letters are a single character follows by \f[CR]:\f[R], so cloud
names must be more than one character long.
.PP
Absolute paths (with or without a drive letter), and relative paths
(with or without a drive letter) are supported.
.PP
Working directory is created at
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsAppData\(rsLocal\(rsrclone\(rsbisync\f[R].
.PP
Note that bisync output may show a mix of forward \f[CR]/\f[R] and back
\f[CR]\(rs\f[R] slashes.
.PP
Be careful of case independent directory and file naming on Windows vs.
case dependent Linux
.SS Filtering
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/
filtering documentation
.UE \c
\ for how filter rules are written and interpreted.
.PP
Bisync\(aqs \f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R] flag slightly extends the
rclone\(aqs \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/#filter-from-read-filtering-patterns-from-a-file
\-\-filter\-from
.UE \c
\ filtering mechanism.
For a given bisync run you may provide \f[I]only one\f[R]
\f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R].
The \f[CR]\-\-include*\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exclude*\f[R], and
\f[CR]\-\-filter\f[R] flags are also supported.
.SS How to filter directories
Filtering portions of the directory tree is a critical feature for
syncing.
.PP
Examples of directory trees (always beneath the Path1/Path2 root level)
you may want to exclude from your sync:
.IP \(bu 2
Directory trees containing only software build intermediate files.
.IP \(bu 2
Directory trees containing application temporary files and data such as
the Windows \f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsAppData\(rs\f[R] tree.
.IP \(bu 2
Directory trees containing files that are large, less important, or are
getting thrashed continuously by ongoing processes.
.PP
On the other hand, there may be only select directories that you
actually want to sync, and exclude all others.
See the Example include\-style filters for Windows user directories
below.
.SS Filters file writing guidelines
.IP "1." 3
Begin with excluding directory trees:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
e.g.
\f[CR]\- /AppData/\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]**\f[R] on the end is not necessary.
Once a given directory level is excluded then everything beneath it
won\(aqt be looked at by rclone.
.IP \(bu 2
Exclude such directories that are unneeded, are big, dynamically
thrashed, or where there may be access permission issues.
.IP \(bu 2
Excluding such dirs first will make rclone operations (much) faster.
.IP \(bu 2
Specific files may also be excluded, as with the Dropbox exclusions
example below.
.RE
.IP "2." 3
Decide if it\(aqs easier (or cleaner) to:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Include select directories and therefore \f[I]exclude everything
else\f[R] \-\- or \-\-
.IP \(bu 2
Exclude select directories and therefore \f[I]include everything
else\f[R]
.RE
.IP "3." 3
Include select directories:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Add lines like: \f[CR]+ /Documents/PersonalFiles/**\f[R] to select which
directories to include in the sync.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]**\f[R] on the end specifies to include the full depth of the
specified tree.
.IP \(bu 2
With Include\-style filters, files at the Path1/Path2 root are not
included.
They may be included with \f[CR]+ /*\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Place RCLONE_TEST files within these included directory trees.
They will only be looked for in these directory trees.
.IP \(bu 2
Finish by excluding everything else by adding \f[CR]\- **\f[R] at the
end of the filters file.
.IP \(bu 2
Disregard step 4.
.RE
.IP "4." 3
Exclude select directories:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Add more lines like in step 1.
For example: \f[CR]\-/Desktop/tempfiles/\f[R], or
\f[CR]\- /testdir/\f[R].
Again, a \f[CR]**\f[R] on the end is not necessary.
.IP \(bu 2
Do \f[I]not\f[R] add a \f[CR]\- **\f[R] in the file.
Without this line, everything will be included that has not been
explicitly excluded.
.IP \(bu 2
Disregard step 3.
.RE
.PP
A few rules for the syntax of a filter file expanding on \c
.UR https://rclone.org/filtering/
filtering documentation
.UE \c
:
.IP \(bu 2
Lines may start with spaces and tabs \- rclone strips leading
whitespace.
.IP \(bu 2
If the first non\-whitespace character is a \f[CR]#\f[R] then the line
is a comment and will be ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
Blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
The first non\-whitespace character on a filter line must be a
\f[CR]+\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Exactly 1 space is allowed between the \f[CR]+/\-\f[R] and the path
term.
.IP \(bu 2
Only forward slashes (\f[CR]/\f[R]) are used in path terms, even on
Windows.
.IP \(bu 2
The rest of the line is taken as the path term.
Trailing whitespace is taken literally, and probably is an error.
.SS Example include\-style filters for Windows user directories
This Windows \f[I]include\-style\f[R] example is based on the sync root
(Path1) set to \f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\f[R].
The strategy is to select specific directories to be synched with a
network drive (Path2).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\- /AppData/\f[R] excludes an entire tree of Windows stored stuff
that need not be synched.
In my case, AppData has >11 GB of stuff I don\(aqt care about, and there
are some subdirectories beneath AppData that are not accessible to my
user login, resulting in bisync critical aborts.
.IP \(bu 2
Windows creates cache files starting with both upper and lowercase
\f[CR]NTUSER\f[R] at \f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\f[R].
These files may be dynamic, locked, and are generally \f[I]don\(aqt
care\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
There are just a few directories with \f[I]my\f[R] data that I do want
synched, in the form of \f[CR]+ /<path>\f[R].
By selecting only the directory trees I want to avoid the dozen plus
directories that various apps make at
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsDocuments\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Include files in the root of the sync point,
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\f[R], by adding the \f[CR]+ /*\f[R] line.
.IP \(bu 2
This is an Include\-style filters file, therefore it ends with
\f[CR]\- **\f[R] which excludes everything not explicitly included.
.IP
.EX
\- /AppData/
\- NTUSER*
\- ntuser*
+ /Documents/Family/**
+ /Documents/Sketchup/**
+ /Documents/Microcapture_Photo/**
+ /Documents/Microcapture_Video/**
+ /Desktop/**
+ /Pictures/**
+ /*
\- **
.EE
.PP
Note also that Windows implements several \(dqlibrary\(dq links such as
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsMy Documents\(rsMy Music\f[R] pointing
to \f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsMusic\f[R].
rclone sees these as links, so you must add \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] to the
bisync command line if you which to follow these links.
I find that I get permission errors in trying to follow the links, so I
don\(aqt include the rclone \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] flag, but then you get
lots of \f[CR]Can\(aqt follow symlink\&...\f[R] noise from rclone about
not following the links.
This noise can be quashed by adding \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R] to the bisync
command line.
.SS Example exclude\-style filters files for use with Dropbox
.IP \(bu 2
Dropbox disallows syncing the listed temporary and configuration/data
files.
The \f[CR]\- <filename>\f[R] filters exclude these files where ever they
may occur in the sync tree.
Consider adding similar exclusions for file types you don\(aqt need to
sync, such as core dump and software build files.
.IP \(bu 2
bisync testing creates \f[CR]/testdir/\f[R] at the top level of the sync
tree, and usually deletes the tree after the test.
If a normal sync should run while the \f[CR]/testdir/\f[R] tree exists
the \f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] phase may fail due to unbalanced
RCLONE_TEST files.
The \f[CR]\- /testdir/\f[R] filter blocks this tree from being synched.
You don\(aqt need this exclusion if you are not doing bisync development
testing.
.IP \(bu 2
Everything else beneath the Path1/Path2 root will be synched.
.IP \(bu 2
RCLONE_TEST files may be placed anywhere within the tree, including the
root.
.SS Example filters file for Dropbox
.IP
.EX
# Filter file for use with bisync
# See https://rclone.org/filtering/ for filtering rules
# NOTICE: If you make changes to this file you MUST do a \-\-resync run.
#         Run with \-\-dry\-run to see what changes will be made.

# Dropbox won\(aqt sync some files so filter them away here.
# See https://help.dropbox.com/installs\-integrations/sync\-uploads/files\-not\-syncing
\- .dropbox.attr
\- \(ti*.tmp
\- \(ti$*
\- .\(ti*
\- desktop.ini
\- .dropbox

# Used for bisync testing, so excluded from normal runs
\- /testdir/

# Other example filters
#\- /TiBU/
#\- /Photos/
.EE
.SS How \-\-check\-access handles filters
At the start of a bisync run, listings are gathered for Path1 and Path2
while using the user\(aqs \f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R].
During the check access phase, bisync scans these listings for
\f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files.
Any \f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files hidden by the
\f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R] are \f[I]not\f[R] in the listings and thus
not checked during the check access phase.
.SS Troubleshooting
.SS Reading bisync logs
Here are two normal runs.
The first one has a newer file on the remote.
The second has no deltas between local and remote.
