2021/05/12 00:49:40 ERROR : Bisync aborted. Must run \-\-resync to recover.
.EE
.SS Denied downloads of \(dqinfected\(dq or \(dqabusive\(dq files
Google Drive has a filter for certain file types (\f[CR].exe\f[R],
\f[CR].apk\f[R], et cetera) that by default cannot be copied from Google
Drive to the local filesystem.
If you are having problems, run with \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R] to see
specifically which files are generating complaints.
If the error is
\f[CR]This file has been identified as malware or spam and cannot be downloaded\f[R],
consider using the flag \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#drive-acknowledge-abuse
\-\-drive\-acknowledge\-abuse
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Google Docs (and other files of unknown size)
As of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#import-export-of-google-documents
Google Docs
.UE \c
\ (including Google Sheets, Slides, etc.)
are now supported in bisync, subject to the same options, defaults, and
limitations as in \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R].
When bisyncing drive with non\-drive backends, the drive \-> non\-drive
direction is controlled by \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#drive-export-formats
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-export\-formats\f[R]
.UE \c
\ (default \f[CR]\(dqdocx,xlsx,pptx,svg\(dq\f[R]) and the non\-drive \->
drive direction is controlled by \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#drive-import-formats
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-import\-formats\f[R]
.UE \c
\ (default none.)
.PP
For example, with the default export/import formats, a Google Sheet on
the drive side will be synced to an \f[CR].xlsx\f[R] file on the
non\-drive side.
In the reverse direction, \f[CR].xlsx\f[R] files with filenames that
match an existing Google Sheet will be synced to that Google Sheet,
while \f[CR].xlsx\f[R] files that do NOT match an existing Google Sheet
will be copied to drive as normal \f[CR].xlsx\f[R] files (without
conversion to Sheets, although the Google Drive web browser UI may still
give you the option to open it as one.)
.PP
If \f[CR]\-\-drive\-import\-formats\f[R] is set (it\(aqs not, by
default), then all of the specified formats will be converted to Google
Docs, if there is no existing Google Doc with a matching name.
Caution: such conversion can be quite lossy, and in most cases it\(aqs
probably not what you want!
.PP
To bisync Google Docs as URL shortcut links (in a manner similar to
\(dqDrive for Desktop\(dq), use:
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-export\-formats url\f[R] (or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#exportformats:~:text=available%20Google%20Documents.-,Extension,macOS,-Standard%20options
alternatives
.UE \c
\&.)
.PP
Note that these link files cannot be edited on the non\-drive side \-\-
you will get errors if you try to sync an edited link file back to
drive.
They CAN be deleted (it will result in deleting the corresponding Google
Doc.)
If you create a \f[CR].url\f[R] file on the non\-drive side that does
not match an existing Google Doc, bisyncing it will just result in
copying the literal \f[CR].url\f[R] file over to drive (no Google Doc
will be created.)
So, as a general rule of thumb, think of them as read\-only placeholders
on the non\-drive side, and make all your changes on the drive side.
.PP
Likewise, even with other export\-formats, it is best to only
move/rename Google Docs on the drive side.
This is because otherwise, bisync will interpret this as a file deleted
and another created, and accordingly, it will delete the Google Doc and
create a new file at the new path.
(Whether or not that new file is a Google Doc depends on
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-import\-formats\f[R].)
.PP
Lastly, take note that all Google Docs on the drive side have a size of
\f[CR]\-1\f[R] and no checksum.
Therefore, they cannot be reliably synced with the
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R] flags.
(To be exact: they will still get created/deleted, and bisync\(aqs delta
engine will notice changes and queue them for syncing, but the
underlying sync function will consider them identical and skip them.)
To work around this, use the default (modtime and size) instead of
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R].
.PP
To ignore Google Docs entirely, use \c
.UR https://rclone.org/drive/#drive-skip-gdocs
\f[CR]\-\-drive\-skip\-gdocs\f[R]
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Usage examples
.SS Cron
Rclone does not yet have a built\-in capability to monitor the local
file system for changes and must be blindly run periodically.
On Windows this can be done using a \f[I]Task Scheduler\f[R], on Linux
you can use \f[I]Cron\f[R] which is described below.
.PP
The 1st example runs a sync every 5 minutes between a local directory
and an OwnCloud server, with output logged to a runlog file:
.IP
.EX
# Minute (0\-59)
#      Hour (0\-23)
#           Day of Month (1\-31)
#                Month (1\-12 or Jan\-Dec)
#                     Day of Week (0\-6 or Sun\-Sat)
#                         Command
  */5  *    *    *    *   /path/to/rclone bisync /local/files MyCloud: \-\-check\-access \-\-filters\-file /path/to/bysync\-filters.txt \-\-log\-file /path/to//bisync.log
.EE
.PP
See \c
.UR https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/crontab.1p.html#INPUT_FILES
crontab syntax
.UE \c
\ for the details of crontab time interval expressions.
.PP
If you run \f[CR]rclone bisync\f[R] as a cron job, redirect
stdout/stderr to a file.
The 2nd example runs a sync to Dropbox every hour and logs all stdout
(via the \f[CR]>>\f[R]) and stderr (via \f[CR]2>&1\f[R]) to a log file.
.IP
.EX
0 * * * * /path/to/rclone bisync /path/to/local/dropbox Dropbox: \-\-check\-access \-\-filters\-file /home/user/filters.txt >> /path/to/logs/dropbox\-run.log 2>&1
.EE
.SS Sharing an encrypted folder tree between hosts
bisync can keep a local folder in sync with a cloud service, but what if
you have some highly sensitive files to be synched?
.PP
Usage of a cloud service is for exchanging both routine and sensitive
personal files between one\(aqs home network, one\(aqs personal notebook
when on the road, and with one\(aqs work computer.
The routine data is not sensitive.
For the sensitive data, configure an rclone \c
.UR https://rclone.org/crypt/
crypt remote
.UE \c
\ to point to a subdirectory within the local disk tree that is
bisync\(aqd to Dropbox, and then set up an bisync for this local crypt
directory to a directory outside of the main sync tree.
.SS Linux server setup
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/path/to/DBoxroot\f[R] is the root of my local sync tree.
There are numerous subdirectories.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/path/to/DBoxroot/crypt\f[R] is the root subdirectory for files
that are encrypted.
This local directory target is setup as an rclone crypt remote named
\f[CR]Dropcrypt:\f[R].
See rclone.conf snippet below.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]/path/to/my/unencrypted/files\f[R] is the root of my sensitive
files \- not encrypted, not within the tree synched to Dropbox.
.IP \(bu 2
To sync my local unencrypted files with the encrypted Dropbox versions I
manually run \f[CR]bisync /path/to/my/unencrypted/files DropCrypt:\f[R].
This step could be bundled into a script to run before and after the
full Dropbox tree sync in the last step, thus actively keeping the
sensitive files in sync.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]bisync /path/to/DBoxroot Dropbox:\f[R] runs periodically via cron,
keeping my full local sync tree in sync with Dropbox.
.SS Windows notebook setup
.IP \(bu 2
The Dropbox client runs keeping the local tree
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsDropbox\f[R] always in sync with
Dropbox.
I could have used \f[CR]rclone bisync\f[R] instead.
.IP \(bu 2
A separate directory tree at
\f[CR]C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsDocuments\(rsDropLocal\f[R] hosts the
tree of unencrypted files/folders.
.IP \(bu 2
To sync my local unencrypted files with the encrypted Dropbox versions I
manually run the following command:
\f[CR]rclone bisync C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsDocuments\(rsDropLocal Dropcrypt:\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The Dropbox client then syncs the changes with Dropbox.
.SS rclone.conf snippet
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Dropbox]\f[R]
type = dropbox
\&...

\f[B][Dropcrypt]\f[R]
type = crypt
remote = /path/to/DBoxroot/crypt          # on the Linux server
remote = C:\(rsUsers\(rsMyLogin\(rsDropbox\(rscrypt   # on the Windows notebook
filename_encryption = standard
directory_name_encryption = \f[B]true\f[R]
password = ...
\&...
.EE
.SS Testing
You should read this section only if you are developing for rclone.
You need to have rclone source code locally to work with bisync tests.
.PP
Bisync has a dedicated test framework implemented in the
\f[CR]bisync_test.go\f[R] file located in the rclone source tree.
The test suite is based on the \f[CR]go test\f[R] command.
Series of tests are stored in subdirectories below the
\f[CR]cmd/bisync/testdata\f[R] directory.
Individual tests can be invoked by their directory name, e.g.
\f[CR]go test . \-case basic \-remote local \-remote2 gdrive: \-v\f[R]
.PP
Tests will make a temporary folder on remote and purge it afterwards.
If during test run there are intermittent errors and rclone retries,
these errors will be captured and flagged as invalid MISCOMPAREs.
Rerunning the test will let it pass.
Consider such failures as noise.
.SS Test command syntax
.IP
.EX
usage: go test ./cmd/bisync [options...]

Options:
  \-case NAME        Name(s) of the test case(s) to run. Multiple names should
                    be separated by commas. You can remove the \(gatest_\(ga prefix
                    and replace \(ga_\(ga by \(ga\-\(ga in test name for convenience.
                    If not \(gaall\(ga, the name(s) should map to a directory under
                    \(ga./cmd/bisync/testdata\(ga.
                    Use \(gaall\(ga to run all tests (default: all)
  \-remote PATH1     \(galocal\(ga or name of cloud service with \(ga:\(ga (default: local)
  \-remote2 PATH2    \(galocal\(ga or name of cloud service with \(ga:\(ga (default: local)
  \-no\-compare       Disable comparing test results with the golden directory
                    (default: compare)
  \-no\-cleanup       Disable cleanup of Path1 and Path2 testdirs.
                    Useful for troubleshooting. (default: cleanup)
  \-golden           Store results in the golden directory (default: false)
                    This flag can be used with multiple tests.
  \-debug            Print debug messages
  \-stop\-at NUM      Stop test after given step number. (default: run to the end)
                    Implies \(ga\-no\-compare\(ga and \(ga\-no\-cleanup\(ga, if the test really
                    ends prematurely. Only meaningful for a single test case.
  \-refresh\-times    Force refreshing the target modtime, useful for Dropbox
                    (default: false)
  \-verbose          Run tests verbosely
.EE
.PP
Note: unlike rclone flags which must be prefixed by double dash
(\f[CR]\-\-\f[R]), the test command flags can be equally prefixed by a
single \f[CR]\-\f[R] or double dash.
.SS Running tests
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]go test . \-case basic \-remote local \-remote2 local\f[R] runs
the \f[CR]test_basic\f[R] test case using only the local filesystem,
syncing one local directory with another local directory.
Test script output is to the console, while commands within scenario.txt
have their output sent to the \f[CR].../workdir/test.log\f[R] file,
which is finally compared to the golden copy.
.IP \(bu 2
The first argument after \f[CR]go test\f[R] should be a relative name of
the directory containing bisync source code.
If you run tests right from there, the argument will be \f[CR].\f[R]
(current directory) as in most examples below.
If you run bisync tests from the rclone source directory, the command
should be \f[CR]go test ./cmd/bisync ...\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The test engine will mangle rclone output to ensure comparability with
golden listings and logs.
.IP \(bu 2
Test scenarios are located in \f[CR]./cmd/bisync/testdata\f[R].
The test \f[CR]\-case\f[R] argument should match the full name of a
subdirectory under that directory.
Every test subdirectory name on disk must start with \f[CR]test_\f[R],
this prefix can be omitted on command line for brevity.
Also, underscores in the name can be replaced by dashes for convenience.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]go test . \-remote local \-remote2 local \-case all\f[R] runs all
tests.
.IP \(bu 2
Path1 and Path2 may either be the keyword \f[CR]local\f[R] or may be
names of configured cloud services.
\f[CR]go test . \-remote gdrive: \-remote2 dropbox: \-case basic\f[R]
will run the test between these two services, without transferring any
files to the local filesystem.
.IP \(bu 2
Test run stdout and stderr console output may be directed to a file,
e.g.
\f[CR]go test . \-remote gdrive: \-remote2 local \-case all > runlog.txt 2>&1\f[R]
.SS Test execution flow
.IP "1." 3
The base setup in the \f[CR]initial\f[R] directory of the testcase is
applied on the Path1 and Path2 filesystems (via rclone copy the initial
directory to Path1, then rclone sync Path1 to Path2).
.IP "2." 3
The commands in the scenario.txt file are applied, with output directed
to the \f[CR]test.log\f[R] file in the test working directory.
Typically, the first actual command in the \f[CR]scenario.txt\f[R] file
is to do a \f[CR]\-\-resync\f[R], which establishes the baseline
\f[CR]{...}.path1.lst\f[R] and \f[CR]{...}.path2.lst\f[R] files in the
test working directory (\f[CR].../workdir/\f[R] relative to the
temporary test directory).
Various commands and listing snapshots are done within the test.
.IP "3." 3
Finally, the contents of the test working directory are compared to the
contents of the testcase\(aqs golden directory.
.SS Notes about testing
.IP \(bu 2
Test cases are in individual directories beneath
\f[CR]./cmd/bisync/testdata\f[R].
A command line reference to a test is understood to reference a
directory beneath \f[CR]testdata\f[R].
For example,
\f[CR]go test ./cmd/bisync \-case dry\-run \-remote gdrive: \-remote2 local\f[R]
refers to the test case in
\f[CR]./cmd/bisync/testdata/test_dry_run\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The test working directory is located at \f[CR].../workdir\f[R] relative
to a temporary test directory, usually under \f[CR]/tmp\f[R] on Linux.
.IP \(bu 2
The local test sync tree is created at a temporary directory named like
\f[CR]bisync.XXX\f[R] under system temporary directory.
.IP \(bu 2
The remote test sync tree is located at a temporary directory under
\f[CR]<remote:>/bisync.XXX/\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]path1\f[R] and/or \f[CR]path2\f[R] subdirectories are created in a
temporary directory under the respective local or cloud test remote.
.IP \(bu 2
By default, the Path1 and Path2 test dirs and workdir will be deleted
after each test run.
The \f[CR]\-no\-cleanup\f[R] flag disables purging these directories
when validating and debugging a given test.
These directories will be flushed before running another test,
independent of the \f[CR]\-no\-cleanup\f[R] usage.
.IP \(bu 2
You will likely want to add \f[CR]\- /testdir/\f[R] to your normal
bisync \f[CR]\-\-filters\-file\f[R] so that normal syncs do not attempt
to sync the test temporary directories, which may have
\f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] miscompares in some testcases which would
otherwise trip the \f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] system.
The \f[CR]\-\-check\-access\f[R] mechanism is hard\-coded to ignore
\f[CR]RCLONE_TEST\f[R] files beneath \f[CR]bisync/testdata\f[R], so the
test cases may reside on the synched tree even if there are check file
mismatches in the test tree.
.IP \(bu 2
Some Dropbox tests can fail, notably printing the following message:
\f[CR]src and dst identical but can\(aqt set mod time without deleting and re\-uploading\f[R]
This is expected and happens due to the way Dropbox handles modification
times.
You should use the \f[CR]\-refresh\-times\f[R] test flag to make up for
this.
.IP \(bu 2
If Dropbox tests hit request limit for you and print error message
\f[CR]too_many_requests/...: Too many requests or write operations.\f[R]
then follow the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/dropbox/#get-your-own-dropbox-app-id
Dropbox App ID instructions
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Updating golden results
Sometimes even a slight change in the bisync source can cause little
changes spread around many log files.
Updating them manually would be a nightmare.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-golden\f[R] flag will store the \f[CR]test.log\f[R] and
\f[CR]*.lst\f[R] listings from each test case into respective golden
directories.
Golden results will automatically contain generic strings instead of
local or cloud paths which means that they should match when run with a
different cloud service.
.PP
Your normal workflow might be as follows:
.IP "1." 3
Git\-clone the rclone sources locally
.IP "2." 3
Modify bisync source and check that it builds
.IP "3." 3
Run the whole test suite \f[CR]go test ./cmd/bisync \-remote local\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
If some tests show log difference, recheck them individually, e.g.:
\f[CR]go test ./cmd/bisync \-remote local \-case basic\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
If you are convinced with the difference, goldenize all tests at once:
\f[CR]go test ./cmd/bisync \-remote local \-golden\f[R]
.IP "6." 3
Use word diff:
\f[CR]git diff \-\-word\-diff ./cmd/bisync/testdata/\f[R].
Please note that normal line\-level diff is generally useless here.
.IP "7." 3
Check the difference \f[I]carefully\f[R]!
.IP "8." 3
Commit the change (\f[CR]git commit\f[R]) \f[I]only\f[R] if you are
sure.
If unsure, save your code changes then wipe the log diffs from git:
\f[CR]git reset [\-\-hard]\f[R].
.SS Structure of test scenarios
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<testname>/initial/\f[R] contains a tree of files that will be set
as the initial condition on both Path1 and Path2 testdirs.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<testname>/modfiles/\f[R] contains files that will be used to
modify the Path1 and/or Path2 filesystems.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<testname>/golden/\f[R] contains the expected content of the test
working directory (\f[CR]workdir\f[R]) at the completion of the
testcase.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<testname>/scenario.txt\f[R] contains the body of the test, in the
form of various commands to modify files, run bisync, and snapshot
listings.
Output from these commands is captured to
\f[CR].../workdir/test.log\f[R] for comparison to the golden files.
.SS Supported test commands
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]test <some message>\f[R] Print the line to the console and to the
\f[CR]test.log\f[R]:
\f[CR]test sync is working correctly with options x, y, z\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]copy\-listings <prefix>\f[R] Save a copy of all \f[CR].lst\f[R]
listings in the test working directory with the specified prefix:
\f[CR]save\-listings exclude\-pass\-run\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]move\-listings <prefix>\f[R] Similar to \f[CR]copy\-listings\f[R]
but removes the source
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]purge\-children <dir>\f[R] This will delete all child files and
purge all child subdirs under given directory but keep the parent
intact.
This behavior is important for tests with Google Drive because removing
and re\-creating the parent would change its ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]delete\-file <file>\f[R] Delete a single file.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]delete\-glob <dir> <pattern>\f[R] Delete a group of files located
one level deep in the given directory with names matching a given glob
pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]touch\-glob YYYY\-MM\-DD <dir> <pattern>\f[R] Change modification
time on a group of files.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]touch\-copy YYYY\-MM\-DD <source\-file> <dest\-dir>\f[R] Change
file modification time then copy it to destination.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]copy\-file <source\-file> <dest\-dir>\f[R] Copy a single file to
given directory.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]copy\-as <source\-file> <dest\-file>\f[R] Similar to above but
destination must include both directory and the new file name at
destination.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]copy\-dir <src> <dst>\f[R] and \f[CR]sync\-dir <src> <dst>\f[R]
Copy/sync a directory.
Equivalent of \f[CR]rclone copy\f[R] and \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]list\-dirs <dir>\f[R] Equivalent to
\f[CR]rclone lsf \-R \-\-dirs\-only <dir>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]bisync [options]\f[R] Runs bisync against \f[CR]\-remote\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-remote2\f[R].
.SS Supported substitution terms
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{testdir/}\f[R] \- the root dir of the testcase
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{datadir/}\f[R] \- the \f[CR]modfiles\f[R] dir under the testcase
root
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{workdir/}\f[R] \- the temporary test working directory
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{path1/}\f[R] \- the root of the Path1 test directory tree
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{path2/}\f[R] \- the root of the Path2 test directory tree
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{session}\f[R] \- base name of the test listings
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{/}\f[R] \- OS\-specific path separator
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{spc}\f[R], \f[CR]{tab}\f[R], \f[CR]{eol}\f[R] \- whitespace
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]{chr:HH}\f[R] \- raw byte with given hexadecimal code
.PP
Substitution results of the terms named like \f[CR]{dir/}\f[R] will end
with \f[CR]/\f[R] (or backslash on Windows), so it is not necessary to
include slash in the usage, for example
\f[CR]delete\-file {path1/}file1.txt\f[R].
