.SS Modification times and hashes
.SS Modification times
The modified time is stored as metadata on the object as
\f[CR]X\-Amz\-Meta\-Mtime\f[R] as floating point since the epoch,
accurate to 1 ns.
.PP
If the modification time needs to be updated rclone will attempt to
perform a server side copy to update the modification if the object can
be copied in a single part.
In the case the object is larger than 5Gb or is in Glacier or Glacier
Deep Archive storage the object will be uploaded rather than copied.
.PP
Note that reading this from the object takes an additional
\f[CR]HEAD\f[R] request as the metadata isn\(aqt returned in object
listings.
.SS Hashes
For small objects which weren\(aqt uploaded as multipart uploads
(objects sized below \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-cutoff\f[R] if uploaded with
rclone) rclone uses the \f[CR]ETag:\f[R] header as an MD5 checksum.
.PP
However for objects which were uploaded as multipart uploads or with
server side encryption (SSE\-AWS or SSE\-C) the \f[CR]ETag\f[R] header
is no longer the MD5 sum of the data, so rclone adds an additional piece
of metadata \f[CR]X\-Amz\-Meta\-Md5chksum\f[R] which is a base64 encoded
MD5 hash (in the same format as is required for
\f[CR]Content\-MD5\f[R]).
You can use base64 \-d and hexdump to check this value manually:
.IP
.EX
echo \(aqVWTGdNx3LyXQDfA0e2Edxw==\(aq | base64 \-d | hexdump
.EE
.PP
or you can use \f[CR]rclone check\f[R] to verify the hashes are OK.
.PP
For large objects, calculating this hash can take some time so the
addition of this hash can be disabled with
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-disable\-checksum\f[R].
This will mean that these objects do not have an MD5 checksum.
.PP
Note that reading this from the object takes an additional
\f[CR]HEAD\f[R] request as the metadata isn\(aqt returned in object
listings.
.SS Reducing costs
.SS Avoiding HEAD requests to read the modification time
By default, rclone will use the modification time of objects stored in
S3 for syncing.
This is stored in object metadata which unfortunately takes an extra
HEAD request to read which can be expensive (in time and money).
.PP
The modification time is used by default for all operations that require
checking the time a file was last updated.
It allows rclone to treat the remote more like a true filesystem, but it
is inefficient on S3 because it requires an extra API call to retrieve
the metadata.
.PP
The extra API calls can be avoided when syncing (using
\f[CR]rclone sync\f[R] or \f[CR]rclone copy\f[R]) in a few different
ways, each with its own tradeoffs.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-size\-only\f[R]
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Only checks the size of files.
.IP \(bu 2
Uses no extra transactions.
.IP \(bu 2
If the file doesn\(aqt change size then rclone won\(aqt detect it has
changed.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rclone sync \-\-size\-only /path/to/source s3:bucket\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-checksum\f[R]
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Checks the size and MD5 checksum of files.
.IP \(bu 2
Uses no extra transactions.
.IP \(bu 2
The most accurate detection of changes possible.
.IP \(bu 2
Will cause the source to read an MD5 checksum which, if it is a local
disk, will cause lots of disk activity.
.IP \(bu 2
If the source and destination are both S3 this is the
\f[B]recommended\f[R] flag to use for maximum efficiency.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rclone sync \-\-checksum /path/to/source s3:bucket\f[R]
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-update \-\-use\-server\-modtime\f[R]
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Uses no extra transactions.
.IP \(bu 2
Modification time becomes the time the object was uploaded.
.IP \(bu 2
For many operations this is sufficient to determine if it needs
uploading.
.IP \(bu 2
Using \f[CR]\-\-update\f[R] along with
\f[CR]\-\-use\-server\-modtime\f[R], avoids the extra API call and
uploads files whose local modification time is newer than the time it
was last uploaded.
.IP \(bu 2
Files created with timestamps in the past will be missed by the sync.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rclone sync \-\-update \-\-use\-server\-modtime /path/to/source s3:bucket\f[R]
.RE
.PP
These flags can and should be used in combination with
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] \- see below.
.PP
If using \f[CR]rclone mount\f[R] or any command using the VFS (eg
\f[CR]rclone serve\f[R]) commands then you might want to consider using
the VFS flag \f[CR]\-\-no\-modtime\f[R] which will stop rclone reading
the modification time for every object.
You could also use \f[CR]\-\-use\-server\-modtime\f[R] if you are happy
with the modification times of the objects being the time of upload.
.SS Avoiding GET requests to read directory listings
Rclone\(aqs default directory traversal is to process each directory
individually.
This takes one API call per directory.
Using the \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] flag will read all info about the
objects into memory first using a smaller number of API calls (one per
1000 objects).
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#fast-list
rclone docs
.UE \c
\ for more details.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-fast\-list \-\-checksum /path/to/source s3:bucket
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] trades off API transactions for memory use.
As a rough guide rclone uses 1k of memory per object stored, so using
\f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] on a sync of a million objects will use
roughly 1 GiB of RAM.
.PP
If you are only copying a small number of files into a big repository
then using \f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] is a good idea.
This finds objects directly instead of through directory listings.
You can do a \(dqtop\-up\(dq sync very cheaply by using
\f[CR]\-\-max\-age\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-no\-traverse\f[R] to copy only
recent files, eg
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-max\-age 24h \-\-no\-traverse /path/to/source s3:bucket
.EE
.PP
You\(aqd then do a full \f[CR]rclone sync\f[R] less often.
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] isn\(aqt required in the top\-up
sync.
.SS Avoiding HEAD requests after PUT
By default, rclone will HEAD every object it uploads.
It does this to check the object got uploaded correctly.
.PP
You can disable this with the \-\-s3\-no\-head option \- see there for
more details.
.PP
Setting this flag increases the chance for undetected upload failures.
.SS Increasing performance
.SS Using server\-side copy
If you are copying objects between S3 buckets in the same region, you
should use server\-side copy.
This is much faster than downloading and re\-uploading the objects, as
no data is transferred.
.PP
For rclone to use server\-side copy, you must use the same remote for
the source and destination.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy s3:source\-bucket s3:destination\-bucket
.EE
.PP
When using server\-side copy, the performance is limited by the rate at
which rclone issues API requests to S3.
See below for how to increase the number of API requests rclone makes.
.SS Increasing the rate of API requests
You can increase the rate of API requests to S3 by increasing the
parallelism using \f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-checkers\f[R]
options.
.PP
Rclone uses a very conservative defaults for these settings, as not all
providers support high rates of requests.
Depending on your provider, you can increase significantly the number of
transfers and checkers.
.PP
For example, with AWS S3, if you can increase the number of checkers to
values like 200.
If you are doing a server\-side copy, you can also increase the number
of transfers to 200.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-transfers 200 \-\-checkers 200 \-\-checksum s3:source\-bucket s3:destination\-bucket
.EE
.PP
You will need to experiment with these values to find the optimal
settings for your setup.
.SS Data integrity
Rclone does its best to verify every part of an upload or download to
the s3 provider using various hashes.
.PP
Every HTTP transaction to/from the provider has a
\f[CR]X\-Amz\-Content\-Sha256\f[R] or a \f[CR]Content\-Md5\f[R] header
to guard against corruption of the HTTP body.
The HTTP Header is protected by the signature passed in the
\f[CR]Authorization\f[R] header.
.PP
All communications with the provider is done over https for encryption
and additional error protection.
.SS Single part uploads
.IP \(bu 2
Rclone uploads single part uploads with a \f[CR]Content\-Md5\f[R] using
the MD5 hash read from the source.
The provider checks this is correct on receipt of the data.
.IP \(bu 2
Rclone then does a HEAD request (disable with
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-no\-head\f[R]) to read the \f[CR]ETag\f[R] back which is
the MD5 of the file and checks that with what it sent.
.PP
Note that if the source does not have an MD5 then the single part
uploads will not have hash protection.
In this case it is recommended to use
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-cutoff 0\f[R] so all files are uploaded as
multipart uploads.
.SS Multipart uploads
For files above \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-cutoff\f[R] rclone splits the file
into multiple parts for upload.
.IP \(bu 2
Each part is protected with both an \f[CR]X\-Amz\-Content\-Sha256\f[R]
and a \f[CR]Content\-Md5\f[R]
.PP
When rclone has finished the upload of all the parts it then completes
the upload by sending:
.IP \(bu 2
The MD5 hash of each part
.IP \(bu 2
The number of parts
.IP \(bu 2
This info is all protected with a \f[CR]X\-Amz\-Content\-Sha256\f[R]
.PP
The provider checks the MD5 for all the parts it has received against
what rclone sends and if it is good it returns OK.
.PP
Rclone then does a HEAD request (disable with
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-no\-head\f[R]) and checks the ETag is what it expects (in
this case it should be the MD5 sum of all the MD5 sums of all the parts
with the number of parts on the end).
.PP
If the source has an MD5 sum then rclone will attach the
\f[CR]X\-Amz\-Meta\-Md5chksum\f[R] with it as the \f[CR]ETag\f[R] for a
multipart upload can\(aqt easily be checked against the file as the
chunk size must be known in order to calculate it.
.SS Downloads
Rclone checks the MD5 hash of the data downloaded against either the
ETag or the \f[CR]X\-Amz\-Meta\-Md5chksum\f[R] metadata (if present)
which rclone uploads with multipart uploads.
.SS Further checking
At each stage rclone and the provider are sending and checking hashes of
\f[B]everything\f[R].
Rclone deliberately HEADs each object after upload to check it arrived
safely for extra security.
(You can disable this with \f[CR]\-\-s3\-no\-head\f[R]).
.PP
If you require further assurance that your data is intact you can use
\f[CR]rclone check\f[R] to check the hashes locally vs the remote.
.PP
And if you are feeling ultimately paranoid use
\f[CR]rclone check \-\-download\f[R] which will download the files and
check them against the local copies.
(Note that this doesn\(aqt use disk to do this \- it streams them in
memory).
.SS Versions
When bucket versioning is enabled (this can be done with rclone with the
\f[CR]rclone backend versioning\f[R] command) when rclone uploads a new
version of a file it creates a \c
.UR https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Versioning.html
new version of it
.UE \c
\ Likewise when you delete a file, the old version will be marked hidden
and still be available.
.PP
Old versions of files, where available, are visible using the
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-versions\f[R] flag.
.PP
It is also possible to view a bucket as it was at a certain point in
time, using the \f[CR]\-\-s3\-version\-at\f[R] flag.
This will show the file versions as they were at that time, showing
files that have been deleted afterwards, and hiding files that were
created since.
.PP
If you wish to remove all the old versions then you can use the
\f[CR]rclone backend cleanup\-hidden remote:bucket\f[R] command which
will delete all the old hidden versions of files, leaving the current
ones intact.
You can also supply a path and only old versions under that path will be
deleted, e.g.
\f[CR]rclone backend cleanup\-hidden remote:bucket/path/to/stuff\f[R].
.PP
When you \f[CR]purge\f[R] a bucket, the current and the old versions
will be deleted then the bucket will be deleted.
.PP
However \f[CR]delete\f[R] will cause the current versions of the files
to become hidden old versions.
.PP
Here is a session showing the listing and retrieval of an old version
followed by a \f[CR]cleanup\f[R] of the old versions.
.PP
Show current version and all the versions with
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-versions\f[R] flag.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q ls s3:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt

$ rclone \-q \-\-s3\-versions ls s3:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt
        8 one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141032\-000.txt
       16 one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
       15 one\-v2016\-07\-02\-155621\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
Retrieve an old version
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q \-\-s3\-versions copy s3:cleanup\-test/one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt /tmp

$ ls \-l /tmp/one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\- 1 ncw ncw 16 Jul  2 17:46 /tmp/one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
Clean up all the old versions and show that they\(aqve gone.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q backend cleanup\-hidden s3:cleanup\-test

$ rclone \-q ls s3:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt

$ rclone \-q \-\-s3\-versions ls s3:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt
.EE
.SS Versions naming caveat
When using \f[CR]\-\-s3\-versions\f[R] flag rclone is relying on the
file name to work out whether the objects are versions or not.
Versions\(aq names are created by inserting timestamp between file name
and its extension.
.IP
.EX
        9 file.txt
        8 file\-v2023\-07\-17\-161032\-000.txt
       16 file\-v2023\-06\-15\-141003\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
If there are real files present with the same names as versions, then
behaviour of \f[CR]\-\-s3\-versions\f[R] can be unpredictable.
.SS Cleanup
If you run \f[CR]rclone cleanup s3:bucket\f[R] then it will remove all
pending multipart uploads older than 24 hours.
You can use the \f[CR]\-\-interactive\f[R]/\f[CR]i\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] flag to see exactly what it will do.
If you want more control over the expiry date then run
\f[CR]rclone backend cleanup s3:bucket \-o max\-age=1h\f[R] to expire
all uploads older than one hour.
You can use \f[CR]rclone backend list\-multipart\-uploads s3:bucket\f[R]
to see the pending multipart uploads.
.SS Restricted filename characters
S3 allows any valid UTF\-8 string as a key.
.PP
Invalid UTF\-8 bytes will be \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#invalid-utf8
replaced
.UE \c
, as they can\(aqt be used in XML.
.PP
The following characters are replaced since these are problematic when
dealing with the REST API:
.PP
