.SS Important note about double slashes (\f[CR]//\f[R])
Object keys containing consecutive forward slashes (\f[CR]//\f[R]) are
\f[B]not supported\f[R] by rclone.
.PP
When rclone encounters an object key with \f[CR]//\f[R] (e.g.,
\f[CR]a//b\f[R]), it will normalize the path to use a single slash
(e.g., \f[CR]a/b\f[R]).
This normalization can cause \(dqobject not found\(dq errors when trying
to access the original object, as rclone will look for the normalized
path instead of the actual object key.
.PP
\f[B]Example:\f[R] \- Original S3 object key:
\f[CR]folder//file.txt\f[R] \- rclone interprets it as:
\f[CR]folder/file.txt\f[R] \- Result: \(dqobject not found\(dq error
when trying to access \f[CR]folder//file.txt\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]Workaround:\f[R] Avoid using consecutive forward slashes
(\f[CR]//\f[R]) in S3 object keys when using rclone.
If you have existing objects with \f[CR]//\f[R] in their keys, you will
need to rename them to use single slashes or access them through other
means.
.SS Multipart uploads
rclone supports multipart uploads with S3 which means that it can upload
files bigger than 5 GiB.
.PP
Note that files uploaded \f[I]both\f[R] with multipart upload
\f[I]and\f[R] through crypt remotes do not have MD5 sums.
.PP
rclone switches from single part uploads to multipart uploads at the
point specified by \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-cutoff\f[R].
This can be a maximum of 5 GiB and a minimum of 0 (ie always upload
multipart files).
.PP
The chunk sizes used in the multipart upload are specified by
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-chunk\-size\f[R] and the number of chunks uploaded
concurrently is specified by \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-concurrency\f[R].
.PP
Multipart uploads will use extra memory equal to:
\f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] × \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-concurrency\f[R] ×
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-chunk\-size\f[R].
Single part uploads do not use extra memory.
.PP
Single part transfers can be faster than multipart transfers or slower
depending on your latency from S3 \- the more latency, the more likely
single part transfers will be faster.
.PP
Increasing \f[CR]\-\-s3\-upload\-concurrency\f[R] will increase
throughput (8 would be a sensible value) and increasing
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-chunk\-size\f[R] also increases throughput (16M would be
sensible).
Increasing either of these will use more memory.
The default values are high enough to gain most of the possible
performance without using too much memory.
.SS Buckets and Regions
With Amazon S3 you can list buckets (\f[CR]rclone lsd\f[R]) using any
region, but you can only access the content of a bucket from the region
it was created in.
If you attempt to access a bucket from the wrong region, you will get an
error,
\f[CR]incorrect region, the bucket is not in \(aqXXX\(aq region\f[R].
.SS Authentication
There are a number of ways to supply \f[CR]rclone\f[R] with a set of AWS
credentials, with and without using the environment.
.PP
The different authentication methods are tried in this order:
.IP \(bu 2
Directly in the rclone configuration file (\f[CR]env_auth = false\f[R]
in the config file):
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]access_key_id\f[R] and \f[CR]secret_access_key\f[R] are required.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]session_token\f[R] can be optionally set when using AWS STS.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Runtime configuration (\f[CR]env_auth = true\f[R] in the config file):
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Export the following environment variables before running
\f[CR]rclone\f[R]:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Access Key ID: \f[CR]AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\f[R] or \f[CR]AWS_ACCESS_KEY\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Secret Access Key: \f[CR]AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\f[R] or
\f[CR]AWS_SECRET_KEY\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
Session Token: \f[CR]AWS_SESSION_TOKEN\f[R] (optional)
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Or, use a \c
.UR https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html
named profile
.UE \c
:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
Profile files are standard files used by AWS CLI tools
.IP \(bu 2
By default it will use the profile in your home directory (e.g.
\f[CR]\(ti/.aws/credentials\f[R] on unix based systems) file and the
\(dqdefault\(dq profile, to change set these environment variables or
config keys:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE\f[R] to control which file or the
\f[CR]shared_credentials_file\f[R] config key.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AWS_PROFILE\f[R] to control which profile to use or the
\f[CR]profile\f[R] config key.
.RE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Or, run \f[CR]rclone\f[R] in an ECS task with an IAM role (AWS only).
.IP \(bu 2
Or, run \f[CR]rclone\f[R] on an EC2 instance with an IAM role (AWS
only).
.IP \(bu 2
Or, run \f[CR]rclone\f[R] in an EKS pod with an IAM role that is
associated with a service account (AWS only).
.IP \(bu 2
Or, use \c
.UR https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-process-credentials.html
process credentials
.UE \c
\ to read config from an external program.
.RE
.PP
With \f[CR]env_auth = true\f[R] rclone (which uses the SDK for Go v2)
should support \c
.UR https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/standardized-credentials.html
all authentication methods
.UE \c
\ that the \f[CR]aws\f[R] CLI tool does and the other AWS SDKs.
.PP
If none of these option actually end up providing \f[CR]rclone\f[R] with
AWS credentials then S3 interaction will be non\-authenticated (see the
anonymous access section for more info).
.SS Assume Role (Cross\-Account Access)
If you need to access S3 resources in a different AWS account, you can
use IAM role assumption.
This is useful for cross\-account access scenarios where you have
credentials in one account but need to access resources in another
account.
.PP
To use assume role, configure the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]role_arn\f[R] \- The ARN (Amazon Resource Name) of the IAM role to
assume in the target account.
Format: \f[CR]arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT\-ID:role/ROLE\-NAME\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]role_session_name\f[R] (optional) \- A name for the assumed role
session.
If not specified, rclone will generate one automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]role_session_duration\f[R] (optional) \- Duration for which the
assumed role credentials are valid.
If not specified, AWS default duration will be used (typically 1 hour).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]role_external_id\f[R] (optional) \- An external ID required by the
role\(aqs trust policy for additional security.
This is typically used when the role is accessed by a third party.
.PP
The assume role feature works with both direct credentials
(\f[CR]env_auth = false\f[R]) and environment\-based authentication
(\f[CR]env_auth = true\f[R]).
Rclone will first authenticate using the base credentials, then use
those credentials to assume the specified role.
.PP
Example configuration for cross\-account access:
.IP
.EX
[s3\-cross\-account]
type = s3
provider = AWS
env_auth = true
region = us\-east\-1
role_arn = arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/CrossAccountS3Role
role_session_name = rclone\-session
role_external_id = unique\-role\-external\-id\-12345
.EE
.PP
In this example: \- Base credentials are obtained from the environment
(IAM role, credentials file, or environment variables) \- These
credentials are then used to assume the role
\f[CR]CrossAccountS3Role\f[R] in account \f[CR]123456789012\f[R] \- An
external ID is provided for additional security as required by the
role\(aqs trust policy
.PP
The target role\(aqs trust policy in the destination account must allow
the source account or user to assume it.
Example trust policy:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqVersion\(dq: \(dq2012\-10\-17\(dq,
  \(dqStatement\(dq: [
    {
      \(dqEffect\(dq: \(dqAllow\(dq,
      \(dqPrincipal\(dq: {
        \(dqAWS\(dq: \(dqarn:aws:iam::SOURCE\-ACCOUNT\-ID:root\(dq
      },
      \(dqAction\(dq: \(dqsts:AssumeRole\(dq,
      \(dqCondition\(dq: {
        \(dqStringEquals\(dq: {
          \(dqsts:ExternalID\(dq: \(dqunique\-role\-external\-id\-12345\(dq
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
.EE
.SS S3 Permissions
When using the \f[CR]sync\f[R] subcommand of \f[CR]rclone\f[R] the
following minimum permissions are required to be available on the bucket
being written to:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]ListBucket\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]DeleteObject\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]GetObject\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PutObject\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PutObjectACL\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CreateBucket\f[R] (unless using s3\-no\-check\-bucket)
.PP
When using the \f[CR]lsd\f[R] subcommand, the
\f[CR]ListAllMyBuckets\f[R] permission is required.
.PP
Example policy:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqVersion\(dq: \(dq2012\-10\-17\(dq,
  \(dqStatement\(dq: [
    {
      \(dqEffect\(dq: \(dqAllow\(dq,
      \(dqPrincipal\(dq: {
        \(dqAWS\(dq: \(dqarn:aws:iam::USER_SID:user/USER_NAME\(dq
      },
      \(dqAction\(dq: [
        \(dqs3:ListBucket\(dq,
        \(dqs3:DeleteObject\(dq,
        \(dqs3:GetObject\(dq,
        \(dqs3:PutObject\(dq,
        \(dqs3:PutObjectAcl\(dq
      ],
      \(dqResource\(dq: [
        \(dqarn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME/*\(dq,
        \(dqarn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME\(dq
      ]
    },
    {
      \(dqEffect\(dq: \(dqAllow\(dq,
      \(dqAction\(dq: \(dqs3:ListAllMyBuckets\(dq,
      \(dqResource\(dq: \(dqarn:aws:s3:::*\(dq
    }
  ]
}
.EE
.PP
Notes on above:
.IP "1." 3
This is a policy that can be used when creating bucket.
It assumes that \f[CR]USER_NAME\f[R] has been created.
.IP "2." 3
The Resource entry must include both resource ARNs, as one implies the
bucket and the other implies the bucket\(aqs objects.
.IP "3." 3
When using s3\-no\-check\-bucket and the bucket already exists, the
\f[CR]\(dqarn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME\(dq\f[R] doesn\(aqt have to be
included.
.PP
For reference, \c
.UR https://gist.github.com/ebridges/ebfc9042dd7c756cd101cfa807b7ae2b
here\(aqs an Ansible script
.UE \c
\ that will generate one or more buckets that will work with
\f[CR]rclone sync\f[R].
.SS Key Management System (KMS)
If you are using server\-side encryption with KMS then you must make
sure rclone is configured with
\f[CR]server_side_encryption = aws:kms\f[R] otherwise you will find you
can\(aqt transfer small objects \- these will create checksum errors.
.SS Glacier and Glacier Deep Archive
You can upload objects using the glacier storage class or transition
them to glacier using a \c
.UR http://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/user-guide/create-lifecycle.html
lifecycle policy
.UE \c
\&.
The bucket can still be synced or copied into normally, but if rclone
tries to access data from the glacier storage class you will see an
error like below.
.IP
.EX
2017/09/11 19:07:43 Failed to sync: failed to open source object: Object in GLACIER, restore first: path/to/file
.EE
.PP
The message shows the object\(aqs actual storage class, for example
\f[CR]DEEP_ARCHIVE\f[R].
.PP
In this case you need to \c
.UR http://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/user-guide/restore-archived-objects.html
restore
.UE \c
\ the object(s) in question before accessing object contents.
The restore section below shows how to do this with rclone.
.PP
Note that rclone only speaks the S3 API it does not speak the Glacier
Vault API, so rclone cannot directly access Glacier Vaults.
.SS Object\-lock enabled S3 bucket
According to AWS\(aqs \c
.UR https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-overview.html#object-lock-permission
documentation on S3 Object Lock
.UE \c
:
.RS
.PP
If you configure a default retention period on a bucket, requests to
upload objects in such a bucket must include the Content\-MD5 header.
.RE
.PP
Rclone automatically computes and includes the Content\-MD5 header when
uploading objects with Object Lock parameters set.
No special configuration is needed.
.SS Setting Object Lock retention
Rclone supports setting Object Lock retention on uploaded objects with
the following options:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-object\-lock\-mode\f[R] \- Set the Object Lock mode
(GOVERNANCE or COMPLIANCE)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-object\-lock\-retain\-until\-date\f[R] \- Set the
retention date (RFC3339 format or duration like \f[CR]365d\f[R],
\f[CR]24h\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-object\-lock\-legal\-hold\-status\f[R] \- Set legal hold
(ON or OFF)
.PP
Example \- upload files with 1 year GOVERNANCE retention:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy local:/data remote:bucket \(rs
    \-\-s3\-object\-lock\-mode GOVERNANCE \(rs
    \-\-s3\-object\-lock\-retain\-until\-date 365d
.EE
.SS Copying Object Lock settings from source
When using \f[CR]\-\-metadata\f[R], you can use the special value
\f[CR]copy\f[R] to preserve the source object\(aqs Object Lock settings:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy source:bucket dest:bucket \(rs
    \-\-metadata \(rs
    \-\-s3\-object\-lock\-mode copy \(rs
    \-\-s3\-object\-lock\-retain\-until\-date copy
.EE
.PP
You can also mix copied and explicit values.
For example, to change the mode from COMPLIANCE to GOVERNANCE while
preserving the original retention date:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy source:bucket dest:bucket \(rs
    \-\-metadata \(rs
    \-\-s3\-object\-lock\-mode GOVERNANCE \(rs
    \-\-s3\-object\-lock\-retain\-until\-date copy
.EE
.SS Additional Object Lock options
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-bypass\-governance\-retention\f[R] \- Required to delete
or overwrite objects with GOVERNANCE mode retention before the retention
date expires
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-bucket\-object\-lock\-enabled\f[R] \- Enable Object Lock
when creating a new bucket with \f[CR]rclone mkdir\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-s3\-object\-lock\-set\-after\-upload\f[R] \- Set Object Lock
via separate API calls after upload (for providers that don\(aqt support
Object Lock headers during PUT)
