.SS Backend commands
Here are the commands specific to the s3 backend.
.PP
Run them with:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend COMMAND remote:
.EE
.PP
The help below will explain what arguments each command takes.
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_backend/
backend
.UE \c
\ command for more info on how to pass options and arguments.
.PP
These can be run on a running backend using the rc command \c
.UR https://rclone.org/rc/#backend-command
backend/command
.UE \c
\&.
.SS restore
Restore objects from GLACIER or INTELLIGENT\-TIERING archive tier.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend restore remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This command can be used to restore one or more objects from GLACIER to
normal storage or from INTELLIGENT\-TIERING Archive Access / Deep
Archive Access tier to the Frequent Access tier.
.PP
Usage examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend restore s3:bucket/path/to/ \-\-include /object \-o priority=PRIORITY \-o lifetime=DAYS
rclone backend restore s3:bucket/path/to/directory \-o priority=PRIORITY \-o lifetime=DAYS
rclone backend restore s3:bucket \-o priority=PRIORITY \-o lifetime=DAYS
rclone backend restore s3:bucket/path/to/directory \-o priority=PRIORITY
.EE
.PP
This flag also obeys the filters.
Test first with \-\-interactive/\-i or \-\-dry\-run flags.
.IP
.EX
rclone \-\-interactive backend restore \-\-include \(dq*.txt\(dq s3:bucket/path \-o priority=Standard \-o lifetime=1
.EE
.PP
All the objects shown will be marked for restore, then:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend restore \-\-include \(dq*.txt\(dq s3:bucket/path \-o priority=Standard \-o lifetime=1
.EE
.PP
It returns a list of status dictionaries with Remote and Status keys.
The Status will be OK if it was successful or an error message if not.
.IP
.EX
[
    {
        \(dqStatus\(dq: \(dqOK\(dq,
        \(dqRemote\(dq: \(dqtest.txt\(dq
    },
    {
        \(dqStatus\(dq: \(dqOK\(dq,
        \(dqRemote\(dq: \(dqtest/file4.txt\(dq
    }
]
.EE
.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqdescription\(dq: The optional description for the job.
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqlifetime\(dq: Lifetime of the active copy in days, ignored for
INTELLIGENT\-TIERING storage.
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqpriority\(dq: Priority of restore: Standard|Expedited|Bulk
.SS restore\-status
Show the status for objects being restored from GLACIER or
INTELLIGENT\-TIERING.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend restore\-status remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This command can be used to show the status for objects being restored
from GLACIER to normal storage or from INTELLIGENT\-TIERING Archive
Access / Deep Archive Access tier to the Frequent Access tier.
.PP
Usage examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend restore\-status s3:bucket/path/to/object
rclone backend restore\-status s3:bucket/path/to/directory
rclone backend restore\-status \-o all s3:bucket/path/to/directory
.EE
.PP
This command does not obey the filters.
.PP
It returns a list of status dictionaries:
.IP
.EX
[
    {
        \(dqRemote\(dq: \(dqfile.txt\(dq,
        \(dqVersionID\(dq: \f[B]null\f[R],
        \(dqRestoreStatus\(dq: {
            \(dqIsRestoreInProgress\(dq: \f[B]true\f[R],
            \(dqRestoreExpiryDate\(dq: \(dq2023\-09\-06T12:29:19+01:00\(dq
        },
        \(dqStorageClass\(dq: \(dqGLACIER\(dq
    },
    {
        \(dqRemote\(dq: \(dqtest.pdf\(dq,
        \(dqVersionID\(dq: \f[B]null\f[R],
        \(dqRestoreStatus\(dq: {
            \(dqIsRestoreInProgress\(dq: \f[B]false\f[R],
            \(dqRestoreExpiryDate\(dq: \(dq2023\-09\-06T12:29:19+01:00\(dq
        },
        \(dqStorageClass\(dq: \(dqDEEP_ARCHIVE\(dq
    },
    {
        \(dqRemote\(dq: \(dqtest.gz\(dq,
        \(dqVersionID\(dq: \f[B]null\f[R],
        \(dqRestoreStatus\(dq: {
            \(dqIsRestoreInProgress\(dq: \f[B]true\f[R],
            \(dqRestoreExpiryDate\(dq: \(dqnull\(dq
        },
        \(dqStorageClass\(dq: \(dqINTELLIGENT_TIERING\(dq
    }
]
.EE
.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqall\(dq: If set then show all objects, not just ones with restore
status.
.SS list\-multipart\-uploads
List the unfinished multipart uploads.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend list\-multipart\-uploads remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This command lists the unfinished multipart uploads in JSON format.
.PP
Usage examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend list\-multipart s3:bucket/path/to/object
.EE
.PP
It returns a dictionary of buckets with values as lists of unfinished
multipart uploads.
.PP
You can call it with no bucket in which case it lists all bucket, with a
bucket or with a bucket and path.
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqrclone\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqInitiated\(dq: \(dq2020\-06\-26T14:20:36Z\(dq,
            \(dqInitiator\(dq: {
                \(dqDisplayName\(dq: \(dqXXX\(dq,
                \(dqID\(dq: \(dqarn:aws:iam::XXX:user/XXX\(dq
            },
            \(dqKey\(dq: \(dqKEY\(dq,
            \(dqOwner\(dq: {
                \(dqDisplayName\(dq: \f[B]null\f[R],
                \(dqID\(dq: \(dqXXX\(dq
            },
            \(dqStorageClass\(dq: \(dqSTANDARD\(dq,
            \(dqUploadId\(dq: \(dqXXX\(dq
        }
    ],
    \(dqrclone\-1000files\(dq: [],
    \(dqrclone\-dst\(dq: []
}
.EE
.SS cleanup
Remove unfinished multipart uploads.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend cleanup remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This command removes unfinished multipart uploads of age greater than
max\-age which defaults to 24 hours.
.PP
Note that you can use \-\-interactive/\-i or \-\-dry\-run with this
command to see what it would do.
.PP
Usage examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend cleanup s3:bucket/path/to/object
rclone backend cleanup \-o max\-age=7w s3:bucket/path/to/object
.EE
.PP
Durations are parsed as per the rest of rclone, 2h, 7d, 7w etc.
.PP
Options:
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqmax\-age\(dq: Max age of upload to delete.
.SS cleanup\-hidden
Remove old versions of files.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend cleanup\-hidden remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This command removes any old hidden versions of files on a versions
enabled bucket.
.PP
Note that you can use \-\-interactive/\-i or \-\-dry\-run with this
command to see what it would do.
.PP
Usage example:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend cleanup\-hidden s3:bucket/path/to/dir
.EE
.SS versioning
Set/get versioning support for a bucket.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend versioning remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This command sets versioning support if a parameter is passed and then
returns the current versioning status for the bucket supplied.
.PP
Usage examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend versioning s3:bucket # read status only
rclone backend versioning s3:bucket Enabled
rclone backend versioning s3:bucket Suspended
.EE
.PP
It may return \(dqEnabled\(dq, \(dqSuspended\(dq or \(dqUnversioned\(dq.
Note that once versioning has been enabled the status can\(aqt be set
back to \(dqUnversioned\(dq.
.SS set
Set command for updating the config parameters.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend set remote: [options] [<arguments>+]
.EE
.PP
This set command can be used to update the config parameters for a
running s3 backend.
.PP
Usage examples:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend set s3: [\-o opt_name=opt_value] [\-o opt_name2=opt_value2]
rclone rc backend/command command=set fs=s3: [\-o opt_name=opt_value] [\-o opt_name2=opt_value2]
rclone rc backend/command command=set fs=s3: \-o session_token=X \-o access_key_id=X \-o secret_access_key=X
.EE
.PP
The option keys are named as they are in the config file.
.PP
This rebuilds the connection to the s3 backend when it is called with
the new parameters.
Only new parameters need be passed as the values will default to those
currently in use.
.PP
It doesn\(aqt return anything.
.SS Anonymous access to public buckets
If you want to use rclone to access a public bucket, configure with a
blank \f[CR]access_key_id\f[R] and \f[CR]secret_access_key\f[R].
Your config should end up looking like this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][anons3]\f[R]
type = s3
provider = AWS
.EE
.PP
Then use it as normal with the name of the public bucket, e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd anons3:1000genomes
.EE
.PP
You will be able to list and copy data but not upload it.
.PP
You can also do this entirely on the command line
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd :s3,provider=AWS:1000genomes
.EE
.SS Providers
.SS AWS S3
This is the provider used as main example and described in the
configuration section above.
.SS AWS Directory Buckets
From rclone v1.69 \c
.UR https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/directory-buckets-overview.html
Directory Buckets
.UE \c
\ are supported.
.PP
You will need to set the \f[CR]directory_bucket = true\f[R] config
parameter or use \f[CR]\-\-s3\-directory\-bucket\f[R].
.PP
Note that rclone cannot yet:
.IP \(bu 2
Create directory buckets
.IP \(bu 2
List directory buckets
.PP
See the \-\-s3\-directory\-bucket flag for more info
.SS AWS Snowball Edge
\c
.UR https://aws.amazon.com/snowball/
AWS Snowball
.UE \c
\ is a hardware appliance used for transferring bulk data back to AWS.
Its main software interface is S3 object storage.
.PP
To use rclone with AWS Snowball Edge devices, configure as standard for
an \(aqS3 Compatible Service\(aq.
.PP
If using rclone pre v1.59 be sure to set \f[CR]upload_cutoff = 0\f[R]
otherwise you will run into authentication header issues as the snowball
device does not support query parameter based authentication.
.PP
With rclone v1.59 or later setting \f[CR]upload_cutoff\f[R] should not
be necessary.
.PP
eg.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][snowball]\f[R]
type = s3
provider = Other
access_key_id = YOUR_ACCESS_KEY
secret_access_key = YOUR_SECRET_KEY
endpoint = http://[IP of Snowball]:8080
upload_cutoff = 0
.EE
