.SS Known limitations
These limitations have been observed with HCP version 9.7.x.
.PP
\f[B]Automatic directory objects\f[R]
.PP
HCP automatically creates zero\-byte directory objects (keys ending in
\f[CR]/\f[R]) for every path component when an object is stored.
These directory objects are managed by HCP internally: deleting one via
the S3 API does not remove it but instead creates a delete marker with a
special version ID (\f[CR]\-3\f[R]) that cannot itself be deleted.
As a result, rclone cannot fully purge a bucket on HCP \(em leftover
directory objects will remain after a \f[CR]purge\f[R] or
\f[CR]delete\f[R] operation.
.PP
\f[B]Transparent gzip decompression\f[R]
.PP
HCP \c
.UR https://docs.hitachivantara.com/r/en-us/content-platform/9.6.x/mk-95hcph002/using-the-hitachi-api-for-amazon-s3/working-with-objects/creating-an-object
transparently decompresses
.UE \c
\ objects uploaded with \f[CR]Content\-Encoding: gzip\f[R] before
storing them.
This means the stored object contains the raw (uncompressed) data, while
the \f[CR]Content\-MD5\f[R] sent by rclone was computed over the
compressed bytes.
HCP will reject such uploads with a \f[CR]BadDigest\f[R] error.
To avoid this, do not set \f[CR]content\-encoding: gzip\f[R] in object
metadata when using HCP.
