.SH Backblaze B2
B2 is \c
.UR https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage
Backblaze\(aqs cloud storage system
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Paths are specified as \f[CR]remote:bucket\f[R] (or \f[CR]remote:\f[R]
for the \f[CR]lsd\f[R] command.)
You may put subdirectories in too, e.g.
\f[CR]remote:bucket/path/to/dir\f[R].
.SS Configuration
Here is an example of making a b2 configuration.
First run
.IP
.EX
rclone config
.EE
.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process.
To authenticate you will either need your Account ID (a short hex
number) and Master Application Key (a long hex number) OR an Application
Key, which is the recommended method.
See below for further details on generating and using an Application
Key.
.IP
.EX
No remotes found, make a new one\(rs?
n) New remote
q) Quit config
n/q> n
name> remote
Type of storage to configure.
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / Backblaze B2
   \(rs \(dqb2\(dq
[snip]
Storage> b2
Account ID or Application Key ID
account> 123456789abc
Application Key
key> 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789
Endpoint for the service \- leave blank normally.
endpoint>
Remote config
Configuration complete.
Options:
\- type: b2
\- account: 123456789abc
\- key: 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789
\- endpoint:
Keep this \(dqremote\(dq remote?
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE
.PP
This remote is called \f[CR]remote\f[R] and can now be used like this
.PP
See all buckets
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd remote:
.EE
.PP
Create a new bucket
.IP
.EX
rclone mkdir remote:bucket
.EE
.PP
List the contents of a bucket
.IP
.EX
rclone ls remote:bucket
.EE
.PP
Sync \f[CR]/home/local/directory\f[R] to the remote bucket, deleting any
excess files in the bucket.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive /home/local/directory remote:bucket
.EE
.SS Application Keys
B2 supports multiple \c
.UR https://www.backblaze.com/docs/cloud-storage-application-keys
Application Keys for different access permission to B2 Buckets
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
You can use these with rclone too; you will need to use rclone version
1.43 or later.
.PP
Follow Backblaze\(aqs docs to create an Application Key with the
required permission and add the \f[CR]applicationKeyId\f[R] as the
\f[CR]account\f[R] and the \f[CR]Application Key\f[R] itself as the
\f[CR]key\f[R].
.PP
Note that you must put the \f[I]applicationKeyId\f[R] as the
\f[CR]account\f[R] \(en you can\(aqt use the master Account ID.
If you try then B2 will return 401 errors.
.SS \-\-fast\-list
This remote supports \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] which allows you to use
fewer transactions in exchange for more memory.
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#fast-list
rclone docs
.UE \c
\ for more details.
.SS Modification times
The modification time is stored as metadata on the object as
\f[CR]X\-Bz\-Info\-src_last_modified_millis\f[R] as milliseconds since
1970\-01\-01 in the Backblaze standard.
Other tools should be able to use this as a modified time.
.PP
Modified times are used in syncing and are fully supported.
Note that if a modification time needs to be updated on an object then
it will create a new version of the object.
.SS Restricted filename characters
In addition to the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#restricted-characters
default restricted characters set
.UE \c
\ the following characters are also replaced:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l c c.
T{
Character
T}@T{
Value
T}@T{
Replacement
T}
_
T{
\(rs
T}@T{
0x5C
T}@T{
＼
T}
.TE
.PP
Invalid UTF\-8 bytes will also be \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#invalid-utf8
replaced
.UE \c
, as they can\(aqt be used in JSON strings.
.PP
Note that in 2020\-05 Backblaze started allowing \ characters in file
names.
Rclone hasn\(aqt changed its encoding as this could cause syncs to
re\-transfer files.
If you want rclone not to replace \ then see the
\f[CR]\-\-b2\-encoding\f[R] flag below and remove the
\f[CR]BackSlash\f[R] from the string.
This can be set in the config.
.SS SHA1 checksums
The SHA1 checksums of the files are checked on upload and download and
will be used in the syncing process.
.PP
Large files (bigger than the limit in \f[CR]\-\-b2\-upload\-cutoff\f[R])
which are uploaded in chunks will store their SHA1 on the object as
\f[CR]X\-Bz\-Info\-large_file_sha1\f[R] as recommended by Backblaze.
.PP
For a large file to be uploaded with an SHA1 checksum, the source needs
to support SHA1 checksums.
The local disk supports SHA1 checksums so large file transfers from
local disk will have an SHA1.
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#features
the overview
.UE \c
\ for exactly which remotes support SHA1.
.PP
Sources which don\(aqt support SHA1, in particular \f[CR]crypt\f[R] will
upload large files without SHA1 checksums.
This may be fixed in the future (see \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/1767
#1767
.UE \c
).
.PP
Files sizes below \f[CR]\-\-b2\-upload\-cutoff\f[R] will always have an
SHA1 regardless of the source.
.SS Transfers
Backblaze recommends that you do lots of transfers simultaneously for
maximum speed.
In tests from my SSD equipped laptop the optimum setting is about
\f[CR]\-\-transfers 32\f[R] though higher numbers may be used for a
slight speed improvement.
The optimum number for you may vary depending on your hardware, how big
the files are, how much you want to load your computer, etc.
The default of \f[CR]\-\-transfers 4\f[R] is definitely too low for
Backblaze B2 though.
.PP
Note that uploading big files (bigger than 200 MiB by default) will use
a 96 MiB RAM buffer by default.
There can be at most \f[CR]\-\-transfers\f[R] of these in use at any
moment, so this sets the upper limit on the memory used.
.SS Versions
The default setting of B2 is to keep old versions of files.
This means when rclone uploads a new version of a file it creates a \c
.UR https://www.backblaze.com/docs/cloud-storage-file-versions
new version of it
.UE \c
\&.
Likewise when you delete a file, the old version will be marked hidden
and still be available.
.PP
Whether B2 keeps old versions of files or not can be adjusted on a per
bucket basis using the \(dqLifecycle settings\(dq on the B2 control
panel or when creating the bucket using the \-\-b2\-lifecycle flag or
after creation using the rclone backend lifecycle command.
.PP
You may opt in to a \(dqhard delete\(dq of files with the
\f[CR]\-\-b2\-hard\-delete\f[R] flag which permanently removes files on
deletion instead of hiding them.
.PP
Old versions of files, where available, are visible using the
\f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R] flag.
These can be deleted as required with \f[CR]delete\f[R].
.PP
It is also possible to view a bucket as it was at a certain point in
time, using the \f[CR]\-\-b2\-version\-at\f[R] flag.
This will show the file versions as they were at that time, showing
files that have been deleted afterwards, and hiding files that were
created since.
.PP
If you wish to remove all the old versions, and unfinished large file
uploads, then you can use the \f[CR]rclone cleanup remote:bucket\f[R]
command which will delete all the old versions of files, leaving the
current ones intact.
You can also supply a path and only old versions under that path will be
deleted, e.g.
\f[CR]rclone cleanup remote:bucket/path/to/stuff\f[R].
.PP
Note that \f[CR]cleanup\f[R] will remove partially uploaded files from
the bucket if they are more than a day old.
If you want more control over the expiry date then run
\f[CR]rclone backend cleanup b2:bucket \-o max\-age=1h\f[R] to remove
all unfinished large file uploads older than one hour, leaving old
versions intact.
.PP
If you wish to remove all the old versions, leaving current files and
unfinished large files intact, then you can use the
\f[CR]rclone backend cleanup\-hidden remote:bucket\f[R] command.
You can also supply a path and only old versions under that path will be
deleted, e.g.
\f[CR]rclone backend cleanup\-hidden remote:bucket/path/to/stuff\f[R].
.PP
When you \f[CR]purge\f[R] a bucket, the current and the old versions
will be deleted then the bucket will be deleted.
.PP
However \f[CR]delete\f[R] will cause the current versions of the files
to become hidden old versions.
.PP
Here is a session showing the listing and retrieval of an old version
followed by a \f[CR]cleanup\f[R] of the old versions.
.PP
Show current version and all the versions with
\f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R] flag.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q ls b2:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt

$ rclone \-q \-\-b2\-versions ls b2:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt
        8 one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141032\-000.txt
       16 one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
       15 one\-v2016\-07\-02\-155621\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
Retrieve an old version
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q \-\-b2\-versions copy b2:cleanup\-test/one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt /tmp

$ ls \-l /tmp/one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
\-rw\-rw\-r\-\- 1 ncw ncw 16 Jul  2 17:46 /tmp/one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
Clean up all the old versions and show that they\(aqve gone.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q cleanup b2:cleanup\-test

$ rclone \-q ls b2:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt

$ rclone \-q \-\-b2\-versions ls b2:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt
.EE
.SS Versions naming caveat
When using \f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R] flag rclone is relying on the
file name to work out whether the objects are versions or not.
Versions\(aq names are created by inserting timestamp between file name
and its extension.
.IP
.EX
        9 file.txt
        8 file\-v2023\-07\-17\-161032\-000.txt
       16 file\-v2023\-06\-15\-141003\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
If there are real files present with the same names as versions, then
behaviour of \f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R] can be unpredictable.
.SS Data usage
It is useful to know how many requests are sent to the server in
different scenarios.
.PP
All copy commands send the following 4 requests:
.IP
.EX
/b2api/v4/b2_authorize_account
/b2api/v1/b2_create_bucket
/b2api/v1/b2_list_buckets
/b2api/v1/b2_list_file_names
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]b2_list_file_names\f[R] request will be sent once for every 1k
files in the remote path, providing the checksum and modification time
of the listed files.
As of version 1.33 issue \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/818
#818
.UE \c
\ causes extra requests to be sent when using B2 with Crypt.
When a copy operation does not require any files to be uploaded, no more
requests will be sent.
.PP
Uploading files that do not require chunking, will send 2 requests per
file upload:
.IP
.EX
/b2api/v1/b2_get_upload_url
/b2api/v1/b2_upload_file/
.EE
.PP
Uploading files requiring chunking, will send 2 requests (one each to
start and finish the upload) and another 2 requests for each chunk:
.IP
.EX
/b2api/v1/b2_start_large_file
/b2api/v1/b2_get_upload_part_url
/b2api/v1/b2_upload_part/
/b2api/v1/b2_finish_large_file
.EE
.SS Viewing versions with \-\-b2\-versions
Versions can be viewed with the \f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R] flag.
When it is set rclone will show and act on older versions of files.
For example
.PP
Listing without \f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R]
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q ls b2:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt
.EE
.PP
And with
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q \-\-b2\-versions ls b2:cleanup\-test
        9 one.txt
        8 one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141032\-000.txt
       16 one\-v2016\-07\-04\-141003\-000.txt
       15 one\-v2016\-07\-02\-155621\-000.txt
.EE
.PP
Showing that the current version is unchanged but older versions can be
seen.
These have the UTC date that they were uploaded to the server to the
nearest millisecond appended to them.
.PP
Note that when using \f[CR]\-\-b2\-versions\f[R] no file write
operations are permitted, so you can\(aqt upload files or delete them.
.SS B2 and rclone link
Rclone supports generating file share links for private B2 buckets.
They can either be for a file for example:
.IP
.EX
\&./rclone link B2:bucket/path/to/file.txt
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/to/file.txt?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
.EE
.PP
or if run on a directory you will get:
.IP
.EX
\&./rclone link B2:bucket/path
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
.EE
.PP
you can then use the authorization token (the part of the url from the
\f[CR]?Authorization=\f[R] on) on any file path under that directory.
For example:
.IP
.EX
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/to/file1?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/file2?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
https://f002.backblazeb2.com/file/bucket/path/folder/file3?Authorization=xxxxxxxx
.EE
