.SS Get your own Box App ID
Here is how to create your own Box App ID for rclone:
.IP "1." 3
Go to the \c
.UR https://app.box.com/developers/console
Box Developer Console
.UE \c
\ and login, then click \f[CR]My Apps\f[R] on the sidebar.
Click \f[CR]Create New App\f[R] and select \f[CR]Custom App\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
In the first screen on the box that pops up, you can pretty much enter
whatever you want.
The \f[CR]App Name\f[R] can be whatever.
For \f[CR]Purpose\f[R] choose automation to avoid having to fill out
anything else.
Click \f[CR]Next\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
In the second screen of the creation screen, select
\f[CR]User Authentication (OAuth 2.0)\f[R].
Then click \f[CR]Create App\f[R].
.IP "4." 3
You should now be on the \f[CR]Configuration\f[R] tab of your new app.
If not, click on it at the top of the webpage.
Copy down \f[CR]Client ID\f[R] and \f[CR]Client Secret\f[R], you\(aqll
need those for rclone.
.IP "5." 3
Under \(dqOAuth 2.0 Redirect URI\(dq, add
\f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:53682/\f[R]
.IP "6." 3
For \f[CR]Application Scopes\f[R], select
\f[CR]Read all files and folders stored in Box\f[R] and
\f[CR]Write all files and folders stored in box\f[R] (assuming you want
to do both).
Leave others unchecked.
Click \f[CR]Save Changes\f[R] at the top right.
.SH Cache
The \f[CR]cache\f[R] remote wraps another existing remote and stores
file structure and its data for long running tasks like
\f[CR]rclone mount\f[R].
.PP
It is \f[B]deprecated\f[R] so not recommended for use with new
installations and may be removed at some point.
.SS Status
The cache backend code is working but it currently doesn\(aqt have a
maintainer so there are \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Abug+label%3A%22Remote%3A+Cache%22
outstanding bugs
.UE \c
\ which aren\(aqt getting fixed.
.PP
The cache backend is due to be phased out in favour of the VFS caching
layer eventually which is more tightly integrated into rclone.
.PP
Until this happens we recommend only using the cache backend if you find
you can\(aqt work without it.
There are many docs online describing the use of the cache backend to
minimize API hits and by\-and\-large these are out of date and the cache
backend isn\(aqt needed in those scenarios any more.
