.SS Anonymous FTP
When connecting to a FTP server that allows anonymous login, you can use
the special \(dqanonymous\(dq username.
Traditionally, this user account accepts any string as a password,
although it is common to use either the password \(dqanonymous\(dq or
\(dqguest\(dq.
Some servers require the use of a valid e\-mail address as password.
.PP
Using on\-the\-fly or \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#connection-strings
connection string
.UE \c
\ remotes makes it easy to access such servers, without requiring any
configuration in advance.
The following are examples of that:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf :ftp: \-\-ftp\-host=speedtest.tele2.net \-\-ftp\-user=anonymous \-\-ftp\-pass=$(rclone obscure dummy)
rclone lsf :ftp,host=speedtest.tele2.net,user=anonymous,pass=$(rclone obscure dummy):
.EE
.PP
The above examples work in Linux shells and in PowerShell, but not
Windows Command Prompt.
They execute the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_obscure/
rclone obscure
.UE \c
\ command to create a password string in the format required by the pass
option.
The following examples are exactly the same, except use an already
obscured string representation of the same password \(dqdummy\(dq, and
therefore works even in Windows Command Prompt:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf :ftp: \-\-ftp\-host=speedtest.tele2.net \-\-ftp\-user=anonymous \-\-ftp\-pass=IXs2wc8OJOz7SYLBk47Ji1rHTmxM
rclone lsf :ftp,host=speedtest.tele2.net,user=anonymous,pass=IXs2wc8OJOz7SYLBk47Ji1rHTmxM:
.EE
.SS Implicit TLS
Rlone FTP supports implicit FTP over TLS servers (FTPS).
This has to be enabled in the FTP backend config for the remote, or with
\f[CR]\-\-ftp\-tls\f[R].
The default FTPS port is \f[CR]990\f[R], not \f[CR]21\f[R] and can be
set with \f[CR]\-\-ftp\-port\f[R].
.SS TLS Options
TLS options for Implicit and Explicit TLS can be set using the following
flags which are specific to the FTP backend:
.IP
.EX
\-\-ftp\-no\-check\-certificate     Do not verify the TLS certificate of the server
\-\-ftp\-disable\-tls13            Disable TLS 1.3 (workaround for FTP servers with buggy TLS)
\-\-ftp\-tls\-cache\-size int       Size of TLS session cache for all control and data connections (default 32)
.EE
.PP
However any of the global TLS flags can also be used such as:
.IP
.EX
\-\-ca\-cert stringArray          CA certificate used to verify servers
\-\-client\-cert string           Client SSL certificate (PEM) for mutual TLS auth
\-\-client\-key string            Client SSL private key (PEM) for mutual TLS auth
\-\-no\-check\-certificate         Do not verify the server SSL certificate (insecure)
.EE
.PP
If these need to be put in the config file so they apply to just the FTP
backend then use the \f[CR]override\f[R] syntax, eg
.IP
.EX
override.ca_cert = XXX
override.client_cert = XXX
override.client_key = XXX
.EE
.SS Restricted filename characters
In addition to the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#restricted-characters
default restricted characters set
.UE \c
\ the following characters are also replaced:
.PP
File names cannot end with the following characters.
Replacement is limited to the last character in a file name:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l c c.
T{
Character
T}@T{
Value
T}@T{
Replacement
T}
_
T{
SP
T}@T{
0x20
T}@T{
␠
T}
.TE
.PP
Not all FTP servers can have all characters in file names, for example:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l c.
T{
FTP Server
T}@T{
Forbidden characters
T}
_
T{
proftpd
T}@T{
\f[CR]*\f[R]
T}
T{
pureftpd
T}@T{
\f[CR]\(rs [ ]\f[R]
T}
.TE
