.SS Anonymous Access
For downloads of objects that permit public access you can configure
rclone to use anonymous access by setting \f[CR]anonymous\f[R] to
\f[CR]true\f[R].
With unauthorized access you can\(aqt write or create files but only
read or list those buckets and objects that have public read access.
.SS Application Default Credentials
If no other source of credentials is provided, rclone will fall back to
\c
.UR https://cloud.google.com/video-intelligence/docs/common/auth#authenticating_with_application_default_credentials
Application Default Credentials
.UE \c
\ this is useful both when you already have configured authentication
for your developer account, or in production when running on a google
compute host.
Note that if running in docker, you may need to run additional commands
on your google compute machine \- \c
.UR https://cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/advanced-authentication#gcloud_as_a_docker_credential_helper
see this page
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
Note that in the case application default credentials are used, there is
no need to explicitly configure a project number.
.SS \-\-fast\-list
This remote supports \f[CR]\-\-fast\-list\f[R] which allows you to use
fewer transactions in exchange for more memory.
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#fast-list
rclone docs
.UE \c
\ for more details.
.SS Custom upload headers
You can set custom upload headers with the \f[CR]\-\-header\-upload\f[R]
flag.
Google Cloud Storage supports the headers as described in the \c
.UR https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gsutil/addlhelp/WorkingWithObjectMetadata
working with metadata documentation
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
Cache\-Control
.IP \(bu 2
Content\-Disposition
.IP \(bu 2
Content\-Encoding
.IP \(bu 2
Content\-Language
.IP \(bu 2
Content\-Type
.IP \(bu 2
X\-Goog\-Storage\-Class
.IP \(bu 2
X\-Goog\-Meta\-
.PP
Eg \f[CR]\-\-header\-upload \(dqContent\-Type text/potato\(dq\f[R]
.PP
Note that the last of these is for setting custom metadata in the form
\f[CR]\-\-header\-upload \(dqx\-goog\-meta\-key: value\(dq\f[R]
.SS Modification times
Google Cloud Storage stores md5sum natively.
Google\(aqs \c
.UR https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/gsutil
gsutil
.UE \c
\ tool stores modification time with one\-second precision as
\f[CR]goog\-reserved\-file\-mtime\f[R] in file metadata.
.PP
To ensure compatibility with gsutil, rclone stores modification time in
2 separate metadata entries.
\f[CR]mtime\f[R] uses RFC3339 format with one\-nanosecond precision.
\f[CR]goog\-reserved\-file\-mtime\f[R] uses Unix timestamp format with
one\-second precision.
To get modification time from object metadata, rclone reads the metadata
in the following order: \f[CR]mtime\f[R],
\f[CR]goog\-reserved\-file\-mtime\f[R], object updated time.
.PP
Note that rclone\(aqs default modify window is 1ns.
Files uploaded by gsutil only contain timestamps with one\-second
precision.
If you use rclone to sync files previously uploaded by gsutil, rclone
will attempt to update modification time for all these files.
To avoid these possibly unnecessary updates, use
\f[CR]\-\-modify\-window 1s\f[R].
