.PP
This remote is called \f[CR]remote\f[R] and can now be used like this
.PP
See all the albums in your photos
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd remote:album
.EE
.PP
Make a new album
.IP
.EX
rclone mkdir remote:album/newAlbum
.EE
.PP
List the contents of an album
.IP
.EX
rclone ls remote:album/newAlbum
.EE
.PP
Sync \f[CR]/home/local/images\f[R] to the Google Photos, removing any
excess files in the album.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive /home/local/image remote:album/newAlbum
.EE
.SS Layout
As Google Photos is not a general purpose cloud storage system, the
backend is laid out to help you navigate it.
.PP
The directories under \f[CR]media\f[R] show different ways of
categorizing the media.
Each file will appear multiple times.
So if you want to make a backup of your google photos you might choose
to backup \f[CR]remote:media/by\-month\f[R].
(\f[B]NB\f[R] \f[CR]remote:media/by\-day\f[R] is rather slow at the
moment so avoid for syncing.)
.PP
Note that all your photos and videos will appear somewhere under
\f[CR]media\f[R], but they may not appear under \f[CR]album\f[R] unless
you\(aqve put them into albums.
.IP
.EX
/
\- upload
    \- file1.jpg
    \- file2.jpg
    \- ...
\- media
    \- all
        \- file1.jpg
        \- file2.jpg
        \- ...
    \- by\-year
        \- 2000
            \- file1.jpg
            \- ...
        \- 2001
            \- file2.jpg
            \- ...
        \- ...
    \- by\-month
        \- 2000
            \- 2000\-01
                \- file1.jpg
                \- ...
            \- 2000\-02
                \- file2.jpg
                \- ...
        \- ...
    \- by\-day
        \- 2000
            \- 2000\-01\-01
                \- file1.jpg
                \- ...
            \- 2000\-01\-02
                \- file2.jpg
                \- ...
        \- ...
\- album
    \- album name
    \- album name/sub
\- shared\-album
    \- album name
    \- album name/sub
\- feature
    \- favorites
        \- file1.jpg
        \- file2.jpg
.EE
.PP
There are two writable parts of the tree, the \f[CR]upload\f[R]
directory and sub directories of the \f[CR]album\f[R] directory.
.PP
The \f[CR]upload\f[R] directory is for uploading files you don\(aqt want
to put into albums.
This will be empty to start with and will contain the files you\(aqve
uploaded for one rclone session only, becoming empty again when you
restart rclone.
The use case for this would be if you have a load of files you just want
to once off dump into Google Photos.
For repeated syncing, uploading to \f[CR]album\f[R] will work better.
.PP
Directories within the \f[CR]album\f[R] directory are also writeable and
you may create new directories (albums) under \f[CR]album\f[R].
If you copy files with a directory hierarchy in there then rclone will
create albums with the \f[CR]/\f[R] character in them.
For example if you do
.IP
.EX
rclone copy /path/to/images remote:album/images
.EE
.PP
and the images directory contains
.IP
.EX
images
    \- file1.jpg
    dir
        file2.jpg
    dir2
        dir3
            file3.jpg
.EE
.PP
Then rclone will create the following albums with the following files in
.IP \(bu 2
images
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
file1.jpg
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
images/dir
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
file2.jpg
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
images/dir2/dir3
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
file3.jpg
.RE
.PP
This means that you can use the \f[CR]album\f[R] path pretty much like a
normal filesystem and it is a good target for repeated syncing.
.PP
The \f[CR]shared\-album\f[R] directory shows albums shared with you or
by you.
This is similar to the Sharing tab in the Google Photos web interface.
