.SS Manual configuration
Run \f[CR]rclone config path\f[R] to see the path of current active
config file, usually \f[CR]YOURHOME/.config/rclone/rclone.conf\f[R].
Open it in your favorite text editor, find section for the base remote
and create new section for hasher like in the following examples:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][Hasher1]\f[R]
type = hasher
remote = myRemote:path
hashes = md5
max_age = \f[B]off\f[R]

\f[B][Hasher2]\f[R]
type = hasher
remote = /local/path
hashes = dropbox,sha1
max_age = 24h
.EE
.PP
Hasher takes basically the following parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]remote\f[R] is required
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]hashes\f[R] is a comma separated list of supported checksums (by
default \f[CR]md5,sha1\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]max_age\f[R] \- maximum time to keep a checksum value in the cache
\f[CR]0\f[R] will disable caching completely \f[CR]off\f[R] will cache
\(dqforever\(dq (that is until the files get changed)
.PP
Make sure the \f[CR]remote\f[R] has \f[CR]:\f[R] (colon) in.
If you specify the remote without a colon then rclone will use a local
directory of that name.
So if you use a remote of \f[CR]/local/path\f[R] then rclone will handle
hashes for that directory.
If you use \f[CR]remote = name\f[R] literally then rclone will put files
\f[B]in a directory called \f[CB]name\f[B] located under current
directory\f[R].
.SS Usage
.SS Basic operations
Now you can use it as \f[CR]Hasher2:subdir/file\f[R] instead of base
remote.
Hasher will transparently update cache with new checksums when a file is
fully read or overwritten, like:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy External:path/file Hasher:dest/path
rclone cat Hasher:path/to/file > /dev/null
.EE
.PP
The way to refresh \f[B]all\f[R] cached checksums (even unsupported by
the base backend) for a subtree is to \f[B]re\-download\f[R] all files
in the subtree.
For example, use \f[CR]hashsum \-\-download\f[R] using \f[B]any\f[R]
supported hashsum on the command line (we just care to re\-read):
.IP
.EX
rclone hashsum MD5 \-\-download Hasher:path/to/subtree > /dev/null
rclone backend dump Hasher:path/to/subtree
.EE
.PP
You can print or drop hashsum cache using custom backend commands:
.IP
.EX
rclone backend dump Hasher:dir/subdir
rclone backend drop Hasher:
.EE
.SS Pre\-Seed from a SUM File
Hasher supports two backend commands: generic SUM file \f[CR]import\f[R]
and faster but less consistent \f[CR]stickyimport\f[R].
.IP
.EX
rclone backend import Hasher:dir/subdir SHA1 /path/to/SHA1SUM [\-\-checkers 4]
.EE
.PP
Instead of SHA1 it can be any hash supported by the remote.
The last argument can point to either a local or an
\f[CR]other\-remote:path\f[R] text file in SUM format.
The command will parse the SUM file, then walk down the path given by
the first argument, snapshot current fingerprints and fill in the cache
entries correspondingly.
.IP \(bu 2
Paths in the SUM file are treated as relative to
\f[CR]hasher:dir/subdir\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The command will \f[B]not\f[R] check that supplied values are correct.
You \f[B]must know\f[R] what you are doing.
.IP \(bu 2
This is a one\-time action.
The SUM file will not get \(dqattached\(dq to the remote.
Cache entries can still be overwritten later, should the object\(aqs
fingerprint change.
.IP \(bu 2
The tree walk can take long depending on the tree size.
You can increase \f[CR]\-\-checkers\f[R] to make it faster.
Or use \f[CR]stickyimport\f[R] if you don\(aqt care about fingerprints
and consistency.
.IP
.EX
rclone backend stickyimport hasher:path/to/data sha1 remote:/path/to/sum.sha1
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]stickyimport\f[R] is similar to \f[CR]import\f[R] but works much
faster because it does not need to stat existing files and skips initial
tree walk.
Instead of binding cache entries to file fingerprints it creates
\f[I]sticky\f[R] entries bound to the file name alone ignoring size,
modification time etc.
Such hash entries can be replaced only by \f[CR]purge\f[R],
\f[CR]delete\f[R], \f[CR]backend drop\f[R] or by full re\-read/re\-write
of the files.
