.EE
.SS Read only
This remote is read only \- you can\(aqt upload files to an HTTP server.
.SS Servers without directory listings
Rclone normally needs the HTTP server to return a parseable directory
listing in order to discover files.
However if the path points directly at a single file (i.e.
it does not end with \f[CR]/\f[R] and the initial HEAD request reports
it as a file), rclone will skip the parent directory listing entirely
and access the file directly.
.PP
This means rclone can be used to download individual files on HTTP
servers that have directory listings disabled, as long as you know the
exact URL of each file.
For example, given a server that serves
\f[CR]https://example.com/path/file.txt\f[R] but returns an error or an
unparseable response for \f[CR]https://example.com/path/\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-http\-url https://example.com :http:path/file.txt /tmp/
.EE
.PP
You can use this as a remote in other rclone commands too:
.IP
.EX
rclone hashsum crc32 \-\-http\-url \(dqhttps://getsamplefiles.com\(dq :archive::http:download/zip/sample\-1.zip
.EE
.PP
If you just want to download a file or multiple files by URL then using
\c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_copyurl/
copyurl
.UE \c
\ is more efficient.
.SS Modification times
Most HTTP servers store time accurate to 1 second.
.SS Checksum
No checksums are stored.
.SS Usage without a config file
Since the http remote only has one config parameter it is easy to use
without a config file:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd \-\-http\-url https://beta.rclone.org :http:
.EE
.PP
or:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd :http,url=\(aqhttps://beta.rclone.org\(aq:
.EE
