.SH iCloud Drive and Photos
.SS Configuration
The initial setup for an iCloud backend involves getting a trust
token/session.
This uses your regular Apple ID password plus 2FA, either from a trusted
device prompt or an SMS code sent to a trusted phone number.
.PP
\f[B]IMPORTANT\f[R]: App\-specific passwords are not accepted.
Only use your regular Apple ID password and 2FA.
.PP
This backend serves two Apple services:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]drive\f[R] \- iCloud Drive (default)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]photos\f[R] \- iCloud Photos
.PP
\f[CR]rclone config\f[R] walks you through the token creation.
The trust token is valid for 30 days.
After which you will have to reauthenticate with
\f[CR]rclone reconnect\f[R] or \f[CR]rclone config\f[R].
.SS Authentication
rclone authenticates with Apple using \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secure_Remote_Password_protocol
SRP (Secure Remote Password)
.UE \c
, the same protocol used by the iCloud web interface.
Your password is never sent to Apple\(aqs servers \-\- instead, a
cryptographic proof is exchanged that verifies you know the password
without revealing it.
.PP
The authentication flow is:
.IP "1." 3
rclone initiates a session with Apple\(aqs identity service
.IP "2." 3
An SRP key exchange takes place (your password is used locally to derive
a key)
.IP "3." 3
Apple sends a 2FA prompt to your trusted devices, or lets you request an
SMS code
.IP "4." 3
After you enter the 2FA code, rclone receives a trust token for future
sessions
.PP
Here is an example of how to make a Photos remote called
\f[CR]icloudphotos\f[R].
For iCloud Drive, leave \f[CR]service\f[R] at its default
\f[CR]drive\f[R] value.
First run:
.IP
.EX
rclone config
.EE
.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process:
.IP
.EX
No remotes found, make a new one?
n) New remote
s) Set configuration password
q) Quit config
n/s/q> n
name> icloudphotos
