.SS Authentication
There are a number of ways of supplying credentials for Azure Files
Storage.
Rclone tries them in the order of the sections below.
.SS Env Auth
If the \f[CR]env_auth\f[R] config parameter is \f[CR]true\f[R] then
rclone will pull credentials from the environment or runtime.
.PP
It tries these authentication methods in this order:
.IP "1." 3
Environment Variables
.IP "2." 3
Managed Service Identity Credentials
.IP "3." 3
Azure CLI credentials (as used by the az tool)
.PP
These are described in the following sections
.SS Env Auth: 1. Environment Variables
If \f[CR]env_auth\f[R] is set and environment variables are present
rclone authenticates a service principal with a secret or certificate,
or a user with a password, depending on which environment variable are
set.
It reads configuration from these variables, in the following order:
.IP "1." 3
Service principal with client secret
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_TENANT_ID\f[R]: ID of the service principal\(aqs tenant.
Also called its \(dqdirectory\(dq ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_ID\f[R]: the service principal\(aqs client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET\f[R]: one of the service principal\(aqs client
secrets
.RE
.IP "2." 3
Service principal with certificate
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_TENANT_ID\f[R]: ID of the service principal\(aqs tenant.
Also called its \(dqdirectory\(dq ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_ID\f[R]: the service principal\(aqs client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH\f[R]: path to a PEM or PKCS12
certificate file including the private key.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD\f[R]: (optional) password for
the certificate file.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_SEND_CERTIFICATE_CHAIN\f[R]: (optional) Specifies
whether an authentication request will include an x5c header to support
subject name / issuer based authentication.
When set to \(dqtrue\(dq or \(dq1\(dq, authentication requests include
the x5c header.
.RE
.IP "3." 3
User with username and password
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_TENANT_ID\f[R]: (optional) tenant to authenticate in.
Defaults to \(dqorganizations\(dq.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_ID\f[R]: client ID of the application the user will
authenticate to
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_USERNAME\f[R]: a username (usually an email address)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_PASSWORD\f[R]: the user\(aqs password
.RE
.IP "4." 3
Workload Identity
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_TENANT_ID\f[R]: Tenant to authenticate in
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_CLIENT_ID\f[R]: Client ID of the application the user will
authenticate to
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE\f[R]: Path to projected service account
token file
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]AZURE_AUTHORITY_HOST\f[R]: Authority of an Azure Active Directory
endpoint (default: login.microsoftonline.com).
.RE
.SS Env Auth: 2. Managed Service Identity Credentials
When using Managed Service Identity if the VM(SS) on which this program
is running has a system\-assigned identity, it will be used by default.
If the resource has no system\-assigned but exactly one user\-assigned
identity, the user\-assigned identity will be used by default.
.PP
If the resource has multiple user\-assigned identities you will need to
unset \f[CR]env_auth\f[R] and set \f[CR]use_msi\f[R] instead.
See the \f[CR]use_msi\f[R] section.
.PP
If you are operating in disconnected clouds, or private clouds such as
Azure Stack you may want to set
\f[CR]disable_instance_discovery = true\f[R].
This determines whether rclone requests Microsoft Entra instance
metadata from \f[CR]https://login.microsoft.com/\f[R] before
authenticating.
Setting this to \f[CR]true\f[R] will skip this request, making you
responsible for ensuring the configured authority is valid and
trustworthy.
.SS Env Auth: 3. Azure CLI credentials (as used by the az tool)
Credentials created with the \f[CR]az\f[R] tool can be picked up using
\f[CR]env_auth\f[R].
.PP
For example if you were to login with a service principal like this:
.IP
.EX
az login \-\-service\-principal \-u XXX \-p XXX \-\-tenant XXX
.EE
.PP
Then you could access rclone resources like this:
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf :azurefiles,env_auth,account=ACCOUNT:
.EE
.PP
Or
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf \-\-azurefiles\-env\-auth \-\-azurefiles\-account=ACCOUNT :azurefiles:
.EE
.SS Account and Shared Key
This is the most straight forward and least flexible way.
Just fill in the \f[CR]account\f[R] and \f[CR]key\f[R] lines and leave
the rest blank.
.SS SAS URL
To use it leave \f[CR]account\f[R], \f[CR]key\f[R] and
\f[CR]connection_string\f[R] blank and fill in \f[CR]sas_url\f[R].
.SS Connection String
To use it leave \f[CR]account\f[R], \f[CR]key\f[R] and \(dqsas_url\(dq
blank and fill in \f[CR]connection_string\f[R].
.SS Service principal with client secret
If these variables are set, rclone will authenticate with a service
principal with a client secret.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]tenant\f[R]: ID of the service principal\(aqs tenant.
Also called its \(dqdirectory\(dq ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_id\f[R]: the service principal\(aqs client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_secret\f[R]: one of the service principal\(aqs client
secrets
.PP
The credentials can also be placed in a file using the
\f[CR]service_principal_file\f[R] configuration option.
.SS Service principal with certificate
If these variables are set, rclone will authenticate with a service
principal with certificate.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]tenant\f[R]: ID of the service principal\(aqs tenant.
Also called its \(dqdirectory\(dq ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_id\f[R]: the service principal\(aqs client ID
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_certificate_path\f[R]: path to a PEM or PKCS12 certificate
file including the private key.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_certificate_password\f[R]: (optional) password for the
certificate file.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_send_certificate_chain\f[R]: (optional) Specifies whether
an authentication request will include an x5c header to support subject
name / issuer based authentication.
When set to \(dqtrue\(dq or \(dq1\(dq, authentication requests include
the x5c header.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] \f[CR]client_certificate_password\f[R] must be obscured \-
see \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_obscure/
rclone obscure
.UE \c
\&.
.SS User with username and password
If these variables are set, rclone will authenticate with username and
password.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]tenant\f[R]: (optional) tenant to authenticate in.
Defaults to \(dqorganizations\(dq.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_id\f[R]: client ID of the application the user will
authenticate to
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]username\f[R]: a username (usually an email address)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]password\f[R]: the user\(aqs password
.PP
Microsoft doesn\(aqt recommend this kind of authentication, because
it\(aqs less secure than other authentication flows.
This method is not interactive, so it isn\(aqt compatible with any form
of multi\-factor authentication, and the application must already have
user or admin consent.
This credential can only authenticate work and school accounts; it
can\(aqt authenticate Microsoft accounts.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] \f[CR]password\f[R] must be obscured \- see \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_obscure/
rclone obscure
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Managed Service Identity Credentials
If \f[CR]use_msi\f[R] is set then managed service identity credentials
are used.
This authentication only works when running in an Azure service.
\f[CR]env_auth\f[R] needs to be unset to use this.
.PP
However if you have multiple user identities to choose from these must
be explicitly specified using exactly one of the
\f[CR]msi_object_id\f[R], \f[CR]msi_client_id\f[R], or
\f[CR]msi_mi_res_id\f[R] parameters.
.PP
If none of \f[CR]msi_object_id\f[R], \f[CR]msi_client_id\f[R], or
\f[CR]msi_mi_res_id\f[R] is set, this is equivalent to using
\f[CR]env_auth\f[R].
.SS Federated Identity Credentials
If these variables are set, rclone will authenticate with federated
identity.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]tenant\f[R]: tenant ID of the storage
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]client_id\f[R]: client ID of the application the user will
authenticate to storage
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]msi_client_id\f[R]: managed identity client ID of the application
the user will authenticate to
.PP
By default \(dqapi://AzureADTokenExchange\(dq is used as scope for token
retrieval over MSI.
This token is then exchanged for actual storage token using
\(aqtenant\(aq and \(aqclient_id\(aq.
.SS Azure CLI tool \f[CR]az\f[R]
If \f[CR]use_az\f[R] is set, use \c
.UR https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/
Azure CLI tool \f[CR]az\f[R]
.UE \c
\ as the sole means of authentication.
.PP
Setting this can be useful if you wish to use the \f[CR]az\f[R] CLI on a
host with a System Managed Identity that you do not want to use.
.PP
