.SS Usage
Paths are specified as \f[CR]remote:bucket\f[R] (or \f[CR]remote:\f[R]
for the \f[CR]lsf\f[R] command.)
You may put subdirectories in too, e.g.
\f[CR]remote:bucket/path/to/dir\f[R].
.PP
Once configured you can then use \f[CR]rclone\f[R] like this.
.SS Create a new bucket
Use the \f[CR]mkdir\f[R] command to create new bucket, e.g.
\f[CR]bucket\f[R].
.IP
.EX
rclone mkdir remote:bucket
.EE
.SS List all buckets
Use the \f[CR]lsf\f[R] command to list all buckets.
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf remote:
.EE
.PP
Note the colon (\f[CR]:\f[R]) character at the end of the command line.
.SS Delete a bucket
Use the \f[CR]rmdir\f[R] command to delete an empty bucket.
.IP
.EX
rclone rmdir remote:bucket
.EE
.PP
Use the \f[CR]purge\f[R] command to delete a non\-empty bucket with all
its content.
.IP
.EX
rclone purge remote:bucket
.EE
.SS Upload objects
Use the \f[CR]copy\f[R] command to upload an object.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-progress /home/local/directory/file.ext remote:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-progress\f[R] flag is for displaying progress information.
Remove it if you don\(aqt need this information.
.PP
Use a folder in the local path to upload all its objects.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-progress /home/local/directory/ remote:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.PP
Only modified files will be copied.
.SS List objects
Use the \f[CR]ls\f[R] command to list recursively all objects in a
bucket.
.IP
.EX
rclone ls remote:bucket
.EE
.PP
Add the folder to the remote path to list recursively all objects in
this folder.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone ls remote:bucket
/path/to/dir/
.EE
.PP
Use the \f[CR]lsf\f[R] command to list non\-recursively all objects in a
bucket or a folder.
.IP
.EX
rclone lsf remote:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.SS Download objects
Use the \f[CR]copy\f[R] command to download an object.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-progress remote:bucket/path/to/dir/file.ext /home/local/directory/
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-progress\f[R] flag is for displaying progress information.
Remove it if you don\(aqt need this information.
.PP
Use a folder in the remote path to download all its objects.
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-\-progress remote:bucket/path/to/dir/ /home/local/directory/
.EE
.SS Delete objects
Use the \f[CR]deletefile\f[R] command to delete a single object.
.IP
.EX
rclone deletefile remote:bucket/path/to/dir/file.ext
.EE
.PP
Use the \f[CR]delete\f[R] command to delete all object in a folder.
.IP
.EX
rclone delete remote:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.SS Print the total size of objects
Use the \f[CR]size\f[R] command to print the total size of objects in a
bucket or a folder.
.IP
.EX
rclone size remote:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.SS Sync two Locations
Use the \f[CR]sync\f[R] command to sync the source to the destination,
changing the destination only, deleting any excess files.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive \-\-progress /home/local/directory/ remote:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-progress\f[R] flag is for displaying progress information.
Remove it if you don\(aqt need this information.
.PP
Since this can cause data loss, test first with the
\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] flag to see exactly what would be copied and
deleted.
.PP
The sync can be done also from Storj to the local file system.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive \-\-progress remote:bucket/path/to/dir/ /home/local/directory/
.EE
.PP
Or between two Storj buckets.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive \-\-progress remote\-us:bucket/path/to/dir/ remote\-europe:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.PP
Or even between another cloud storage and Storj.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive \-\-progress s3:bucket/path/to/dir/ storj:bucket/path/to/dir/
.EE
.SS Limitations
\f[CR]rclone about\f[R] is not supported by the rclone Storj backend.
Backends without this capability cannot determine free space for an
rclone mount or use policy \f[CR]mfs\f[R] (most free space) as a member
of an rclone union remote.
.PP
See \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#optional-features
List of backends that do not support rclone about
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/commands/rclone_about/
rclone about
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Known issues
If you get errors like \f[CR]too many open files\f[R] this usually
happens when the default \f[CR]ulimit\f[R] for system max open files is
exceeded.
Native Storj protocol opens a large number of TCP connections (each of
which is counted as an open file).
For a single upload stream you can expect 110 TCP connections to be
opened.
For a single download stream you can expect 35.
This batch of connections will be opened for every 64 MiB segment and
you should also expect TCP connections to be reused.
If you do many transfers you eventually open a connection to most
storage nodes (thousands of nodes).
.PP
To fix these, please raise your system limits.
You can do this issuing a \f[CR]ulimit \-n 65536\f[R] just before you
run rclone.
To change the limits more permanently you can add this to your shell
startup script, e.g.
\f[CR]$HOME/.bashrc\f[R], or change the system\-wide configuration,
usually \f[CR]/etc/sysctl.conf\f[R] and/or
\f[CR]/etc/security/limits.conf\f[R], but please refer to your operating
system manual.
