.SH Union
The \f[CR]union\f[R] backend joins several remotes together to make a
single unified view of them.
.PP
During the initial setup with \f[CR]rclone config\f[R] you will specify
the upstream remotes as a space separated list.
The upstream remotes can either be a local paths or other remotes.
.PP
The attributes \f[CR]:ro\f[R], \f[CR]:nc\f[R] and \f[CR]:writeback\f[R]
can be attached to the end of the remote to tag the remote as \f[B]read
only\f[R], \f[B]no create\f[R] or \f[B]writeback\f[R], e.g.
\f[CR]remote:directory/subdirectory:ro\f[R] or
\f[CR]remote:directory/subdirectory:nc\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]:ro\f[R] means files will only be read from here and never written
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]:nc\f[R] means new files or directories won\(aqt be created here
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]:writeback\f[R] means files found in different remotes will be
written back here.
See the writeback section for more info.
.PP
Subfolders can be used in upstream remotes.
Assume a union remote named \f[CR]backup\f[R] with the remotes
\f[CR]mydrive:private/backup\f[R].
Invoking \f[CR]rclone mkdir backup:desktop\f[R] is exactly the same as
invoking \f[CR]rclone mkdir mydrive:private/backup/desktop\f[R].
.PP
There is no special handling of paths containing \f[CR]..\f[R] segments.
Invoking \f[CR]rclone mkdir backup:../desktop\f[R] is exactly the same
as invoking \f[CR]rclone mkdir mydrive:private/backup/../desktop\f[R].
