.SS Provider notes
See below for notes on specific providers.
.SS Fastmail Files
Use \f[CR]https://webdav.fastmail.com/\f[R] or a subdirectory as the
URL, and your Fastmail email \f[CR]username\(atdomain.tld\f[R] as the
username.
Follow \c
.UR https://www.fastmail.help/hc/en-us/articles/360058752854-App-passwords
this documentation
.UE \c
\ to create an app password with access to \f[CR]Files (WebDAV)\f[R] and
use this as the password.
.PP
Fastmail supports modified times using the \f[CR]X\-OC\-Mtime\f[R]
header.
.SS ownCloud
Click on the settings cog in the bottom right of the page and this will
show the WebDAV URL that rclone needs in the config step.
It will look something like
\f[CR]https://example.com/remote.php/webdav/\f[R].
.PP
ownCloud supports modified times using the \f[CR]X\-OC\-Mtime\f[R]
header.
.SS Nextcloud
This is configured in an identical way to ownCloud.
Note that Nextcloud initially did not support streaming of files
(\f[CR]rcat\f[R]) whereas ownCloud did, but \c
.UR https://github.com/nextcloud/nextcloud-snap/issues/365
this
.UE \c
\ seems to be fixed as of 2020\-11\-27 (tested with rclone v1.53.1 and
Nextcloud Server v19).
.SS ownCloud Infinite Scale
The WebDAV URL for Infinite Scale can be found in the details panel of
any space in Infinite Scale, if the display was enabled in the personal
settings of the user through a checkbox there.
.PP
Infinite Scale works with the chunking \c
.UR https://tus.io
tus
.UE \c
\ upload protocol.
The chunk size is currently fixed 10 MB.
.SS Sharepoint Online
Rclone can be used with Sharepoint provided by OneDrive for Business or
Office365 Education Accounts.
This feature is only needed for a few of these Accounts, mostly
Office365 Education ones.
These accounts are sometimes not verified by the domain owner \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/1975
github#1975
.UE \c
.PP
This means that these accounts can\(aqt be added using the official API
(other Accounts should work with the \(dqonedrive\(dq option).
However, it is possible to access them using webdav.
.PP
To use a sharepoint remote with rclone, add it like this: First, you
need to get your remote\(aqs URL:
.IP \(bu 2
Go \c
.UR https://onedrive.live.com/about/en-us/signin/
here
.UE \c
\ to open your OneDrive or to sign in
.IP \(bu 2
Now take a look at your address bar, the URL should look like this:
\f[CR]https://[YOUR\-DOMAIN]\-my.sharepoint.com/personal/[YOUR\-EMAIL]/_layouts/15/onedrive.aspx\f[R]
.PP
You\(aqll only need this URL up to the email address.
After that, you\(aqll most likely want to add \(dq/Documents\(dq.
That subdirectory contains the actual data stored on your OneDrive.
.PP
Add the remote to rclone like this: Configure the \f[CR]url\f[R] as
\f[CR]https://[YOUR\-DOMAIN]\-my.sharepoint.com/personal/[YOUR\-EMAIL]/Documents\f[R]
and use your normal account email and password for \f[CR]user\f[R] and
\f[CR]pass\f[R].
If you have 2FA enabled, you have to generate an app password.
Set the \f[CR]vendor\f[R] to \f[CR]sharepoint\f[R].
.PP
Your config file should look like this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][sharepoint]\f[R]
type = webdav
url = https://[YOUR\-DOMAIN]\-my.sharepoint.com/personal/[YOUR\-EMAIL]/Documents
vendor = sharepoint
user = YourEmailAddress
pass = encryptedpassword
.EE
.SS Sharepoint with NTLM Authentication
Use this option in case your (hosted) Sharepoint is not tied to OneDrive
accounts and uses NTLM authentication.
.PP
To get the \f[CR]url\f[R] configuration, similarly to the above, first
navigate to the desired directory in your browser to get the URL, then
strip everything after the name of the opened directory.
.PP
Example: If the URL is: \c
.UR https://example.sharepoint.com/sites/12345/Documents/Forms/AllItems.aspx
.UE \c
.PP
The configuration to use would be: \c
.UR https://example.sharepoint.com/sites/12345/Documents
.UE \c
.PP
Set the \f[CR]vendor\f[R] to \f[CR]sharepoint\-ntlm\f[R].
.PP
NTLM uses domain and user name combination for authentication, set
\f[CR]user\f[R] to \f[CR]DOMAIN\(rsusername\f[R].
.PP
Your config file should look like this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][sharepoint]\f[R]
type = webdav
url = https://[YOUR\-DOMAIN]/some\-path\-to/Documents
vendor = sharepoint\-ntlm
user = DOMAIN\(rsuser
pass = encryptedpassword
.EE
.SS Required Flags for SharePoint
As SharePoint does some special things with uploaded documents, you
won\(aqt be able to use the documents size or the documents hash to
compare if a file has been changed since the upload / which file is
newer.
.PP
For Rclone calls copying files (especially Office files such as .docx,
\&.xlsx, etc.)
from/to SharePoint (like copy, sync, etc.), you should append these
flags to ensure Rclone uses the \(dqLast Modified\(dq datetime property
to compare your documents:
.IP
.EX
\-\-ignore\-size \-\-ignore\-checksum \-\-update
.EE
.SS Rclone
Use this option if you are hosting remotes over WebDAV provided by
rclone.
Read rclone serve webdav for more details.
.PP
rclone serve supports modified times using the \f[CR]X\-OC\-Mtime\f[R]
header.
.SS dCache
dCache is a storage system that supports many protocols and
authentication/authorisation schemes.
For WebDAV clients, it allows users to authenticate with username and
password (BASIC), X.509, Kerberos, and various bearer tokens, including
\c
.UR https://www.dcache.org/manuals/workshop-2017-05-29-Umea/000-Final/anupam_macaroons_v02.pdf
Macaroons
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenID_Connect
OpenID\-Connect
.UE \c
\ access tokens.
.PP
Configure as normal using the \f[CR]other\f[R] type.
Don\(aqt enter a username or password, instead enter your Macaroon as
the \f[CR]bearer_token\f[R].
.PP
The config will end up looking something like this.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][dcache]\f[R]
type = webdav
url = https://dcache...
vendor = other
user =
pass =
bearer_token = your\-macaroon
.EE
.PP
There is a \c
.UR https://github.com/sara-nl/GridScripts/blob/master/get-macaroon
script
.UE \c
\ that obtains a Macaroon from a dCache WebDAV endpoint, and creates an
rclone config file.
.PP
Macaroons may also be obtained from the dCacheView
web\-browser/JavaScript client that comes with dCache.
.SS OpenID\-Connect
dCache also supports authenticating with OpenID\-Connect access tokens.
OpenID\-Connect is a protocol (based on OAuth 2.0) that allows services
to identify users who have authenticated with some central service.
.PP
Support for OpenID\-Connect in rclone is currently achieved using
another software package called \c
.UR https://github.com/indigo-dc/oidc-agent
oidc\-agent
.UE \c
\&.
This is a command\-line tool that facilitates obtaining an access token.
Once installed and configured, an access token is obtained by running
the \f[CR]oidc\-token\f[R] command.
The following example shows a (shortened) access token obtained from the
\f[I]XDC\f[R] OIDC Provider.
.IP
.EX
paul\(atcelebrimbor:\(ti$ oidc\-token XDC
eyJraWQ[...]QFXDt0
paul\(atcelebrimbor:\(ti$
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R] Before the \f[CR]oidc\-token\f[R] command will work, the
refresh token must be loaded into the oidc agent.
This is done with the \f[CR]oidc\-add\f[R] command (e.g.,
\f[CR]oidc\-add XDC\f[R]).
This is typically done once per login session.
Full details on this and how to register oidc\-agent with your OIDC
Provider are provided in the \c
.UR https://indigo-dc.gitbooks.io/oidc-agent/
oidc\-agent documentation
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
The rclone \f[CR]bearer_token_command\f[R] configuration option is used
to fetch the access token from oidc\-agent.
.PP
Configure as a normal WebDAV endpoint, using the \(aqother\(aq vendor,
leaving the username and password empty.
When prompted, choose to edit the advanced config and enter the command
to get a bearer token (e.g., \f[CR]oidc\-agent XDC\f[R]).
.PP
The following example config shows a WebDAV endpoint that uses
oidc\-agent to supply an access token from the \f[I]XDC\f[R] OIDC
Provider.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][dcache]\f[R]
type = webdav
url = https://dcache.example.org/
vendor = other
bearer_token_command = oidc\-token XDC
.EE
.SH Yandex Disk
\c
.UR https://disk.yandex.com
Yandex Disk
.UE \c
\ is a cloud storage solution created by \c
.UR https://yandex.com
Yandex
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Configuration
Here is an example of making a yandex configuration.
First run
.IP
.EX
rclone config
.EE
.PP
This will guide you through an interactive setup process:
.IP
.EX
No remotes found, make a new one?
n) New remote
s) Set configuration password
n/s> n
name> remote
Type of storage to configure.
Choose a number from below, or type in your own value
[snip]
XX / Yandex Disk
   \(rs \(dqyandex\(dq
[snip]
Storage> yandex
Yandex Client Id \- leave blank normally.
client_id>
Yandex Client Secret \- leave blank normally.
client_secret>
Remote config
Use web browser to automatically authenticate rclone with remote?
 * Say Y if the machine running rclone has a web browser you can use
 * Say N if running rclone on a (remote) machine without web browser access
If not sure try Y. If Y failed, try N.
y) Yes
n) No
y/n> y
If your browser doesn\(aqt open automatically go to the following link: http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth
Log in and authorize rclone for access
Waiting for code...
Got code
Configuration complete.
Options:
\- type: yandex
\- client_id:
\- client_secret:
\- token: {\(dqaccess_token\(dq:\(dqxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\(dq,\(dqtoken_type\(dq:\(dqOAuth\(dq,\(dqexpiry\(dq:\(dq2016\-12\-29T12:27:11.362788025Z\(dq}
Keep this \(dqremote\(dq remote?
y) Yes this is OK
e) Edit this remote
d) Delete this remote
y/e/d> y
.EE
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/remote_setup/
remote setup docs
.UE \c
\ for how to set it up on a machine without an internet\-connected web
browser available.
.PP
Note that rclone runs a webserver on your local machine to collect the
token as returned from Yandex Disk.
This only runs from the moment it opens your browser to the moment you
get back the verification code.
This is on \f[CR]http://127.0.0.1:53682/\f[R] and this it may require
you to unblock it temporarily if you are running a host firewall.
.PP
Once configured you can then use \f[CR]rclone\f[R] like this (replace
\f[CR]remote\f[R] with the name you gave your remote):
.PP
See top level directories
.IP
.EX
rclone lsd remote:
.EE
.PP
Make a new directory
.IP
.EX
rclone mkdir remote:directory
.EE
.PP
List the contents of a directory
.IP
.EX
rclone ls remote:directory
.EE
.PP
Sync \f[CR]/home/local/directory\f[R] to the remote path, deleting any
excess files in the path.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive /home/local/directory remote:directory
.EE
.PP
Yandex paths may be as deep as required, e.g.
\f[CR]remote:directory/subdirectory\f[R].
.SS Modification times and hashes
Modified times are supported and are stored accurate to 1 ns in custom
metadata called \f[CR]rclone_modified\f[R] in RFC3339 with nanoseconds
format.
.PP
The MD5 hash algorithm is natively supported by Yandex Disk.
.SS Emptying Trash
If you wish to empty your trash you can use the
\f[CR]rclone cleanup remote:\f[R] command which will permanently delete
all your trashed files.
This command does not take any path arguments.
.SS Quota information
To view your current quota you can use the
\f[CR]rclone about remote:\f[R] command which will display your usage
limit (quota) and the current usage.
.SS Restricted filename characters
The \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#restricted-characters
default restricted characters set
.UE \c
\ are replaced.
.PP
Invalid UTF\-8 bytes will also be \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#invalid-utf8
replaced
.UE \c
, as they can\(aqt be used in JSON strings.
.SS Application folder
If you registered your own OAuth application with Yandex and only
granted it the \(dqApplication Folder\(dq permission
(\f[CR]cloud_api:disk.app_folder\f[R]) instead of full disk access, then
you must set the \f[CR]app_folder\f[R] option to \f[CR]true\f[R].
Yandex Disk requires paths to be addressed with an \f[CR]app:/\f[R]
prefix instead of the usual \f[CR]disk:/\f[R] prefix when using this
restricted scope, and rclone will return a 403 Forbidden error on every
request if this option isn\(aqt set correctly for the token in use.
