.SS Paths on Windows
On Windows there are many ways of specifying a path to a file system
resource.
Local paths can be absolute, like
\f[CR]C:\(rspath\(rsto\(rswherever\f[R], or relative, like
\f[CR]..\(rswherever\f[R].
Network paths in UNC format, \f[CR]\(rs\(rsserver\(rsshare\f[R], are
also supported.
Path separator can be either \f[CR]\(rs\f[R] (as in
\f[CR]C:\(rspath\(rsto\(rswherever\f[R]) or \f[CR]/\f[R] (as in
\f[CR]C:/path/to/wherever\f[R]).
Length of these paths are limited to 259 characters for files and 247
characters for directories, but there is an alternative extended\-length
path format increasing the limit to (approximately) 32,767 characters.
This format requires absolute paths and the use of prefix
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rs\f[R], e.g.
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rsD:\(rssome\(rsvery\(rslong\(rspath\f[R].
For convenience rclone will automatically convert regular paths into the
corresponding extended\-length paths, so in most cases you do not have
to worry about this (read more below).
Using the same prefix \f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rs\f[R] it is also possible to
specify path to volumes identified by their GUID, e.g.
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rsVolume{b75e2c83\-0000\-0000\-0000\-602f00000000}\(rssome\(rspath\f[R].
.SS Long paths
Rclone handles long paths automatically, by converting all paths to \c
.UR https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/maximum-file-path-limitation
extended\-length path format
.UE \c
, which allows paths up to 32,767 characters.
.PP
This conversion will ensure paths are absolute and prefix them with the
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rs\f[R].
This is why you will see that your paths, for instance
\f[CR].\(rsfiles\f[R] is shown as path
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rsC:\(rsfiles\f[R] in the output, and
\f[CR]\(rs\(rsserver\(rsshare\f[R] as
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs?\(rsUNC\(rsserver\(rsshare\f[R].
.PP
However, in rare cases this may cause problems with buggy file system
drivers like \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues/261
EncFS
.UE \c
\&.
To disable UNC conversion globally, add this to your
\f[CR].rclone.conf\f[R] file:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][local]\f[R]
nounc = \f[B]true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
If you want to selectively disable UNC, you can add it to a separate
entry like this:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][nounc]\f[R]
type = local
nounc = \f[B]true\f[R]
.EE
.PP
And use rclone like this:
.PP
\f[CR]rclone copy c:\(rssrc nounc:z:\(rsdst\f[R]
.PP
This will use UNC paths on \f[CR]c:\(rssrc\f[R] but not on
\f[CR]z:\(rsdst\f[R].
Of course this will cause problems if the absolute path length of a file
exceeds 259 characters on z, so only use this option if you have to.
.SS Symlinks / Junction points
Normally rclone will ignore symlinks or junction points (which behave
like symlinks under Windows).
.PP
If you supply \f[CR]\-\-copy\-links\f[R] or \f[CR]\-L\f[R] then rclone
will follow the symlink and copy the pointed to file or directory.
Note that this flag is incompatible with \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] /
\f[CR]\-l\f[R].
.PP
This flag applies to all commands.
.PP
For example, supposing you have a directory structure like this
.IP
.EX
$ tree /tmp/a
/tmp/a
├── b \-> ../b
├── expected \-> ../expected
├── one
└── two
    └── three
.EE
.PP
Then you can see the difference with and without the flag like this
.IP
.EX
$ rclone ls /tmp/a
        6 one
        6 two/three
.EE
.PP
and
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-L ls /tmp/a
     4174 expected
        6 one
        6 two/three
        6 b/two
        6 b/one
.EE
.PP
Symlinks which point to a parent directory would loop forever, so rclone
reports them as errors and skips them.
.SS \-\-local\-links, \-\-links, \-l
Normally rclone will ignore symlinks or junction points (which behave
like symlinks under Windows).
.PP
If you supply this flag then rclone will copy symbolic links from the
local storage, and store them as text files, with a
\f[CR].rclonelink\f[R] suffix in the remote storage.
.PP
The text file will contain the target of the symbolic link (see
example).
.PP
This flag applies to all commands.
.PP
For example, supposing you have a directory structure like this
.IP
.EX
$ tree /tmp/a
/tmp/a
├── file1 \-> ./file4
└── file2 \-> /home/user/file3
.EE
.PP
Copying the entire directory with \(aq\-l\(aq
.IP
.EX
rclone copy \-l /tmp/a/ remote:/tmp/a/
.EE
.PP
The remote files are created with a \f[CR].rclonelink\f[R] suffix
.IP
.EX
$ rclone ls remote:/tmp/a
       5 file1.rclonelink
      14 file2.rclonelink
.EE
.PP
The remote files will contain the target of the symbolic links
.IP
.EX
$ rclone cat remote:/tmp/a/file1.rclonelink
\&./file4

$ rclone cat remote:/tmp/a/file2.rclonelink
/home/user/file3
.EE
.PP
Copying them back with \(aq\-l\(aq
.IP
.EX
$ rclone copy \-l remote:/tmp/a/ /tmp/b/

$ tree /tmp/b
/tmp/b
├── file1 \-> ./file4
└── file2 \-> /home/user/file3
.EE
.PP
A \f[CR].rclonelink\f[R] file whose contents are too long to be a path
(more than 128 KiB) is refused rather than turned into a symlink.
.PP
However, if copied back without \(aq\-l\(aq
.IP
.EX
$ rclone copyto remote:/tmp/a/ /tmp/b/

$ tree /tmp/b
/tmp/b
├── file1.rclonelink
└── file2.rclonelink
.EE
.PP
If you want to copy a single file with \f[CR]\-l\f[R] then you must use
the \f[CR].rclonelink\f[R] suffix.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone copy \-l remote:/tmp/a/file1.rclonelink /tmp/c

$ tree /tmp/c
/tmp/c
└── file1 \-> ./file4
.EE
.PP
Note that \f[CR]\-\-local\-links\f[R] just enables this feature for the
local backend.
\f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] and \f[CR]\-l\f[R] enable the feature for all
supported backends and the VFS.
.PP
Note that this flag is incompatible with \f[CR]\-copy\-links\f[R] /
\f[CR]\-L\f[R].
.SS Symlink targets and the destination
When rclone recreates a \f[CR].rclonelink\f[R] file as a symlink on
local storage, the symlink can point anywhere \- including, with an
absolute path or one using \f[CR]../\f[R] \- to a location outside the
directory you are copying into.
This is normal \- rclone reproduces whatever target the link had, so
backups round\-trip faithfully.
.PP
What rclone will \f[B]not\f[R] do is \f[I]write through\f[R] such a
link.
If a remote you are copying from contains both a symlink and a file or
directory that would be placed inside it \- for example a
\f[CR]dir.rclonelink\f[R] pointing somewhere outside the destination,
alongside a \f[CR]dir/file.txt\f[R] \- rclone refuses to follow the
symlink when writing \f[CR]dir/file.txt\f[R].
The offending file is skipped with an error, the rest of the transfer
continues, and the skipped file is counted in the error summary printed
at the end of the run.
.PP
This protects you from a malicious or compromised remote using
\f[CR]\-l\f[R] / \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] to plant a symlink and then write
through it to somewhere outside your destination.
Ordinary symlink round\-trips, and symlinks that stay inside the
destination, are unaffected.
.PP
If you have intentionally pre\-created a symlinked directory inside your
destination and want rclone to write into the directory it points at, do
not use \f[CR]\-l\f[R] / \f[CR]\-\-links\f[R] for that copy, or remove
the symlink first.
.SS Restricting filesystems with \-\-one\-file\-system
Normally rclone will recurse through filesystems as mounted.
.PP
However if you set \f[CR]\-\-one\-file\-system\f[R] or \f[CR]\-x\f[R]
this tells rclone to stay in the filesystem specified by the root and
not to recurse into different file systems.
.PP
For example if you have a directory hierarchy like this
.IP
.EX
root
├── disk1     \- disk1 mounted on the root
│\ \  └── file3 \- stored on disk1
├── disk2     \- disk2 mounted on the root
│\ \  └── file4 \- stored on disk12
├── file1     \- stored on the root disk
└── file2     \- stored on the root disk
.EE
.PP
Using \f[CR]rclone \-\-one\-file\-system copy root remote:\f[R] will
only copy \f[CR]file1\f[R] and \f[CR]file2\f[R].
E.g.
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q \-\-one\-file\-system ls root
        0 file1
        0 file2
.EE
.IP
.EX
$ rclone \-q ls root
        0 disk1/file3
        0 disk2/file4
        0 file1
        0 file2
.EE
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] Rclone (like most unix tools such as \f[CR]du\f[R],
\f[CR]rsync\f[R] and \f[CR]tar\f[R]) treats a bind mount to the same
device as being on the same filesystem.
.PP
\f[B]NB\f[R] This flag is only available on Unix based systems.
On systems where it isn\(aqt supported (e.g.
Windows) it will be ignored.
