.SH Bugs and Limitations
.SS Limitations
.SS Directory timestamps aren\(aqt preserved on some backends
As of \f[CR]v1.66\f[R], rclone supports syncing directory modtimes, if
the backend supports it.
Some backends do not support it \-\- see \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/
overview
.UE \c
\ for a complete list.
Additionally, note that empty directories are not synced by default
(this can be enabled with \f[CR]\-\-create\-empty\-src\-dirs\f[R].)
.SS Rclone struggles with millions of files in a directory/bucket
Currently rclone loads each directory/bucket entirely into memory before
using it.
Since each rclone object takes 0.5k\-1k of memory this can take a very
long time and use a large amount of memory.
.PP
Millions of files in a directory tends to occur on bucket\-based remotes
(e.g.
S3 buckets) since those remotes do not segregate subdirectories within
the bucket.
.SS Bucket\-based remotes and folders
Bucket\-based remotes (e.g.
S3/GCS/Swift/B2) do not have a concept of directories.
Rclone therefore cannot create directories in them which means that
empty directories on a bucket\-based remote will tend to disappear.
.PP
Some software creates empty keys ending in \f[CR]/\f[R] as directory
markers.
Rclone doesn\(aqt do this as it potentially creates more objects and
costs more.
This ability may be added in the future (probably via a flag/option).
.SS Bugs
Bugs are stored in rclone\(aqs GitHub project:
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Abug
Reported bugs
.UE \c
.IP \(bu 2
\c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+milestone%3A%22Known+Problem%22
Known issues
.UE \c
.SH Frequently Asked Questions
.SS Do all cloud storage systems support all rclone commands
Yes they do.
All the rclone commands (e.g.
\f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]copy\f[R], etc.)
will work on all the remote storage systems.
.SS Can I copy the config from one machine to another
Sure!
Rclone stores all of its config in a single file.
If you want to find this file, run \f[CR]rclone config file\f[R] which
will tell you where it is.
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/remote_setup/
remote setup docs
.UE \c
\ for more info.
.SS How do I configure rclone on a remote / headless box with no browser?
This has now been documented in its own \c
.UR https://rclone.org/remote_setup/
remote setup page
.UE \c
\&.
.SS How can I get rid of the \(dqConfig file not found\(dq notice?
If you see a notice like \(aqNOTICE: Config file \(dqrclone.conf\(dq not
found\(aq, this means you have not configured any remotes.
.PP
If you need to configure a remote, see the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#configure
config help docs
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
If you are using rclone entirely with \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#backend-path-to-dir
on the fly remotes
.UE \c
, you can create an empty config file to get rid of this notice, for
example:
.IP
.EX
rclone config touch
.EE
.SS Can rclone sync directly from drive to s3
Rclone can sync between two remote cloud storage systems just fine.
.PP
Note that it effectively downloads the file and uploads it again, so the
node running rclone would need to have lots of bandwidth.
.PP
The syncs would be incremental (on a file by file basis).
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
rclone sync \-\-interactive drive:Folder s3:bucket
.EE
.SS Using rclone from multiple locations at the same time
You can use rclone from multiple places at the same time if you choose
different subdirectory for the output, e.g.
.IP
.EX
Server A> rclone sync \-\-interactive /tmp/whatever remote:ServerA
Server B> rclone sync \-\-interactive /tmp/whatever remote:ServerB
.EE
.PP
If you sync to the same directory then you should use rclone copy
otherwise the two instances of rclone may delete each other\(aqs files,
e.g.
.IP
.EX
Server A> rclone copy /tmp/whatever remote:Backup
Server B> rclone copy /tmp/whatever remote:Backup
.EE
.PP
The file names you upload from Server A and Server B should be different
in this case, otherwise some file systems (e.g.
Drive) may make duplicates.
.SS Why doesn\(aqt rclone support partial transfers / binary diffs like rsync?
Rclone stores each file you transfer as a native object on the remote
cloud storage system.
This means that you can see the files you upload as expected using
alternative access methods (e.g.
using the Google Drive web interface).
There is a 1:1 mapping between files on your hard disk and objects
created in the cloud storage system.
.PP
Cloud storage systems (at least none I\(aqve come across yet) don\(aqt
support partially uploading an object.
You can\(aqt take an existing object, and change some bytes in the
middle of it.
.PP
It would be possible to make a sync system which stored binary diffs
like rsync does, instead of whole objects, but that would break the 1:1
mapping of files on your hard disk to objects in the remote cloud
storage system.
.PP
All the cloud storage systems support partial downloads of content, so
it would be possible to make partial downloads work.
However to make this work efficiently this would require storing a
significant amount of metadata, which breaks the desired 1:1 mapping of
files to objects.
.SS Can rclone do bi\-directional sync?
Yes, since rclone v1.58.0, \c
.UR https://rclone.org/bisync/
bidirectional cloud sync
.UE \c
\ is available.
.SS Can I use rclone with an HTTP proxy?
Yes.
rclone will follow the standard environment variables for proxies,
similar to cURL and other programs.
.PP
In general the variables are called \f[CR]http_proxy\f[R] (for services
reached over \f[CR]http\f[R]) and \f[CR]https_proxy\f[R] (for services
reached over \f[CR]https\f[R]).
Most public services will be using \f[CR]https\f[R], but you may wish to
set both.
.PP
The content of the variable is \f[CR]protocol://server:port\f[R].
The protocol value is the one used to talk to the proxy server, itself,
and is commonly either \f[CR]http\f[R] or \f[CR]socks5\f[R].
.PP
Slightly annoyingly, there is no \f[I]standard\f[R] for the name; some
applications may use \f[CR]http_proxy\f[R] but another one
\f[CR]HTTP_PROXY\f[R].
The \f[CR]Go\f[R] libraries used by \f[CR]rclone\f[R] will try both
variations, but you may wish to set all possibilities.
So, on Linux, you may end up with code similar to
.IP
.EX
export http_proxy=http://proxyserver:12345
export https_proxy=$http_proxy
export HTTP_PROXY=$http_proxy
export HTTPS_PROXY=$http_proxy
.EE
.PP
Note: If the proxy server requires a username and password, then use
.IP
.EX
export http_proxy=http://username:password\(atproxyserver:12345
export https_proxy=$http_proxy
export HTTP_PROXY=$http_proxy
export HTTPS_PROXY=$http_proxy
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]NO_PROXY\f[R] allows you to disable the proxy for specific
hosts.
Hosts must be comma separated, and can contain domains or parts.
For instance \(dqfoo.com\(dq also matches \(dqbar.foo.com\(dq.
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
export no_proxy=localhost,127.0.0.0/8,my.host.name
export NO_PROXY=$no_proxy
.EE
.PP
Note that the FTP backend does not support \f[CR]ftp_proxy\f[R] yet.
.PP
You can use the command line argument \f[CR]\-\-http\-proxy\f[R] to set
the proxy, and in turn use an override in the config file if you want it
set for a single backend, eg \f[CR]override.http_proxy = http://...\f[R]
in the config file.
.PP
The FTP and SFTP backends have their own \f[CR]http_proxy\f[R] settings
to support an HTTP CONNECT proxy ( \c
.UR https://rclone.org/ftp/#ftp-http-proxy
\-\-ftp\-http\-proxy
.UE \c
\ and \c
.UR https://rclone.org/ftp/#sftp-http-proxy
\-\-sftp\-http\-proxy
.UE \c
\ )
.SS Rclone gives x509 SSL root certificates error
\f[CR]x509: failed to load system roots and no roots provided\f[R] means
that \f[CR]rclone\f[R] can\(aqt find the SSL root certificates.
Likely you are running \f[CR]rclone\f[R] on a NAS with a cut\-down Linux
OS, or possibly on Solaris.
.PP
\f[CR]x509: certificate signed by unknown authority\f[R] error may occur
on outdated systems, where \f[CR]rclone\f[R] can\(aqt verify the server
with the SSL root certificates.
.PP
Rclone (via the Go runtime) tries to load the root certificates from
these places on Linux.
.IP
.EX
\(dq/etc/ssl/certs/ca\-certificates.crt\(dq, // Debian/Ubuntu/Gentoo etc.
\(dq/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca\-bundle.crt\(dq,   // Fedora/RHEL
\(dq/etc/ssl/ca\-bundle.pem\(dq,             // OpenSUSE
\(dq/etc/pki/tls/cacert.pem\(dq,            // OpenELEC
.EE
.PP
So doing something like this should fix the problem.
It also sets the time which is important for SSL to work properly.
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p /etc/ssl/certs/
curl \-o /etc/ssl/certs/ca\-certificates.crt https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bagder/ca\-bundle/master/ca\-bundle.crt
ntpclient \-s \-h pool.ntp.org
.EE
.PP
The two environment variables \f[CR]SSL_CERT_FILE\f[R] and
\f[CR]SSL_CERT_DIR\f[R], mentioned in the \c
.UR https://godoc.org/crypto/x509
x509 package
.UE \c
, provide an additional way to provide the SSL root certificates on Unix
systems other than macOS.
.PP
Note that you may need to add the \f[CR]\-\-insecure\f[R] option to the
\f[CR]curl\f[R] command line if it doesn\(aqt work without.
.IP
.EX
curl \-\-insecure \-o /etc/ssl/certs/ca\-certificates.crt https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bagder/ca\-bundle/master/ca\-bundle.crt
.EE
.PP
On macOS, you can install \c
.UR https://formulae.brew.sh/formula/ca-certificates
ca\-certificates
.UE \c
\ with Homebrew, and specify the SSL root certificates with the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#ca-cert-stringarray
\-\-ca\-cert
.UE \c
\ flag.
.IP
.EX
brew install ca\-certificates
find $(brew \-\-prefix)/etc/ca\-certificates \-type f
.EE
.SS Rclone gives Failed to load config file: function not implemented error
Likely this means that you are running rclone on Linux version not
supported by the go runtime, ie earlier than version 2.6.23.
.PP
See the \c
.UR https://golang.org/doc/install
system requirements section in the go install docs
.UE \c
\ for full details.
.SS All my uploaded docx/xlsx/pptx files appear as archive/zip
This is caused by uploading these files from a Windows computer which
hasn\(aqt got the Microsoft Office suite installed.
The easiest way to fix is to install the Word viewer and the Microsoft
Office Compatibility Pack for Word, Excel, and PowerPoint 2007 and later
versions\(aq file formats
.SS tcp lookup some.domain.com no such host
This happens when rclone cannot resolve a domain.
Please check that your DNS setup is generally working, e.g.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# both should print a long list of possible IP addresses\f[R]
dig www.googleapis.com          \f[I]# resolve using your default DNS\f[R]
dig www.googleapis.com \(at8.8.8.8 \f[I]# resolve with Google\(aqs DNS server\f[R]
.EE
.PP
If you are using \f[CR]systemd\-resolved\f[R] (default on Arch Linux),
ensure it is at version 233 or higher.
Previous releases contain a bug which causes not all domains to be
resolved properly.
.PP
The Go resolver decision can be influenced with the
\f[CR]GODEBUG=netdns=...\f[R] environment variable.
This also allows to resolve certain issues with DNS resolution.
On Windows or MacOS systems, try forcing use of the internal Go resolver
by setting \f[CR]GODEBUG=netdns=go\f[R] at runtime.
On other systems (Linux, *BSD, etc) try forcing use of the system name
resolver by setting \f[CR]GODEBUG=netdns=cgo\f[R] (and recompile rclone
from source with CGO enabled if necessary).
See the \c
.UR https://golang.org/pkg/net/#hdr-Name_Resolution
name resolution section in the go docs
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Failed to start auth webserver on Windows
.IP
.EX
Error: config failed to refresh token: failed to start auth webserver: listen tcp 127.0.0.1:53682: bind: An attempt was made to access a socket in a way forbidden by its access permissions.
\&...
yyyy/mm/dd hh:mm:ss Fatal error: config failed to refresh token: failed to start auth webserver: listen tcp 127.0.0.1:53682: bind: An attempt was made to access a socket in a way forbidden by its access permissions.
.EE
.PP
This is sometimes caused by the Host Network Service causing issues with
opening the port on the host.
.PP
A simple solution may be restarting the Host Network Service with eg.
Powershell
.IP
.EX
Restart\-Service hns
.EE
.SS The total size reported in the stats for a sync is wrong and keeps changing
It is likely you have more than 10,000 files that need to be synced.
By default, rclone only gets 10,000 files ahead in a sync so as not to
use up too much memory.
You can change this default with the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#max-backlog-int
\-\-max\-backlog
.UE \c
\ flag.
.SS Rclone is using too much memory or appears to have a memory leak
Rclone is written in Go which uses a garbage collector.
The default settings for the garbage collector mean that it runs when
the heap size has doubled.
.PP
However it is possible to tune the garbage collector to use less memory
by \c
.UR https://dave.cheney.net/tag/gogc
setting GOGC
.UE \c
\ to a lower value, say \f[CR]export GOGC=20\f[R].
This will make the garbage collector work harder, reducing memory size
at the expense of CPU usage.
.PP
The most common cause of rclone using lots of memory is a single
directory with millions of files in.
.PP
Before rclone v1.70 has to load this entirely into memory as rclone
objects.
Each rclone object takes 0.5k\-1k of memory.
There is \c
.UR https://github.com/rclone/rclone/wiki/Big-syncs-with-millions-of-files
a workaround for this
.UE \c
\ which involves a bit of scripting.
.PP
However with rclone v1.70 and later rclone will automatically save
directory entries to disk when a directory with more than \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#list-cutoff
\f[CR]\-\-list\-cutoff\f[R]
.UE \c
\ (1,000,000 by default) entries is detected.
.PP
From v1.70 rclone also has the \c
.UR https://rclone.org/docs/#max-buffer-memory
\-\-max\-buffer\-memory
.UE \c
\ flag which helps particularly when multi\-thread transfers are using
too much memory.
.SS Rclone changes fullwidth Unicode punctuation marks in file names
For example: On a Windows system, you have a file with name
\f[CR]Test：1.jpg\f[R], where \f[CR]：\f[R] is the Unicode fullwidth
colon symbol.
When using rclone to copy this to your Google Drive, you will notice
that the file gets renamed to \f[CR]Test:1.jpg\f[R], where \f[CR]:\f[R]
is the regular (halfwidth) colon.
.PP
The reason for such renames is the way rclone handles different \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#restricted-filenames
restricted filenames
.UE \c
\ on different cloud storage systems.
It tries to avoid ambiguous file names as much and allow moving files
between many cloud storage systems transparently, by replacing invalid
characters with similar looking Unicode characters when transferring to
one storage system, and replacing back again when transferring to a
different storage system where the original characters are supported.
When the same Unicode characters are intentionally used in file names,
this replacement strategy leads to unwanted renames.
Read more under section \c
.UR https://rclone.org/overview/#restricted-filenames-caveats
caveats
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Why does rclone fail to connect over TLS but another client works?
If you see TLS handshake failures (or packet captures show the server
rejecting all offered ciphers), the server/proxy may only support legacy
TLS cipher suites (for example RSA key\-exchange ciphers such as
\f[CR]RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA256\f[R], or old 3DES ciphers).
Recent Go versions (which rclone is built with) have \f[B]removed
insecure ciphers from the default list\f[R], so rclone may refuse to
negotiate them even if other tools still do.
.PP
If you can\(aqt update/reconfigure the server/proxy to support modern
TLS (TLS 1.2/1.3) and ECDHE\-based cipher suites you can re\-enable
legacy ciphers via \f[CR]GODEBUG\f[R]:
.IP \(bu 2
Windows (cmd.exe):
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
set GODEBUG=tlsrsakex=1
rclone copy ...
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Windows (PowerShell):
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
$env:GODEBUG=\(dqtlsrsakex=1\(dq
rclone copy ...
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Linux/macOS:
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
GODEBUG=tlsrsakex=1 rclone copy ...
.EE
.RE
.PP
If the server only supports 3DES, try:
.IP
.EX
GODEBUG=tls3des=1 rclone ...
.EE
.PP
This applies to \f[B]any rclone feature using TLS\f[R] (HTTPS, FTPS,
WebDAV over TLS, proxies with TLS interception, etc.).
Use these workarounds only long enough to get the server/proxy updated.
